items/lem-qftx-global-microlocal-pullback.md:107:1.2 Here is the needed nonstationary estimate, including its dependence on the amplitude. On a fixed compact coordinate set let $\Phi(x;\eta,\xi)=f(x)\cdot\eta-x\cdot\xi$, put $R=|\eta|+|\xi|\ge1$, and suppose $|\nabla_x\Phi|\ge cR$ throughout the amplitude support. Every fixed $x$ derivative of $\nabla\Phi$ is at most $C_\alpha R$. Differentiating the quotient shows that every fixed derivative of $b=\nabla\Phi/(2\pi i|\nabla\Phi|^2)$ is at most $C'_\alpha R^{-1}$. The operator $L=b\cdot\nabla_x$ satisfies $L e^{2\pi i\Phi}=e^{2\pi i\Phi}$. Repeated integration by parts gives $$\left|\int e^{2\pi i\Phi}a(x)\,dx\right|\le C_q R^{-q}\max_{|\alpha|\le q}\sup|\partial^\alpha a|.$$ Indeed $L^*a=-\sum_i\partial_i(b_i a)$, and induction gives a finite sum of derivatives of $a$ through order $q$ times products of $q$ coefficient derivatives, each contributing $R^{-1}$. Compact support removes boundary terms. Constants depend only on finitely many derivatives of $f$, the compact set and $c$, and are uniform for every frequency in the specified region. The same proof with $\xi=0$ applies to $f(x)\cdot\eta$. [given, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-global-microlocal-pullback.md:109:2.1 We construct the smooth approximants rather than assume a distributional regularization theorem. By [F5] choose $b\ge0$ compactly supported and equal to one on a neighborhood of a closed box of positive volume inside the unit ball. Rescale its support into the unit ball. By [F6], $0<\int b<\infty$; put $\rho=b/\int b$ and $\rho_\epsilon(z)=\epsilon^{-n}\rho(z/\epsilon)$. For a compactly supported distribution $v$, define $v_\epsilon(y)=\langle v(z),\rho_\epsilon(y-z)|dz|\rangle$. It is smooth by [F4], supported in the $\epsilon$-neighborhood of $\operatorname{supp}v$, and has common compact support for $0<\epsilon\le\epsilon_0$. Pairing against a test $h$ and using [F4] gives $\langle v_\epsilon,h\rangle=\langle v,h_\epsilon\rangle$, where $h_\epsilon(z)=\int\rho(w)h(z+\epsilon w)dw$. Every derivative of $h_\epsilon-h$ tends uniformly to zero on the common compact support, by uniform continuity of the corresponding derivative of $h$. The finite-order bound proves weak convergence and a common order bound, because the averaging bounds every derivative seminorm of $h_\epsilon$ by the corresponding seminorm of $h$ on a fixed enlarged compact. The same interchange, or Fourier testing, gives $$\widehat{v_\epsilon}(\eta)=\widehat\rho(\epsilon\eta)\widehat v(\eta),\qquad |\widehat\rho(\epsilon\eta)|\le\int\rho=1.$$ Hence all polynomial and regular-cone bounds for $v$ are uniform in $\epsilon$. For a smaller local cutoff $a$, first choose $b_0=1$ near its support, inside a wavefront-regularity localization. For sufficiently small $\epsilon$, $a(\rho_\epsilon*v)=a(\rho_\epsilon*(b_0v))$; step 1.1 gives the uniform regular-cone bound. The finitely many initial terms of a sequence $\epsilon_j\downarrow0$ are smooth and have their own finite rapid bounds. Compactness and finite subdivision into regular base/cone patches handle any compact cutoff/cone pair disjoint from $\operatorname{WF}(v)$. Thus these approximants are controlled on each smaller patch. [A1, F1, F2, F4, F5, F6, step 1.1, construct]
