items/pthm-fd-px06-prediction.md:79:[F3] [[thm-fd-x06]]: under $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, the exact exterior fields solve Stokes, have resultant $6\pi\mu aU$, and are unique only in the stated uniform decay class.
items/pthm-fd-px06-prediction.md:83:1.1 By [F3], the displayed fields are classical on the exterior, solve the force-free Stokes system [A2, F1], satisfy no-slip and the uniform far-field condition, and lie in the stated decay class. To compute the resultant, write $U_t=U-(U\cdot n)n$ on $S_a$. From $u(rn)=A(r)U+B(r)r^2(U\cdot n)n$, the values $A(a)=B(a)=0$, $A'(a)=3/(2a)$ and $B'(a)=-3/(2a^3)$ give $u=0$ and $\partial_nu=(3/(2a))U_t$ at $r=a$. The tangential derivatives of the zero boundary trace vanish, hence $\nabla u=(3/(2a))U_t\otimes n$ there. Thus $(\nabla u)^Tn=0$, $(\nabla u)n=(3/(2a))U_t$, and $p=p_\infty-(3\mu/(2a))(U\cdot n)$ on $S_a$, so $\sigma n=-p_\infty n+(3\mu/(2a))U$. Parameterize the sphere by $\Phi(\theta,\varphi)=a(\sin\theta\cos\varphi,\sin\theta\sin\varphi,\cos\theta)$; its area density is $|\Phi_\theta\times\Phi_\varphi|=a^2\sin\theta$, so $\operatorname{Area}(S_a)=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\pi a^2\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\varphi=4\pi a^2$. The antipodal parameter change $(\theta,\varphi)\mapsto(\pi-\theta,\varphi+\pi\ {\rm mod}\ 2\pi)$ satisfies $\Phi\mapsto-\Phi$, has absolute Jacobian one and preserves $\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\varphi$; thus $\int_{S_a}n\,dS=0$. Integrating the stress expression therefore gives $\int_{S_a}\sigma n\,dS=6\pi\mu aU$. [given, A1, A2, F1, F3, algebra, construct]
items/prop-qftx-helicity-chern-obstruction.md:74:[F3] Under $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, if $M$ is an oriented smooth $n$-manifold with boundary and $\eta$ is a compactly supported smooth $(n-1)$-form, then $\int_Md\eta=\int_{\partial M}j^*\eta$ with outward-normal-first boundary orientation. [[thm-general-stokes-theorem]]
items/ex-nrqm-coulomb-no-full-discrete-eigenbasis.md:73:1.1 [The vector and its coefficients] Since $g$ is compactly supported in $(0,\infty)$ and $Y_{00}$ is bounded, $\psi$ is a finite-block element in $L^2$; by [F1] its polar transform is supported in the $(0,0)$-block with entry $g$, so $\|\psi\|_2=\|g\|_2<\infty$ and $\psi\ne0$. This is (a). [F1, given, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-mellin-incoming-and-outgoing-propagation.md:56:2.1 Smooth functions compactly supported in $\rho$, with smooth angular dependence, are dense for the norm $\|u\|_2+\|\partial_\rho u\|_2$: first multiply by $\chi(\rho/m)$ for a fixed smooth cutoff $\chi=1$ near zero; the derivative error from the cutoff is bounded by $\|\chi'\|_\infty\|u\|_2/m$, and the two tail errors tend to zero. Next convolve in $\rho$ with a compactly supported smooth approximate identity, which converges in $L^2$ for both $u$ and its weak derivative; finally smooth the angular variable in a finite smooth coordinate cover of the sphere with a partition of unity. This angular smoothing converges in both norms because it acts only in $\omega$ and commutes with $\partial_\rho$. Each resulting inverse $T_x$ image belongs to $C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\})$. These images therefore form a graph core. Every $\psi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^3)$ also has $T_x\psi$ and $\partial_\rho T_x\psi$ in $L^2$, by the polar change of variables, and differentiation gives $T_x^*(-i\partial_\rho)T_x\psi=-i(x\cdot\nabla+3/2)\psi$. The larger space $C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^3)$ is consequently a core as well. Countable approximation sequences suffice for these density arguments. [step 1.1, F1, algebra]
items/pthm-emx-exterior-maxwell.md:128:4.1 Take $t_0<t_1$ in $I$ and let $e_0,e_1,e_2$ be the ordered vertices of $\Delta^2$. The product prism has the oriented triangulation $P=\tau_0-\tau_1+\tau_2$, with $\tau_0=[(t_0,e_0),(t_1,e_0),(t_1,e_1),(t_1,e_2)]$, $\tau_1=[(t_0,e_0),(t_0,e_1),(t_1,e_1),(t_1,e_2)]$, and $\tau_2=[(t_0,e_0),(t_0,e_1),(t_0,e_2),(t_1,e_2)]$. In coordinates $(t,u^1,u^2)$, their ordered vertex determinants are $t_1-t_0,-(t_1-t_0),t_1-t_0$, so the coefficients orient all three simplices consistently. Applying the signed-face formula [F7] cancels their internal faces and gives $\partial P=\iota_{t_1\#}\Delta^2-\iota_{t_0\#}\Delta^2-P(\partial\Delta^2)$, where $\iota_t(u)=(t,u)$ and $P(\partial\Delta^2)=P([e_1,e_2])-P([e_0,e_2])+P([e_0,e_1])$, with $P([e_a,e_b])=[(t_0,e_a),(t_1,e_a),(t_1,e_b)]-[(t_0,e_a),(t_0,e_b),(t_1,e_b)]$. For each singular simplex $\sigma$ in $z$, compose these parameter maps with $q_\sigma(t,u)=(t,\sigma(u))$. The top and bottom chains become the time-slice copies of $z$; after summing with the real coefficients of $z$, side terms cancel because $\partial z=0$. Each composite $q=q_\sigma\circ\tau_i$ is a smooth singular 3-simplex into $U$: the affine parameter maps and the smooth-simplex extensions of $\sigma$ give an extension to a neighbourhood, which may be restricted so its image lies in the open set $U$. Hence $q^*F$ is a $C^1$ two-form on a neighbourhood of its standard parameter simplex. Write $F=\frac12F_{\mu\nu}(y)dy^\mu\wedge dy^\nu$ in laboratory coordinates. By [F10], the pullback coefficients are $\eta_{ij}=F_{\mu\nu}(q)\partial_iq^\mu\partial_jq^\nu$, so $(d\eta)_{kij}=\partial_k\eta_{ij}+\partial_i\eta_{jk}+\partial_j\eta_{ki}$. By [F9], the terms differentiating $F_{\mu\nu}$ are $(\partial_\lambda F_{\mu\nu})(q)(\partial_kq^\lambda\partial_iq^\mu\partial_jq^\nu+\partial_iq^\lambda\partial_jq^\mu\partial_kq^\nu+\partial_jq^\lambda\partial_kq^\mu\partial_iq^\nu)$, which is exactly $(dF)_{\lambda\mu\nu}(q)\partial_kq^\lambda\partial_iq^\mu\partial_jq^\nu$. The remaining terms are $F_{\mu\nu}(q)(\partial_{ki}q^\mu\partial_jq^\nu+\partial_iq^\mu\partial_{kj}q^\nu+\partial_{ij}q^\mu\partial_kq^\nu+\partial_jq^\mu\partial_{ik}q^\nu+\partial_{jk}q^\mu\partial_iq^\nu+\partial_kq^\mu\partial_{ji}q^\nu)$. The first and fourth cancel after interchanging $\mu,\nu$ and using $\partial_{ki}q=\partial_{ik}q$; the second and fifth cancel by $\partial_{kj}q=\partial_{jk}q$; the third and sixth cancel by $\partial_{ij}q=\partial_{ji}q$. Each cancellation uses $F_{\mu\nu}=-F_{\nu\mu}$ and [F6]. Thus $d(q^*F)=q^*(dF)=0$ by step 2.1. The $C^1$ Stokes identity [F8] applies to each pullback on the standard parameter 3-simplex, so the boundary integrals sum to zero. The top and bottom terms are $\Phi_z(t_1)$ and $-\Phi_z(t_0)$ because the time-slice pullback of $F=\mathcal B-dt\wedge\mathcal E$ is $\mathcal B$ by [F1]. Finite-chain linearity in [F1] gives $\Phi_z(t_1)-\Phi_z(t_0)=0$. Hence the cycle flux is constant. [F1, F6, F7, F8, F9, F10, step 2.1, given] ∎
items/lem-sr-scalar-field-euler-lagrange.md:51:[F2] Compact-support integration by parts: if $f:\Omega\to\mathbb R$ is $C^1$ and compactly supported and $g:\Omega\to\mathbb R$ is $C^1$, then for each $a$, $\int_\Omega(\partial_af)g\,d^4x=-\int_\Omega f(\partial_ag)\,d^4x$. ([[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]]\,(b))
