items/ex-emx-resonance-outside-coercivity.md:81:2.1 To verify the closure-defined condition in [F2], choose smooth one-dimensional cutoffs chi_delta^x,chi_delta^y,chi_delta^z that vanish within distance delta of their interval endpoints, equal one at distance at least 2delta, take values in [0,1], and satisfy |(chi_delta^.)'|<=C/delta. Put chi_delta=chi_delta^x chi_delta^y chi_delta^z and u_delta=chi_delta u; then u_delta is compactly supported and smooth in Omega. The L^2 norm of u_delta-u tends to zero because its support lies in boundary layers of volume O(delta). Also curl(u_delta-u)=(chi_delta-1)curl u+grad(chi_delta) x u. The first term tends to zero in L^2 by the same shrinking-support bound. In the second term, the z-cutoff derivative is parallel to u and has zero cross product; on the x- and y-boundary layers, |f_mn|<=C delta by the vanishing sine factor, so each remaining product |(chi_delta^.)' f_mn| is uniformly bounded on a set of volume O(delta), and its L^2 norm tends to zero. Hence u_delta->u in the H(curl) graph norm and u lies in H_0(curl;Omega). [F2, step 1.1, construct, algebra]
