items/lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density.md:110:1.2 For $k\in\mathbb Z^D$, put $e_k(x)=\exp(2\pi i k\cdot x/L)$, $Q=[-L/2,L/2]^D$, and $\widehat\Phi(k)=L^{-D}\int_Q\Phi(x)\overline{e_k(x)}\,dx$. These are Riemann integrals by [F7]. Periodic integration by parts in one coordinate has no boundary term: the endpoint product agrees by periodicity, and [F8] converts the integral of a derivative to that endpoint difference. Writing $\Delta=\sum_{j=1}^D\partial_j^2$, applying this identity to $(1-\Delta)^q\Phi$, and using the character derivative from [F6], gives $\widehat{(1-\Delta)^q\Phi}(k) =(1+(2\pi/L)^2|k|^2)^q\widehat\Phi(k)$. The left side is bounded by the volume of $Q$ times the supremum of the continuous function $(1-\Delta)^q\Phi$, so $$ |\widehat\Phi(k)|\le C_{\Phi,q}\bigl(1+|k|^2\bigr)^{-q}. $$ For integer $m\ge1$, the number of lattice points in $m\le|k|_\infty<m+1$ is at most $(2m+3)^D-(2m-1)^D\le C_D(m+1)^{D-1}$, by expanding the two powers; the shell $|k|_\infty<1$ is finite. Thus, for every multi-index $\alpha$, choosing $q$ with $2q>D+|\alpha|$ makes $\sum_k |k|^{|\alpha|}|\widehat\Phi(k)|$ finite. The Fourier series and each derivative series therefore converge absolutely and uniformly on the torus. Integrating each uniformly convergent derivative series along a coordinate segment and using [F8] shows it is the corresponding derivative of the uniformly convergent Fourier sum; iterating over coordinates gives the same assertion for every multi-index. [F6, F7, F8, algebra, algebra]
items/thm-fd-smooth-polytropic-euler-ivp.md:109:1.3 The analytic suppliers [F2]–[F4] apply under the standing Countable Choice assumption [F6, A1]. For integer $m\ge s$, put $Q=\Lambda^m q_N$ with $\Lambda$ the multiplier $(1+|k|^2)^{1/2}$. Pair the projected equation with $Q$. Since $S_N$ is self-adjoint and commutes with $\Lambda$, the projection disappears in the inner product. For each $j$, expand $\Lambda^m(A_j(q_N)\partial_jq_N)=A_j(q_N)\partial_jQ+[\Lambda^m,A_j(q_N)]\partial_jq_N$. Symmetry and periodic integration by parts give $\int Q^{\mathsf T}A_j(q_N)\partial_jQ=-\frac12\int Q^{\mathsf T}(\partial_jA_j(q_N))Q$, whose absolute value is at most $C\|\nabla q_N\|_\infty\|q_N\|_{H^m}^2$. The embedding in [F3] bounds this by $C_m\|q_N\|_{H^s}\|q_N\|_{H^m}^2$. Because each $A_j$ is linear in $q_N$, [F4] bounds all commutator terms by the same expression. Thus $\frac12\frac d{dt}\|q_N\|_{H^m}^2\le C_m\|q_N\|_{H^s}\|q_N\|_{H^m}^2$. [given, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, A1, algebra]
