items/lem-fd-negative-space-nonlinear-bound.md:68:1.1 Put $w_k=(1+L^2|\kappa_k|^2)^r$. For any sequence $c$ with $\sum_kw_k^{-1}|c_k|^2<\infty$, Cauchy–Schwarz gives $|\sum_kc_k\cdot\widehat\varphi(-k)|\le\|c\|_{H^{-r}}(\sum_kw_k|\widehat\varphi(-k)|^2)^{1/2}$. Repeated integration by parts bounds the last factor by a constant times $p_M(\varphi)$ for an integer $M>r+d/2$, because smooth Fourier coefficients decay as $(1+|k|)^{-M}$. Thus the displayed pairing is absolutely convergent and continuous, so defines a periodic distribution; testing on one character recovers each $c_k$, proving injectivity. It agrees with the regular distribution of an $L^2$ field by Fourier truncation and Parseval. Since $\|P_k\|\le1$, coefficient multiplication by $P_k$ is a contraction on $H^{-r}$, preserves real conjugate symmetry, and agrees on $L^2$ with the supplied projection. For $A=a\otimes a\in L^1$, its row divergence is the continuous functional $\langle\operatorname{div}A,\varphi\rangle=-\sum_{i,j}\int A_{ij}\partial_j\varphi_i\,d\mu$, bounded by $\sum_{i,j}\|A_{ij}\|_1p_1(\varphi)$. Now $a_i a_j\in L^1$ and Cauchy–Schwarz gives $|\widehat{a_i a_j}(k)|\le\|a_i\|_2\|a_j\|_2$, hence the Euclidean tensor norm of this coefficient is at most $C_d\|a\|_2^2$. The unprojected divergence coefficient is $b_k=i\sum_j\kappa_{k,j}\widehat{a_{\cdot}a_j}(k)$ and has size at most $C_d|\kappa_k|\|a\|_2^2$. [given, F1, F2, F3, algebra]
