items/rem-local-versus-known-open-global-fluid-scope.md:55:1. **No global-in-time smooth conclusion from this construction.** The existence time decreases as $\|u_0\|_{H^s}$ grows, and the proof supplies no all-time bound for either $\nu>0$ or $\nu=0$. This is a statement about this proof, not a certification of the current global status of fluid equations. On 11 September 2026 the Clay Mathematics Institute announced that the Navier-Stokes Millennium Problem concerning three-dimensional Euclidean space had “apparently been settled” and said that its evaluation process is deliberately unhurried ([CMI announcement](https://www.claymath.org/news/navier-stokes-announcement/)). That announcement was read here; no new announced proof has been audited here, and no implication from a Euclidean, forced or otherwise different problem to the unforced periodic $\mathbb T^3$ equations of this page has been established. The older cited sources describe open problems as of their publication dates; they do not justify an unqualified present-day assertion that all these global questions remain open, nor does an announcement alone establish a theorem in this library.
