items/thm-gr-density-divergence-and-finite-chain-flux.md:102:[L4] Stokes' theorem for smooth singular chains: for a finite smooth singular $k$-chain $c$ — a finite formal real sum of smooth singular $k$-simplices with the signed face differential, the convention of the library definition `def-smooth-singular-chain-and-cochain-complexes` — and a smooth $(k-1)$-form $\omega$, $\int_c d\omega=\int_{\partial c}\omega$; integration over chains is additive in the chain ([[thm-stokes-theorem-for-smooth-singular-chains]], [[def-integral-of-a-form-over-a-smooth-singular-simplex]], [[def-gr-metric-volume-form-and-current-flux]] clause (b)).
items/thm-gr-density-divergence-and-finite-chain-flux.md:110:**Proof technique:** direct; compute the derivative of the current form in an oriented chart, apply Stokes' theorem to chains, and evaluate the pullback on a non-null face by decomposing the current into normal and tangential parts.
items/thm-gr-density-divergence-and-finite-chain-flux.md:116:2.1 (Stokes flux balance.) Let $C$ be a smooth singular $4$-chain and apply [L4] with $k=4$ and $\omega=\iota_J\mathrm{vol}_g$: $\int_{\partial C}\iota_J\mathrm{vol}_g=\int_C d(\iota_J\mathrm{vol}_g)=\int_C(\nabla_aJ^a)\mathrm{vol}_g$ by step 1.1. By the definition of $\Phi_J$ as the integral over a chain this is $\Phi_J(\partial C)=\int_C(\nabla_aJ^a)\mathrm{vol}_g$. [step 1.1, L4, given]
items/lem-tgr-spectral-thermometer.md:132:[F5] Absolute Fubini applies to the compactly supported time integrals used to form the autocorrelation of $h$. [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]
items/lem-tgr-spectral-thermometer.md:140:2.1 Since $h\in C_c^\infty$ by [F6], each derivative of $\widehat h$ is obtained by transforming a compactly supported smooth function $(-iu)^j h(u)$; repeated integration by parts shows every such derivative decreases faster than any inverse power. Hence $\int|z||\widehat h(z)|^2dz<\infty$. Plancherel in [F4], followed by $z=2\pi\xi$, yields $$\int_{\mathbb R}|\widehat h(z)|^2dz=2\pi\|h\|_2^2.$$ Changing variables $z=\mathsf T(\nu-\omega)$ in 1.1 therefore gives $$\frac{R_{\mathsf T}^{(C)}(\nu)}{\mathsf T\|h\|_2^2} =\frac{1}{2\pi\|h\|_2^2}\int_{\mathbb R} S(\nu-z/\mathsf T)|\widehat h(z)|^2\,dz.$$ [A1, F4, F6, step 1.1, given, algebra]
items/prop-qftx-free-euclidean-reflection-positive.md:120:2.2 By step 1.1, the Countable Choice hypotheses of [F6] and [F7] are satisfied. Put $\omega_{\mathbf k}=\sqrt{|\mathbf k|^2+m^2}>0$. The function $K_\omega(u)=e^{-\omega|u|}/(2\omega)$ is integrable. Splitting its Fourier transform into the two half-lines gives $\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-ip_0u}K_\omega(u)\,du =\frac{1}{2\omega}\left(\frac{1}{\omega+ip_0}+\frac{1}{\omega-ip_0}\right) =\frac{1}{p_0^2+\omega^2}.$ By injectivity of the Fourier automorphism on tempered distributions [F6], this identifies the inverse kernel of $(p_0^2+\omega^2)^{-1}$; the inverse normalization is $1/(2\pi)$ under the stated convention [F7]. Since $f,g$ are compactly supported in $t>0$, reflecting the first time variable changes $t-s$ to $-(t+s)$; its absolute value is $t+s$. Integrating the spatial variables by their Fourier transforms yields the stated formula. The exchanges of integrals are valid: $\int_{\mathbb R}(p_0^2+\omega_{\mathbf k}^2)^{-1}dp_0=\pi/\omega_{\mathbf k}\le\pi/m$, while repeated spatial integration by parts gives rapid $\mathbf k$-decay of $\widehat f(t,\mathbf k)$ and $\widehat g(s,\mathbf k)$ uniformly on their compact time supports. [F6, F7, step 1.1, given, algebra]
items/pthm-emg-xs-dyonic-model.md:77:In particular $q_e^2+q_m^2=q_0^2$, so the metric is unchanged. On each sphere-containing full-sphere exterior region, $\int_{S_r}\mathcal F_\beta=4\pi q_m$; hence a global smooth real one-form potential for $\mathcal F_\beta$ there is impossible if $q_m\ne0$, by finite-chain Stokes. In the nonrotating case $a=0$, the corresponding dyonic RN field also has the explicit north/south patch potentials and exact global-potential criterion of [[prop-emg-xs-rn-local-gauges]]. No charge quantization, ADM/Komar identification or observed object is asserted.
items/pthm-emg-xs-dyonic-model.md:117:The global potential in [F8] and Stokes' theorem [F5] give $\int_{S_r}\mathcal F_0=\int_{S_r}d\mathcal A_0=0$. By [F7], $\star_g^2=-1$ on two-forms, so integrating $\mathcal F_\beta=\cos\beta\,\mathcal F_0+\sin\beta\star_g\mathcal F_0$ and its Hodge dual directly yields $q_e=q_0\cos\beta$ and $q_m=q_0\sin\beta$. Therefore $q_e^2+q_m^2=q_0^2(\cos^2\beta+\sin^2\beta)=q_0^2$, and every KN metric coefficient depending on charge through this sum is unchanged. This local integral calculation proves the stated flux values. [F2, F5, F7, F8, step 1.1, algebra]
items/lem-tgr-constant-linear-energy-causality.md:93:2.2 For periodic smooth data, integration by parts in a coordinate with maximal $|k_j|$ gives $|\widehat U_k(0)|\le C_N(1+|k|)^{-N}$ for every $N$. The series $U(t,x)=\sum_{k\in\mathbb Z^3}e^{2\pi i k\cdot x/L}E(t,k/L)\widehat U_k(0)$ and every differentiated series converge uniformly on compact time intervals, by the same matrix-exponential derivative bounds and the lattice-shell estimate that a unit-width shell in $\mathbb Z^3$ has $O((1+r)^2)$ points. Hence it is a global smooth periodic solution and solves the PDE termwise. The modewise $M$-estimate from step 1.2 yields contraction of every weighted coefficient norm. For $s>3/2$, Cauchy–Schwarz and $M\ge\lambda_{\min}(M)I$ give $\sum_k|\widehat U_k(t)|\le\lambda_{\min}(M)^{-1/2}(\sum_k(1+|k/L|^2)^{-s})^{1/2}(\sum_k(1+|k/L|^2)^s\widehat U_k(t)^*M\widehat U_k(t))^{1/2}$; the first sum converges by the shell bound, proving the asserted uniform norm estimate. Integrating the equation against each periodic exponential shows that every solution coefficient solves the same finite-dimensional ODE, proving uniqueness. [step 1.2, algebra]
items/def-emg-classical-charged-scalar-finite-regularity-system.md:78:For a continuous compactly supported real top form $\omega$, define
items/def-emg-classical-charged-scalar-finite-regularity-system.md:134:3.1 A real $C^3$ function $\chi$ gives $A'=A+d\chi\in C^2$ and $\varphi'=e^{i\lambda\chi}\varphi\in C^2$. The product rule gives $P'=e^{i\lambda\chi}P$ and $F'=F$, so the action density is unchanged. Pullback by a smooth orientation-preserving diffeomorphism also preserves the classical class and the density formulas. For compactly supported transformations, step 1.2 and [F3] give invariance of the relative action. These are mathematical invariances, not a physical adoption or a solution-existence theorem. [given, F3, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
