items/thm-fd-x07.md:51:In dimension three, let $\rho_0>0$, $\nu=\mu/\rho_0$, and suppose already-existing time-independent fields $u_{\rm NS},u_{\rm S}\in C^2(\overline\Omega;\mathbb R^3)$ and $p_{\rm NS},p_{\rm S}\in C^1(\overline\Omega)$ are divergence-free, have the same boundary velocity, and satisfy the constant-density incompressible Navier-Stokes and Stokes equations $\rho_0(u_{\rm NS}\cdot\nabla)u_{\rm NS}+\nabla p_{\rm NS}-\mu\Delta u_{\rm NS}=0$ and $\nabla p_{\rm S}-\mu\Delta u_{\rm S}=0$. If $f=-\rho_0(u_{\rm NS}\cdot\nabla)u_{\rm NS}$, then the preceding estimate applies to $w=u_{\rm NS}-u_{\rm S}$ and $q=p_{\rm NS}-p_{\rm S}$ whenever the stated Poincare bound holds. This is an a-posteriori residual estimate for already-given fields; it asserts no solution existence, no exterior-domain bound, and no error bound from Reynolds number alone. The constant $C_\Omega=h$ established for the periodic channel is not transferred to an exterior domain.
items/thm-fd-x07.md:57:[Tong, Chapter 2, Couette and Stokes-flow context; not the residual estimate](https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/fluid-mechanics/fluids2.pdf)
items/thm-fd-x07.md:61:**Given:** The fields, domain, residual equation, zero trace, and Poincare bound in [A1]. For the comparison clause, use the exact formal Navier-Stokes/Stokes conventions in [F1].
items/thm-fd-x07.md:65:[F1] [[def-fd-x00]] on $fd-x01$ defines, for constants $\rho_0>0$ and $\mu>0$ with $\nu=\mu/\rho_0$, the pointwise constant-density incompressible equation $u_t+(u\cdot\nabla)u+\rho_0^{-1}\nabla p-\nu\Delta u=0$, $\nabla\cdot u=0$. Its steady Stokes system is $\rho_0^{-1}\nabla p-\nu\Delta u=0$, $\nabla\cdot u=0$, and omits both the time derivative and convection.
items/lem-csm-frontier-two-velocity-contraction.md:102:1.1 For each $n\in\mathbb Z$, set $v=c/\epsilon$, $\alpha=\lambda/\epsilon^2$, $M_n=\begin{pmatrix}-i\kappa_n v-\alpha&\alpha\\ \alpha&i\kappa_n v-\alpha\end{pmatrix}$, and $U_n(0)=(\widehat f_{+,0}(n),\widehat f_{-,0}(n))^{\mathsf T}$. Realify $z\in\mathbb C^2$ by $\mathcal Rz=(\operatorname{Re}z_1,\operatorname{Re}z_2,\operatorname{Im}z_1,\operatorname{Im}z_2)\in\mathbb R^4$, and for $M=P+iQ$ put $\mathcal RM=\begin{pmatrix}P&-Q\\Q&P\end{pmatrix}$. Direct coordinate multiplication gives $\mathcal R(Mz)=(\mathcal RM)\mathcal Rz$ and $|\mathcal Rz|_2^2=|z_1|^2+|z_2|^2$. By [F6], $\mathbb R^4$ is Banach; [F7] applied to each row bounds every real $4\times4$ matrix as an operator, and [F8]–[F9] specify the Euclidean and operator norms. By [F5], $\mathcal RU_n(t):=e^{t\mathcal RM_n}\mathcal RU_n(0)$ defines a solution of the realified mode IVP for all $t\ge0$, and pulling back by $\mathcal R$ defines $U_n(t)$. The matrix-valued ODE supplier [F4] gives uniqueness of this vector solution on each compact interval $[0,T]$: embed any vector solution as the first column of a $4\times4$ matrix solution with the other columns zero, and apply [F4]. For any smooth solution of the PDE, integration by parts in real and imaginary parts separately, using [F3], gives these same Fourier mode equations, so the constructed modes are the only possible ones. Define the candidate pair by the Fourier series $F^\epsilon(t,x):=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}U_n(t)e_n(x)$, with $e_n$ from [F1]. Since real initial data satisfy $U_{-n}(0)=\overline{U_n(0)}$ and $M_{-n}=\overline{M_n}$, uniqueness gives $U_{-n}(t)=\overline{U_n(t)}$, so this series is real. The skew-Hermitian transport part contributes zero to the derivative of $|U_n|^2$, and direct multiplication of the collision matrix gives $(|U_n|^2)'=-2\alpha|\widehat f_+^\epsilon(n,t)-\widehat f_-^\epsilon(n,t)|^2\le0$. For every integer $r\ge0$ and $q>r+1$, periodicity and [F3] give $\widehat{\partial_x^q f_{\pm,0}}(n)=(i\kappa_n)^q\widehat f_{\pm,0}(n)$ by repeated integration by parts; hence $|\widehat f_{\pm,0}(n)|\le |\kappa_n|^{-q}\int_0^L|\partial_x^qf_{\pm,0}(x)|\,dm_L(x)$ for $n\ne0$. Grouping $2^j\le |n|<2^{j+1}$ then shows $\sum_n(1+|\kappa_n|)^r|U_n(0)|<\infty$. The mode bound $|U_n(t)|\le|U_n(0)|$ and $|M_n|=O(1+|n|)$ imply absolute uniform convergence on every compact time interval of the Fourier series and of each differentiated series, since each space derivative contributes a factor $i\kappa_n$ and each time derivative a factor $M_n$. Fourier orthonormality [F2] gives the series coefficients $U_n$; their mode equations and the uniformly convergent differentiated series then verify the PDE termwise. At $t=0$ the series agrees with the prescribed smooth data by Fourier completeness [F2], first in $L^2$ and hence pointwise by continuity. Thus it is a global smooth solution of the Cauchy problem. Any other smooth solution has the same mode equations and initial coefficients; [A1] and [F2] make the solutions equal as $L^2(\mathbb T_L,dm_L;\mathbb C^2)$ classes, hence almost everywhere. Both solutions are smooth and therefore continuous in $x$. If their difference were nonzero at some $x_0$, continuity would give a nonempty open arc on which its norm is bounded below by a positive constant; every nonempty open arc has positive normalized Lebesgue measure $dm_L$, contradicting equality almost everywhere. Thus they agree pointwise on $\mathbb T_L$. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7, F8, F9, given, algebra]
items/thm-tdmath-regular-energy-shell-measure.md:85:4.1 Fix a closed subset $F$ of a nonempty shell. If $F=\varnothing$, both measures assign it zero. Otherwise let $\chi$ be a continuous compactly supported cutoff equal to one on the compact shell; the same finite-ball bump construction used in 1.1 supplies it. In the ambient band set $g_n(x)=\chi(x)\max\{0,1-n\,\operatorname{dist}(x,F)\}$. By [F7], $g_n$ is continuous, $0\le g_n\le1$, and its restriction to $\Sigma_E$ converges pointwise to $\mathbf1_F$. Because $\phi_t$ maps the shell onto itself, $g_n\circ\phi_t$ converges there to $\mathbf1_{\phi_t^{-1}F}$. Apply step 3.1 to each $g_n$ and use dominated convergence [F6] for the finite shell measure from step 2.2; this gives $\mu_E(\phi_t^{-1}F)=\mu_E(F)$ for every closed $F\subseteq\Sigma_E$. Closed sets form a pi-system generating the shell Borel sigma-algebra, and both $\mu_E$ and its pushforward have the same finite total mass because $\phi_t$ maps the shell onto itself. [F5, F6, F7, step 2.2, step 3.1]
