items/pthm-fd-px10-prediction.md:87:[F2] [[def-fd-x00]]: the formal constant-density incompressible Navier–Stokes system is $\partial_tu+(u\cdot\nabla)u+\rho_0^{-1}\nabla p-(\mu/\rho_0)\Delta u=0$, $\operatorname{div}u=0$, with componentwise vector Laplacian. This formal definition asserts no physical solution.
items/lem-qftx-tempered-tensor-products.md:84:3.1 Define $w(\Phi)=u(h)$. Applying the bound in step 1.1 to the finitely many $x$ seminorms with indices at most $M_u$, and then the displayed estimate in step 2.1, bounds $|w(\Phi)|$ by a finite constant times the maximum of finitely many product Schwartz seminorms of $\Phi$. Hence $w$ is continuous on $\mathcal S(\mathbb R^{p+q})$. For $\Phi=\varphi\otimes\psi$, the inner pairing is $\varphi(x)v(\psi)$, giving the claimed product value; on compactly supported tests the formula is the same iterated pairing as [F6]. [F1, F3, F6, step 1.1, step 2.1, given, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-tempered-tensor-products.md:86:4.1 Finite sums of Schwartz product tests are dense in $\mathcal S(\mathbb R^{p+q})$. Indeed, choose $\chi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^p)$ and $\eta\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^q)$ equal to one near the unit balls by [F4], and set $\chi_R(x)=\chi(x/R)$ and $\eta_R(y)=\eta(y/R)$. The product rule and rapid decay show $\chi_R(x)\eta_R(y)\Phi(x,y)\to\Phi(x,y)$ in every product Schwartz seminorm: the undifferentiated tails lie outside balls of radius proportional to $R$, and each differentiated cutoff contributes a factor $R^{-j}$ on the same tails. For fixed $R$, [F5] approximates this compactly supported smooth function by finite sums of product tests with common compact support; on that support, uniform convergence of all derivatives implies convergence in every Schwartz seminorm. Thus any two continuous functionals with the same product values agree on a dense subspace and hence everywhere. The same density argument, iterated over finitely many factors, shows that different parenthesizations of the constructed tensor product agree; this also agrees with the compact-test associativity in [F6]. [F1, F4, F5, step 3.1] ∎ 
