items/thm-fd-bounded-global-weak-existence.md:122:5.1 For a test field taking values in a fixed finite span of the $e_j$, the Galerkin identity is exact for all sufficiently large $N$. Uniform coefficient convergence passes the time and initial terms, strong tensor convergence from step 4.1 passes the transport term, and weak convergence in $L^2V$ passes viscosity. To pass to any compactly supported $C^1$ curve $\phi:[0,\infty)\to V$, let $Q_K$ be the $V$-orthogonal projection onto $\operatorname{span}(e_1,\ldots,e_K)$. It is a contraction and converges strongly to the identity by [F2]; finite nets of the compact images of $\phi$ and $\phi_t$ make this convergence uniform in time. The nonlinear error is bounded by the uniform $L^{4/3}_tV^*$ norm from [F4] times $\|\phi-Q_K\phi\|_{L^4_tV}$; the time and viscosity errors tend to zero by the $L^\infty H$ and $L^2V$ bounds. Thus the full weak identity holds. [given, F2, F4, step 2.1, step 4.1]
