items/prop-fd-pressure-reconstruction.md:102:The coefficients are bounded in $k$ by $C_d\|u(t)\|_2^2$. For a smooth spacetime test $\phi$, repeated spatial integration by parts makes $\widehat\phi(k,t)$ decay faster than any power of $|k|$, uniformly with each time derivative on its compact support. Therefore $\langle\pi,\phi\rangle=\int\sum_k\widehat\pi(k,t)\widehat\phi(-k,t)dt$ converges absolutely and is bounded by a constant times one finite-order seminorm $p_{K,m}$. Its absolute-value seminorm belongs to the family defining the test topology, so it defines a spacetime distribution with zero spatial mean. [given, F1, F3, F4]
items/prop-fd-pressure-reconstruction.md:106:3.1 If $\pi_1,\pi_2$ are two such mean-zero distributions, their difference $Q$ has zero spatial gradient. Its $k$th spatial Fourier coefficient, a distribution in time, therefore vanishes for every $k\ne0$ since $i\kappa_k\ne0$; its zero coefficient vanishes by the mean-zero normalization. For any compactly time-supported smooth periodic test $\phi(t,x)$, repeated integration by parts in $x$ makes its spatial Fourier coefficients and all their time derivatives decay faster than every power of $|k|$, uniformly on the compact time support. Thus its rectangular Fourier partial sums converge to $\phi$ in the smooth test-function topology. Continuity of $Q$ and vanishing of all its spatial Fourier coefficients give $\langle Q,\phi\rangle=0$. Hence $Q=0$ as a spacetime distribution, proving uniqueness. [given, F2, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
items/prop-qft-four-dimensional-log-scale-anomaly.md:114:1.2 Let $\sigma_3$ be the polar surface measure on $S^3$. By [F7]–[F9] and Tonelli, $$\pi^2=\int_{\mathbb R^4}e^{-|x|^2}\,d^4x=\sigma_3(S^3)\int_0^\infty e^{-r^2}r^3\,dr=\frac{\sigma_3(S^3)}2.$$ The last integral is $1/2$ by $s=r^2$ and integration by parts. Therefore $\sigma_3(S^3)=2\pi^2$. [F5, F6, F7, F8, F9, algebra]
items/prop-qft-four-dimensional-log-scale-anomaly.md:118:2.2 Subtracting the two definitions gives $$T_\mu(\varphi)-T_\nu(\varphi)=-\frac{\varphi(0)}{16\pi^4}\int_{\mathbb R^4\setminus\{0\}}\frac{\chi(\mu x)-\chi(\nu x)}{|x|^4}\,d^4x.$$ The difference vanishes on a fixed neighborhood of $0$ and outside a fixed compact set, so it is absolutely integrable. Apply [F7] separately to its positive and negative parts; the integral is $\int_{S^3}\int_0^\infty[\chi(\mu r\omega)-\chi(\nu r\omega)]\,dr/r\,d\sigma_3(\omega)$. For $\mu$ in a compact interval $[\mu_0,\mu_1]\subset(0,\infty)$ choose $0<a<b$ so every first or second derivative of $\chi$ vanishes unless $a\le|y|\le b$. Writing $s=\log\mu$ and $g(s,r,\omega)=\chi(e^s r\omega)$, the first and second $s$-derivatives vanish unless $a/\mu_1\le r\le b/\mu_0$. On this annulus, $|\partial_s^2g|\le b\|\nabla\chi\|_\infty+b^2\|D^2\chi\|_\infty=:M$, by the chain rule; these suprema are finite because $\chi$ is smooth and compactly supported. For $s$ in a smaller compact interval and $h$ sufficiently small, the one-variable Taylor estimate gives $|(g(s+h,r,\omega)-g(s,r,\omega))/h-\partial_sg(s,r,\omega)|\le M|h|/2$ uniformly in $r,\omega$. The annulus has finite $dr/r$ measure and $S^3$ has finite measure by step 1.2, so integrating this uniform error proves differentiation under the polar integral. For each $\omega$, $$\int_0^\infty\frac{\partial}{\partial\log\mu}\chi(\mu r\omega)\,\frac{dr}{r} =\int_0^\infty\nabla\chi(s\omega)\cdot\omega\,ds =\chi(\infty\omega)-\chi(0)=-1,$$ where $s=\mu r$, $\chi=1$ near $0$, and $\chi$ is compactly supported. Since the difference integral vanishes at $\mu=\nu$, its value is $-\sigma_3(S^3)\log(\mu/\nu)$. With step 1.2 this proves the displayed formula for $T_\mu-T_\nu$. [F2, F7, F12, step 1.2, algebra]
items/lem-emg-fx-local-linear-wave-control.md:121:3.1 Differentiate $\mathcal E_k$ and substitute the differentiated system from step 2.2. Symmetry and periodic integration by parts give $-2\int_{\mathbb T^3}w_\alpha^TA^i\partial_iw_\alpha\,dx =\int_{\mathbb T^3}w_\alpha^T(\partial_iA^i)w_\alpha\,dx,$ with no boundary term. All coefficient, lower-order, commutator and source terms are bounded by step 2.2 and Cauchy–Schwarz. For smooth $u$, classical derivatives are its weak derivatives in [F6]. Parseval with the normalized measure $d\mu=(2\pi)^{-3}dx$ gives $\sum_{|\alpha|\le k}\|\partial^\alpha u\|_{L^2(dx)}^2\asymp_k\|u\|_{H^k}^2$: at frequency $\ell$, the polynomial $\sum_{|\alpha|\le k}|\ell^\alpha|^2$ is bounded above by a constant times $(1+|\ell|^2)^k$; for $|\ell|\le1$ its $\alpha=0$ term gives a lower bound, and for $|\ell|>1$ choose $j$ with $|\ell_j|\ge|\ell|/\sqrt3$ and use the $\alpha_j=k$ term. Thus this proof establishes equivalence of the item's Fourier norm and the supplier's weak-derivative norm; the bounds $aI\le A^0\le bI$ therefore make $\mathcal E_k$ equivalent to the Fourier norm used in the Statement. The differentiated system now gives $\frac{d}{dt}\mathcal E_k(t)\le C\mathcal E_k(t) +C\mathcal E_k(t)^{1/2}\|s(t)\|_{H^k}.$ Here $C$ depends on $k$ and the stated coefficient bounds. [A1, given, F6, step 2.2, algebra]
