items/pthm-qft-scalar-free-properties.md:157:1.2 For a Schwartz test, integration by parts transfers $\Box-\mu^2$ from
items/thm-qftx-scalar-eg-induction.md:135:[F11] [[lem-qftx-balanced-local-functional-representatives]]: every local functional has a unique finite balanced-jet representative, supported in its actual functional support; the statement specifies the proper-orthochronous Poincare action on fields and functionals and proves equivariance of the balanced decomposition. Extensions prescribed on balanced labels extend consistently to general jet labels by differentiation and integration by parts.
items/thm-qftx-scalar-eg-induction.md:161:6.1 For each $t_\Gamma$ in relative dimension $d=4(n-1)$, proceed as follows. If $\delta_\Gamma=-\infty$, choose a compact cutoff $\chi=1$ near zero and use the no-subtraction dyadic series $T(\varphi)=t((1-\chi)\varphi)+\sum_{k\ge0}t((\chi(2^k\cdot)-\chi(2^{k+1}\cdot))\varphi)$. Fix a compact annulus $K\subset\mathbb R^d\setminus\{0\}$ and a real $\omega<d$. On the complete fixed-support test space $\mathcal D_K$, put $L_\rho(\psi)=\rho^\omega(t_\Gamma)_\rho(\psi)$. Since $\operatorname{sd}_0(t_\Gamma)=-\infty$, for every $\omega$ there is a decay exponent $\eta<\omega$, so $L_\rho(\psi)\to0$ for each fixed $\psi$. On every compact scale interval $[\epsilon,1]$ the scaled tests vary continuously in one fixed-support test space, so $\sup_{0<\rho\le1}|L_\rho(\psi)|<\infty$. The closed sets $E_N=\{\psi:\sup_\rho|L_\rho(\psi)|\le N\}$ cover $\mathcal D_K$. Completeness and Baire under DC, supplied by the already assumed AC, give $\psi_0\in\mathcal D_K$, an integer $m\ge0$, and $a>0$ with $\psi_0+\{\zeta:p_m(\zeta)<a\}\subset E_N$ for some positive integer $N$. Subtraction gives $|L_\rho(\zeta)|\le2N$ there; scaling $\zeta=a\psi/(2p_m(\psi))$ yields $|L_\rho(\psi)|\le(4N/a)p_m(\psi)$ when $p_m(\psi)>0$, while $p_m(\psi)=0$ implies $\psi=0$. Hence $|(t_\Gamma)_\rho(\psi)|\le C\rho^{-\omega}p_m(\psi)$ uniformly. This is the fixed-annulus Baire proof, with the arbitrarily negative decay exponent replacing the finite-real-degree hypothesis. Its $k$th shell is bounded by $C_\varphi2^{-k(d-\omega)}$, proving convergence and continuity. For the scaled test $\rho^{-d}\varphi(\cdot/\rho)$ only shells of radius $\lambda\le C_\varphi\rho$ occur, and their sum is $O(\rho^{-\omega})$. The same series is independent of $\omega$, so arbitrarily low $\omega$ prove degree $-\infty$; telescoping on punctured tests proves it extends $t$. Otherwise [F7] applies with its finite real degree. Choose the extension linearly on a basis of the balanced labels, and extend its total-derivative labels by [F11]. For any fixed finite jet order this uses a finite-dimensional polynomial space; compatible bases over all finite orders give a linear extension on their algebraic union. The graph and derivative bounds remain finite because total differentiation adds only its finite order. Descendant coefficients obey these engineering upper bounds, including any forced contact terms, rather than an incompatible requirement to preserve the exact degree of a vanished punctured derivative combination. The extension has degree $\delta_\Gamma$ on each freely extended balanced graph coefficient. For the independent balanced coefficients of finite real degree [F8] gives the stated finite-jet ambiguity; for degree $-\infty$, [F10] implies uniqueness because a nonzero difference supported at zero would have degree at least $d$. Lift the chosen relative extension back to $\mathbb M^n$ by translation: in coordinates $z_i=x_i-x_n$ and $y=x_n$, pair the relative distribution with $z\mapsto\int_{\mathbb M}\Psi(y+z_1,\ldots,y+z_{n-1},y)\,d^4y$. The inner function is a compactly supported smooth test by differentiation under a compact integral. This defines a translation-covariant distribution extending the off-diagonal coefficient. Apply these extensions to the numerical family $\nu_n^0$ from step 5.1, obtaining a multilinear map $\nu_n^1$ from actual local functionals to $\mathbb C\llbracket\hbar\rrbracket$. Its density-label kernels satisfy the numerical derivative identities by construction; thus [F11] makes it well-defined on the quotient by integration by parts. [A1, F1, F4, F5, F7, F8, F10, F11, F12, step 1.3, step 5.1, construct]
items/prop-fd-fluid-equation-scaling.md:44:First, for $T>0$ and either $\Omega=\mathbb R^d$ or $\Omega=Q_\ell$, let $u\in C^\infty([0,T]\times\Omega;\mathbb R^d)$ and $\pi\in C^\infty([0,T]\times\Omega;\mathbb R)$ be a velocity-pressure pair solving the unforced incompressible Navier–Stokes system
items/prop-fd-fluid-equation-scaling.md:83:1.1 For the Navier–Stokes scaling, the chain rule gives $(u_\lambda)_t=\lambda^3u_t(\lambda^2t,\lambda x)$, $(u_\lambda\cdot\nabla)u_\lambda=\lambda^3(u\cdot\nabla)u(\lambda^2t,\lambda x)$, $\nabla\pi_\lambda=\lambda^3\nabla\pi(\lambda^2t,\lambda x)$, and $\Delta u_\lambda=\lambda^3\Delta u(\lambda^2t,\lambda x)$. Also $\operatorname{div}u_\lambda=\lambda^2\operatorname{div}u$. Thus the full equation and divergence constraint retain the same viscosity. [given, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-intervals-dirichlet-diagonal-domain-identification.md:85:3.1 [Endpoint traces and second derivative] With $u\in D(A_D)$, $Du=w$, and the partial sums $u_N$ of step 2.1, the trace bound of [[lem-nrqm-intervals-endpoint-trace-and-half-line-decay]] clause (a) gives $|u_N^*(x)-u^*(x)|\le L^{-1/2}\|u_N-u\|+L^{1/2}\|Du_N-Du\|\qquad(x\in[0,L]),$ and the right side tends to $0$ because $u_N\to u$ and $Du_N\to Du$ in $L^2$ by [F1] and step 1.1; hence $u_N^*\to u^*$ uniformly and, since $u_N^*(0)=u_N^*(L)=0$, also $u^*(0)=u^*(L)=0$. To identify the weak derivative of $w$, put $w_N:=\sum_{n\le N}c_nDe_n$ and $v_N:=\sum_{n\le N}c_nD^2e_n=-\sum_{n\le N}\lambda_nc_ne_n$. By step 1.1, $w_N\to w$ in $L^2$; by [F2] and $\sum_n\lambda_n^2|c_n|^2<\infty$, $v_N\to-A_Du$ in $L^2$. For each $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(0,L)$, integration by parts [F3] applied to $De_n$ and $\varphi$ gives $\int_0^Lw_N\overline{\varphi'}=-\int_0^Lv_N\overline\varphi$. Passing to the two $L^2$ limits proves $Dw=-A_Du$. Hence $Du=w\in H^1(0,L)$, $D^2u=-A_Du$, and $u\in D(B)$ with $Bu=-D^2u=A_Du$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, F1, F2, F3]
