items/thm-fd-periodic-pressure-recovery.md:81:3.1 If another mean-zero periodic distribution $\widetilde\pi$ gives the same decomposition, then $\nabla S=0$ for $S=\widetilde\pi-\pi$. The derivative convention implies $\widehat{\partial_jS}(k)=ik_j\widehat S(k)$, so for each $k\ne0$ the vector equation $ik\widehat S(k)=0$ gives $\widehat S(k)=0$; the zero mode also vanishes because $S$ has mean zero. To see that this forces $S=0$ as a distribution, fix $\phi\in C^\infty(Q_d)$. For every integer $N\ge1$, periodic integration by parts gives $w_k^{2N}|\widehat\phi(k)|\le\|(1-\Delta)^N\phi\|_{L^1(d\mu)}$. Choosing $N$ arbitrarily large, the lattice shell bound $\#\{k\in\mathbb Z^d:n\le|k|<n+1\}\le C_d(n+1)^{d-1}$ follows by placing disjoint unit cubes at those lattice points: their union lies in an annulus of fixed enlarged width, whose volume is $O_d((n+1)^{d-1})$; the $n=0$ shell is finite. This bound shows that the Fourier series of $\phi$ and each of its differentiated series converge absolutely and uniformly. Its sum is therefore a smooth function with the same Fourier coefficients as $\phi$; by [F5] the two functions agree in $L^2$, hence everywhere by continuity. Thus finite Fourier sums converge to $\phi$ in every seminorm $p_m$. The distribution $S$ vanishes on every character and hence on every finite Fourier sum; its continuity gives $\langle S,\phi\rangle=0$. Since $\phi$ was arbitrary, $S=0$, proving uniqueness among all mean-zero periodic distributions. [given, F1, F2, F5, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
items/lem-fd-rectangular-test-divergence-inverse.md:37:      locator: "Chapter 6 §6.1, PDF pp. 55–56: Stokes pressure and inf–sup context; the explicit rectangle right inverse is proved here."
items/cex-gr-negative-sound-speed-derivative-breaks-evolution.md:75:2.1 (Failure of fixed-Sobolev continuity on smooth modes.) Fix $\tau>0$ and an integer $r\ge0$. In step 1.1 take $A_n=(1+n^2)^{-r/2}e^{-an\tau/2}$. Under the derivative-sum norm of [L3], $\|\cos(nx^1)\|_{H^r}^2=\tfrac12\sum_{j=0}^r n^{2j}$, which lies between positive constants times $(1+n^2)^r$, uniformly for $n\ge1$. Consequently the initial H^r norm tends to zero, whereas the density H^r norm at $\tau$ is at least a positive constant times $e^{an\tau/2}$ and tends to infinity. Each datum and solution is a single trigonometric polynomial and hence smooth; indeed the data tend to zero in every fixed derivative norm because exponential decay dominates every power. Any other classical solution of the same datum has the same coefficient at $(n,0,0)$ by step 1.1. For any such density lying in $H^r$, integration by parts gives $n^r|\widehat f(n,0,0)|=|\widehat{D_1^rf}(n,0,0)|\le\|D_1^rf\|_2\le\|f\|_{H^r}$. The coefficient inequality follows from $|\widehat h(k)|\le\int|h|\le\|h\|_2$; the second bound follows by expanding $0\le\int(|h|-\int|h|)^2$ on the normalized probability space. Consequently every classical solution choice has density $H^r$ norm at least a positive constant times $e^{an\tau/2}$. Thus no choice of a solution operator, even restricted to these smooth trigonometric data, can be continuous at zero from $H^r$ data to $H^r$ values at any fixed positive time. [step 1.1, L3, construct, algebra]
items/cex-gr-negative-sound-speed-derivative-breaks-evolution.md:77:2.2 (A genuinely smooth datum with no linearized Sobolev solution.) Set $f_0(x)=\sum_{n\ge1}e^{-\sqrt n}\cos(nx^1)$ and $v_0=0$. For every integer $q\ge0$, $\sum_{n\ge1}n^qe^{-\sqrt n}<\infty$: group the terms with $j^2\le n<(j+1)^2$, whose contribution is at most $(2j+1)(j+1)^{2q}e^{-j}$, a summable polynomial times a geometric sequence. Thus each spatial derivative series converges uniformly; the fundamental theorem of calculus applied to partial sums and their uniformly convergent derivative series shows successively that $f_0$ is smooth with those derivatives. Suppose a periodic $C^1$ linearized solution existed on $[0,\tau]$. At each Fourier frequency $k$, differentiation under the integral and periodic integration by parts give $g_k'= -H_0 i k\cdot w_k$ and $H_0w_k'=a^2 i k g_k$. Thus $g_k$ is $C^2$ with $g_k''=a^2|k|^2g_k$. The explicit initial coefficients and zero velocity, together with uniqueness of this elementary ODE, force its coefficient at $(n,0,0)$ to be $\tfrac12e^{-\sqrt n}\cosh(an t)$. For every $t>0$ these coefficients tend to infinity, since $an t-\sqrt n\to\infty$, whereas every Fourier coefficient of the continuous density at that fixed time has absolute value at most $\int|f(t,x)|\,d\mu\le\sup_x|f(t,x)|<\infty$. This contradiction excludes a periodic classical solution on every positive time interval, without requiring an unproved inference from coefficient summability. This explicit datum is smooth; polynomially decaying coefficients of a fixed order would not have established that assertion. [step 1.1, L2, L3, construct, algebra]
