items/def-qftx-scaling-cocycle.md:85:[F1] [[def-qftx-local-time-ordered-system]]: a scalar time-ordered system is a sequence of symmetric maps on compactly supported finite-jet polynomial functionals of a smooth real scalar field, with $T_0=1$, $T_1=\operatorname{id}$, and the stated causal, covariance, scaling, and formal unitarity conditions.
items/ex-emgmath-scalar-plane-wave.md:55:where $C\ne0$ is dimensionless, $\mathbf k\in\mathbb R^3$ has units $\mathrm m^{-1}$, and $\omega:=c\sqrt{|\mathbf k|^2+m_s^2}>0$ has units $\mathrm s^{-1}$. For the scalar action of [[def-emg-charged-scalar-functional]], this is an exact solution of the local background scalar equation, equivalently stationary under compactly supported scalar variations. The infinite-volume action value need not be finite; only the local equation and compactly supported variations are used. If the coupling $\lambda\ne0$, the same configuration has a nonzero derived current although $F=dA=0$, so it fails the sourced Maxwell equation of [[lem-emg-maxwell-current-forms]]. It is therefore a background scalar solution, not a solution of the coupled scalar–Maxwell system. No relation to $\hbar$ or empirical data is used.
items/pthm-nrqm-angular-momentum-orbital-eigenstates-and-quantum-numbers.md:42:physical_scope: "A conditional consequence of P1, P2, P5 and P6 for the massive spin-s model and its orbital generators: for a smooth compactly supported radial profile times a spherical harmonic, the model vector is an eigenvector of the third orbital generator with eigenvalue hbar m, the Casimir quadratic form takes the value hbar^2 l(l+1), and the orbital quantum numbers are those of the spherical harmonic family. The statement concerns the spatial factor only, uses no dynamics and no measurement model, and asserts no spectral decomposition of the full spatial Hilbert space, no hydrogen spectrum and no empirical result."
items/thm-emg-maxwell-gauss-propagation.md:72:3.1 The field $n$ is $C^1$ and transverse to $\Sigma_0$ by hypothesis. Its local flow map $\Phi(\tau,x)$ is $C^1$ and the differential at $\tau=0$ maps the time direction to $n$ and the slice directions identically, hence is invertible. By [F3], after shrinking, $(\tau,x)$ are flow coordinates. Pullback preserves distributional closedness of these continuous three-forms under the $C^1$ flow chart: test against compactly supported smooth functions, use the change-of-variables formula, and approximate the resulting compactly supported $C^1$ test functions in their $C^1$ norm. In these coordinates $n=\partial_\tau$ and $\mathrm{vol}_g=J(\tau,x)d\tau\wedge dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3$ with continuous $J>0$. Thus $dU=0$ reads $\partial_\tau(Jq)=0$ distributionally, and $dV=0$ reads $\partial_\tau(Jr)=0$. A continuous function whose distributional derivative in $\tau$ vanishes is constant in $\tau$: convolving in $\tau$ gives smooth functions with zero derivative, and uniform convergence on compact subcylinders passes that constancy to the original function; testing against spatial test functions first gives the same conclusion when both variables are present. Their initial values vanish by step 1.1, so $q=r=0$ throughout the flow neighborhood. [given, step 1.1, step 2.1, F3, algebra]
items/thm-fd-periodic-smooth-incompressible-energy.md:74:1.1 By [F1], $u$ is smooth, divergence-free, and periodic and satisfies the full equation in the Statement. Taking the $L^2$ inner product with $u$ gives $\frac12\frac{d}{dt}\|u\|_2^2=-\int (u\cdot\nabla u)\cdot u\,d\mu-\int\nabla\pi\cdot u\,d\mu+\nu\int u\cdot\Delta u\,d\mu$. The transport integral is $\frac12\int u\cdot\nabla|u|^2\,d\mu=0$, since it is the integral of a periodic divergence and $\operatorname{div}u=0$. Periodic integration by parts gives $\int\nabla\pi\cdot u\,d\mu=-\int\pi\,\operatorname{div}u\,d\mu=0$ and $\int u\cdot\Delta u\,d\mu=-\|\nabla u\|_2^2$. Hence $\frac12\frac{d}{dt}\|u\|_2^2=-\nu\|\nabla u\|_2^2$. [given, A1, F1, algebra]
