items/pthm-nrqm-driven-dynamics-linear-drive-rotating-wave-error.md:131:- **Where the error comes from.** The two counter-rotating terms $e^{\pm2i\omega t}B$ are exactly the pieces dropped by the rotating-wave approximation; the supplier's integration by parts converts their size $\|B\|$ and frequency $2|\omega|$ into the prefactor $|\Omega_{1}|/(2|\omega|)$ and their time-dependence into the linear-in-$\tau$ factor. For a specified operator-norm tolerance $\eta>0$, the sufficient condition supplied by the contract is $\varepsilon(\tau)\le\eta$, which depends on both detuning and coupling. When $\Omega_1=0$ the comparison is exact for every elapsed time; a large value of this upper bound alone does not establish failure of the approximation.
items/lem-nrqmx-bounded-symbol-estimate.md:112:[F10] For $C^1$ functions $f,g$ on a line, integration by parts applies when $f'g$ and $fg'$ are integrable and $fg$ vanishes at both ends. Applied one coordinate at a time, with Fubini, it applies to the Gaussian packets and their derivatives here: the Gaussian factors and all their derivatives decay rapidly, while the symbol derivatives are bounded ([[lem-complex-integration-by-parts-on-intervals-and-decaying-lines]]).
items/lem-qsm-matrix-exponential-response.md:61:1.1 On a compact interval of $s$, the matrix norms of $M(s)$ and $M'(s)$ are bounded. The exponential power series and its differentiated series converge uniformly there, since the derivative of $M^n$ is $\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}M^jM'M^{n-1-j}$, whose norm is at most $n\|M\|^{n-1}\|M'\|$. Expanding both exponentials in the integral, integrating term by term, and using $\int_0^1(1-u)^ju^k\,du=j!k!/(j+k+1)!$ (obtained by integration by parts) gives the same differentiated series. This proves the Duhamel formula. [given, algebra]
items/thm-nrqmx-rank-one-chern-holonomy.md:68:[F4] [[lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface]]: Stokes holds for the $C^1$ connection forms on the two compact subsurfaces, including their piecewise-$C^2$ boundaries.
items/lem-em-rectangular-pec-energy-completeness.md:80:5.2 The limit $(E,B)$ of step 4.1 is a weak solution in the sense of step 1.1. Indeed, each truncation $(E_M,B_M)$ solves the equations pointwise, hence also in the pairing formulation, because for smooth periodic fields the distributional pairings coincide with the integral pairings and the cur sign convention is the same. For a smooth periodic test pair $(\Psi,\Theta)$ compactly supported in $t$, the differences satisfy $|\iint(E_M-E)\cdot\partial_t\Psi\,d\nu\,dt|\le\sup_{t}\|(E_M-E)(t)\|_{L^2}\cdot\int\|\partial_t\Psi(t)\|_{L^2}\,dt$, and $\int\|\partial_t\Psi(t)\|\,dt<\infty$ because $\Psi$ is compactly supported in time and continuous, so the bound tends to $0$ by the uniform convergence of step 4.1; the pairings with $\operatorname{curl}\Psi$ and $\operatorname{curl}\Theta$, and the corresponding pairings of $B_M$, are handled identically. Passing to the limit in the two displayed identities therefore shows that $(E,B)$ is a weak solution. The divergence constraints hold in the limit because the class $\mathcal C$ is closed, as recorded in step 1.1. [step 4.1, step 1.1, step 3.1, algebra]
items/lem-qft-free-time-ordered-kernel.md:97:1.1 For an integer $M\ge2$, integration by parts in $\mathbf x$ gives
items/lem-qft-free-time-ordered-kernel.md:109:as a distribution. This is the slice of $H_\mu$ for $t>0$. For $t<0$, replacing $\mathbf k$ by $-\mathbf k$ identifies its slice with $W_\mu(-t,-\mathbf x);$ the same substitution proves $H_\mu(t,\mathbf x)=H_\mu(-t,-\mathbf x)$. For real tests, the vacuum expectation of the field commutator is $W_\mu(f,g)-W_\mu(g,f)$; [F6] identifies it with a distribution supported in the causal cone. Thus the two Wightman orderings agree on spacelike-separated supports. For a compactly supported smooth spatial test supported away from $\mathbf x=0$, both slice pairings are continuous in $t$ by [F9] and the integrable bound in 1.1, so no additional term supported at $t=0$ occurs away from the coincidence point. The partial Fourier formula itself specifies the extension at coincidence. [F1, F4, F5, F6, F7, F9, step 1.1, algebra]
items/def-emx-transverse-fourier-field.md:288:1.1 In the whole-space componentwise convention, the absolute-tail estimate in the Definition makes the integer-radius integrals of each real or imaginary component a real Cauchy sequence. Its convergence follows from [F8], and the same uniform tail estimate gives that limit for all real radii tending to infinity. This argument applies separately to each of the finitely many components and introduces no choice principle. Since $K$ is compact and excludes $0$, it has positive distance from the origin. Thus extending $a$ by zero across a neighborhood of $0$ gives a smooth compactly supported map on all of $\mathbb R^3$. For any compact time interval $J$, each function $f_t(k):=a(k)\exp(-ic|k|t)$ and each of its $t$-derivatives is smooth and supported in $K$; all its derivatives are uniformly bounded on $K\times J$. Every integral in the Definition is therefore an ordinary Riemann integral of a continuous compactly supported function. Differentiating in $x$ or $t$ under the integral is justified because the corresponding difference quotients converge uniformly on $K$ for each fixed $(x,t)$: the phase derivatives in $(x,t)$ are bounded because $k$ ranges over $K$ and $t$ lies in $J$, while the complex exponential has unit modulus. The one-variable mean-value estimate for the exponential is therefore uniform in $k$. The absolute-integral estimate in [F5] then bounds the difference between the integrals by the volume of a containing cube times the uniform error, which tends to zero. Iterating this argument for the resulting derivatives proves existence and continuity of every mixed space-time derivative. [given, F3, F5, F6, F8, construct]
items/def-emx-transverse-fourier-field.md:290:2.1 For multi-indices $\alpha,\beta$, differentiating in $x$ multiplies the integrand by $(ik)^\beta$. To control a factor $x_j$, integrate the $k_j$-derivative of the product of the compactly supported factor and $\exp(ik\cdot x)$ over a containing cube. The one-dimensional FTC in [F5] gives zero boundary terms because the factor vanishes near both endpoints; Riemann-Fubini in [F5] integrates that identity over the other two coordinates. Repeating this argument transfers every monomial $x^\alpha$ to derivatives in $k$, leaving an integral of $\partial_k^\alpha((ik)^\beta f_t(k))$ times a unit-modulus exponential, up to the fixed factor of modulus one produced by integration by parts. Its absolute value is uniformly bounded in $x$ and locally uniformly in $t$. The same argument applies after any $t$-derivative, since it only adds the smooth factor $(-ic|k|)^q$ on $K$. By [F1], $A_+$, $A$, $E$, and $B$ are therefore Schwartz in $x$ at each $t$, with the claimed local bounds. [step 1.1, F1, F5, algebra]
