items/thm-fluid-uniqueness-and-continuous-dependence.md:50:[F1] For smooth fields on $\mathbb T^3$: $\int_{\mathbb T^3}\partial_jf\,dx=0$ and $\int_{\mathbb T^3}f\partial_jg\,dx=-\int_{\mathbb T^3}(\partial_jf)g\,dx$ (periodic integration by parts); $\langle\Delta f,f\rangle=-\|\nabla f\|_{L^2}^2$ ([[def-periodic-vector-sobolev-space-and-leray-projection]], [[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]]).
items/thm-fluid-uniqueness-and-continuous-dependence.md:69:1.1 For either smooth solution $z=u$ or $z=v$, put $Y(t)=\|z(t)\|_{H^s}$. Pair its equation with $\Lambda^{2s}z$, using periodic integration by parts and the commuting orthogonal Leray projection. The dissipative term is nonpositive since $\nu\ge0$, while claims 2-3 of [[lem-tame-transport-commutator]] give $$\tfrac12(Y^2)'\le C_sY^3.$$ With $Y_\varepsilon=(Y^2+\varepsilon^2)^{1/2}$ this implies $Y_\varepsilon'\le C_sY_\varepsilon^2$, hence $Y_\varepsilon(t)\le Y_\varepsilon(0)/(1-C_sY_\varepsilon(0)t)$ while the denominator is positive, by differentiating $1/Y_\varepsilon$. Letting $\varepsilon\downarrow0$ gives $Y(t)\le Y(0)/(1-C_sY(0)t)\le2B$ for $t\le T=(2C_sB)^{-1}$; if $Y(0)=0$ the same limit gives $Y=0$. This establishes the bound required in [F3] for both solutions, without substituting $2B$ for the weaker exponential bound in the existence supplier. [given, F1, F2, algebra]
