items/lem-qftx-transverse-center-scaling-bound.md:104:The Schwartz part has the same estimate by integration by parts on the fixed support of $\chi$. An intermediate exponent between $a_j$ and $s_j$ even makes each displayed seminorm tend to zero. Ordinary weak convergence follows from [F1]. Since $v_j=u_j$ near zero, every fixed output localization uses these same dilates for all sufficiently small $\rho$. This proves the required directional control rather than inferring it from pointwise wavefront inclusion of the rescaled family. [F1, F3, F4, F5, step 2.1, step 1.2, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-transverse-center-scaling-bound.md:106:4.1 Shrink the cones and localization neighborhoods as in step 1.2, retaining a little angular margin. The product exists there by [F3], iterated over the finitely many factors. For a fixed compactly supported test $\varphi$, its pairing with the product of the localized scaled factors is the absolutely convergent Fourier integral of $\widehat\varphi(-\sum_j\eta_j)$ times the factor transforms. Here is a uniform integrable bound. Compactness of normalized frequency tuples and the joint no-cancellation condition imply that either $|\sum_j\eta_j|\ge c\sum_j|\eta_j|$, or some $\eta_j$ lies in a regular cone and $|\eta_j|\ge c\sum_k|\eta_k|$, for a fixed $c>0$, apart from bounded frequencies. In the first region the Schwartz decay of $\widehat\varphi$ controls the total frequency size; in the second region step 3.1 supplies arbitrarily high decay from that factor. All other localized factor transforms have fixed uniform polynomial bounds by step 1.1 and [F4]. Choosing the decay power larger than the total polynomial degree plus $Dr+1$ gives an integrable majorant independent of $\rho$. Each localized factor transform tends pointwise to zero by weak convergence. Dominated convergence therefore proves $\rho^{\sum_j s_j}\prod_j(u_j)_\rho\to0$. Dilation commutes with the locally defined product by the pullback formula [F3], so this is precisely the scaling estimate for the product. Let every $s_j$ decrease to $a_j$ to obtain the claimed bound. [F1, F3, F4, F5, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
items/lem-rpm-curved-charge-action-variation.md:121:[F10] [[thm-integration-by-parts]]: for $C^1$ scalar functions on a compact interval, integration by parts gives the endpoint product minus the integral of the derivative of the other factor.
items/lem-tame-transport-commutator.md:26:    - title: "E. Wiedemann (ed. J. Skipper), Lecture Notes: Navier-Stokes Equations (Universität Ulm, WS 2018/19), Section 4.2 (the H^m energy identity, the vanishing transport term after integration by parts and the Sobolev estimates bounding the commutator)"
items/lem-tame-transport-commutator.md:67:1.1 For trigonometric polynomials $u,w$ with $\operatorname{div}u=0$, integration by parts [F4] gives $\int((u\cdot\nabla)w)\cdot w\,dx=\sum_{i,j}\int u_j(\partial_jw_i)w_i\,dx=\frac12\sum_j\int u_j\partial_j|w|^2dx=-\frac12\int(\operatorname{div}u)|w|^2dx=0$. The map $(u,w)\mapsto\int((u\cdot\nabla)w)\cdot w\,dx$ is continuous on $H^m\times H^m$: indeed, $\bigl|\int((u\cdot\nabla)w)\cdot w\,dx\bigr|\le\|u\|_{L^\infty}\|\nabla w\|_{L^2}\|w\|_{L^2}\le C_m\|u\|_{H^m}\|w\|_{H^m}^2$ by the embedding bound [F5] and [F3]; since $P_Nu$ is divergence-free and $P_Nu\to u$, $P_Nw\to w$ in $H^m$ by [F4], the identity extends from polynomials to all $u,w\in H^m$, proving claim 1. [F3, F4, F5]
items/pthm-nrqm-approximations-barrier-transmission-asymptotics.md:148:4.1 [Scope] The proof uses only the declared smooth compactly supported profile, its two separated simple turning points, the local Airy connections and the exact boundary-defined scattering solution. It uses no rectangular wall, coalescing or multiple turn, barrier-top uniformity, resonant multiwell, wave packet, detector, apparatus or sampling assumption. Thus the claimed exclusions and scope hold. [F1, step 1.3, step 2.1, given] ∎
items/lem-qftx-microcausal-contraction-compatibility.md:105:2.1 For a compactly supported smooth test $h$, consider $$\int_{(\mathbb R^d)^s}\left(\prod_{j=1}^s\widehat v_j(\eta_j)\right)\widehat h\!\left(-\sum_j\eta_j\right)d\eta_1\cdots d\eta_s.$$ It is absolutely convergent. In the first region of step 1.1, use arbitrary rapid decay of the indicated comparable-size regular factor and polynomial bounds for the others; in the second, use arbitrary rapid decay of $\widehat h$. For every prescribed $Q$ both bounds are at most $C_Qp_{n_Q}(h)(1+R)^{-Q}$ after increasing the rapid exponent above $Q+\sum_jm_j$; the finite region $R\le1$ has the same bound with a larger constant. Choose $Q>sd$. Integration proves a finite-seminorm distribution estimate. For smooth compact factors the value is exactly $\int h\prod_jv_j$ by Fourier inversion and absolutely integrable Fubini. Approximate every $v_j$ by the controlled smooth sequences in [F3], with independent approximation indices. Their uniform bounds give the same integrable majorant. Each Fourier factor converges pointwise, so dominated convergence identifies every joint or iterated approximation limit with this one integral. This defines a simultaneous product and proves its invariance under reordering the factors. [A1, F3, F5, F6, step 1.1, construct, algebra]
items/prop-emg-compact-charge-compatibility.md:58:[F4] [[lem-finite-chart-localization-defines-choice-free-integration-and-compact-stokes]]: For a compactly supported smooth $2$-form $\eta$ on an oriented boundaryless smooth three-manifold, its finite chart integral satisfies $\int_\Sigma d\eta=0$.
items/thm-nrqmx-wigner-marginals.md:103:2.1 To see that nonnegativity fails, set $v_\epsilon(x)=\sqrt{2/\epsilon}(\pi\epsilon)^{-1/4}x e^{-x^2/(2\epsilon)}$. Substituting $x=\sqrt\epsilon\,s$ and applying [F6] at Gaussian parameter $1/4$ gives $\int e^{-s^2}ds=\sqrt\pi$; one integration by parts [F7] then gives $\int s^2e^{-s^2}ds=\sqrt\pi/2$, hence $\int x^2e^{-x^2/\epsilon}dx=\sqrt\pi\,\epsilon^{3/2}/2$ and $\|v_\epsilon\|_2=1$. At the origin, $$ W_\epsilon[v_\epsilon](0,0)=-(2\pi\epsilon)^{-1}\frac{1}{2\epsilon\sqrt{\pi\epsilon}}\int y^2e^{-y^2/(4\epsilon)}\,dy=-\frac1{\pi\epsilon}<0. $$ Here the Gaussian second moment is obtained from [F6] at $\xi=0$ by one integration by parts [F7]. Thus $W_\epsilon[v_\epsilon]$ is a normalized Schwartz Wigner distribution with a negative value. [given, A1, F6, F7, algebra] ∎ 
items/thm-gr-compact-metric-and-timelike-curve-variation.md:139:[L4] For a smooth metric family with compactly supported variation: if $w(s)$ is the density of $g(s)$, then $\partial_sw(s)|_0=-\tfrac12w\,g_{ab}q^{ab}$; equivalently $\partial_sw(s)|_0=\tfrac12w\,g^{ab}h_{ab}$ ([[lem-gr-metric-divergence-and-density-formula]] clause (a), [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]).
items/thm-gr-compact-metric-and-timelike-curve-variation.md:147:[L8] A continuous chart coefficient supported in a fixed compact subset of $x(U)$ is extended by zero to $\mathbb R^4$ and integrated on a containing closed nondegenerate rectangle. It is integrable by [[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]], with the integral of [[def-multidimensional-darboux-integral]]. For a $C^1$ compactly supported coefficient $f$, zero extension is $C^1$; [[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]] and [[thm-ftc-second-part]] give $\int\partial_af=0$ because the endpoint values in that coordinate vanish. For a jointly smooth parameter coefficient $A(s,x)$ with fixed compact support, choose a compact parameter interval about $0$. Uniform continuity of $\partial_s A$ on its product with the containing rectangle ([[thm-heine-cantor-metric]]) and [[cor-mean-value-theorem]] imply $(A(s,x)-A(0,x))/s\to\partial_sA(0,x)$ uniformly in $x$. The Darboux sums give $|\int f-\int h|\le\operatorname{vol}(Q)\sup_Q|f-h|$ for continuous functions on $Q$ ([[def-multidimensional-darboux-sums]]); hence the derivative passes through this integral.
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-angular-radial-unitary-reduction.md:109:1.1 [Polar measurability and the angular expansion] For continuous compactly supported $\psi$, $(r,\Omega)\mapsto\psi(r\Omega)$ is continuous on $(0,\infty)\times S^2$, and the polar formula [F3] gives $\int_{\mathbb R^3}|\psi|^2dx=\int_0^\infty r^2\int_{S^2}|\psi(r\Omega)|^2d\sigma dr$. For a general $L^2$ class, take a finite Lebesgue-measurable representative and use [F8] on its real and imaginary parts to obtain Borel representatives, setting them to zero on the Borel null sets where they are not finite; their combination is a finite Borel representative $\psi_0$. If $\psi_0$ and $\widetilde\psi_0$ are two such representatives, their Borel disagreement set is Lebesgue-null; applying [F3] to its indicator shows that their polar pullbacks agree for $r^2dr\,d\sigma$-almost every $(r,\Omega)$, so the angular coefficient functions agree for almost every $r$. Thus the coefficients depend only on the $L^2$ class. Since $(r,\Omega)\mapsto\psi_0(r\Omega)$ is Borel, completed-product Fubini [F3] gives measurable sections and Parseval [F2] gives $\int_{S^2}|\psi_0(r\Omega)|^2d\sigma=\sum_{l,m}|c_{lm}(r)|^2$ for almost every $r$. The polar formula [F3] then yields $\int_{\mathbb R^3}|\psi|^2dx=\int_0^\infty r^2\sum_{l,m}|c_{lm}(r)|^2dr$. [F1, F2, F3, F8, given, algebra]
