items/ex-em-compact-variation-example.md:206:3.1 For the same data, the second form of [F4] gives $S[A+\partial^\sharp\chi_\pm]-S[A]=-c^{-1}\int_U(\partial_\mu j^\mu)\chi_\pm\,d\lambda$; here $\partial_\mu j^\mu=\partial_0(c\rho(x^0))=c\rho'(x^0)$ by [F7] and [F15], so the right-hand side is $-\int_U\rho'(x^0)\varphi_\pm(x^0)\psi\,d\lambda=-\bigl(\int_{-1}^1\rho'\varphi_\pm\,dx^0\bigr)\bigl(\int\psi\bigr)$ by Fubini [F10]; by [F17] the one-variable integrals equal their Riemann integrals, and integration by parts [F12] applied to $\rho$ and $\varphi_\pm$ has vanishing endpoint terms because both are compactly supported, giving $\int\rho'\varphi_\pm=-\int\rho\varphi_\pm'=\mp\int\rho$, which reproduces exactly the value $\pm(\int\rho)(\int\psi)$ of step 2.2. If instead $\rho$ is constant on the whole neighbourhood, then $\partial_\mu j^\mu=c\rho'=0$, and [F4] (b) gives $S[A+\partial^\sharp\chi]=S[A]$ for every compact gauge variation $\chi$; no globally monotone compact function occurs anywhere. [step 2.2, F4, F7, F10, F12, F15, F17, algebra]
