items/prop-gr-scalar-field-stress-energy-conservation.md:92:[L2] The metric volume form satisfies $\delta\mathrm{vol}_g=-\tfrac12g_{ab}q^{ab}\mathrm{vol}_g$ along a metric family with inverse-metric variation $q$, and $\nabla_aJ^a\mathrm{vol}_g=d(\iota_J\mathrm{vol}_g)$; the integral of a compactly supported divergence vanishes ([[lem-gr-metric-divergence-and-density-formula]], [[def-gr-metric-volume-form-and-current-flux]], [[lem-finite-chart-localization-defines-choice-free-integration-and-compact-stokes]], [[thm-gr-density-divergence-and-finite-chain-flux]] clause (a) — the flux identity is used only through the vanishing of the compactly supported divergence integral).
items/prop-gr-scalar-field-stress-energy-conservation.md:96:[L4] The action difference of [[def-gr-scalar-field-matter-model]] clause (b) is an integral of a compactly supported smooth top-form difference, not of either possibly nonintegrable total density. For fixed compact support choose finitely many fixed smooth chart cutoffs with sum $1$ near that support, as in [[lem-finite-chart-localization-defines-choice-free-integration-and-compact-stokes]]. In each chart extend the cutoff coefficient by zero to a containing rectangle; it is continuous and integrable by [[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]] and [[def-multidimensional-darboux-integral]]. Its parameter derivative is uniformly continuous on a compact parameter interval times this rectangle by [[thm-heine-cantor-metric]]. The mean value theorem ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]]) gives uniform convergence of parameter difference quotients; the estimate $|\int f-\int h|\le\operatorname{vol}(Q)\sup|f-h|$, obtained from [[def-multidimensional-darboux-sums]], passes the derivative through the chart integral. Summing the finitely many chart derivatives proves differentiation under this global compact-difference integral.
items/prop-gr-scalar-field-stress-energy-conservation.md:102:**Proof technique:** direct; vary the Lagrangian density in the field and in the metric, integrate by parts using the vanishing of compactly supported divergence integrals, and differentiate the stress-energy formula.
items/prop-gr-scalar-field-stress-energy-conservation.md:106:2.1 (Integration by parts.) Consider the smooth compactly supported vector field $J^b:=\delta\varphi\,g^{ab}\partial_a\varphi$; by [L1], $\nabla_bJ^b=(\partial_b\delta\varphi)g^{ab}\partial_a\varphi+\delta\varphi\,\Box_g\varphi$, and $\int_M\nabla_bJ^b\mathrm{vol}_g=0$ by [L2]. Hence $\int_M(g^{ab}\partial_a\varphi)\partial_b\delta\varphi\,\mathrm{vol}_g=-\int_M\delta\varphi\,\Box_g\varphi\,\mathrm{vol}_g$, and step 1.1 gives $\frac{d}{du}\Delta S=\frac{K}{c}\int_M(\Box_g\varphi-V'(\varphi))\delta\varphi\,\mathrm{vol}_g$, which is the variation formula in (a). Every compactly supported smooth scalar $f$ is an admissible first variation, by $\varphi_u=\varphi+uf$. Let $E=\Box_g\varphi-V\prime(\varphi)$. If all such variations are stationary, positivity of $K/c$ gives $\int Ef\,\mathrm{vol}_g=0$. On any oriented coordinate chart and for every compactly supported symmetric tensor $q$, take $f=g_{ab}q^{ab}$, extended by zero. Then $\int(Eg_{ab})q^{ab}w\,d^4x=0$, so [L5] gives $Eg=0$. Nondegeneracy of $g$ forces $E=0$ at every point. Conversely $E=0$ makes every variation derivative vanish by the displayed formula. This proves all of (a). [step 1.1, L1, L2, L5, algebra]
