items/pthm-em-integral-maxwell.md:136:1.2 For the patch identities of (a), fix a patch test geometry $(D,r)$ and put $S=r[D]$, with parameter derivatives $r_u,r_v$ and oriented area vector $r_u\times r_v$. The flux functions $\Phi_B(t):=\int_D\langle B(r(u,v),t),r_u(u,v)\times r_v(u,v)\rangle\,dudv$ and $\Phi_E$ are differentiable on $I$ with $\Phi_B'(t)=\int_S\partial_tB(\cdot,t)\cdot n_S\,dS$ and $\Phi_E'(t)=\int_S\partial_tE(\cdot,t)\cdot n_S\,dS$: the integrands are the continuous functions $\langle B(r(u,v),t),r_u\times r_v\rangle$ with continuous $t$-derivative $\langle\partial_tB(r(u,v),t),r_u\times r_v\rangle$ on a neighbourhood of the compact Jordan region $D$ times a compact interval around $t$, [F6] identifies the Riemann integral with the Lebesgue integral, and [F7] differentiates under the integral. Stokes' theorem [F5] applied to the $C^1$ fields gives $\oint_{\partial S}E(\cdot,t)\cdot d\mathbf r=\int_S\operatorname{curl}E(\cdot,t)\cdot n_S\,dS$ and the corresponding identity for $B$, in the induced orientation of the patch; substituting the Faraday law $\operatorname{curl}E=-\partial_tB$ of [F1] gives $\oint_{\partial S}E(\cdot,t)\cdot d\mathbf r=-\int_S\partial_tB(\cdot,t)\cdot n_S\,dS=-\Phi_B'(t)$, and substituting the Ampere law gives $\oint_{\partial S}B(\cdot,t)\cdot d\mathbf r=\mu_0\int_SJ(\cdot,t)\cdot n_S\,dS+c^{-2}\Phi_E'(t)$. [F1, F2, F5, F6, F7, given, algebra]
items/pthm-em-integral-maxwell.md:140:1.4 For the curl equations fix a point $p\in\Omega$ and let $(j,k)$ be the cyclic pair with $e_j\times e_k=e_i$ for a coordinate index $i$. Put $W:=\{(u,v)\in\mathbb R^2:p+ue_j+ve_k\in\Omega\}$, an open set, and $\varphi_i(u,v):=\bigl(\operatorname{curl}E+\partial_tB\bigr)(p+ue_j+ve_k)\cdot e_i$ on $W$, a continuous function. For every closed axis-parallel square $Q\subseteq W$ with side $\ell>0$ the affine patch $r(u,v):=p+ue_j+ve_k$ over $Q$ is a patch test geometry with $r_u\times r_v=e_i$ and image in $\Omega$; Stokes [F5], the differentiability of its flux function and the Faraday identity of the hypothesis give $\int_Q\varphi_i\,dudv=\int_Q\langle(\operatorname{curl}E)\circ r,r_u\times r_v\rangle\,dudv+\frac{d}{dt}\int_Q\langle B\circ r,r_u\times r_v\rangle\,dudv=\oint_{\partial S}E\cdot d\mathbf r+\frac{d}{dt}\int_SB\cdot n_S\,dS=0$. Hence the continuous function $\varphi_i$ has vanishing integral over every closed axis-parallel square contained in $W$, so the planar clause of [F8] gives $\varphi_i=0$ on $W$. The point $p\in\Omega$ was chosen arbitrarily, and $(0,0)\in W$ because $p\in\Omega$, so evaluating at $(0,0)$ gives $(\operatorname{curl}E+\partial_tB)(p)\cdot e_i=0$ for every $p\in\Omega$; letting $i$ run over the three coordinate indices, $\operatorname{curl}E=-\partial_tB$ on $\Omega$. The same argument with the Ampere identity in place of Faraday gives $\operatorname{curl}B=\mu_0J+c^{-2}\partial_tE$, the flux derivative producing $c^{-2}\Phi_E'$. [F2, F3, F5, F6, F7, F8, given, algebra]
items/pthm-em-integral-maxwell.md:146:The passage between the differential and integral forms over the declared test geometries is the standard localization argument of the cited treatments, written here with the library's own divergence theorem, patch Stokes theorem, parameter-integral and cube-localization interfaces. The textbooks supply the formulation and the physical reading; the deductions use only the imported mathematical items and the adopted smooth-branch postulate.
