items/lem-qftx-two-dimensional-covariance-log-integrability.md:141:4.1 Let $R_\rho>0$ satisfy $\operatorname{supp}\rho\subseteq B(0,R_\rho)$. For $f_{\epsilon,x}(u)=\rho_\epsilon(x-u)$ and $f_{\delta,y}(v)=\rho_\delta(y-v)$, the Gaussian covariance [F1] and the kernel representation in steps 1.1–2.1 give $\mathbb E[\Phi_\epsilon(x)\Phi_\delta(y)]=\int\!\!\int f_{\epsilon,x}(u)f_{\delta,y}(v)G_m(u-v)d^2u\,d^2v$. This integral is absolute because the tests are bounded and compactly supported and $G_m\in L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}$. Put $z=x-y$, $a=x-u$, and $b=y-v$; [F15] preserves Lebesgue measure and yields $\int\!\!\int\rho_\epsilon(a)\rho_\delta(b)G_m(z-a+b)d^2a\,d^2b$. With $w=a-b$, Fubini [F3] gives this as $\int G_m(z-w)\kappa_{\epsilon,\delta}(w)d^2w=C_{\epsilon,\delta}(z)$. The nonnegative correlation kernel $\kappa_{\epsilon,\delta}$ has integral one, support in a disk of radius $2R_\rho q$ for $q=\max(\epsilon,\delta)$, and $\|\kappa_{\epsilon,\delta}\|_\infty\le\|\rho\|_\infty q^{-2}$. Its support shrinks to zero, so continuity of $G_m$ away from zero gives $C_{\epsilon,\delta}(z)\to G_m(z)$ for each $z\ne0$. To get the uniform bound, fix bounded $|z|\le R$. If $|z|$ exceeds twice the support radius, then $|z-w|\ge|z|/2$ throughout the convolution and step 2.1 bounds the integrand by $K_{m,R}(1+|\log|z||)$. Otherwise $|z|\le4R_\rho q$ and $|z-w|\le6R_\rho q$; the scaled dyadic-annulus estimate in step 3.1 gives $\int_{|u|\le6R_\rho q}|G_m(u)|d^2u\le K_mq^2(1+|\log q|)$. Multiplying by $\|\kappa\|_\infty$ bounds the convolution by $K_m(1+|\log q|)\le K_{m,R}(1+|\log|z||)$ because $q\ge|z|/(4R_\rho)$. Choose $\epsilon_{m,R}$ small enough that $6R_\rho q\le1$. This proves the uniform estimate for all $0<\epsilon,\delta\le\epsilon_{m,R}$. [F1, F3, F4, F15, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, given, algebra] ∎
