items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-laguerre-bound-energy-exhaustion.md:84:2.2 [The form identity gives the operator domain and $H^2$ transport] The substitution of [F1] shows that $u$ solves $-u''+(l(l+1)/r^2-\gamma/r)u=-k^2u$ on $(0,\infty)$. Against $w\in C_c^\infty(0,\infty)$, integration by parts gives $a_l(u,w)=\int(u'\overline{w'}+l(l+1)u\overline w/r^2-\gamma u\overline w/r)dr=-k^2\langle u,w\rangle$. The equality between the kinetic forms follows by integrating $(u\overline w/r)'$, with no boundary term because $w$ is compactly supported away from zero. Boundedness of $a_l$ and density in [F2] extend the identity to every $w\in V_l$. The actual definition of $D(A_l)$ now gives $u\in D(A_l)$ and $A_lu=-k^2u$. The vector with this one $(l,m)$ component belongs to the direct-sum graph domain, so the exact block identity gives $\psi=u(r)Y_{lm}(\Omega)/r\in D(C)=H^2(\mathbb R^3)$ and $C\psi=-k^2\psi$ for every $m$. This argument uses no assertion that $e^{-k|x|}$ is smooth at $x=0$. [step 1.2, F1, F2, F3, algebra]
