items/lem-emx-surface-divergence-c1-representation.md:70:[F3] Compactly supported $C^1$ functions in a coordinate chart satisfy ordinary integration by parts on a rectangle containing their support, with iterated integrals justified by Fubini. [[thm-integration-by-parts]] [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]
items/lem-emx-surface-divergence-c1-representation.md:82:2.1 Let $\phi\in C^1(S)$ have compact support in this chart. By the definition of the surface gradient, $K\cdot\nabla_S\phi=K^a\partial_a(\phi\circ X)$. The surface-area formula and [F3] give $ -\int_SK\cdot\nabla_S\phi\,dS=-\int_VJ_XK^a\partial_a(\phi\circ X)\,du=\int_V\partial_a(J_XK^a)(\phi\circ X)\,du=\int_Sg_X\phi\,dS. $ Here the repeated $a$ is summed over $1,2$; compact support lets each product $J_XK^a(\phi\circ X)$ extend by zero as a $C^1$ function, and integration by parts on a rectangle containing the support has no boundary term. Thus $g_X$ from step 1.1 represents $\operatorname{Div}_S K$ on this chart. [step 1.1, F1, F3, given, algebra]
items/pthm-nrqm-scattering-resolvent-tail-asymptotic-completeness.md:153:5.1 For $V(x)=-\kappa/|x|$ with $\kappa>0$ and any split $V=V_{\mathrm{loc}}+V_{\mathrm{tail}}$ into a compactly supported part and the remainder, the remainder equals $-\kappa/|x|$ for all sufficiently large $|x|$, so for every $\delta>0$ the quotient $(1+|x|/L_*)^{1+\delta}|V_{\mathrm{tail}}(x)|=\kappa(1+|x|/L_*)^{1+\delta}/|x|$ tends to $+\infty$ as $|x|\to\infty$; hence no constant $C$ satisfies the sufficient decay bound of [F2]'s final paragraph, and the explicit form of (c) does not cover the Coulomb potential. This proves the sufficient-decay exclusion in (d). [F2, step 1.2, step 4.1, given, algebra] ∎
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-kummer-recurrence-and-termination.md:105:4.2 [Integrability and actual form-domain approximation] Suppose $j\in\mathbb N_0$. By step 3.1, $u=u_1$ is a polynomial times $r^{l+1}e^{-kr}$. Near zero $u=O(r^{l+1})$, $u'=O(r^l)$, $u/r=O(r^l)$; at infinity each is bounded by an exponential times a polynomial. Their squared integrals are finite: on $(0,1)$ compare with $r^{2l}$, while at infinity a polynomial times $e^{-2kr}$ is integrable, as follows by bounding each power using the exponential series or repeated integration by parts. Thus $u,u',u/r\in L^2$, and $u$ has zero trace at zero. To show membership in the actual completion $V_l$, take smooth cutoffs $\eta_\varepsilon$ vanishing below $\varepsilon$, equal to one above $2\varepsilon$, with $|\eta_\varepsilon'|\le K/\varepsilon$, and $\chi_R$ equal to one below $R$, vanishing above $2R$, with $|\chi_R'|\le K/R$. The products $u_{\varepsilon,R}=\eta_\varepsilon\chi_Ru$ lie in $C_c^\infty(0,\infty)$. The omitted $L^2$, derivative and $u/r$ tails tend to zero; the added derivative at zero obeys $\|\eta_\varepsilon'u\|_2^2\le4K^2\int_0^{2\varepsilon}|u/r|^2dr\to0$, and the added derivative at infinity obeys $\|\chi_R'u\|_2^2\le K^2R^{-2}\int_R^{2R}|u|^2dr\to0$. Hence $u_{\varepsilon,R}\to u$ in $V_l$, for instance along $\varepsilon=1/n$, $R=n$. This proves (d)'s $L^2$ claim and establishes $u\in V_l$. [step 3.1, step 3.2, F1, F3, given, construct, algebra]
items/ex-em-dielectric-interface-example.md:65:**Proof technique:** local compact-test interface integration by parts on the plane followed by substitution of the constitutive relations.
items/ex-em-dielectric-interface-example.md:73:[L1] One-dimensional integration by parts: for differentiable $u,v$ on $[a,b]$ with integrable derivatives, $\int_a^bu\,v'=u(b)v(b)-u(a)v(a)-\int_a^bu'\,v$ ([[thm-integration-by-parts]]); the integral of a derivative is the difference of endpoint values ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]).
items/ex-em-dielectric-interface-example.md:87:1.2 The same per-line integration by parts in $t$, with $\psi$ of compact $t$-support and the plane stationary and null by [L4], gives $\langle\partial_tF,\psi\rangle=\int_U\{\partial_tF\}\psi$ for every test $\psi$: time differentiation at the fixed plane contributes no sheet term, and the piecewise derivatives are locally bounded by the joint one-sided $C^1$ hypothesis. [L1, L2, L4]
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-jost-volterra-solution.md:102:4.2 [First integration by parts improves the derivative] Fix real $k>0$ and write $Q(r)=2i\theta'(r)=2i(k+a/r)$ and $h(r)=v(r)/r^2$. Since $\theta'\ge k$, $Q^{-1}$ is bounded and its first two derivatives are $O(r^{-2})$ and $O(r^{-3})$. Initially $v=O(1)$ and $v'=O(r^{-1})$, so $h=O(r^{-2})$, $h'=O(r^{-3})$ and $(h/Q)'=O(r^{-3})$. Integration by parts, whose boundary term vanishes at infinity, gives $I(r)=-e^{2i\theta(r)}h(r)/Q(r)-\int_r^\infty e^{2i\theta(s)}(h/Q)'(s)ds=O(r^{-2})$. The exact derivative identity then gives $v'=O(r^{-2})$; the ODE from step 3.1 gives $v''=O(r^{-2})$. [step 2.1, step 3.1, F3, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-jost-volterra-solution.md:106:5.2 [Second integration gives the amplitude coefficient] Now $h''=v''/r^2-4v'/r^3+6v/r^4=O(r^{-4})$. Put $j=(h/Q)'$. Then $j=O(r^{-3})$, $j'=O(r^{-4})$ and $(j/Q)'=O(r^{-4})$. A second integration by parts gives $\int_r^\infty e^{2i\theta}j=-e^{2i\theta(r)}j(r)/Q(r)-\int_r^\infty e^{2i\theta}(j/Q)'=O(r^{-3})$. Therefore $I(r)=-e^{2i\theta(r)}v(r)/(2i\theta'(r)r^2)+O(r^{-3})$, $v'=bv/(2i\theta'r^2)+O(r^{-3})=b/(2ik r^2)+O(r^{-3})$, and integration to the known limit $v(\infty)=1$ gives $v=1-b/(2ik r)+O(r^{-2})$. Since $-b/(2ik)=-a/(2k)+i[a^2+l(l+1)]/(2k)$ and $|w|=1$, the coefficient is $|f|^2=|v|^2=1-a/(kr)+O(r^{-2})$. These estimates are uniform when real $k$ is restricted to a compact subset of $(0,\infty)$, because all preceding constants and the lower bound for $\theta'$ can be chosen uniformly. [step 4.2, step 2.1, step 3.1, F3, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-jost-volterra-solution.md:110:7.1 [The mixed term has a bounded integral] For fixed $R>r_0$, integration by parts gives $\int_R^L f^2=[e^{2i\theta}v^2/Q]_R^L-\int_R^L e^{2i\theta}(v^2/Q)'$. The endpoint term is uniformly bounded and $(v^2/Q)'=2vv'/Q+v^2(Q^{-1})'=O(r^{-2})$ is integrable, hence the whole integral is bounded independently of $L$. Expanding $|Af+B\overline f|^2=(|A|^2+|B|^2)|f|^2+2\operatorname{Re}(A\overline B f^2)$ and integrating step 5.2 yields $\int_R^L|Af+B\overline f|^2=(|A|^2+|B|^2)[L-R-(a/k)\log(L/R)]+O(1)$, with $a/k=\gamma/(2k^2)$. If at least one coefficient is nonzero the integral diverges, since its linear term dominates the logarithm; if both vanish it is zero. Every positive-energy eigenfunction in $D(A_l)$ is an $L^2$ classical solution by [F6], hence step 6.1 and this divergence exclude such an eigenfunction. [step 6.1, step 5.2, F3, F6, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-partial-wave-cross-section-convergence.md:98:1.1 (Angular normalization, the plane wave and smooth amplitude.) For a rotation $T\in SO(3)$ fixing $d$, let $R_Tw(x)=w(Tx)$ on $L^2(B_R)$. Rotation preserves the ball and Lebesgue measure, so $R_T$ is unitary. Substituting $y=Tz$ in the kernel integral and using $G_k(T(x-z))=G_k(x-z)$ and $V(Tz)=V(z)$ almost everywhere gives $R_TK_k=K_kR_T$. Also $R_Te_d=e_d$. Thus $(I+K_k)R_Tu=e_d=(I+K_k)u$, and invertibility in [F1] gives $R_Tu=u$. Applying the same substitution to the integral continuation and the amplitude integral yields $\Psi(Tx)=\Psi(x)$ and $f_k(T\Omega)=f_k(\Omega)$. Only integral-equation uniqueness is used. Thus these functions depend on the angle only through $t=\Omega\cdot d$. The amplitude is genuinely smooth without differentiating $v$: the source $q$ is compactly supported and in $L^1$, and every angular derivative of $e^{-ik\Omega\cdot y}$ is bounded on $S^2\times B_R$, so differentiation under this integral gives derivatives of every order. Rodrigues integration by parts $l$ times gives $\int_{-1}^1P_l(t)^2\,dt=(2l)!/[2^{2l}(l!)^2]\int_{-1}^1(1-t^2)^l\,dt=2/(2l+1)$; the last integral follows from $I_0=2$ and $I_l=2lI_{l-1}/(2l+1)$ by one integration by parts. Consequently $\int_{S^2}P_l(\Omega\cdot d)P_n(\Omega\cdot d)\,d\sigma=4\pi\delta_{ln}/(2l+1)$. Define $j_0(z)=\sin z/z$, $y_0(z)=-\cos z/z$, and recursively $j_{l+1}=lj_l/z-j_l'$, $y_{l+1}=ly_l/z-y_l'$ for $z>0$. Rodrigues integration by parts also gives $\int_{-1}^1e^{izt}P_l(t)\,dt=2i^lj_l(z)$: the resulting expression is $i^lz^l/(2^ll!)\int_{-1}^1e^{izt}(1-t^2)^l\,dt$, whose quotient by $2i^l$ equals $j_l(z)$ by induction using the displayed recurrence and integration by parts in $t$. Therefore the orthogonal angular expansion of the plane wave is $e^{ikrt}=\sum_{l\ge0}(2l+1)i^lj_l(kr)P_l(t)$ in $L^2(S^2)$ for each fixed $r$. In particular the normalization of $P_l$, of the incident coefficients and of the spherical Bessel functions has been derived rather than imported from a Coulomb spectral transform. [given, F1, F2, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-partial-wave-cross-section-convergence.md:100:1.2 (Regular solution for a bounded measurable radial potential.) For fixed $l$ set $Q(r)=v(r)-k^2$ and seek $\varphi_l=r^{l+1}h_l$. The equation $-\varphi_l''+[l(l+1)/r^2+v(r)]\varphi_l=k^2\varphi_l$ is equivalent to $(r^{2l+2}h_l')'=r^{2l+2}Qh_l$. Its normalized regular solution is the Volterra equation $h_l(r)=1+\frac1{2l+1}\int_0^r t[1-(t/r)^{2l+1}]Q(t)h_l(t)\,dt$. On every $[0,L]$, the kernel has absolute value at most $Mt/(2l+1)$ with $M=\|Q\|_{L^\infty(0,L)}$, so the $n$th iterated integral is bounded by $[Mr^2/(2(2l+1))]^n/n!$. The resulting uniformly convergent series gives a continuous real solution, and applying the same estimate to differences proves uniqueness; solutions constructed on different finite intervals agree. Differentiating the integral gives $h_l'(r)=r^{-2l-2}\int_0^r t^{2l+2}Q(t)h_l(t)\,dt$, whence $h_l=1+O(r^2)$, $h_l'=O(r)$ as $r\downarrow0$, and $\varphi_l,\varphi_l'$ are locally absolutely continuous with the radial equation valid almost everywhere. Conversely a solution with $\varphi_l/r^{l+1}\to1$ has no integration constant in $r^{2l+2}h_l'$ (a nonzero constant would make $h_l$ unbounded near zero), so it satisfies this Volterra equation. Near zero $h_l\neq0$, and reduction of order gives an independent solution $\varphi_l(r)\int_{r_0}^r\varphi_l(t)^{-2}\,dt$ with leading nonzero term proportional to $r^{-l}$. Such a branch is incompatible with the local $H^2$ stationary domain: for $l\ge1$ its reduced coefficient is not in $L^2(dr)$ near zero, while for $l=0$ its unreduced coefficient has a nonzero $1/r$ term with radial derivative proportional to $r^{-2}$ and is not locally $H^1(\mathbb R^3)$. Thus every projected radial channel of the $H^2_{\mathrm{loc}}$ stationary solution is a multiple of this regular $\varphi_l$. Projection and the radial equation are justified on compact annuli by smooth approximation, the polar Laplacian identity and angular integration by parts; the angular eigenfunction identity follows with the negative derivative sign in [F2]. No derivative of $v$ or regularity stronger than boundedness has entered. [given, F2, F3, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-partial-wave-cross-section-convergence.md:106:4.1 (Convergence of the full series.) Completeness in [F2] first gives $f_k=\sum_l c_l(f_k)P_l$ in $L^2(S^2)$. For $l\ge1$, repeated integration by parts on the smooth compact sphere gives $[l(l+1)]^N c_l(f_k)=(2l+1)(4\pi)^{-1}\int_{S^2}(-\Delta_{S^2})^Nf_k\,P_l\,d\sigma$, hence $|c_l(f_k)|\le C_N\sqrt{2l+1}\,[l(l+1)]^{-N}$ by Cauchy–Schwarz and the norm computed in step 1.1. All these derivatives exist by the compact-source differentiation argument there; no smoothness of $v$ is required. Also $|P_l(t)|\le1$ for $-1\le t\le1$: the finite-binomial expansion of $\pi^{-1}\int_0^\pi(t+i\sqrt{1-t^2}\cos s)^l\,ds$ is the Rodrigues polynomial $P_l(t)$, while the modulus of the integrand is at most one. Choosing $N\ge2$ therefore gives $\sum_l\sup_{|t|\le1}|c_l(f_k)P_l(t)|<\infty$, including the finite $l=0$ term. The series is consequently absolutely and uniformly convergent, and its limit agrees with the $L^2$ expansion and hence everywhere with the smooth amplitude. With step 3.1 these coefficients give the complete expansion in (a). [step 1.1, step 3.1, F2, algebra]
items/pthm-tgrx-noether-charge-decomposition.md:153:The global-compatibility condition is separate from the local integration by parts: the result does not assert that an arbitrary closed global charge shift is exact. The entropy-variation theorem uses only this representative class. Higher-derivative curvature terms require replacing $P$ by the full curvature Euler derivative and a separate statement.
items/lem-csm-examples-gamma-gaussian-tools.md:94:5.1 Fix the integer $r\ge0$. Step 4.1 evaluated $I_r(a)=\Gamma(r+\tfrac12)a^{-r-1/2}$ for every $a>0$ using only convergent improper substitutions, and step 2.2 proved the Gamma recurrence by integration by parts; neither argument used parameter differentiation. By [F5], differentiation of this established explicit function gives $$I_r'(a)=-(r+\tfrac12)\Gamma(r+\tfrac12)a^{-r-3/2}=-\Gamma(r+\tfrac32)a^{-r-3/2}=-I_{r+1}(a).$$ The same positive-base power formula makes this derivative continuous on $(0,\infty)$, hence on a neighborhood of every compact $K\subset(0,\infty)$. This proves the full parameter clause without interchanging a derivative and an improper integral. [F5, step 2.2, step 4.1, algebra]
items/ex-nrqmx-spin-half-monopole.md:76:[F3] [[lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface]]: for a $C^1$ one-form on a compact oriented surface region with finitely many piecewise-$C^2$ boundary arcs and corners, Stokes holds with the outward-normal-first orientation.
items/ex-nrqmx-spin-half-monopole.md:90:4.1 A southern frame is $u_S=(e^{-i\phi}\cos(\theta/2),\sin(\theta/2))^T=e^{-i\phi}u_N$; it is regular at the south pole and together with $u_N$ covers $S^2$. Along the equator the transition is $g(\phi)=e^{-i\phi}$, so $(2\pi i)^{-1}\int_0^{2\pi}g^{-1}dg=-1$. The two-hemisphere clutching clause in [F2] therefore gives Chern number $-1$, matching the direct curvature integral in step 3.1 and fixing the orientation sign. Pull these frames back to the standard closed parameter disk filling $S$. Their inverse images form an open cover of the disk; by [F5] it has a Lebesgue number $\delta>0$. Choose $N$ with $\sqrt2/N<\delta$, set $h=1/N$, and intersect the disk with the closed axis-aligned squares $[i h,(i+1)h]\times[j h,(j+1)h]$. Retain only intersections $C_{ij}$ with nonempty interior. There are finitely many; their interiors are disjoint and their closures cover the disk, since every disk point is a limit of interior points off the grid lines and a subsequence lies in one of finitely many retained cells. Each cell has diameter at most $\sqrt2h<\delta$, so it lies in one frame patch. Each is a compact convex region whose boundary, after zero-length pieces are discarded, is a finite union of regular straight or circular arcs meeting at finitely many corners; the C1 Stokes item [F3] applies. On a cell $C_{ij}$, its frame connection $A_{ij}$ is $C^1$ because the filling is $C^2$, and [F3] gives $\int_{\partial C_{ij}}A_{ij}=\int_{C_{ij}}F$. Since one frame covers the cell, its boundary holonomy is $\exp(-\int_{\partial C_{ij}}A_{ij})=\exp(-\int_{C_{ij}}F)$. Choose a unit vector at each vertex of this finite cell decomposition and write each oriented edge transport as its unit-modulus scalar in those endpoint vectors. In the product of the cell-boundary holonomies every internal edge occurs once in each direction, so its scalar and inverse cancel; only the outer boundary loop remains. Thus $\operatorname{Hol}(C)=\exp(-\int_SF)=\exp(-i\Omega/2)$. If a second filling is chosen, [F4] says its solid angle differs by $4\pi k$ for some integer $k$, so this exponential is independent of the filling. For a latitude circle the north-cap integral is $\Omega=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\theta\sin u\,du\,d\phi=2\pi(1-\cos\theta)$. [given, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
