items/def-emg-fx-continuous-compact-top-form-integral.md:73:where each Euclidean integral is the Riemann integral over any closed nondegenerate rectangle whose interior contains its support. Such finite cutoffs exist, each zero extension is continuous and compactly supported, and the value is independent of the chosen charts, cutoffs and bounding rectangles. This is a linear functional on $C_c^0(\Lambda^4T^*M)$. For fixed cutoffs and bounding rectangles $Q_i$, it satisfies $|\mathcal I_M(\omega)|\le\sum_i\operatorname{vol}(Q_i)\sup_{Q_i}|\widetilde f_i|$; thus uniform convergence of these finitely many zero-extended coefficients implies convergence of their integrals. If an open set $V\subseteq M$ contains $K$, then $\mathcal I_V(\omega|_V)=\mathcal I_M(\omega)$, with the restricted orientation. On smooth compactly supported top forms it equals the previously defined smooth manifold integral.
items/def-emg-fx-continuous-compact-top-form-integral.md:97:[F3] For an injective $C^1$ map $g:U\to\mathbb R^4$ on an open set with invertible derivative, a compactly supported Riemann-integrable $f$ with support inside $g(U)$ satisfies $\int f=\int (f\circ g)|\det Dg|$, with the transformed integrand extended by zero ([[cor-change-of-variables-for-compactly-supported-functions]]).
items/def-emg-fx-continuous-compact-top-form-integral.md:101:[F5] Under Countable Choice the existing integral of a smooth compactly supported top form is the chart-partition sum, independent of atlas, partition and refinement, and local to any open neighborhood of the support ([[def-integral-of-a-compactly-supported-top-form-on-an-oriented-manifold]], [[thm-global-form-integration-is-independent-of-the-atlas-partition-and-refinement]]). Its stated smoothness hypothesis is not used to assert an integral for nonsmooth forms.
items/def-emg-fx-continuous-compact-top-form-integral.md:113:4.1 Let $(\chi_i,U_i)$ and $(\tau_j,V_j)$ be two finite cutoff systems for $K$. Every $\chi_i\tau_j\omega$ has compact support contained in $U_i\cap V_j$. Near $K$ each cutoff sum is one, and outside $K$ the form vanishes, so $\chi_i\omega=\sum_j\chi_i\tau_j\omega$ and $\tau_j\omega=\sum_i\chi_i\tau_j\omega$. All sums are finite. Apply linearity [F4] and the chart comparison of step 3.1 to each product term. Both proposed integrals equal the same finite double sum, proving independence of cutoff systems and charts. Step 2.1 already proves bounding-rectangle independence. For two compactly supported forms take cutoffs near the union of their supports, which is compact; [F4] then proves linearity in the forms and scalar coefficients. [given, F3, F4, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]
