items/thm-fd-weak-smooth-uniqueness.md:95:Using $v_t=-\operatorname{div}(v\otimes v)-\nabla q+\nu\Delta v$, the pressure pairing vanishes because $u$ is distributionally divergence free. Periodic integration by parts then rewrites the integrand as $(v\otimes v,\nabla u)+(u\otimes u,\nabla v)-2\nu(\nabla u,\nabla v)$. [given, F1, F5, algebra]
items/thm-fd-weak-smooth-uniqueness.md:97:2.1 Testing the smooth equation by $v$, periodic integration by parts gives $\frac12\frac d{dt}\|v\|_2^2+\nu\|\nabla v\|_2^2=0$: the transport integral is $\int\operatorname{div}(v|v|^2/2)=0$, the pressure term is $-\int q\operatorname{div}v=0$, and the Laplacian term is $-\|\nabla v\|_2^2$. Integrating gives the energy equality for $v$. Add it to the weak energy inequality for $u$ and subtract twice the cross identity of step 1.1. The viscous terms combine to $2\nu\int_0^t\|\nabla(u-v)\|_2^2ds$. With $w=u-v$, incompressibility and periodic integration by parts give
