items/lem-em-pec-cavity-eigenfield-boundary-regularity.md:84:5.1 Tangential mixed estimate: for $k=1,2$ put $W=\delta_h^kU$ and test with $V=-\delta_{-h}^k(\eta^2W)$ on a nested patch; the test is admissible by [F2], discrete integration by parts and the shifted product rule give the principal term $\sum_m\int\eta^2a^{ij}(x+he_k)\partial_jW_m\partial_iW_m\ge\alpha\|\eta\nabla W\|_2^2$, and all remaining terms involve $\delta_ha$, $\nabla\eta$, $\nabla U$, $W$ and the Robin product; [F3] bounds them by $\epsilon\|\eta\nabla W\|_2^2+C_\epsilon\|U\|_{H^1}^2$ on the larger patch using $\|W\|_2\le C\|\partial_kU\|_2$ and $\|\delta_{-h}(\eta^2W)\|_2\le C(\|\eta\nabla W\|_2+\|W\|_2)$ from the elementary line formula and Cauchy-Schwarz, the $f$ pairing is bounded without differentiating $f$ using the displayed bound for the test in $L^2$; transfer the quotient in the $G$ pairing to $G$ and use $\|\delta_hG\|_2\le C\|G\|_{H^1}$, and transfer the boundary quotient to $h$ using $\|\delta_hh\|_{L^2(B)}\le C\|h\|_{H^1(B)}$. The exact Robin quotient is $\int_B\beta(y+h)\eta^2|W_3|^2+\int_B(\delta_h\beta)U_3\eta^2W_3$. The trace estimate of [F1] $\|Tz\|_2^2\le\epsilon\|\nabla z\|_2^2+C_\epsilon\|z\|_2^2$ absorbing the Robin and boundary-datum terms even when $\beta$ has either sign; choosing the finitely many epsilons small gives $\|\eta\nabla\delta_h^kU\|_2\le C(\|U\|_{H^1}+\|f\|_2+\|G\|_{H^1}+\|h\|_{H^1(B)})$ uniformly for small $h\ne0$. [step 4.1, F1, F2, F3, algebra]
items/lem-em-pec-cavity-eigenfield-boundary-regularity.md:88:7.1 Bootstrap with boundary commutators: suppose $U\in H^{r+1}$ locally, $r\ge1$; then $f\in H^r$ and $G\in H^{r+1}$ because the eigenvalue term is $\lambda JU$ and the coefficients are smooth. For a tangential multi-index $\alpha$ of order $r$ put $Z=D_y^\alpha U\in H^1$; its first two traces still vanish, and trace commutation is justified by extending localized $H^{r+1}$ classes with the integer-order half-space extension [F4], mollifying in that norm and passing the continuous $H^1$ trace identity to the limit. Applying the weak system of step 4.1 to $Z$ by tangential integration by parts leaves the same principal and Robin terms with bulk datum $D_y^\alpha f\in L^2$, divergence datum $\widetilde G_i=D_y^\alpha G_i-\sum_{0<\gamma\le\alpha}\binom{\alpha}{\gamma}(D_y^\gamma a^{ij})\partial_jD_y^{\alpha-\gamma}U\in H^1$ and boundary commutator $\widetilde h=-\sum_{0<\gamma\le\alpha}\binom{\alpha}{\gamma}(D_y^\gamma\beta)TD_y^{\alpha-\gamma}U_3\in H^1(B)$: indeed $|\alpha-\gamma|\le r-1$, so the indicated derivative is $H^2$ and its trace and tangential derivative traces are $L^2$ by the $H^1$ trace and the just-proved commutation. Steps 5.1-6.1 then give $Z\in H^2$, controlling total order $r+2$ with at most two normal derivatives; differentiating the normal-recovery equation of step 6.1 and inducting on the number of normal derivatives at fixed total order (the non-$(3,3)$ terms have strictly fewer normal derivatives, and all differentiated coefficients and data have the available order) yields $U\in H^{r+2}$; nested patches and the finite boundary/interior cover give $u\in H^m(\Omega)$ for every integer $m$ with $\|u\|_{H^m}\le C_{m,\Omega,\lambda}\|u\|_{H^1}$. [step 4.1, step 5.1, step 6.1, F4, F3, algebra]
items/pthm-csm-dk-qualified-mean-field-limit.md:96:2.1 For fixed $k$ and $T$, the first term in the [F2] bound tends to zero as $N\to\infty$, and its second term tends to zero because the initial empirical expected Wasserstein distance tends to zero for compactly supported iid data. The constants are uniform for $|s|\le T$. Therefore the fixed-$k$ laws converge in every bounded-Lipschitz test on each finite time interval. This is a conditional model deduction with the stated preparation and scaling; no experimental sampling or alternate collision limit enters the argument. [F2, given, algebra] ∎
items/lem-em-smooth-domain-elliptic-regularity.md:61:[F3] **Difference quotients.** The first difference quotient, its discrete integration by parts, the increment estimate $\|\delta_h^iu\|_{L^2(\Omega')}\le\|\partial_iu\|_{L^2}$, the converse characterization and the weak Leibniz rule are available ([[def-first-difference-quotient]], [[lem-difference-quotient-integration-by-parts]], [[thm-difference-quotient-characterisation-of-w-one-p-for-p-greater-than-one]], [[lem-weak-leibniz-rule-with-a-smooth-factor]]).
items/lem-em-smooth-domain-elliptic-regularity.md:77:2.1 Tangential estimate: for a tangential direction $k$ put $w=\delta_h^ku$ and test the local weak identity with $v=-\delta_{-h}^k(\eta^2w)$ for a cutoff $\eta$ supported in a smaller patch; the discrete integration by parts, the shifted product rule and the increment estimates of [F3] give the principal bound $\alpha\|\eta\nabla w\|_2^2$ against cutoff terms bounded by $C\|u\|_{H^1}[\|\eta\nabla w\|_2+\|w\|_2]$, with $\|\delta_ha\|_\infty\le\|\partial_ka\|_\infty$ and the $f,G$ contributions controlled by Cauchy-Schwarz; the absorption inequality $2AB\le(\alpha/2)A^2+2B^2/\alpha$ [F4] yields a bound $\|\eta\nabla\delta_h^ku\|_2\le C(\|u\|_{H^1}+\|f\|_{C^0}+\|G\|_{C^1})$ uniform in small $h$. [step 1.1, F3, F4, algebra]
items/lem-em-smooth-domain-elliptic-regularity.md:79:3.1 Transmission surface terms and two-sided estimates. In an interface chart flatten the sheet to $B=\{s=0\}$, with half-boxes $P_+$ and $P_-$. The weak identity is $$\sum_{\pm}\int_{P_\pm}a^\pm_{ij}\partial_ju\,\partial_iv=\sum_{\pm}\int_{P_\pm}(f^\pm v+G^\pm_i\partial_iv)+\int_B\sigma_B\,Tv,$$ where $a^\pm,f^\pm,G^\pm$ are smooth up to their respective sides, $a^\pm$ are uniformly positive, and $\sigma_B$ is the sheet density multiplied by the smooth surface Jacobian. The solution and admissible tests have one common trace, since they are globally $H^1$; tangential translations preserve this common trace. For a tangential direction $k$ use the single global test $v=-\delta_{-h}^k(\eta^2\delta_h^ku)$, whose restrictions agree on the sheet. Discrete integration by parts on $B$ changes the sheet term to $\int_B(\delta_h^k\sigma_B)T(\eta^2\delta_h^ku)$. The cutoff is compactly supported in the chart, so no edge term occurs; smoothness bounds $\|\delta_h^k\sigma_B\|_{L^2(B)}$ independently of $h$. By [F6] the trace of $\eta^2\delta_h^ku$ is bounded by its $H^1$ norm on either side. Cauchy-Schwarz and Young's inequality bound the sheet term by $\epsilon\|\eta\nabla\delta_h^ku\|_{L^2(P_+\cup P_-)}^2+C_\epsilon(1+\|u\|_{H^1(P_+\cup P_-)}^2)$, since the increment estimate bounds $\delta_h^ku$ in $L^2$. The two positive principal terms sum, and the piecewise coefficient commutators have the same bounds as in step 2.1: tangential differences do not cross the flat sheet. Absorbing the finitely many $\epsilon$ terms yields the uniform two-sided tangential estimate. The distributional limit argument below gives tangential derivatives of the gradient on both sides, and expansion of the local weak equation recovers the pure normal second derivative separately with $a^\pm_{33}\ge\alpha>0$. This proves piecewise $H^2$ with the sheet charge retained. [F2, F3, F4, F6, step 2.1, algebra]
items/lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential.md:81:1.1 The integral in $g_j$ is the spacetime Fourier transform of a compactly supported smooth function, hence decreases faster than every inverse power of $|(\omega,\mathbf k)|$ by repeated integration by parts. Near $\mathbf k=0$, it is bounded by $\int|j|$, while the squared prefactor is $1/(2\omega)=1/(2|\mathbf k|)$. The radial integral of this bound is a constant times $\int_0^1r^2/r\,dr<\infty$. At infinity the rapid Fourier decay and $\|P_T\|\le1$ make the squared norm integrable. Also $P_Tg_j=g_j$ almost everywhere. Therefore $g_j\in\mathcal H_T$. [A1, F1, given, algebra]
items/lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential.md:83:2.1 The one-particle space $\mathcal H_T$ is also separable, although [F2] applies to every complex Hilbert space. Let $D$ be the countable dense subset of real $L^2(\mathbb R^3)$ supplied by [F4]. Its sixfold product is countable by [F5]; identifying each tuple with a three-component complex function whose real and imaginary components lie in $D$ gives a dense subset of $\mathcal H$. Density follows by approximating the six real $L^2$ components, since the squared complex-vector norm is the sum of their squared norms. Hence $\mathcal H$ is separable in its norm topology by [F6]. The image of this countable set under the bounded projection $M_{P_T}$ is countable and dense in $\mathcal H_T$: for $u\in\mathcal H_T$, $M_{P_T}u=u$, so projected approximants to $u$ converge to $u$. Thus $\mathcal H_T$ is separable in its norm topology by [F6], and [F2] applies. By [F3], a vector in $\mathcal D_{\rm fin}$ has only finitely many nonzero sectors. On each such sector [F2] bounds creation and annihilation by the corresponding square-root particle number times $\|g_j\|\|\Psi_n\|$. Thus both maps and their sum are defined on $\mathcal D_{\rm fin}$. The same estimates bound every matrix element between fixed finite-particle vectors by a constant times $\|g_j\|$. On each fixed compact support, repeated integration by parts in step 1.1 bounds $\|g_j\|$ by finitely many smooth-test seminorms, so these matrix elements are continuous in the test-function topology. This proves the asserted distributional smearing without defining a point field. [F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, step 1.1, given]
