items/lem-nrqmx-c2-projector-chern-integrality.md:122:because each compactly supported derivative is uniformly continuous. For sufficiently small $\epsilon$, the convolved supports remain inside their charts. Summing the finitely many pulled-back approximants gives a smooth matrix field $Q_\epsilon\to P$ in $C^2$; replacing it by $(Q_\epsilon+Q_\epsilon^*)/2$ keeps it smooth Hermitian and preserves this convergence. [A1, F1, F3, F6, F11, construct, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-compact-scattering-defects.md:145:8.1 For $v\in C_c^\infty(I)$ compare $Se^{-itk^2}v$ with $T\overline\zeta e^{-itk^2}v$. On $r\ge1$ their $e^{ikr}$ coefficients agree, and their difference, up to an irrelevant overall sign, is $\sqrt{2/\pi}(2i)^{-1}\int_I e^{-ikr-itk^2}(1-\overline\zeta(k)^2)v(k)\,dk$. For $t\to+\infty$ its phase derivative is $-r-2tk$, whose magnitude is at least a positive constant times $r+t$ on $I$. Repeated integration by parts, with no endpoint terms, bounds it by $C_N(r+t)^{-N}$; integration of the squared bound over $r\ge1$ tends to zero for $N\ge1$. On $0<r<1$ both packet integrals are $O(t^{-N})$ uniformly in $r$, by integrating against $-tk^2$; their amplitude derivatives are bounded uniformly on the finite rectangle. Thus $\|e^{-ith_0}Sv-e^{-ith}T\overline\zeta v\|\to0$. Multiplying by $e^{ith}$ identifies the actual Cook wave as $\Omega_+Sv=T\overline\zeta v$ on this dense packet class. This identification has used neither completeness nor a stationary/dynamical correspondence theorem. [step 4.1, step 6.1, step 7.1, algebra]
items/lem-em-smooth-newtonian-upgrade.md:44:Then $Nf\in C^\infty(\mathbb R^3)$, $-\Delta Nf=f$ pointwise, and for every multi-index $\alpha$ the spatial derivative satisfies $D^\alpha Nf=N(D^\alpha f)$. In particular, for smooth compactly supported $\rho$ the field $E=-\varepsilon_0^{-1}\nabla N\rho$ is smooth. The statement is about the explicit integral transform of compactly supported smooth data and does not assume smoothness of the kernel at the pole; the distributional identity $-\Delta Nf=f$ is the one supplied by the Newtonian-potential theorem ([[thm-newtonian-potential-solves-poisson-distributionally]]), upgraded here from a distributional to a pointwise identity for smooth data.
