items/pthm-emg-potentials-and-topological-limits.md:72:[A3] For the punctured-Minkowski specialization equip $M_*$ with the future time orientation selected by $N_*:=\partial_{x^0}$; its displayed metric gives $g(N_*,N_*)=-1$, and its orientation is the displayed product orientation. This is the canonical Lorentzian tuple of the supplier. The current draft [[lem-emg-sphere-flux-holonomy]] states the smooth Cartesian punctured-space form, its unit-sphere flux and Stokes obstruction, the smooth north/south potentials and fixed-$\kappa_0$ transition criterion, and the flat example $A=\kappa_0a\,\nu$ with $\nu=-iw^{-1}dw$ and holonomy $e^{-2\pi ia}$. On an angle chart $w=e^{i\phi}$, its global angular one-form is $\nu=d\phi$. Its complete local proof gives an ordered tetrahedral finite-cycle Stokes argument and gauge-period calculation. The gluing supplier now uses the actual local set-size definition and constructs the normalized bundle and curvature. The exact interfaces match; no independent acceptance is asserted.
items/pthm-emg-potentials-and-topological-limits.md:88:2.2 For $b\ne0$, step 1.2 identifies the explicit field and its canonical Lorentzian tuple with [A3]. That supplier proves that $F_b$ has no global smooth one-form potential, using its ordered tetrahedral smooth singular two-cycle $c$ with $\partial c=0$ and $\int_cF_b=4\pi b$. Its proof establishes the radial projection as an orientation-preserving one-sheeted parametrization and justifies this integral by its finite-parametrization integration supplier. If a global $A$ satisfied $dA=F_b$, finite-chain Stokes [F2] would give $4\pi b=\int_c dA=\int_{\partial c}A=0$, a contradiction. Thus the stated obstruction follows with the exact supplier hypotheses and integration bridge. [A3, F2, step 1.2, algebra]
