items/lem-qftx-hermite-coefficient-decay.md:80:2.1 By [F3] each Hermite factor is a polynomial times $e^{-x_j^2/2}$, so $h_\alpha$ and all its derivatives decay rapidly enough for integration by parts against any Schwartz function. The operator $H^r$ is a finite sum of polynomial-coefficient derivatives and maps Schwartz space continuously to itself, hence $H^r f\in L^2$. Repeated integration by parts in $x_1$ and $x_2$ has no boundary terms; it gives $\langle H^r f,h_\alpha\rangle=\langle f,H^r h_\alpha\rangle=\lambda_\alpha^r\langle f,h_\alpha\rangle$. [F3, step 1.1, algebra]
items/post-cm02-drag.md:21:physical_scope: "Adoption of a constitutive force model for the interaction of a labeled particle with a medium: a linear drag force $-c\\dot x$ with a declared coefficient $c\\ge0$, optionally combined with a constant applied force. The model is phenomenological and restricted: it is adopted for a stated regime (slow motion in a viscous medium, the range in which the linear/Stokes form is used), its coefficient is a model parameter depending on body and medium, and the model neglects the random component of the environment's force. The item asserts no measured value, no derivation of the coefficient from the equation of motion, and no claim that the model applies to a given real motion."
