items/lem-cm-discrete-periodic-summation-by-parts.md:75:- The identity (a) is the discrete analogue of integration by parts on the circle; cyclicity is exactly what makes the boundary term vanish. On a chain with free or fixed ends the boundary terms would appear explicitly, and such models are not covered by this statement.
items/thm-csm-frontier-two-velocity-heat-limit.md:77:[F4] [[thm-ftc-second-part]]: integration of a continuous derivative on a compact interval gives the endpoint difference. Applied repeatedly to periodic smooth initial data, it gives rapid decay of their Fourier coefficients by integration by parts.
items/thm-csm-frontier-two-velocity-heat-limit.md:87:3.1 Repeated integration by parts in the real and imaginary parts using [F4] gives $\sum_n(1+|\kappa_n|)^r|\widehat{\rho_0}(n)|<\infty$ for every integer $r\ge0$; convergence follows by grouping $2^j\le|n|<2^{j+1}$ after choosing more than $r+1$ derivatives. Therefore the heat series and every differentiated series converge uniformly on compact time intervals, and its coefficient equation $y_n'=-D\kappa_n^2y_n$ proves $\partial_tu=D\partial_x^2u$ with $u(0)=\rho_0$. For any other smooth periodic solution $w$, integration by parts in its Fourier coefficients gives $\widehat w(n)'=-D\kappa_n^2\widehat w(n)$ with initial value $\widehat{\rho_0}(n)$. Multiplying by $e^{D\kappa_n^2t}$ shows that $e^{D\kappa_n^2t}\widehat w(n,t)$ has zero derivative, so $\widehat w(n,t)=\widehat{\rho_0}(n)e^{-D\kappa_n^2t}$ for every $n$ and $t$. Fourier completeness [F3] therefore gives $w(t)=u(t)$ as $L^2(\mathbb T_L,dm_L)$ classes for each fixed $t$, hence almost everywhere. Both are continuous in $x$; if they differed at a point, their difference would be bounded away from zero on some nonempty open arc, which has positive normalized Lebesgue measure $dm_L$. This contradicts equality almost everywhere, so $w(t,x)=u(t,x)$ pointwise for every $t,x$. [A1, F3, F4, step 2.1, algebra]
items/pthm-emx-canonical-kinetic-integral.md:43:      locator: "Section 'Angular momenta and geometric phases', Box 1, PDF pp. 3–4; contextual discussion only. The global identity and its boundary term are proved by componentwise integration by parts here."
items/ex-emg-fx-small-constant-field-ball.md:143:1.2 Smoothness bootstrap, initial gain. Set $u=u_\varepsilon=\psi_\varepsilon-1$ and $f=(\varepsilon^2a^2/4)\psi_\varepsilon^{-3}$, so $-\Delta u=f$ and $u=0$ on $\partial\Omega$. The branch gives $u\in C^{2,\alpha}(\overline\Omega)$ and $\psi_\varepsilon\ge1$. The identities $\partial_i(\psi^{-3})=-3\psi^{-4}\partial_i\psi$ and $\partial_{ij}(\psi^{-3})=12\psi^{-5}(\partial_i\psi)(\partial_j\psi)-3\psi^{-4}\partial_{ij}\psi$ follow from [F11] and the product rule and show that $f\in C^2(\overline\Omega)$, with bounded derivatives through order two, hence $f\in H^2(\Omega)$ by integration by parts against compactly supported tests. By [F6], $u\in H^1_0(\Omega)$. Its classical equation tested against $C_c^\infty(\Omega)$ gives the weak identity; continuity of both sides in the $H^1$ test norm and the defining density of $C_c^\infty$ in $H^1_0$ extend it to every test in $H^1_0$. Applying [F8] with $m=2$ yields $u\in H^4(\Omega)$. [A1, F2, F6, F7, F8, F11, algebra]
items/lem-csm-frontier-ssep-heat-flow.md:83:1.1 Since $0\le\rho_0\le1$ and $dx(\mathbb T^d)=1$, $\rho_0\in L^1$ and $|\widehat\rho_0(k)|\le1$ for every $k$. For any smooth $G$ and integer $m\ge1$, periodic integration by parts gives $(1+4\pi^2|k|^2)^m\widehat G(k)=\widehat{(1-\Delta)^mG}(k)$, hence $|\widehat G(k)|\le\|(1-\Delta)^mG\|_{L^1}(1+4\pi^2|k|^2)^{-m}$. If $q\ge0$ is fixed, choose $m>(d+q)/2$; grouping lattice points by $r=\|k\|_\infty$ gives at most $(2r+1)^d-(2r-1)^d\le C_dr^{d-1}$ points in shell $r$. The shell contribution to $\sum_k(1+|k|)^q|\widehat G(k)|$ is at most $C r^{d-1+q-2m}$. Grouping shells into dyadic blocks $2^j\le r<2^{j+1}$ bounds the $j$th block by $C'2^{j(d+q-2m)}$, a convergent geometric series since $2m>d+q$. Thus $\sum_k(1+|k|)^q|\widehat G(k)|<\infty$. [A1, F1, construct, algebra]
items/lem-csm-frontier-ssep-heat-flow.md:91:3.2 Parseval and the heat coefficients give $\|\rho_t-\rho_0\|_2^2=\sum_k|\widehat\rho_0(k)|^2|e^{-4\pi^2|k|^2t}-1|^2\to0$ as $t\downarrow0$: choose a finite set whose tail in the convergent series $\sum_k|\widehat\rho_0(k)|^2$ is small, then take the limit on that finite set. For $\epsilon>0$ put $a_\epsilon(z)=\frac12(1-z/\sqrt{z^2+\epsilon^2})$ and $\Phi_\epsilon(z)=\int_0^z\int_0^r a_\epsilon(q)\,dq\,dr$. Then $\Phi_\epsilon$ is smooth and convex, $\Phi_\epsilon(0)=\Phi_\epsilon'(0)=0$, and $0\le\Phi_\epsilon(z)\le z^2/2$. On any interval $[a,b]\subset(0,T]$, the heat equation, chain rule, and periodic integration by parts give $\int\Phi_\epsilon(\rho_b)-\int\Phi_\epsilon(\rho_a)=-\int_a^b\int\Phi_\epsilon''(\rho_s)\sum_{i=1}^d|\partial_{x_i}\rho_s|^2\,dx\,ds\le0$. For each real $z$, the defining double integral tends to $\frac12(z^-)^2$, where $z^-:=\max\{-z,0\}$; dominated convergence on the bounded range of $\rho$ over $[a,b]\times\mathbb T^d$ gives the same monotonicity for $\|\rho_t^-\|_2^2$. Letting $a\downarrow0$, the $L^2$ continuity just proved and $\rho_0\ge0$ imply $\rho_t^-=0$. The assumed datum already gives the required bound when $T=0$. [A1, F1, F2, step 2.1, algebra]
items/lem-csm-frontier-ssep-heat-flow.md:93:4.1 For the upper bound put $b_\epsilon(z)=\frac12(1+(z-1)/\sqrt{(z-1)^2+\epsilon^2})$ and $\Psi_\epsilon(z)=\int_1^z\int_1^r b_\epsilon(q)\,dq\,dr$. This is smooth and convex with $\Psi_\epsilon(1)=\Psi_\epsilon'(1)=0$ and $0\le\Psi_\epsilon(z)\le(z-1)^2/2$. The same integration by parts gives $\int\Psi_\epsilon(\rho_b)\le\int\Psi_\epsilon(\rho_a)$ for $0<a<b$, and $\Psi_\epsilon(z)\to\frac12((z-1)^+)^2$, where $r^+:=\max\{r,0\}$. Dominated convergence and $L^2$ continuity at zero, together with $\rho_0\le1$, give $(\rho_t-1)^+=0$. The zero Fourier coefficient is unchanged by the heat multiplier, so $\int\rho_t=\widehat\rho_0(0)=\int\rho_0$. The assumed datum already gives the bound when $T=0$. [A1, F1, F2, step 3.2, algebra]
items/cex-qftx-exact-differentiated-scaling-degree.md:43:      locator: Section 3.2, printed pp. 24–25, integration by parts and derivative-compatible extension; the massless contact-term witness is calculated locally.
items/cex-qftx-exact-differentiated-scaling-degree.md:75:[F2] [[lem-qftx-balanced-local-functional-representatives]] and the definition of local functionals give $\int fP\phi=\int(Pf)\phi$ by integration by parts, with the same actual functional as input.
items/ex-nrqmx-single-mode-squeezing.md:106:1.1 Work in Schrödinger coordinates via [F1], with inner product linear in the first argument. Put $bf=(xf+f')/\sqrt2$ and $b^\dagger f=(xf-f')/\sqrt2$ for $f\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R)$; both expressions again lie in $\mathcal S$. For $c_n=\langle f,g_n\rangle$, integration by parts gives $\langle bf,g_m\rangle=\langle f,b^\dagger g_m\rangle=\sqrt{m+1}c_{m+1}$ and $\langle b^\dagger f,g_m\rangle=\sqrt m c_{m-1}$ for $m\ge1$, with the latter coefficient zero for $m=0$. The boundary terms vanish since $f$ and each Hermite function are Schwartz. Completeness and Parseval in [F1] give $\sum_{n\ge0}n|c_n|^2=\|bf\|^2<\infty$ and $\sum_{n\ge0}(n+1)|c_n|^2=\|b^\dagger f\|^2<\infty$. Therefore [F5] identifies the actual closed Fock operators with $a=b$ and $a^*=b^\dagger$ on every Schwartz vector, not only on finite Hermite sums. Since $bf$ is Schwartz, $f\in D(a^*a)=D(N)$, and direct differentiation gives $a^*af=b^\dagger bf=(x^2f-f''-f)/2=((Q^2+P^2)/(2\hbar)-I/2)f$. Thus the ladder, adjoint and number identities needed below all hold on the transported Schwartz domain. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F5, algebra]
items/ex-emg-xs-rn-charge-sign.md:66:2.1 At fixed $x^0$ and admitted radius $r>0$, the sphere map $u\mapsto(x^0,r,u)$ from the oriented unit sphere into the RN product is a smooth embedding; it is a product-factor inclusion, so it is injective with injective differential and has the continuous inverse given by the sphere projection. The source sphere is compact and has no boundary, and the fields are smooth on a neighborhood since $r>0$ and $f(r)\ne0$. Thus its pulled-back field and dual are smooth compactly supported top forms, and the flux integrals below are the well-defined operation in [F4]. The two fields are $\mathcal F_\pm=\mp\ell r^{-2}dx^0\wedge dr$, so $\mathcal F_-=-\mathcal F_+$. If $f>0$, use the positively oriented coframe $e^0=\sqrt f\,dx^0$, $e^1=f^{-1/2}dr$, $e^2=r\,d\theta$, $e^3=r\sin\theta\,d\phi$. If $f<0$, use $e^0=(-f)^{-1/2}dr$, $e^1=-(-f)^{1/2}dx^0$, $e^2=r\,d\theta$, $e^3=r\sin\theta\,d\phi$. In both cases $dx^0\wedge dr=e^0\wedge e^1$ and $e^2\wedge e^3=r^2\omega_{S^2}$, so [F2] gives $\star_g(dx^0\wedge dr)=-r^2\omega_{S^2}$ and $\star_g\mathcal F_\pm=\pm\ell\,\omega_{S^2}$. For the explicit sphere parametrization $s(\theta,\phi)=(\sin\theta\cos\phi,\sin\theta\sin\phi,\cos\theta)$, differentiation gives $s\cdot(s_\theta\times s_\phi)=\sin\theta$, so the outward form pulls back to $\sin\theta\,d\theta\wedge d\phi$, as in [F1]. The sine density is continuous, hence integrable by [F3]; its primitive is $-\cos\theta$. Its integral on $\delta\le\theta\le\pi-\delta$, $0\le\phi\le2\pi$ is $4\pi\cos\delta$, and the two omitted caps have total area $4\pi(1-\cos\delta)\to0$ by the same bounded polar density; the seam has zero width in the azimuthal integral. Thus $\int_{S^2}\omega_{S^2}=2\pi[-\cos\theta]_0^\pi=4\pi$. The normalized oriented electric sphere fluxes are therefore $(4\pi)^{-1}\int_{S_r}\star_g\mathcal F_\pm=\pm\ell$. Thus the metric does not determine the sign of the electric flux parameter. [F1, F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
items/cex-fd-vacuum-velocity-determination.md:52:Let $\Omega=(0,1)^3$ metres and $I=(0,T_f)$ with $T_f>0$. The weak conservative mass and momentum equations use mass density $\rho$, velocity $u$, momentum density $\rho u$, mass flux $\rho u$, momentum flux $\rho u\otimes u-\sigma$, and momentum source $\rho b$, tested against compactly supported $C^1$ scalar and vector fields with the conservative initial-data terms. Set $\rho\equiv0$, $\sigma\equiv0$, $b\equiv0$, and initial mass and momentum densities equal to zero. Both the velocity field $u_1\equiv0$ and any fixed nonzero constant velocity field $u_2\equiv v_*$ (with $v_*\in\mathbb R^3$ in metres per second) are bounded and smooth. The two choices have the same zero conservative mass and momentum variables and satisfy the same weak mass and momentum equations, although $u_1\ne u_2$ everywhere. Thus those conservative equations alone do not determine a velocity on the set where $\rho=0$. The example concerns the formal vacuum degeneracy of the equations, not a fluid-particle velocity in physical vacuum, and makes no claim about an energy or constitutive equation.
items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-quadratic-free-packet-decay.md:69:1.1 [Exact Fourier normalization and initial packet] Put $g(x)=\int e^{ik\cdot x}a(k)dk$. Differentiation in $x$ multiplies the compact smooth integrand by powers of $ik$, and integration by parts in $k$ turns multiplication by powers of $x$ into derivatives of that compact smooth integrand. Hence every $x^\alpha\partial_x^\beta g$ is bounded, so $g$ is Schwartz and in $L^1\cap L^2$. More explicitly $(1+|x|^2)^2g(x)=\int e^{ik\cdot x}(1-\Delta_k)^2a(k)dk$, so $\|g\|_1\le\|(1-\Delta_k)^2a\|_1\int(1+|x|^2)^{-2}dx<\infty$. Substituting $k=2\pi\xi$ into the defining integral and Fourier inversion gives $\mathcal F_2g(\xi)=(2\pi)^3a(2\pi\xi)$. Thus the inverse Fourier transform of $e^{-4\pi^2it|\xi|^2}\mathcal F_2g$ is exactly $\psi_t$, with no additional $2\pi$ in its phase. [F1, F2, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-quadratic-free-packet-decay.md:75:3.1 [All-order nonstationary integration by parts] The operator $L_v=c_v\cdot\nabla_k$ satisfies $L_ve^{it\phi_v}=it e^{it\phi_v}$ for every real nonzero $t$. Repeated integration by parts on the compact amplitude, using the formal adjoint $B_v$, gives $\psi_t(tv)=(it)^{-N}\int_Oe^{it\phi_v(k)}B_v^Na(k)dk$ for every $N\ge0$. Step 1.2 yields $\sup_{v\in K'}|\psi_t(tv)|\le C_{K',N}|t|^{-N}$, independently of the sign of $t$. If the Statement's “every integer $N$” includes $N<0$, the elementary bound $|\psi_t(tv)|\le\|a\|_1\le\|a\|_1|t|^{-N}$ for $|t|\ge1$ supplies those cases as well. Combining this with step 2.1 proves both unchanged public estimates. [step 2.1, step 1.2, F2, algebra] ∎
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-atomic-dipole-matrix-element-domain.md:89:[F5] Polar volume integration is $dx=r^2dr\,d\sigma$ ([[thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure]]); nonnegative product integrals use [[thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces]], and absolutely convergent product integrals use [[thm-tonelli-and-fubini-for-completed-product-measures]]. For $a>0$ and integer $p\ge0$, integration by parts starting with $p=0$ gives $\int_0^\infty r^pe^{-ar}dr=p!/a^{p+1}$; the boundary terms vanish since a sufficiently high exponential-series term bounds every fixed polynomial power ([[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[thm-derivative-of-exponential]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]). Cauchy-Schwarz for the $L^2$ and $L^2(S^2,\sigma)$ pairings ([[cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two]], [[def-inner-product-space]], [[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]], [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]]).
items/lem-nrqmx-finite-maslov-phase-parity.md:92:3.1 Put $V(t)=J_0K(t)$ and $m(t)=\int_0^t\|V(s)\|\,ds$. The iterated-integral series for the matrix equation has $n$th term bounded by $m(t)^n/n!$: the scalar product integrand is symmetric in its $n$ variables and the ordered simplex is one of the $n!$ equal regions of their product cube, up to null diagonals. Thus the series converges uniformly, with sum bounded by $e^{m(T)}$. It satisfies $S(t)=I+\int_0^tV(s)S(s)\,ds$, since the series under that integral is dominated by $\|V(s)\|e^{m(T)}$. For any integrable scalar $f$, choose $M$ with $\int_{|f|>M}|f|<\varepsilon/2$; then $\int_E|f|\le M\lambda(E)+\varepsilon/2$, proving absolute continuity of its primitive by the finite disjoint-interval definition. Applied entrywise to the integral equation, this proves absolute continuity of $S$, and [F6] gives $\dot S=VS$ almost everywhere. If two solutions have the same initial value, their continuous difference obeys $\Delta(t)=\int_0^tV(s)\Delta(s)\,ds$. Iterating this equation $n$ times gives $\|\Delta(t)\|\le\sup_s\|\Delta(s)\|m(T)^n/n!$, which tends to zero. This proves uniqueness of the classical absolutely continuous solution. Product differentiation gives $(S^TJ_0S)'=0$ almost everywhere, so [F5] and its initial value give symplecticity at every time. A finite subdivision, using uniform continuity and invertibility of $S$, makes each $S(t)S(t_j)^{-1}$ lie in the neighborhood of step 1.1. Set $U(t)=L(S(t)S(t_j)^{-1})U(t_j)$, with $U(0)=I$. To justify the Schwartz calculus, restrict each parameter path to a small compact neighborhood where the local factors are smooth. The seminorm estimates of step 1.1, also for their parameter derivatives, imply $p((L(\alpha)-L(\beta))f)\le C_{p,q}|\alpha-\beta|q(f)$ there and $p(\partial_t L(\alpha(t))f)\le C_{p,q}|\alpha'(t)|q(f)$ almost everywhere, for some Schwartz seminorm $q$. One can obtain the first bound by integrating along the straight parameter segment inside that neighborhood; finite smaller neighborhoods suffice. Matrix inversion and the local factor parameters have bounded derivatives on those sets, and $|S'|\le e^{m(T)}\|V\|$ is integrable. A finite-dimensional smooth map with bounded derivative on the small convex parameter neighborhood is Lipschitz there, hence takes an absolutely continuous parameter curve to an absolutely continuous curve; at points of differentiability of that curve, its differentiability gives the chain rule directly. The same derivative estimate in each seminorm proves this assertion for the explicit Schwartz-valued factors. Products are covered by the two-variable version of those bounds. Thus these estimates prove absolute continuity in every seminorm, the chain and product rules almost everywhere, and the same assertions for the inverse factors. Step 2.1 therefore gives $i\hbar\dot U=H_KU$ almost everywhere, with continuous matching at joins and covariance from step 1.1. If two such evolutions have the same initial vector, their difference $w(t)$ is absolutely continuous in $L^2$ and satisfies the same equation. Its squared norm is absolutely continuous and has derivative $2\operatorname{Re}\langle-iH_Kw/\hbar,w\rangle=0$ almost everywhere, because the real symmetrically ordered expression is symmetric on Schwartz space by integration by parts. By [F5] the difference remains zero. Density and unitarity extend uniqueness to all $L^2$. Thus existence, uniqueness, inverse continuity and covariance hold for the full integrable coefficient class in the Statement. [F1, F4, F5, F6, step 1.1, step 2.1, construct, algebra]
items/lem-nrqmx-landau-form-diagonalization.md:110:[F2] The $2\pi$-frequency Fourier transform on $L^1$ has kernel $e^{-2\pi i y\xi}$ ([[def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn]]), and its extension to $L^2$ is unitary ([[thm-plancherel]]). The wave-number convention in the Statement is the unitary rescaling $\mathcal F_y g(k)=(2\pi)^{-1/2}\widehat g(k/(2\pi))$; for Schwartz $g$ it satisfies $\mathcal F_y(-i\partial_y g)=k\mathcal F_y g$ by integration by parts, and Fourier transform preserves Schwartz space ([[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]]). The Euclidean $L^2$ product identification and Fubini apply ([[lem-nrqm-composites-euclidean-l2-product-unitary]], [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]); the integration-by-parts identity used for the derivative multiplier is [[thm-integration-by-parts-for-absolutely-continuous-functions]].
items/lem-nrqmx-landau-form-diagonalization.md:121:[F4] Translation of one Lebesgue variable preserves its integral, and integration by parts holds for smooth compactly supported functions ([[cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions]], [[thm-integration-by-parts-for-absolutely-continuous-functions]]).
items/pthm-em-dipole-scattering-cross-section.md:105:[F7] Window construction toolbox: smooth compactly supported cutoffs and localized bumps exist with the properties used below ([[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]], [[def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump]]); integration by parts holds on a bounding rectangle ([[thm-integration-by-parts]], [[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]]), and the Riemann-Lebesgue comparison identifies the Lebesgue and Riemann integrals of the continuous integrands used there ([[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]]); the first fundamental theorem gives the derivative of an integral over a moving endpoint ([[thm-ftc-first-part]]), and a continuous function on an interval with zero derivative is constant ([[cor-zero-derivative-implies-constant]]). Rectangles, cubes and their volumes are those of [[def-multidimensional-rectangle-and-volume]]; continuous real functions on nondegenerate closed rectangles are Riemann integrable ([[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]). The integral is monotone ([[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]]), so a nonnegative continuous cutoff equal to $1$ near a nonempty cube has a strictly positive finite integral; dividing by that integral normalizes the spatial bump.
items/pthm-em-dipole-scattering-cross-section.md:121:3.2 [Matched compact-window realization] Let the window be a compact interval $W$ in the source-time line and let $a_0,b_0\in\mathbb R^3$. Choose a real $C^\infty$ cutoff $\eta$ in time, equal to $1$ on a neighbourhood of $W$, supported in a compact interval $(t_0,t_1)$ strictly containing $W$, as supplied by [F7], and set $p_\eta:=\eta\,(p_{\rm h}+a_0+b_0u)$. Choose a nonempty closed cube $Q\subset B_a$ and a cutoff $\chi\in C_c^\infty(B_a)$ with $0\le\chi\le1$ and $\chi=1$ near $Q$, by [F7]. On a bounding rectangle, Fubini and integral monotonicity give $0<\operatorname{vol}(Q)\le C:=\int\chi<\infty$; set $\sigma:=\chi/C$, which is nonnegative, smooth, supported in $B_a$ and has $\int\sigma=1$ and set $\rho(y,u):=-p_\eta(u)\cdot\nabla\sigma(y)$, $J(y,u):=p_\eta'(u)\sigma(y)$; both are $C^\infty$ and vanish outside $\overline B_a(0)\times[t_0,t_1]$, and $\partial_u\rho+\operatorname{div}J=-p_\eta'\cdot\nabla\sigma+p_\eta'\cdot\nabla\sigma=0$, so the conservation equation holds. Integrating by parts on a rectangle containing $B_a$ and using Fubini as in [F7], $\int_{B_a}\rho(y,u)\,dy=-p_\eta(u)\cdot\int_{B_a}\nabla\sigma=0$, so the total charge vanishes, and $\int_{B_a}y_i\rho(y,u)\,dy=-\sum_jp_{\eta,j}(u)\int_{B_a}y_i\partial_j\sigma(y)\,dy=p_{\eta,i}(u)$ because $\int_{B_a}y_i\partial_j\sigma=-\delta_{ij}\int_{B_a}\sigma=-\delta_{ij}$ by the same integration by parts; hence the actual moment of the source is $p_{\rm act}=p_\eta$. On $W$ one has $\eta\equiv1$, so $p_{\rm act}''=p_{\rm h}''$ there and the mismatch $M$ vanishes on the window; applying the fidelity bounds of step 2.2 to this source preserves every finite-radius and source-variation error term $D,\Gamma_E,\Gamma_B$ computed from it, and outside the window the moment is tapered, so no eternal harmonic moment is asserted. [step 2.2, F6, F7, algebra]
