items/pthm-fd-rectangular-weak-pressure.md:116:1.1 For each side interval set $m_i=(a_i+b_i)/2$, $d_i=(b_i-a_i)/4>0$, and $r_i(x_i)=(x_i-m_i)/d_i$. Define $g_i(x_i)=\exp(-1/(1-r_i(x_i)^2))$ when $|r_i(x_i)|<1$ and $g_i=0$ otherwise. Its support is the compact interval $[m_i-d_i,m_i+d_i]\subset(a_i,b_i)$. Each repeated derivative inside that support is a finite sum of a polynomial in $r_i$ times a negative power of $1-r_i^2$ times the exponential; since $y^{-N}e^{-1/y}\to0$ as $y\downarrow0$ for every $N$, all derivatives vanish at the support endpoints and $g_i\in C_c^\infty((a_i,b_i))$. Positivity and compact support give $I_i=\int_{a_i}^{b_i}g_i\,dx_i\in(0,\infty)$. Put $\eta_i=g_i/I_i$ and $\theta(x)=\eta_1(x_1)\eta_2(x_2)\eta_3(x_3)$; then $\theta\in C_c^\infty(\Omega)$ and Fubini gives $\int_\Omega\theta=1$. Set $\nu=\mu/\rho_0>0$. The domain, initial datum, closure spaces, AC, and Countable Choice match [F2], so choose its global weak velocity $u$ with the stated initial and restart energy inequalities. To match the stress model, first take compactly supported smooth solenoidal $u,\phi$. Then $2D(u):\nabla\phi=\nabla u:\nabla\phi+\partial_j u_i\partial_i\phi_j$; integration by parts makes the last term $-\int u_i\partial_i(\operatorname{div}\phi)=0$. The pressure term also vanishes because $\operatorname{div}\phi=0$. The bilinear forms are continuous in $V$, so density extends this identity to the closure-space weak velocity. Hence [F2] is exactly the weak velocity input for [A1]. [given, A1, F1, F2, A2, A3, algebra]
items/pthm-em-biot-savart.md:63:[F3] For a closed embedded $C^2$ arc-length curve $\gamma$ and $I\in\mathbb R$, the tangent line current $j$ has $\operatorname{div}j=0$ distributionally; for any family of nonnegative smooth radial unit-mass kernels supported in $B_\varepsilon(0)$ the mollified fields $j_\varepsilon=j*\eta_\varepsilon$ are smooth, compactly supported and divergence-free; their Biot-Savart fields converge with every derivative uniformly on every compact $K$ disjoint from $\gamma$ to $B_\gamma(x)=\frac{\mu_0I}{4\pi}\int_0^L\frac{\gamma'(s)\times(x-\gamma(s))}{|x-\gamma(s)|^3}\,ds$; and the same field has a locally integrable representative on $\mathbb R^3$, is smooth off $\gamma$, satisfies $\operatorname{div}T_{B_\gamma}=0$ and $\operatorname{curl}T_{B_\gamma}=\mu_0j$ globally, and decays with $|B_\gamma(x)|\le\frac{\mu_0|I|L}{4\pi(|x|-a)^2}$ for $|x|>a$ when $\gamma\subset B_a(0)$ ([[lem-em-thin-current-limit]]).
items/pthm-em-biot-savart.md:79:2.1 By [F3], the tangent line current $j$ of the declared loop is divergence-free, its mollified currents $j_\varepsilon$ are smooth, compactly supported and divergence-free, so, taking $\Omega=\mathbb R^3$ for this loop construction, each $j_\varepsilon$ is a compact smooth conserved current of the class [F4](b); step 1.1 applied with $J:=j_\varepsilon$ identifies the field of $j_\varepsilon$ with its displayed Biot-Savart integral. The supplier's every-derivative uniform convergence on every compact $K$ disjoint from $\gamma$ therefore identifies the limit field with $B_\gamma(x)=\frac{\mu_0I}{4\pi}\int_0^L\frac{\gamma'(s)\times(x-\gamma(s))}{|x-\gamma(s)|^3}\,ds$ on the complement of $\gamma$. [step 1.1, F3, F4]
items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-free-gaussian-dispersive-kernel.md:89:4.1 [The full intersection class and domain preservation] For $\psi\in L^1\cap L^2$, choose Schwartz $\psi_n\to\psi$ jointly in $L^1$ and $L^2$: first truncate the input in support and value, then convolve each bounded compactly supported truncation with a smooth compact approximate identity and choose a small enough radius for both norm errors. Translation continuity in each $L^p$, $p=1,2$, follows first for finite box-step functions by the volume of symmetric differences and then by their elementary density; thus the convolution errors tend to zero in both norms. Each approximant is compactly supported smooth and hence Schwartz; at most countable choices are used. The uniform kernel bound gives $\|K_t\psi_n-K_t\psi\|_\infty\le(4\pi|t|)^{-3/2}\|\psi_n-\psi\|_1\to0$, while the unitary extension of step 3.1 converges in $L^2$ to $e^{-itH_0}\psi$. The squared-error subsequence argument of step 3.1 identifies these two limits almost everywhere, proving (a) on the full $L^1\cap L^2$ class. The absolutely convergent integral is everywhere defined and directly bounded by $(4\pi|t|)^{-3/2}\|\psi\|_1$, proving (b). Finally multiplication by $e^{-itq}$ preserves exactly the domain $q\mathcal F_2\psi\in L^2$ and its weighted norm, so $H_0e^{-itH_0}\psi=e^{-itH_0}H_0\psi$ and $\|H_0e^{-itH_0}\psi\|_2=\|H_0\psi\|_2$. This proves (c) and all assertions. [step 3.1, F1, F4, algebra] ∎
items/ex-emx-polarization-latitude-phase.md:58:[F1] [[pthm-emx-helicity-rotation-weight]] states that in a single unit frame defined on a neighborhood of the loop, $\mathrm{Hol}_\gamma=\exp(i\oint_\gamma A)$. For its Stokes clause, when $\gamma$ is piecewise smooth with associated finite smooth singular path chain $c_\gamma$, a finite smooth singular $2$-chain $\Sigma$ in one unit-frame domain satisfying $\partial\Sigma=c_\gamma$ gives $\mathrm{Hol}_\gamma=\exp(i\int_\Sigma F_\sigma)$.
items/lem-tgr-cattaneo-stability-causality.md:88:2.1 Fix a periodic solution from [F1]. For $k\ne0$, choose a coordinate $j$ with $|k_j|\ge |k|/\sqrt3$; periodic integration by parts $N$ times and $|\widehat{\partial_j^N U}_k|\le\sup_x|\partial_j^N U(t,x)|$ give $|\widehat U_k(t)|\le C_{N,t,L}(1+|k|)^{-N}$. For each integer $n\ge1$, the disjoint unit cubes centered at lattice points with $n\le|k|<n+1$ lie in an annulus of bounded extra width and volume $O((n+1)^2)$, so the shell has $O((n+1)^2)$ points. Hence $\sum_k(1+|k|)^{-N}<\infty$ for $N>3$, and the Fourier series converges absolutely and uniformly. To identify its sum with $U$, use the product Fejer kernel: in one dimension the normalized kernel is $F_N(y)=\frac1{L(N+1)}|\sum_{\ell=0}^N e^{2\pi i\ell y/L}|^2$. It is nonnegative with integral one, and for torus distance $|y|\ge\delta$ with $0<\delta<L/2$, the geometric-sum formula bounds it by $[L(N+1)\sin^2(\pi\delta/L)]^{-1}$. For the three-dimensional product, the mass outside the cube of coordinate distances less than $\delta$ is bounded by the sum of the three one-dimensional tail masses, hence tends to zero. Uniform continuity of $U(t,\cdot)$ then shows that convolution with these kernels converges uniformly to $U(t,\cdot)$. The convolutions are finite Fourier sums with weights $\prod_{j=1}^3(1-|k_j|/(N+1))_+$; absolute convergence makes the same sums converge uniformly to $\sum_k\widehat U_k(t)e^{2\pi i k\cdot x/L}$. Thus this series equals $U(t,x)$. [F1, step 1.4, algebra]
items/ex-em-elementary-mathematical-prerequisites-hypothesis-boundaries.md:64:Then $r$ is of class $C^2$ on the open set $\mathbb R^2\times I$, the tangent vectors are $r_u=(a,0,0)$ and $r_v=(0,a,0)$ with $r_u\times r_v=(0,0,a^2)\ne0$ on $D^\circ\times I^\circ=D^\circ\times I$, the field $B$ is constant with $\operatorname{div}B=0$, and $v$ is $C^1$ on $\mathbb R^3\times I$ with $v(r(u,v,t),t)=(a'(t)u,a'(t)v,0)=r_t(u,v,t)$; so the data satisfy the standing hypotheses of [[def-em-moving-patch]]. For every $t\in I$ the parametrized flux is $\Phi(t)=a(t)^2$, hence $\Phi'(t)=2a(t)a'(t)$; the fixed-time surface integral of $\partial_tB$ is $0$; and $\operatorname{curl}(v\times B)=-2(a'(t)/a(t))\,e_3$, so patch Stokes gives $\oint_{\partial S(t)}(v\times B)\cdot d\mathbf r=-2a(t)a'(t)$, exactly the sign in the formula of [[lem-em-moving-flux]]. Dropping the term $-\oint(v\times B)\cdot d\mathbf r$ — that is, treating the moving patch as fixed — would leave $\Phi'=0$, which the direct derivative contradicts whenever $a'(t)\ne0$. All maps are of the stated regularity and no singular support is involved; the example verifies the sign convention and the necessity of the motion term, and asserts no physical law.
items/ex-em-elementary-mathematical-prerequisites-hypothesis-boundaries.md:78:[L5] Patch Stokes: for a $C^2$ patch over a finite elementary Green region and a $C^1$ field $G$ on a neighbourhood of its image, $\oint_{\varphi(\partial D)}G\cdot d\mathbf r=\int_D\langle(\operatorname{curl}G)\circ\varphi,\varphi_u\times\varphi_v\rangle$, with the induced boundary chain, the positive boundary orientation and the line integrals as cited ([[thm-the-classical-stokes-theorem-for-a-c2-surface-patch]], [[def-the-induced-boundary-chain-of-a-c2-surface-patch]], [[def-positive-orientation-for-elementary-region-boundaries]], [[def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths]], [[def-oriented-unit-normal-and-flux-of-a-surface-patch]]).
items/ex-em-elementary-mathematical-prerequisites-hypothesis-boundaries.md:82:**Proof technique:** direct; evaluate the parametrized flux and its derivative, evaluate the two terms of the transport identity by explicit vector calculus and Stokes, and compare.
items/ex-em-elementary-mathematical-prerequisites-hypothesis-boundaries.md:84:1.1 Setting and regularity. The map $r(u,v,t)=(a(t)u,a(t)v,0)$ is of class $C^2$ on the open set $\mathbb R^2\times I$, since it is polynomial in $(u,v)$ with $C^2$ coefficients, with $r_u=(a,0,0)$, $r_v=(0,a,0)$ and $r_u\times r_v=(0,0,a^2)\ne0$ on $D^\circ\times I$ because $a>0$; further $v(x,t)=\frac{a'(t)}{a(t)}(x_1,x_2,0)$ is $C^1$ jointly, and $v(r(u,v,t),t)=\frac{a'}{a}(au,av,0)=(a'u,a'v,0)=r_t(u,v,t)$, while $\operatorname{div}B=0$ for the constant field $B=e_3$. Thus the geometric and analytic hypotheses of [L1] hold, and $D$ is the elementary Green region of [L2]. At each time $D$ has connected interior and $r(\cdot,t)$ is injective since $a(t)>0$, so it is a regular patch for the Stokes application [L5]. [given, L1, L2, L4, algebra]
items/ex-em-elementary-mathematical-prerequisites-hypothesis-boundaries.md:88:2.2 The two terms on the right-hand side. Since $B$ is constant, $\partial_tB\equiv0$ and the surface integral $\int_{S(t)}\partial_tB\cdot n\,dS$ vanishes. For the boundary term, [L4] gives $v\times B=\frac{a'}{a}(x_1,x_2,0)\times(0,0,1)=\frac{a'}{a}(x_2,-x_1,0)$ and then $\operatorname{curl}(v\times B)=\bigl(0,0,\partial_1(-\tfrac{a'}{a}x_1)-\partial_2(\tfrac{a'}{a}x_2)\bigr)=(0,0,-2\tfrac{a'}{a})=-2\tfrac{a'}{a}e_3$ at time $t$; consequently $(\operatorname{curl}(v\times B))\circ r=-2\tfrac{a'}{a}e_3$ is constant on $D$, and patch Stokes [L5] together with the constant-integral fact [L3] gives $\oint_{\partial S(t)}(v\times B)\cdot d\mathbf r=\int_D\langle-2\tfrac{a'}{a}e_3,(0,0,a^2)\rangle\,du\,dv=-2\tfrac{a'}{a}a^2\int_Ddu\,dv=-2a(t)a'(t)$. [step 1.1, L3, L4, L5, algebra]
items/ex-em-elementary-mathematical-prerequisites-hypothesis-boundaries.md:95:- The computation uses no choice principle; it is finite algebra and one application of the published Stokes theorem for a patch over the unit square.
items/def-fd-creeping-stokes-model.md:4:title: Restricted incompressible creeping-flow Stokes model
items/def-fd-creeping-stokes-model.md:48:Use the Newtonian Cartesian inertial chart convention of the earlier classical-frame definition, with spatial coordinates $x$ in metres. Let $\Omega\subseteq\mathbb R^3$ be open, $\rho_0>0$ constant density in $\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, $\mu>0$ constant dynamic viscosity in $\mathrm{Pa\,s}$, $u\in C^2(\Omega;\mathbb R^3)$ velocity in $\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, $p\in C^1(\Omega)$ pressure in pascals, and $b\in C^0(\Omega;\mathbb R^3)$ specified body acceleration in $\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. Set $D=(\nabla u+\nabla u^{\mathsf T})/2$ in $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, $(\operatorname{div}\sigma)_i=\sum_j\partial_j\sigma_{ij}$, and $(\Delta u)_i=\sum_j\partial_{jj}u_i$; $\nabla$ has units $\mathrm{m^{-1}}$ and $\Delta$ has units $\mathrm{m^{-2}}$. The stress $\sigma=-pI+2\mu D$ has units Pa. The selected steady creeping Stokes branch is
items/lem-discrete-young-and-sobolev-product.md:27:    - title: "E. Wiedemann (ed. J. Skipper), Lecture Notes: Navier-Stokes Equations (Universität Ulm, WS 2018/19), Section 2.1 and Section 4.2 (Fourier characterisation of H^s on T^3 and the product/transport estimates used in the H^m energy inequalities)"
items/lem-emg-maxwell-action-variation.md:137:[F12] Under $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, a smooth chart cover admits a smooth subordinate partition of unity ([[thm-smooth-partitions-of-unity-exist-on-manifolds]]). The integral of a compactly supported top form is its finite sum of chart integrals ([[def-integral-of-a-compactly-supported-top-form-on-an-oriented-manifold]]), independent of cover and partition ([[thm-global-form-integration-is-independent-of-the-atlas-partition-and-refinement]]). In a positive chart the metric volume coefficient is $w$.
items/lem-emg-maxwell-action-variation.md:139:[A1] The current batch-2 carriers [[def-emg-local-potentials-and-circle-data]] and [[lem-emg-circle-connection-gluing]] exist, and their inspected interface matches the use here: $F=\kappa_0 f$ is the physical curvature two-form of a normalized connection on a fixed principal $U(1)$ bundle, given by a supplied countable open cover, smooth transition cocycle and compatible local forms; the gluing lemma states that two connections with the same transitions differ by a global smooth base one-form, so a compactly supported global physical one-form $\alpha$ is an allowed variation and $\delta F=d\alpha$. The normalization $\kappa_0>0$ rescales the circle connection and is not identified with $\hbar$ or an electric charge. Both carriers are currently draft; the gluing construction now uses the actual set-size definition authored on emg-mg02, together with the other six typed foundational conventions on that page. The fixed-sector variation interface is unchanged. These authored drafts do not assert independent acceptance.
items/lem-emg-maxwell-action-variation.md:150:1.1 Interpret a compactly supported variation as a smooth family equal to its background off one fixed compact set $K$ for all sufficiently small parameters. Connection variations $A+s\alpha$ have $K=\operatorname{supp}\alpha$ and $F(s)=F+s\,d\alpha$ in the fixed sector. For a prescribed metric direction $q$, put $h_{ab}=-g_{ac}g_{bd}q^{cd}$ and $g(s)=g+sh$. If $K=\varnothing$, then $h=0$ and the metric is unchanged. Otherwise, to choose one parameter interval valid on all of $K$, fix $p\in K$ and make a constant linear coordinate change so that $g(p)$ has matrix $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. By continuity choose a relatively compact chart neighborhood on which $\|g(x)-\eta\|_{\mathrm{op}}<1/2$ and $\|h(x)\|_{\mathrm{op}}$ is bounded. Choose $\delta_p>0$ so $|s|\|h(x)\|_{\mathrm{op}}<1/2$ there for $|s|<\delta_p$. Then $A(x,s):=g(x)+sh(x)$ is positive on the fixed three-dimensional spatial coordinate subspace and negative on the time coordinate line. It is nondegenerate: if $A(x,s)v=0$, then $v=-\eta^{-1}(A(x,s)-\eta)v$, contradicting $\|\eta^{-1}\|_{\mathrm{op}}=1$ and $\|A(x,s)-\eta\|_{\mathrm{op}}<1$. Thus its inertia has three positive and one negative directions, exactly $(-,+,+,+)$. Such neighborhoods cover $K$; compactness gives a finite subcover and the minimum of their parameter bounds works on all of $K$. Outside $K$, $h=0$ and the metric is unchanged. Thus the action-density difference $\omega(s)-\omega(0)$ is a smooth top form supported in a common $K$, even when neither background action is finite. Its integral in [F12] is the relative action; additivity of integrals makes successive relative differences consistent. No subtraction of infinite action values is involved. [given, A1, F3, F12, construct]
items/lem-emg-maxwell-action-variation.md:172:5.1 Adding [A2] to step 4.1 gives $\delta_A(S_{\mathrm{EM}}+S_{\mathrm m})[\alpha] =(\mu_0c)^{-1}\int_M(\nabla_aF^{ab}+\mu_0j^b)\alpha_b\,\mathrm{vol}_g$. If $\nabla_aF^{ab}+\mu_0j^b=0$, this integral vanishes for every allowed $\alpha$. Conversely, if it vanishes for every smooth compactly supported one-form supported in one chart rectangle, fix a point and suppose a component $K^{b_0}:=\nabla_aF^{ab_0}+\mu_0j^{b_0}$ is nonzero there. By continuity, shrink to a coordinate ball with compact closure where $K^{b_0}$ has one strict sign and $w>0$. In coordinates centered at that point define $\phi(x)=\exp(-1/(r^2-|x|^2))$ for $|x|<r$ and $\phi(x)=0$ for $|x|\ge r$. Every derivative approaching $|x|=r$ is a finite sum of a polynomial in $1/(r^2-|x|^2)$ times the same exponential, and $s^{-m}e^{-1/s}\to0$ as $s\downarrow0$ for every $m\ge0$; hence $\phi$ is a smooth nonnegative compactly supported function, positive inside the ball. Set $\alpha=\phi\,dx^{b_0}$ in the chart and extend it by zero outside the compact support; this gives an admissible global one-form in [A1]. The bump and rectangle are explicitly constructed, so the ambient convention [F5] introduces no arbitrary choice. The product $K^{b_0}\phi w$ is continuous and supported in a compact rectangle containing the bump support, so [F8] makes its full integral well-defined. At the center, $|K^{b_0}\phi w|>0$; continuity gives a smaller closed nondegenerate rectangle $R$ on which this coefficient has one strict sign and absolute value at least some $\eta>0$. By [F8] it is Riemann integrable there. Applying [F11] to its sign-adjusted coefficient shows that the absolute value of the integral over $R$ is at least $\eta\operatorname{vol}(R)>0$. On the entire bump support the product has the same sign, since $K^{b_0}$ has one sign on the ball and $\phi,w$ are nonnegative; therefore its full integral over the support cannot vanish. This contradicts the assumed stationarity integral. Thus every component $K^b$ vanishes at every point. Conversely its pointwise vanishing makes the variation zero for every compactly supported global one-form by step 4.1. This proves the stated global stationarity equivalence under [A2]–[A3]. [given, A1, A2, A3, F1, F5, F8, F11, step 4.1, construct, algebra]
