items/pthm-fd-periodic-weak-continuation.md:99:1.1 For the classical model, $\operatorname{div}(u\otimes u)=(u\cdot\nabla)u$ follows componentwise from $\operatorname{div}u=0$, and $\operatorname{div}(2\mu D(u))=\mu\Delta u+\mu\nabla\operatorname{div}u=\mu\Delta u$. When tested against a periodic divergence-free $\phi$, the pressure term pairs to zero. For weak $u,\phi\in V$, the diffusion form has the same meaning: $2D(u):\nabla\phi=\nabla u:\nabla\phi+\partial_j u_i\partial_i\phi_j$. Finite Fourier truncations of $u$ and $\phi$ remain divergence free, are smooth, and converge to them in $H^1$ by [F1]; for each truncation, periodic integration by parts makes the integral of the cross term $-\int u_i\partial_i(\operatorname{div}\phi)=0$. Continuity of the bilinear forms in $H^1$ passes this identity to the limit. Thus division by $\rho_0$ gives exactly the weak identity in [F2] with $\nu=\mu/\rho_0>0$. [given, A1, F1, F2, algebra]
items/thm-fd-x10.md:24:scope: "Existing smooth incompressible Navier-Stokes perturbation periodic in x,z and zero trace at y=0,h; energy decays if |S|h^2/nu<2 on its assumed smooth existence interval. This is a sufficient, nonsharp bound, not global three-dimensional regularity or a transition threshold; the planned dependency closure carries AC_omega."
items/thm-fd-x10.md:35:    - title: "David Tong, Fluid Mechanics lecture notes, Chapter 2: The Navier-Stokes Equation"
items/thm-fd-x10.md:48:Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$. Let $L_x,L_z,h,\nu,\rho_0>0$, $S\in\mathbb R$, and $T>0$. For $L>0$, define $\mathbb T_L=\mathbb R/(L\mathbb Z)$ and set $Q=\mathbb T_{L_x}\times(0,h)\times\mathbb T_{L_z}$, with Lebesgue integration over one fundamental cell. Define $\mu:=\rho_0\nu>0$, so $\nu=\mu/\rho_0$ as in the formal convention of definition def-fd-x00. Let $U_b(x,y,z)=(Sy,0,0)$. Let $u\in C^1([0,T];C^2(\overline Q;\mathbb R^3))$ and $p\in C([0,T];C^1(\overline Q))$, periodic in $x,z$. Regard these fields as periodic lifts $\widetilde u:[0,T]\times\mathbb R\times[0,h]\times\mathbb R\to\mathbb R^3$ and $\widetilde p:[0,T]\times\mathbb R\times[0,h]\times\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$, with periods $L_x,L_z$ in $x,z$; all spatial derivatives and equations are interpreted on these lifts. For $a=(a_1,a_2,a_3)\in\mathbb R^3$, use the Euclidean norm $|a|=(a_1^2+a_2^2+a_3^2)^{1/2}$ as in the norm convention in theorem thm-fd-x07. With ordinary Cartesian derivatives, $\nabla p=(\partial_xp,\partial_yp,\partial_zp)$, $\nabla\cdot u=\partial_xu_1+\partial_yu_2+\partial_zu_3$, $(u\cdot\nabla)u=\sum_{j=1}^3u_j\partial_{x_j}u$, and $(\Delta u)_i=\partial_{xx}u_i+\partial_{yy}u_i+\partial_{zz}u_i$ for $i=1,2,3$. Suppose $u$ is an already-existing solution of the constant-density incompressible Navier-Stokes equation $u_t+(u\cdot\nabla)u+\rho_0^{-1}\nabla p-\nu\Delta u=0$, $\nabla\cdot u=0$, with wall values $u|_{y=0}=0$ and $u|_{y=h}=Sh(1,0,0)$. Define $w=u-U_b$ and $E(t)=\int_Q|w(t,x,y,z)|^2\,dx\,dy\,dz$. If $|S|h^2/\nu<2$, then for every $t\in[0,T]$,
items/thm-fd-x10.md:82:2.1 Put $E(t)=\int_Q|w|^2$. Multiply the perturbation equation in step 1.1 by $w$ and integrate over one cell. The $U_b$ transport integral is $\frac12\int_Q\nabla\cdot(U_b|w|^2)=0$: $\nabla\cdot U_b=0$, its normal component vanishes at the $y$ walls, and paired periodic-face terms cancel. The nonlinear transport integral is $\frac12\int_Q\nabla\cdot(w|w|^2)=0$ because $\nabla\cdot w=0$, $w=0$ at the walls, and periodic-face terms cancel. The pressure integral is $-\int_Q\nabla\cdot(qw)+\int_Qq\nabla\cdot w=0$ for the same wall and periodic reasons. Componentwise integration by parts gives $\int_Qw\cdot\Delta w=-\|\nabla w\|_{L^2(Q)}^2$, with zero wall term and canceling periodic faces. Since $(w\cdot\nabla)U_b=S w_2(1,0,0)$, the remaining identity is $\frac12E'(t)=-\nu\|\nabla w\|_{L^2(Q)}^2-S\int_Qw_1w_2$. These integrations use the one-dimensional FTC on one periodic cell, not the bounded $C^1$ Euclidean divergence theorem. [given, A1, step 1.1, algebra]
items/thm-emg-local-classical-charged-scalar-development.md:158:7.1 (Local and whole-surface comparison.) On a smaller compact cylinder the two smooth states lie in a common convex admissible neighborhood. Subtract their equations; the coefficient mean-value formula puts their difference $v$ in $A^\mu(u_1)\partial_\mu v+C v=0$, with bounded $C$. If $A^0\ge aI$ and $b\ge\sup_{|\xi|=1}\|\xi_iA^i\|$, choose a shrinking ball with speed $\sigma>b/a$. Symmetry and spatial integration by parts give $E'\le CE$ for $E=\int v^{\mathsf T}A^0v$, since the side-flux matrix $\sigma A^0+\nu_iA^i$ is nonnegative. The zero initial difference gives $v=0$ in both time directions by Gronwall. This is the local coupled energy comparison in [L10], now applied after the internal gauge has actually been fixed. Undoing the gauges gives a local metric isometry and the stated matter-gauge equivalence. Future data-fixing metric isometries have unique germs: their differentials fix tangents and future normals, hence they agree along the normal geodesics from the initial patch. The injective variable-width normal collars and germ-patching construction of [[thm-gr-local-geometric-uniqueness]] therefore yield one metric isometry near the entire initial surface; no uniform width is imposed. For the existence/gluing argument of [L10], choose the common normal product collar and put each local potential into its temporal gauge there, with unchanged initial $A_\Sigma$. On overlaps the local isometry comparisons just proved identify the metrics and $F$; two temporal-gauge potentials with the same initial spatial restriction and same $F$ agree, since their difference has zero time component, zero exterior derivative and zero initial pullback. On the common smooth normal-coordinate metric, their temporal-gauge scalar/potential blocks have the same coefficients and complete data, so the scalar-block version of the local energy comparison gives equality of the scalar representatives; no harmonic-coordinate equation for that common metric is needed for this matter-only comparison. Thus the local fields patch on the literal collar, as required for the smooth-development application in step 5.2. [given, F1, L4, L5, L6, L10, L11, L12, step 4.1, step 5.2, step 6.1, algebra]
items/cex-fd-weak-products.md:33:    - title: "L. C. Berselli and S. Spirito, On the Existence of Leray–Hopf Weak Solutions to the Navier–Stokes Equations, Fluids 6(1), 42 (2021)"
items/cex-fd-weak-products.md:68:The oscillatory cosine has zero pairing eventually with every finite trigonometric polynomial and its $L^2$ norm is uniformly bounded; density and Cauchy–Schwarz therefore show it converges weakly to zero in $L^2$. Thus $u_n\otimes u_n\rightharpoonup\tfrac12 e_2\otimes e_2$ in $L^2$. Since the torus measure is one, every essentially bounded matrix test satisfies $\|\Phi\|_2\le\|\Phi\|_\infty$, so this convergence also holds against every weak $L^1$ test. In particular the constant test $\Phi=e_2\otimes e_2$ gives $\int(u_n\otimes u_n):\Phi\,d\mu=1/2$ for every $n$, whereas $\int(u\otimes u):\Phi\,d\mu=0$. Hence the tensors do not converge weakly in $L^1$ to $u\otimes u=0$; the same smooth constant test also rules out convergence to zero as periodic distributions. This refutes the false statement. These fields are only a functional-analytic witness and are not claimed to solve Navier–Stokes. [given, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
items/thm-fd-reduced-ode-global-energy.md:32:    - title: "Emil Wiedemann, Lecture Notes: Navier-Stokes Equations, edited by Jack Skipper, Universität Ulm, Winter 2018/19"
items/thm-fd-reduced-ode-global-energy.md:54:This is a global result for the specified finite coefficient ODE only; it asserts no convergence to, existence of, or uniqueness for a solution of the infinite-dimensional Navier–Stokes equations. The Countable Choice assumption is inherited from the periodic Fourier-Sobolev framework; the finite-dimensional proof makes no further choice.
items/thm-fd-reduced-ode-global-energy.md:84:2.2 For each $i,j,k$, periodic integration by parts and $\operatorname{div}v_j=0$ give $c_{ijk}+c_{kji}=\int_{Q_d}v_j\cdot\nabla(v_k\cdot v_i)\,d\mu=\int_{Q_d}\operatorname{div}(v_j(v_k\cdot v_i))\,d\mu=0$, since opposite faces of a periodic cell cancel. Thus $c_{kji}=-c_{ijk}$; the finite contraction $S=\sum_{i,j,k}c_{ijk}a_i a_j a_k$ satisfies $S=-S$ after swapping $i$ and $k$, so $S=0$. Also $\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}a_i a_j=\int_{Q_d}|\nabla u_a|^2\,d\mu$. Multiplying the coefficient equations by $a_i$, summing over $i$, and using $\frac{d}{dt}\frac12|a|^2=\sum_i a_i a_i'$ proves the energy identity on $I$. Suppose for contradiction that the positive endpoint $\beta:=\sup(I\cap(0,\infty))$ is finite. [given, F1, step 1.1, assume-contra, algebra]
