items/lem-three-dimensional-radial-carleman-integer-weight.md:90:1.1 **Change of variables.** Assume first $u\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\})$. The map $(t,n)\mapsto e^{-t}n$ is a diffeomorphism of $\mathbb R\times S^2$ onto $\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\}$; since $u$ is smooth and compactly supported away from the origin, the functions $v(t,n):=e^{-t/2}u(e^{-t}n)$ and $w(t,n):=e^{st}v(t,n)$ are smooth and $w$ is compactly supported in $t$ uniformly in $n$. By [L1] with the substitution $r=e^{-t}$, whose derivative $|\mathrm dr/\mathrm dt|=e^{-t}=r$ is supplied by [L2], the measure becomes $dx=e^{-3t}\,dt\,d\sigma(n)$; writing $u(x)=e^{(1/2-s)t}w(t,n)$ gives $$r^{-2\tau}|u|^2\,dx=e^{2\tau t}e^{(1-2s)t}|w|^2e^{-3t}\,dt\,d\sigma=|w|^2\,dt\,d\sigma,$$ because $2\tau-2s-2=0$. Hence $\int_{\mathbb R^3}r^{-2\tau}|u|^2\,dx=\int_{\mathbb R}\int_{S^2}|w|^2\,d\sigma\,dt$. [L2, L1, L4]
