items/ex-fd-x06-application.md:63:2.1 The dimensionless ratio is $(1)(10^{-3})(1)/(1)=10^{-3}$. It does not change the Stokes-model hypotheses or create an error bound for Navier–Stokes. [given, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
items/thm-fdmath-mean-fluctuation-energy-transfer.md:78:1.1 By [F1], the mean field satisfies $\partial_t\bar u+\operatorname{div}(\bar u\otimes\bar u)+\nabla\bar p=\nu\Delta\bar u-\operatorname{div}R$ with $\operatorname{div}\bar u=0$. Differentiate $E_m$ under the integral, which is valid by the stated regularity and compactness of the periodic cell. For every continuously differentiable periodic scalar $g$, $\int_{\mathbb T^d}\partial_{x_s}g\,dx=0$, since integration over $x_s\in[0,2\pi]$ gives equal endpoint values. Thus the convection and pressure terms integrate to zero because they are respectively $\operatorname{div}(|\bar u|^2\bar u/2)$ and $\operatorname{div}(\bar p\bar u)$; periodic integration by parts gives $\int\bar u\cdot\Delta\bar u=-\int|\nabla\bar u|^2$ and $-\int\bar u\cdot\operatorname{div}R=\int R:\nabla\bar u$. This proves the mean-energy identity. [given, A1, F1, algebra]
items/thm-fdmath-mean-fluctuation-energy-transfer.md:80:2.1 Dot the $j$th equation in [A1] with $u_j$, integrate, and multiply by $a_j$. The time term is $\frac12a_j\frac{d}{dt}\int|u_j|^2$. The convection and pressure terms are periodic divergences, respectively $\operatorname{div}(|u_j|^2u_j/2)$ and $\operatorname{div}(p_ju_j)$, because $\operatorname{div}u_j=0$; their integrals vanish by the periodic-cell argument in step 1.1. Periodic integration by parts gives the viscous contribution $-\nu a_j\int|\nabla u_j|^2$. Summing over $j$ yields $\frac{d}{dt}(\frac12\int\sum_j a_j|u_j|^2)=-\nu\int\sum_j a_j|\nabla u_j|^2$. [given, A1, step 1.1, algebra]
