items/lem-csm-dk-smooth-tests-determine-phase-probability.md:66:1.1 Fix a bounded Lipschitz function $f:Z\to\mathbb R$. Use $\lambda_d$ and $m_{\mathbb T^d}$ from [F5]. Choose the smooth cutoff $\chi$ of [F2] and set $\eta(u)=c\chi(2u)$ on $\mathbb R^d$, where $c=(\int_{\mathbb R^d}\chi(2u)\,d\lambda_d(u))^{-1}$; its integral is positive and finite because $\chi=1$ on the unit ball and is supported in the radius-two ball. Thus $\eta$ is a nonnegative smooth probability density supported in the unit ball. For $0<\epsilon<1/4$, define $\eta_\epsilon^{\mathbb R}(v)=\epsilon^{-d}\eta(v/\epsilon)$ and periodize it to $\mathbb T^d$ by $\eta_\epsilon^{\mathbb T}(x)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z^d}\epsilon^{-d}\eta((\widetilde x+n)/\epsilon)$, where $\widetilde x$ is any representative. The sum is locally finite and independent of the representative; the fundamental-domain formula for $m_{\mathbb T^d}$ in [F5], followed by the change of variables $u=(\widetilde x+n)/\epsilon$, gives integral one, and its support satisfies $d_{\mathbb T^d}(x,0)\le\epsilon$. Set $f_\epsilon(x,v)=\int_{\mathbb T^d}\int_{\mathbb R^d}\eta_\epsilon^{\mathbb T}(x-y)\eta_\epsilon^{\mathbb R}(v-w)f(y,w)\,d\lambda_d(w)\,dm_{\mathbb T^d}(y)$. Differentiating the compactly supported smooth kernels under the integral shows that $f_\epsilon$ is smooth, and their nonnegativity and unit integrals give $\|f_\epsilon\|_\infty\le\|f\|_\infty$. On their support, $d_{\mathbb T^d}(x,y)\le\epsilon$ and $|v-w|\le\epsilon$, so $\|f_\epsilon-f\|_\infty\le2\epsilon\operatorname{Lip}(f)$. For each $R>0$, $\chi_R(v)f_\epsilon(x,v)$ is in $C_c^\infty(Z)$, so its integrals agree by the hypothesis. Let $R\to\infty$ and apply [F3] to each probability measure; then let $\epsilon\downarrow0$ and use uniform convergence. Thus $\mu$ and $\nu$ have equal integrals for every bounded Lipschitz $f$. [F1, F2, F3, F5, given, construct]
items/thm-csm-dk-finite-n-complete-flow.md:97:4.1 On each finite time slab the rapid bounds on all phase derivatives make $\partial_tF_N$ integrable and supply one integrable majorant for its time difference quotients; [F6] therefore permits differentiation under the $d\Omega_N$ integral. Integrate the equation in step 3.1 over all phase variables. The $q_i$-derivative terms have zero integral by periodicity. For each force term, $K(q_i-q_j)$ is independent of momenta, so integration by parts in $p_i$ and $p_j$ has zero boundary terms by the rapid tails. Fubini [F5] justifies these coordinate integrations and their order. Hence $\frac d{dt}\int F_N(t)\,d\Omega_N=0$; its value is $1$ at $t=0$ by [A2], so $F_N(t)$ remains normalized. The same permutation identity from step 2.1 and [A2] give $F_N(t,\sigma z)=F_N(t,z)$. Finally, the vector field derivatives of every order are bounded globally: its first derivative has entries $1/m$ and $DK$, and higher derivatives are bounded derivatives of the periodic force. The variational equations and [F3] bound every finite state derivative of $\Phi_{-t}^N$ on the time slab. Since $|p_i(t)-p_i(0)|\le K_*|t|$, the momentum norms differ by at most $\sqrt N K_*|t|$; the chain rule transfers every rapid-decay bound of $F_N^0$ to $F_N(t)$ uniformly on the slab. [A1, A2, F1, F3, F5, F6, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
items/pthm-qft-example-dirac-magnetic-benchmark.md:140:1.1 Let $\xi$ be a compactly supported smooth complex spinor variation and write $\delta\bar\psi=\bar\xi$. Vary the symmetrized spinor action in [F1] while holding $a_a$ fixed. Since $\gamma^a$ is constant and $D_a\bar\xi=\partial_a\bar\xi+iq_na_a\bar\xi$, compact integration by parts gives $\int(D_a\bar\xi)\gamma^a\psi\,d^4x=-\int\bar\xi\gamma^aD_a\psi\,d^4x$: the derivative term changes sign, and the connection term becomes $+iq_na_a$ on the right, matching the conjugate-charge term on the left. Thus $\delta(S_{\rm cl}/\hbar)=2\operatorname{Re}\int\xi^\dagger\beta(i\gamma^aD_a-\mu)\psi\,d^4x$. No field product outside the smooth classical action is used. [given, F1, F2, A1, algebra]
