items/pthm-emx-lorentz-susceptibility.md:97:[F2] The real exponential has derivative $\exp'=\exp$, the complex exponential is defined by its series and is entire with the same derivative, sine and cosine have their usual derivatives, products satisfy the product rule, and integration by parts applies componentwise on finite intervals to the oscillator kernels and $C^2$ histories used below ([[def-complex-exponential]], [[thm-derivative-of-exponential]], [[thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself]], [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[thm-integration-by-parts]]).
items/pthm-emx-lorentz-susceptibility.md:110:2.1 Fix one oscillator, denote its parameters by $a,\omega_0,\gamma$, assume $\gamma>0$, and set $g(t)=0$ for $t<0$. For $t\ge0$, define the retarded kernel by $g(t)=e^{-\gamma t/2}\sin(\alpha t)/\alpha$ if $0<\gamma<2\omega_0$, $g(t)=te^{-\omega_0t}$ if $\gamma=2\omega_0$, and $g(t)=\bigl(e^{-(\gamma/2-\beta)t}-e^{-(\gamma/2+\beta)t}\bigr)/(2\beta)$ if $\gamma>2\omega_0$, where $\alpha=\sqrt{\omega_0^2-\gamma^2/4}$ and $\beta=\sqrt{\gamma^2/4-\omega_0^2}$ in their respective cases. Differentiating with [F2] and the affine rule proved in step 1.1 gives $g(0)=0$, $g'(0+)=1$ and $g''+\gamma g'+\omega_0^2g=0$ for $t>0$. The underdamped $g,g'$ decay at rate $\gamma/2$; the critical formula and derivative are bounded by $Ce^{-\omega_0t/2}$; the overdamped formula and derivative are bounded by $Ce^{-(\gamma/2-\beta)t}$, with $\gamma/2-\beta>0$. Hence $g,g'\in L^1(0,\infty)$. For each positive decay rate $\lambda$, $te^{-\lambda t}$ is integrable by integration by parts on $[0,T]$ and [F3] as $T\to\infty$, so also $tg\in L^1(0,\infty)$. [step 1.1, given, A1, F2, F3, algebra]
items/pthm-emx-lorentz-susceptibility.md:118:5.1 The normalized kernel $K=(a/\varepsilon_0)g$ is real, causal and locally absolutely continuous; step 2.1 gives $K,K'\in L^1$ and $sK\in L^1$. For $\operatorname{Im}z>0$, step 1.1 differentiates $e^{izs}$, and exponential decay makes the boundary terms vanish in integration by parts, so $\int_0^\infty g'(s)e^{izs}ds=-izG(z)$ and $\int_0^\infty g''(s)e^{izs}ds=-1-z^2G(z)$, where $G(z)=\int_0^\infty g(s)e^{izs}ds$. The Green equation gives $(\omega_0^2-z^2-i\gamma z)G(z)=1$, hence the normalized transfer is $(a/\varepsilon_0)/(\omega_0^2-z^2-i\gamma z)$. Summing the finite kernels gives $\chi_{\mathrm{norm}}=\chi_{\mathrm{abs}}/\varepsilon_0$; with [F1], this kernel satisfies every hypothesis of [L1], which yields causality, upper-half-plane analyticity and the unsubtracted Hilbert relations for the strictly damped branch. Multiplication by $\varepsilon_0$ gives the same relations for $\varepsilon-\varepsilon_\infty=\chi_{\mathrm{abs}}$. [step 1.1, A1, step 2.1, step 4.1, F1, L1, algebra]
items/lem-fd-weak-jump-consistency.md:78:1.1 By [A2] and [F2], $P$ and $P^{-1}$ preserve the Lebesgue sigma-algebra and measure. Pullback by $P$ bijects nonnegative simple minorants and preserves their level-set measures, so the nonnegative integral is preserved by its defining supremum; applying positive and negative parts gives the same conclusion for the integrable tested scalar products in [F1]. Thus the time-first coordinates and the spatial-first weak definition give the same test integrals. For a test $\phi$ supported in $\mathcal O$, apply the product rule to the space-time current $(U^\pm,F_1^\pm,F_2^\pm,F_3^\pm)$ separately on $\mathcal O^-$ and $\mathcal O^+$. Coordinatewise integration by parts in $t,x_1,x_2,x_3$ gives $\int_{\mathcal O}(U\cdot\partial_t\phi+F:\nabla_x\phi+S\cdot\phi)=-\int_{\mathcal O^-}W^-\cdot\phi-\int_{\mathcal O^+}W^+\cdot\phi-\int_{J\times D}K_{\rm graph}\cdot\phi(t,x',h(t,x'))\,dx'\,dt$, where $W^\pm=\partial_tU^\pm+\operatorname{div}_xF^\pm-S^\pm$ and $K_{\rm graph}=[F_3]-\partial_th[U]-\nabla_{x'}h\cdot[F_{1,2}]$. Compact support removes all outer-boundary terms; the remaining graph term is the difference of the two side conormal fluxes. [given, F1, F2, A1, A2, construct, algebra]
items/lem-fd-weak-jump-consistency.md:82:2.1 If the weak identity holds, choose tests supported wholly inside either side in step 1.1. Each continuous component of $W^\pm$ must vanish: a nonzero value would have a fixed strict sign on a small ball and contradict the identity for a nonnegative $C^1$ bump there. With these bulk terms zero, step 1.1 gives zero integral of $K_{\rm graph}$ against every trace of a test. Any compactly supported $C^1$ graph test $\zeta(t,x')$ extends to the patch as $\phi(t,x',x_3)=\zeta(t,x')\eta(x_3-h(t,x'))$, where $\eta$ is a $C^1$ cutoff with $\eta(0)=1$ and sufficiently small support. Such a uniform tube lies in $\mathcal O$ because the graph over $\operatorname{supp}\zeta$ is compact in this open patch. A nonzero component of continuous $K_{\rm graph}$ would have a fixed sign on a small graph patch and contradict the identity. Hence $W^\pm=0$ and $K_{\rm graph}=0$. Conversely, these equalities make the weak integral zero by step 1.1, and step 1.2 gives the stated jump condition. [step 1.1, step 1.2, given, F1, A1]
items/lem-qftx-finite-lorentz-cocycle-splitting.md:92:extending each integrand by zero outside its chart. Each real and imaginary part is continuous with compact support inside the chart, so [F1]–[F2] make each coordinate integral finite. The chart weights are nonnegative, and their partition-of-unity sum gives $I(1)$; applying the triangle inequality to the complex Riemann sums and passing to their limits gives $|I(h)|\le\sup_{S^3}|h|\,I(1)$. The number $I(1)$ is finite and positive: at $x=(1,0,0,0)$ one partition function is positive on a neighborhood, and on a small coordinate cube there the integrand has a positive lower bound. Thus $\nu(h):=I(h)/I(1)$ is complex-linear, normalized, positive on nonnegative real functions, and satisfies $|\nu(h)|\le\sup_{S^3}|h|$. Its chart definition is independent of a finite subordinate chart partition: insert the second partition into each summand; each product has compact support in the chart overlap, and the metric identity $G(x)=DT(x)^{\mathsf T}G(Tx)DT(x)$ gives $\sqrt{\det G(x)}=|\det DT(x)|\sqrt{\det G(Tx)}$. The real and imaginary chart integrands, extended by zero, are continuous and compactly supported in the overlap image, and the transition is a smooth diffeomorphism of open subsets of $\mathbb R^3$. Apply the Riemann formula [F3] to these parts separately to transfer each compactly supported integral; summing the finite terms gives equality. For $q=(a,b)\in S^3$ and $x=(c,d)\in\mathbb C^2\cong\mathbb R^4$, matrix left multiplication induces $L_q(c,d)=(ac-b\overline d,ad+b\overline c)$. This map is real-linear and $|ac-b\overline d|^2+|ad+b\overline c|^2=(|a|^2+|b|^2)(|c|^2+|d|^2)=|c|^2+|d|^2$, since the two cross terms cancel. Its inverse is left multiplication by the inverse matrix, so $L_q$ is orthogonal and an isometry of the induced metric; using the transported charts and partition in the same identity gives $\nu(h\circ L_q)=\nu(h)$. Extend $\nu$ to continuous $V$-valued maps by the same coordinatewise rule and call this extension $\nu_V$. Define the product functional on continuous $F:G\to V$ by $\mu(F)=\nu_V(q\mapsto\nu_V(r\mapsto F(q,r)))$. For continuity, fix $q_0\in S^3$ and $\varepsilon>0$. For each $r\in S^3$, continuity of the finitely many coordinate functions of $F$ at $(q_0,r)$ gives neighborhoods in both factors on which their values differ from $F(q_0,r)$ by less than $\varepsilon/3$. Compactness of the second $S^3$ selects finitely many such neighborhoods; intersecting their first-factor neighborhoods gives $U\ni q_0$ with $\sup_r|F_j(q,r)-F_j(q_0,r)|<2\varepsilon/3$ for each coordinate $j$ and $q\in U$. The scalar bound for $\nu$ therefore makes each coordinate of $q\mapsto\nu_V(r\mapsto F(q,r))$ continuous. The product functional is linear, normalized, and left invariant by applying the factor identity twice. Since $b$ is continuous on compact $G$ and $V$ is finite-dimensional, $\bar v:=\mu(b)$ exists coordinatewise. Left invariance and the cocycle formula give $R(h)\bar v+b(h)=\mu(g\mapsto b(hg))=\bar v$ for every $h\in G$. [F1, F2, F3, L1, step 1.2, step 3.1, construct]
items/thm-fd-x04.md:25:    - title: "David Tong, Fluid Mechanics lecture notes, Chapter 2: The Navier–Stokes Equation"
items/thm-fd-x04.md:59:Both fields satisfy the exact stationary Navier–Stokes equations for every choice of the positive constants and also satisfy the corresponding steady Stokes equations, because their time derivative and convective acceleration vanish. The pipe uniqueness claim uses regularity on the full disk, including the axis. Both uniqueness claims are restricted to the stated one-dimensional ansatz; neither asserts uniqueness among all flows. The domains are infinite or fully developed and give no finite entrance or exit error estimate. Under SI interpretation, $h,a,x,y,z,r$ are in metres, $u,V_0,V_1,v,w$ in metres per second, $\rho_0$ in kilograms per cubic metre, $\mu$ in pascal-seconds, $\nu$ in square metres per second, $p,p_0$ in pascals, and $G$ in pascals per metre. The channel flux per unit width is in square metres per second and the pipe volume flux is in cubic metres per second.
items/thm-fd-x04.md:73:[F1] [[def-fd-x00]]: the current formal definition sets $\nu=\mu/\rho_0$, defines the pointwise constant-density incompressible Navier–Stokes equation and its steady Stokes specialization, defines divergence and curl from Cartesian partial derivatives, fixes the componentwise Laplacian and row-component Jacobian conventions, and defines stress by $\sigma=-pI_3+\mu(D_xu+(D_xu)^{\mathsf T})$.
items/thm-fd-x04.md:79:1.1 For the plane channel, $\operatorname{div}u=0$, $\partial_tu=0$, $(u\cdot\nabla)u=v(y)\partial_xu=0$, $\Delta u=(v''(y),0,0)$, and $\nabla p=(-G,0,0)$. The $x$ component of the stated Navier–Stokes equation is therefore $-G/\rho_0-\nu v''=0$, equivalently $\mu v''=-G$. [given, F1, A1, algebra]
items/thm-fd-x04.md:87:4.1 Polar integration yields $Q_{\rm pipe}=2\pi\int_0^a r\,G(a^2-r^2)/(4\mu)\,dr=\pi Ga^4/(8\mu)$. In both geometries the computed time derivative and convective term vanish, so removing them gives exactly the steady Stokes equations as well. [step 1.2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
items/ex-csm-ideal-isobaric.md:69:1.1 Integrating the momentum factors gives [F1]; integrating the unit shape coordinates gives one, while the configuration scaling contributes $V^N$. Hence $$\Delta_N=\frac{1}{N!V_0a_0^n}\left(\frac{2\pi m}{\beta}\right)^{n/2} \int_0^\infty V^Ne^{-sV}dV,\qquad s=\beta P_{\rm ext}>0.$$ Let $I_j(s)=\int_0^\infty V^je^{-sV}dV$. Direct integration gives $I_0=1/s$; integration by parts, with $V^je^{-sV}\to0$ at infinity and vanishing lower boundary for $j\ge1$, gives $I_j=jI_{j-1}/s$. Thus $I_j=j!/s^{j+1}$, and $I_N=N!/s^{N+1}$ cancels the $N!$ in the reference; the displayed finite positive normalizer follows. [A1, A2, F1, algebra]
