items/lem-qft-qed-coulomb-constraint.md:48:The canonical momenta are $\boldsymbol\pi_A=\epsilon_0(\dot{\mathbf A}+\nabla\phi)$, with unit $\mathrm{C\,m^{-2}}$, and $\pi_\phi=0$. Periodic integration by parts gives the fixed-time Hamiltonian
items/lem-qft-qed-coulomb-constraint.md:74:1.2 Integrating this equation over the periodic box at any fixed $t$ gives $0=L^{-3}\int(-\Delta\phi)\,d^3\mathbf x=L^{-3}\epsilon_0^{-1}\int\rho\,d^3\mathbf x$, since each periodic first derivative has equal boundary values on opposite faces. Thus neutrality is necessary at each time. For neutral data, fix a compact interval $K\subset I$ and integers $r,M\ge0$. Differentiating the Fourier coefficient under the integral gives $\partial_t^r\widehat\rho_k(t)=L^{-3}\int_{[0,L]^3}\partial_t^r\rho(\mathbf x,t)e^{-2\pi i k\cdot\mathbf x/L}\,d^3\mathbf x$. For $k\ne0$, repeated periodic integration by parts $M$ times in a coordinate $j$ with $|k_j|\ge|k|/\sqrt3$ gives $|\partial_t^r\widehat\rho_k(t)|\le C_{r,M,K}(1+|k|)^{-M}$ uniformly for $t\in K$; boundedness of the finitely many low modes is absorbed in the constant. Since $\widehat\phi_k=\widehat\rho_k/(\epsilon_0|2\pi k/L|^2)$ for $k\ne0$, a mixed derivative $\partial_t^r\partial_{\mathbf x}^{\alpha}$ of its Fourier series is bounded termwise on $K\times T_L^3$ by a constant times $|k|^{|\alpha|-M-2}$. Taking $M>|\alpha|+1$ makes this series absolutely and uniformly summable over $\mathbb Z^3\setminus\{0\}$. Thus every mixed derivative series converges uniformly on compact time intervals, and the Fourier construction is jointly smooth. [given, algebra]
items/lem-qft-qed-coulomb-constraint.md:78:3.1 At any fixed $t$, if two zero-mean solutions existed, their difference $u$ would be smooth, periodic, satisfy $\Delta u=0$, and have $\widehat u_0=0$. Periodic integration by parts in each Fourier coefficient gives $\widehat{\Delta u}_k=-|2\pi k/L|^2\widehat u_k$; since $\Delta u=0$, every nonzero-mode coefficient vanishes, and the zero-mode coefficient vanishes by the zero-mean condition. Completeness of the Fourier basis [F1] therefore gives $u=0$ in $L^2$, hence everywhere by continuity. Parseval applied to $\nabla\phi$ and to $\phi,\rho$ yields
