items/pthm-fd-creeping-lipschitz-pressure.md:65:Assume Countable Choice. Let $D^*\subset\mathbb R^3$ be a nonempty bounded connected Lipschitz open set whose boundary has a finite rigidly moved graph-chart atlas, contained in a slab $(a,b)\times\mathbb R^2$, and set $C_P(D^*):=b-a$. Let $\ell_0>0$ metres and $U_0>0$ metres per second be reference length and velocity; the physical domain is $\Omega_{\mathrm{phys}}=\ell_0D^*$. Fix constant density $\rho_0>0$ kilograms per cubic metre and constant dynamic viscosity $\mu_0>0$ pascal seconds. Write $\nu^*:=\mu_0/(\rho_0U_0\ell_0)>0$ for the dimensionless kinematic viscosity and $\nu_{\mathrm{phys}}:=\mu_0/\rho_0$ for its dimensional value in square metres per second. For each continuous body-acceleration field $b_i\in C^0(\Omega_{\mathrm{phys}};\mathbb R^3)\cap L^2(\Omega_{\mathrm{phys}};\mathbb R^3)$ in metres per second squared, set $g_i^*(y):=\ell_0b_i(\ell_0y)/U_0^2$. Use the forced creeping Stokes branch defined on this page: the velocity $u_i^*\in V:=H^1_0(D^*;\mathbb R^3)$ with gradient norm $\|v\|_V=\|\nabla v\|_{L^2(D^*)}$ has zero trace on the selected stationary no-slip wall, the pressure $q_i^*\in Q:=L^2_0(D^*)=\{q\in L^2(D^*):\int_{D^*}q\,dy=0\}$ is the spatial mean-zero multiplier, and the weak model equation is
items/pthm-fd-creeping-lipschitz-pressure.md:69:where $\varepsilon(w)=(\nabla w+\nabla w^{\mathsf T})/2$ and $F_i(v):=\int_{D^*}g_i^*(y)\cdot v(y)\,dy$. The physical pressure is recovered from $p_{i,\mathrm{phys}}(\ell_0y)=\rho_0U_0^2q_i^*(y)$ using this mean-zero gauge. Let $m\ge1$ be an integer and let $S_1,\ldots,S_m:Q\to\mathbb R$ be bounded real-linear pressure sensors with $\|S_j\|_{Q'}:=\sup_{\|q\|_{L^2(D^*)}\le1}|S_j(q)|$. Define the physical pressure-output vector $\mathcal P_{\mathrm{phys}}(b_i):=\rho_0U_0^2(S_1(q_i^*),\ldots,S_m(q_i^*))\in\mathbb R^m$. The earlier Stokes-load solvability and sensor-stability theorem gives the weak solution pairs and yields
items/pthm-fd-creeping-lipschitz-pressure.md:73:where $C_{\mathrm{div}}(D^*)<\infty$ satisfies: there is a bounded linear right inverse $B_{D^*}:Q\to V$ with $\operatorname{div}B_{D^*}q=q$ and $\|\nabla B_{D^*}q\|_{L^2(D^*)}\le C_{\mathrm{div}}(D^*)\|q\|_Q$. This is a conditional stability result for the adopted forced creeping model. It does not infer that inertia is negligible from $\operatorname{Re}$, estimate the error against the full Navier–Stokes model at finite Reynolds number, fix an absolute pressure gauge, or report an experiment.
