items/pthm-fd-creeping-lipschitz-pressure.md:119:3.1 By the definition of the physical outputs, their difference is the dimensionless sensor vector in step 2.1 multiplied by the positive pressure scale $\rho_0U_0^2$. Substitute the load bound from step 1.1 and the sensor estimate from step 2.1, then multiply by this scale to obtain the claimed inequality. The right side has pressure units, and the result is conditional on the adopted creeping balance, not an approximation estimate for full Navier–Stokes flow. [given, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
items/lem-tgr-planck-integrals.md:64:3.1 For $m\ge1$ and $p=0,1,2,3$, put $J_p(m)=\int_0^\infty x^pe^{-mx}\,dx$. Direct integration gives $J_0(m)=1/m$; integration by parts on $[0,R]$ and then $R\to\infty$ gives $J_p(m)=pJ_{p-1}(m)/m$, since $R^pe^{-mR}\to0$ (for example, $e^{mR}\ge(mR)^{p+1}/(p+1)!$). Hence $J_3(m)=6/m^4$ and $J_2(m)=2/m^3$. The finite geometric identity and its limit give $(e^x-1)^{-1}=\sum_{m\ge1}e^{-mx}$ for $x>0$. The partial sums $x^3\sum_{m\le N}e^{-mx}$ are nonnegative measurable and increase pointwise; the monotone-convergence theorem [F3] for the integral defined in [F4] gives the first integral as $\sum_{m\ge1}J_3(m)=6\sum_{m\ge1}m^{-4}=\pi^4/15$. [A1, F3, F4, step 2.1, algebra]
