items/lem-nrqm-intervals-integration-by-parts.md:74:[F4] Fix a real $\rho\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R)$ with $\int\rho=1$, for example $\rho=\sigma'$ from [F3], whose integral is $\sigma(1)-\sigma(0)=1$ by [F1]. Set $\rho_\delta(t)=\delta^{-1}\rho(t/\delta)$. By [[lem-complex-translation-and-approximate-identity-interfaces]], $\|\rho_\delta*g-g\|_{L^2(\mathbb R)}\to0$ for $g\in L^2(\mathbb R)$, and convolution maps compactly supported locally integrable inputs to smooth compactly supported functions with $(\rho_\delta*g)'=\rho_\delta'*g$. Step 1.2 proves the needed derivative commutation for its particular zero extension by integration by parts; classical derivatives of smooth functions are weak derivatives ([[lem-classical-derivatives-are-weak-derivatives]]).
items/lem-nrqm-intervals-integration-by-parts.md:80:1.1 [Bounded integration by parts] Let $u,v\in H^1(0,L)$. By [F2], the products $u^*\overline{v^*}$ and the derivative classes $u'\overline v$ and $u\overline{v'}$ are integrable; by [F1] in its complex form applied to $F=u^*$ and $G=v^*$ (whose real and imaginary parts are absolutely continuous with $L^1$ derivatives), $\int_0^L\bigl(u'\overline v+u\overline{v'}\bigr) =u^*(L)\overline{v^*(L)}-u^*(0)\overline{v^*(0)},$ which is exactly (a). [F1, F2]
items/thm-nrqmx-schwartz-moyal-remainder.md:106:[F9] Complex integration by parts holds on a line when both derivative products are integrable and the endpoint products vanish. Its coordinatewise use below is justified by Fubini; Schwartz Fourier factors and their derivatives make the products integrable and the endpoint terms zero ([[lem-complex-integration-by-parts-on-intervals-and-decaying-lines]]).
items/thm-fd-global-linear-acoustics.md:86:3.1 For each scalar component $f$ of the smooth initial data and every integer $N\ge0$, periodic integration by parts gives $(1+|k|^2)^N\widehat f(k)=\widehat{(1-\Delta)^Nf}(k)$, where $\Delta=\sum_{j=1}^d\partial_{x_j}^2$; opposite periodic-face terms cancel. Since $(1-\Delta)^Nf$ is continuous on the compact torus, its $L^1$ norm $C_{f,N}$ is finite, and $|\widehat f(k)|\le C_{f,N}(1+|k|^2)^{-N}$.
