items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-no-positive-compact-potential-eigenvalues.md:96:1.2 (Angular test functions and the exterior radial equation.) The harmonics of [F1] are smooth on the whole sphere: for $m\ge0$, their formula is the restriction of a constant multiple of $(x_1+ix_2)^mP_l^{(m)}(x_3)$ to $S^2$, and the negative indices are conjugates. In angle coordinates, with $t=\cos\theta$, direct differentiation gives $-\sin^{-1}\theta\,\partial_\theta(\sin\theta\,\partial_\theta w(t))=-((1-t^2)w'(t))'$ and $-\sin^{-2}\theta\,\partial_\varphi^2 e^{im\varphi}=m^2(1-t^2)^{-1}e^{im\varphi}$. Thus the associated Legendre equation gives $-\Delta_{S^2}Y_{lm}=l(l+1)Y_{lm}$ away from the poles and hence everywhere by smoothness. Define $f_{lm}(r)=\int_{S^2}\psi(r\omega)\overline{Y_{lm}(\omega)}\,d\sigma(\omega)$ for almost every $r>R$ and $\alpha_{lm}(r)=rf_{lm}(r)$. On every compact radial interval $[a,b]\subset(R,\infty)$, the smooth polar changes of coordinates and angular integration send the locally $H^2$ function $\psi$ to $f_{lm}\in H^2(a,b)$: this follows by mollifying $\psi$ on a slightly larger annulus, applying the ordinary chain rule to the smooth approximants, and passing in $H^2$; the coordinate Jacobians and coefficients and their first two derivatives are bounded on a finite sphere-chart cover of that annulus. The polar Laplacian formula is valid here without a domain assumption on an infinite angular diagonal operator: for a smooth Cartesian $h$, writing $G(r,\omega)=h(r\omega)$, tangential differentiation gives $\Delta_{S^2}G=r^2\sum_{i,j}(\delta_{ij}-\omega_i\omega_j)\partial_i\partial_jh-2r\partial_rG$, while $\partial_r^2G=\sum_{i,j}\omega_i\omega_j\partial_i\partial_jh$; addition yields $\Delta h=\partial_r^2G+2r^{-1}\partial_rG+r^{-2}\Delta_{S^2}G$. The same identity passes to distributions by the smooth approximants just described. Testing the exterior equation $-\Delta\psi=k^2\psi$ against $\eta(r)Y_{lm}(\omega)$, $\eta\in C_c^\infty((R,\infty))$, and using the polar Laplacian and angular integration by parts gives $(r^2f_{lm}')'+(k^2r^2-l(l+1))f_{lm}=0$ distributionally. Consequently $\alpha_{lm}''+(k^2-l(l+1)r^{-2})\alpha_{lm}=0$ on $(R,\infty)$. The smooth approximants just constructed converge in radial $H^2$; [F5] therefore supplies a $C^1$ representative of $\alpha_{lm}$ on each compact radial interval. Its distributional second derivative is the continuous function $-[k^2-l(l+1)r^{-2}]\alpha_{lm}$, so twice taking its continuous primitive gives a $C^2$ function with the same second derivative; the difference is affine, since local mollifications have second derivative zero. Thus $\alpha_{lm}$ is $C^2$, and repeated differentiation of this equation gives smoothness. This requires no extra regularity of $V$. [given, F1, F3, F5]
items/thm-fd-x08.md:12:scope: "An exact one-dimensional unsteady Stokes layer on t>0,y>0 with the initial-wall corner excluded, plus a separate smooth periodic sine-shear Navier–Stokes solution whose advected material-loop circulation decays. No general boundary-layer or viscous circulation theorem is claimed."
