items/thm-fd-x08.md:45:with Cartesian derivatives and componentwise Laplacian; its unsteady Stokes system omits the convective term. Set $I=(0,\infty)$ and $\Omega_+=\mathbb R\times(0,\infty)\times\mathbb R$, with coordinates $(x,y,z)$. Define $I_0=\int_0^\infty e^{-r^2}\,dr$ and $E(s)=I_0^{-1}\int_s^\infty e^{-r^2}\,dr$ for $s\ge0$. On $I\times\Omega_+$ define $u:I\times\Omega_+\to\mathbb R^3$ by $u(t,x,y,z)=(U E(y/(2\sqrt{\nu t})),0,0)$ and let $p$ be any constant scalar field. The field is smooth for $t>0,y>0$ and satisfies the pointwise unsteady Stokes equation $\partial_tu+\rho_0^{-1}\nabla p-\nu\Delta u=0$ and $\operatorname{div}u=0$. It also solves the full constant-density Navier–Stokes system because its convective term vanishes. For each fixed $t>0$ and $x,z\in\mathbb R$, its one-sided boundary limit is $\lim_{y\downarrow0}u(t,x,y,z)=(U,0,0)$ and its limit as $y\to\infty$ is $0$; for each fixed $x,z\in\mathbb R$ and $y>0$, its limit as $t\downarrow0$ is $0$. The corner $(t,y)=(0,0)$ is excluded from the classical claim. For every $x,z\in\mathbb R$, $t>0$, $0<\varepsilon<1$, and $y\ge2\sqrt{\nu t}\sqrt{\log(1/\varepsilon)}$, the absolute value of the first velocity component satisfies $|U E(y/(2\sqrt{\nu t}))|\le\varepsilon|U|$.
items/thm-fd-x08.md:47:Separately, let $\mathbb T_L=\mathbb R/(L\mathbb Z)$ for $L>0$, set $\mathbb T^3=\mathbb T_{L_x}\times\mathbb T_{L_y}\times\mathbb T_{L_z}$, and let $\pi:\mathbb R^3\to\mathbb T^3$ be the quotient map, where $L_x,L_y,L_z>0$. Equip this quotient with the flat smooth structure and metric induced by Euclidean translations, identifying each tangent space with $\mathbb R^3$ through the derivative of $\pi$. In this branch, the Navier–Stokes equations mean that the periodic lift satisfies the displayed constant-density system pointwise on $\mathbb R^3$. Choose an integer $m\ge1$, put $k=2\pi m/L_y$, and let $A\in\mathbb R$. The periodic lift $\widetilde u(t,x,y,z)=(A e^{-\nu k^2t}\sin(ky),0,0)$ defines a smooth vector field $u$ on $\mathbb T^3$ by $u(t,\pi(q))=d\pi_q(\widetilde u(t,q))$; with constant pressure, it is an exact Navier–Stokes solution for all $t\in\mathbb R$. Choose $c,b>0$ with $c<L_x/2$, $b<L_y/2$, and $0<\alpha:=kb<\pi$. Define the closed lifted loop and its quotient by
