items/thm-fd-x08.md:65:[F1] [[def-fd-x00]]: for constant density, $\nu=\mu/\rho_0$ and the incompressible Navier–Stokes equation is $u_t+(u\cdot\nabla)u+\rho_0^{-1}\nabla p-\nu\Delta u=0$ with $\operatorname{div}u=0$; Cartesian derivatives and componentwise Laplacian are used. This supplies the pointwise system for the half-space field and, through the periodic-lift convention in the Statement, for the torus field. The Euclidean material-loop definition there has domain in an open subset of $\mathbb R^3$; it is not used for the torus loop, whose material condition and circulation are defined through lifts in the Statement.
items/pthm-em-fixed-vacuum-jumps.md:189:[F6] Integration tools: the Fubini theorem for $L^1$ functions on sigma-finite product spaces ([[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]), integration by parts on a compact interval ([[thm-integration-by-parts]]), and the Riemann-Lebesgue comparison ([[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]]).
items/pthm-em-fixed-vacuum-jumps.md:203:1.3 Spacetime interface formula for a component: for each fixed $t$ the spatial restriction of the hypothesis satisfies the interface-derivative lemma on the spatial slice, so for a compactly supported spacetime test $\psi$ the identity $\partial_iF_j=\{\partial_iF_j\}+n_i[F_j]\,\delta_S$ may be applied slicewise and integrated in $t$; a time-independent finite partition of unity subordinate to stationary graph neighbourhoods of the spatial support of $\psi$, with cutoffs as in [F5], makes the terms $F_j\partial_i\psi$, $(\partial_iF_j)\psi$ and $n_i[F_j]\psi$ absolutely integrable on the compact support, because the piecewise derivatives and the continuous jumps are bounded there and the surface measure of the compact sheet is finite by [F1]; Fubini [F6] then justifies integrating the slicewise identity, giving $\partial_iF_j=\{\partial_iF_j\}+n_i[F_j]\otimes\delta_{S\times I}$ for the spacetime distribution, where the sheet term acts as $\int_I\int_Sn_i[F_j]\psi\,dS\,dt$. For the time derivative, on each spatial side the field is $C^1$, and compact-support integration by parts on a compact interval containing the time support of each spatially localized piece, followed by the Riemann-Lebesgue comparison and Fubini, gives $-\int F_j\partial_t\psi=\int(\partial_tF_j)\psi$ with no endpoint contribution, so $\partial_tF_j=\{\partial_tF_j\}$ carries no sheet term: the interface is stationary. [F1, F3, F5, F6, given, algebra]
items/pthm-em-fixed-vacuum-jumps.md:209:4.1 For the general weak-trace branch the same interface formula of step 1.3 is available with the weaker justification: the one-sided spatial $C^1$ extensions give the slicewise lemma at every time, the additional local $L^1$ hypotheses on the piecewise first derivatives make the localized terms of step 1.3 integrable, the measurable locally integrable traces make the sheet pairings $\int_I\int_Sa\psi\,dS\,dt$ well defined by the ordinary integrals for every test $\psi$, and Fubini still applies; the temporal identity holds because each fixed off-sheet point has a $C^1$ dependence in $t$ and integration by parts against a compactly supported test is justified by the integrable derivative. Consequently each of the four sheet pairings of the Statement vanishes for every test; no product of distributions is formed, only the pairing of a locally integrable sheet density with a compact smooth test. For the almost-everywhere conclusion, use the countable family of ambient bumps with rational centres and rational radii $0<r<R$ with $\overline B_R(q)\subseteq\Omega$, each fixed by the scaled-bump construction of [F5] rather than chosen per instance; for each such spatial test $\varphi$, Fubini gives $H_\varphi(t)=\int_Sa(x,t)\varphi(x)\,dS\in L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}(I)$, and product tests $\varphi(x)\eta(t)$ give $\int_IH_\varphi\eta=0$ for all temporal tests. Thus [F9] gives a null set of exceptional times for each such test, and the countable union of these null sets is null by [F7]. At each remaining time $t$, if some continuous-in-$x$ density $a(\cdot,t)$ were nonzero at a point $x_0\in S$, its fixed sign would persist on a small graph neighbourhood of $x_0$; density of the rationals [F8] provides one of the rational balls inside that neighbourhood whose inner ball contains $x_0$, and a positive-area graph cell inside that inner ball, as in [F4], makes the pairing nonzero against the corresponding bump, a contradiction. Hence all four identities hold at every $x\in S$ for almost every $t\in I$; if the jump densities are additionally jointly continuous, the same positive-area-cell argument applied on nondegenerate time intervals upgrades the identities to all $(x,t)$. [F1, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7, F8, F9, step 1.3, step 3.1, given, algebra]
items/thm-fd-periodic-inviscid-limit.md:44:Assume Countable Choice. Let $d\in\{1,2,3\}$, let $s\ge4$ be an integer, and let $u_0:Q_d\to\mathbb R^d$ be smooth, real, and divergence-free, where $Q_d=\mathbb R^d/(2\pi\mathbb Z)^d$ has normalized Lebesgue measure and divergence-free means $\sum_{j=1}^d\partial_j(u_0)_j=0$. The local incompressible theorem supplies, on a common interval $[0,T]$ independent of $0\le\nu\le1$, smooth solutions $u^\nu$ of the periodic Navier–Stokes equation and $v=u^0$ of periodic Euler with the same initial data. For every $t\in[0,T]$,
