items/thm-fd-periodic-global-weak-existence.md:85:Choose real orthonormal coordinates on $X_N$, whose finite Fourier coefficients satisfy the real conjugation and solenoidal conditions. The right side preserves this real space and is a polynomial vector field $F_N:\mathbb R^m\to\mathbb R^m$. It is continuous on the open time-state domain $\mathbb R\times\mathbb R^m$; its derivative is bounded on each closed coefficient ball, so integration along line segments gives a Lipschitz bound there. Thus [F6] gives a unique local solution through the projected initial value. Pairing with $u_N$ removes $P,\Pi_N$ by orthogonality, and periodic integration by parts gives $\int (u_N\cdot\nabla)u_N\cdot u_N=\int\operatorname{div}(u_N|u_N|^2/2)=0$. Thus
items/thm-fd-periodic-global-weak-existence.md:93:3.1 For a finite Fourier divergence-free test, uniform coefficient convergence passes the time-linear terms, strong $L^2_tH$ convergence passes the quadratic term by [F3], and the viscous term passes after integration by parts as $+\nu(u_N,\Delta\phi)$, using strong $L^2_tH$ convergence. A smooth periodic test and its time derivative have uniformly convergent Fourier truncations on their compact time support, by repeated integration by parts in space; the uniform energy bound controls the truncation errors. Thus the weak identity in [F2] holds for every admissible test, including its initial term because $\Pi_Nu_0\to u_0$ in $H$. [given, F1, F2, F3, step 2.2]
items/lem-cm-piecewise-c1-jump-derivative.md:95:[L2] Complex Lebesgue integration by parts: for complex $C^1$ functions $u,v$ on a closed interval $[a,b]$, $a<b$, one has $\int_a^bu'v=u(b)v(b)-u(a)v(a)-\int_a^buv'$ and $\int_a^bu'=u(b)-u(a)$ ([[lem-complex-integration-by-parts-on-intervals-and-decaying-lines]]).
items/lem-cm-piecewise-c1-jump-derivative.md:117:2.1 On the left half-interval define $\bar p_-(t):=p(t)$ for $t<t_*$ and $\bar p_-(t_*):=p(t_*^-)$; on $[a,t_*]$ this extension is continuous. For $t<t_*$, the mean value theorem [L4] on $[t,t_*]$ gives $(\bar p_-(t_*)-\bar p_-(t))/(t_*-t)=p'(\xi_t)$ for some $t<\xi_t<t_*$. As $t\uparrow t_*$ this tends to $p'(t_*^-)$, proving the endpoint derivative. Its derivative is continuous up to $t_*$; extend beyond $t_*$ by the affine function $p(t_*^-)+p'(t_*^-)(t-t_*)$ to obtain a $C^1$ function on an open neighborhood of $[a,t_*]$. On $[a,t_*]$, $p$ and $\bar p_-$ agree except at $t_*$, so [L3] and [L5] give $\int_{[a,t_*]}p\varphi'\,d\lambda=\int_a^{t_*}\bar p_-\varphi'\,d\lambda$. Lebesgue integration by parts [L2] for the $C^1$ pair $(\bar p_-,\varphi)$ gives $$\int_a^{t_*}\bar p_-\varphi'=\bar p_-(t_*)\varphi(t_*)-\bar p_-(a)\varphi(a)-\int_a^{t_*}\bar p_-'\varphi=p(t_*^-)\varphi(t_*)-\int_{[a,t_*]}p'_{\mathrm{reg}}\varphi\,d\lambda,$$ because $\varphi(a)=0$ and $\bar p_-'=p'_{\mathrm{reg}}$ on $(a,t_*)$; the last integrals agree by [L3] and [L5]. [step 1.2, given, L2, L3, L4, L5, algebra]
