items/def-fd-periodic-solenoidal-spaces.md:100:2.1 By [F1], coefficient sequences in the displayed weighted square-sum define unique $L^2$ classes, and Fourier truncations converge in the stated $H^s$ norm whenever that sum is finite. The conjugation condition is exactly the real-valued condition. Testing the periodic divergence against $e_{-k}$ gives $i\kappa_k\cdot\widehat u(k)$; hence distributional divergence zero implies $k\cdot\widehat u(k)=0$ for each $k\ne0$. Conversely, if these coefficients vanish, the divergence pairs to zero with every trigonometric polynomial. For any smooth periodic test, repeated integration by parts makes its Fourier coefficients decay faster than every power of $|k|$, so its Fourier partial sums converge with every derivative uniformly; continuity of the divergence functional then gives zero pairing with that test. Thus the Fourier constraint is equivalent to distributional divergence zero, and the formulas define the stated real Hilbert coefficient spaces and projections. [given, F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-transported-radial-endpoint-realization.md:105:[F7] On $C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\})$ one has $\int|\nabla\psi|^2=\int(-\Delta\psi)\overline\psi$ by the divergence theorem applied to the compactly supported field $\overline\psi\nabla\psi$, and $C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\})$ is dense in $H^1(\mathbb R^3;\mathbb C)$: for $u\in H^1$ and the radial cutoffs $\eta_\varepsilon$ with $\eta_\varepsilon=1$ off $B(0,\sqrt\varepsilon)$, $0$ on $B(0,\varepsilon)$ and $|\nabla\eta_\varepsilon|\le c/\bigl(r\log(1/\varepsilon)\bigr)$, the Hardy bound of [[lem-nrqm-coulomb-three-dimensional-hardy-bound]] gives $\|u\nabla\eta_\varepsilon\|_2^2\le4c^2\|\nabla u\|_2^2/\log(1/\varepsilon)^2\to0$ and dominated convergence gives $\|\eta_\varepsilon u-u\|_{H^1}\to0$ ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]], [[cor-vector-forms-of-the-divergence-theorem]], [[thm-dominated-convergence]], [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]]).
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-transported-radial-endpoint-realization.md:117:1.2 Let $\mathcal W$ be the Hilbert direct sum of $V_l$ over $(l,m)$, with squared norm $\sum\|u_{lm}\|_{V_l}^2$. On finite compact smooth radial sums, the corrected angular-radial block identity and Euclidean integration by parts give $\|\psi\|_{H^1}^2=\sum\|u_{lm}\|_{V_l}^2$ and $q(\psi,\varphi)=\sum a_l(u_{lm},v_{lm})$, where $q=\int\nabla\psi\cdot\overline{\nabla\varphi}-\gamma\int\psi\overline\varphi/|x|$. Polar integration gives the actual $r^{-1}$ potential weight. To establish the necessary full $H^1$ density, first use smooth density and the origin-removing cutoffs of [F7], followed by compact support and mollification, to reduce to $\psi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\})$. [F1, F2, F7, F10, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-transported-radial-endpoint-realization.md:121:2.2 For such an annular smooth $\psi$, let $\psi_N=P_N\psi$ be the angular truncation $l\le N$. All its radial coefficients are smooth with the same annular support. The polar formula gives $\Delta\psi_N=P_N\Delta\psi$: radial differentiation commutes with the finite projection, and angular integration by parts gives $\langle-\Delta_{S^2}\psi(r\cdot),Y_{lm}\rangle=\lambda_l\langle\psi(r\cdot),Y_{lm}\rangle$. This latter identity uses only the supplied finite-span eigenidentity, with a local Green argument for the other smooth function: excise caps of angular radius $\delta$, integrate the chart expression, cancel periodic azimuth boundaries, and let $\delta\downarrow0$; smooth intrinsic gradients are bounded and cap boundaries have length $O(\delta)$, so their terms vanish. Angular completeness and polar integration give $\psi_N\to\psi$ and $\Delta\psi_N\to\Delta\psi$ in $L^2$. For the smooth annular residual $R_N$, integration by parts gives $\|\nabla R_N\|^2=\langle-\Delta R_N,R_N\rangle\le\|\Delta R_N\|\|R_N\|\to0$. Thus finite compact smooth radial sums are $H^1$-dense with the gradient tail actually controlled. [step 1.2, F1, F7, F10, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-transported-radial-endpoint-realization.md:125:4.1 For $u\in C_c^\infty(0,\infty)$, integration by parts gives $a_l(u,v)=\langle L_lu,v\rangle$ first for core $v$, where $L_lu=-u''+(\lambda_l/r^2-\gamma/r)u\in L^2$. Both sides are continuous in $V_l$, so the identity extends to every $v\in V_l$. Thus the core is contained in $D(A_l)$ and this operator domain is $L^2$-dense before any adjoint is taken. The representing $f$ is unique by $V_l$ density. Symmetry of the form gives symmetry of $A_l$. For $y\in D(A_l^*)$, solve $(A_l+\mu)x=(A_l^*+\mu)y$ by step 3.1; then $y-x\in\ker(A_l^*+\mu)=\operatorname{ran}(A_l+\mu)^\perp=0$. Hence $A_l=A_l^*$ on exactly its defined domain, and $\langle A_lu,u\rangle=a_l[u]\ge-2\gamma^2\|u\|^2$. [step 3.1, step 2.1, F5, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-transported-radial-endpoint-realization.md:131:5.2 For $u\in D(A_l)$, test its form identity against core $v$. Distributional integration by parts gives $-u''+(\lambda_l/r^2-\gamma/r)u=A_lu$; the cross terms in $(u'-u/r)\overline{(v'-v/r)}$ cancel by integrating $(u\overline v/r)'$. On a compact interval away from zero the potential coefficient is bounded, so $u''\in L^2$ there and step 2.1 gives $u,u'\in L^2$. Thus $u\in H^2_{\mathrm{loc}}$, and [F9] gives its $C^1$ representative and locally absolutely continuous derivative. If $A_lu=Eu$, the right side for $u''$ is continuous, so integration makes $u'$ of class $C^1$ and the equation holds everywhere with $u\in C^2$. This proves (c) without regularity at the origin. [step 4.1, step 2.1, F9, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-transported-radial-endpoint-realization.md:135:6.1 For $\psi\in H^2=D(C)$ and $\varphi\in H^1$, weak Fourier integration by parts and three-dimensional Hardy give $q(\psi,\varphi)=\langle C\psi,\varphi\rangle$: prove it first for core $\varphi$ and extend by $H^1$ continuity, using $\psi/|x|\in L^2$. By step 4.2, $u=U\psi\in\mathcal W$. Fix one coordinate $j=(l,m)$ and $v\in V_l$; then $\varphi=U^{-1}(v\delta_j)\in H^1$ and the full form identity gives $a_l(u_j,v)=\langle(UC\psi)_j,v\rangle$. Hence $u_j\in D(A_l)$ and $A_lu_j=(UC\psi)_j$. The latter coordinates are square summable, so $C\subseteq U^*\widehat A U$. Both operators are self-adjoint; taking adjoints reverses the inclusion, giving equality. This proves the full iff graph-domain statement and action formula (d), also for $\gamma=0$ using its explicit free operator. [step 4.2, step 5.3, F1, F2, F7, algebra]
items/lem-qft-free-action-equations.md:82:For each coordinate, the product $\partial_a\phi\,h$ has compact support; one-dimensional integration by parts on that coordinate has zero boundary term, and Fubini [F4] permits integrating the remaining coordinates. Hence
items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-dollard-channel-completeness.md:99:6.1 Use the CC polar unitary of [F3], $\mathcal U\psi=u_{lm}(r)=r\int\psi(r\omega)\overline{Y_{lm}}d\sigma$. On finite compact smooth radial sums its finite-block identity gives $\|\psi\|_{H^1}^2=\sum\|u_{lm}\|_{V_l}^2$ and $q(\psi,\varphi)=\sum a_l(u_{lm},v_{lm})$, with the genuine potential weight $r^{-1}$. These finite sums are $H^1$-dense: approximate by smooth functions, remove zero with radial cutoffs having gradient $O(1/[r\log(1/\varepsilon)])$ and use Hardy to control the added derivative; compactly cut off and smooth away from zero. For a smooth annular $\psi$, its angular truncations $\psi_N=P_N\psi$ satisfy $\Delta\psi_N=P_N\Delta\psi$. Radial differentiation commutes with the projection; angular Green integration gives $\langle-\Delta_{S^2}\psi(r\cdot),Y_{lm}\rangle=l(l+1)\langle\psi(r\cdot),Y_{lm}\rangle$. This uses the finite-span eigenidentity with a direct local Green calculation: excise polar caps, cancel periodic azimuth boundaries and let the cap radius tend to zero, since smooth intrinsic gradients are bounded and boundary lengths tend to zero. Parseval gives $\psi_N\to\psi$, $\Delta\psi_N\to\Delta\psi$ in $L^2$, and $\|\nabla(\psi-\psi_N)\|^2\le\|\Delta(\psi-\psi_N)\|\|\psi-\psi_N\|\to0$. Conversely finite core families are dense in the square-summable $V_l$ family space, so the finite isometry extends onto it from $H^1$. The full form identity extends by Cauchy–Schwarz; the Coulomb sum is uniformly bounded by $2(\sum\|u_{lm}'\|^2)^{1/2}(\sum\|v_{lm}\|^2)^{1/2}$. Define $\widehat A=\bigoplus A_l$ on the square-summable graph domain. Its symmetry and coordinate resolvents with norm at most one at $\pm i$ prove self-adjointness. For $\psi\in H^2=D(C)$ and $\varphi\in H^1$, weak integration by parts and Hardy give $q(\psi,\varphi)=\langle C\psi,\varphi\rangle$. Test $\varphi=\mathcal U^{-1}(v\delta_{lm})$ with arbitrary $v\in V_l$: the exact form criterion gives $u_{lm}\in D(A_l)$ and $A_lu_{lm}=(\mathcal UC\psi)_{lm}$. The latter images are square summable; hence $C\subseteq\mathcal U^*\widehat A\mathcal U$. Both sides are self-adjoint, so equality follows by adjoints. This proves the exact block/domain identity under CC, also at $\gamma=0$, without borrowing the AC-bearing endpoint theorem. [step 5.1, F1, F2, F3, F4, algebra]
items/lem-gr-fx-schwarzschild-gaussian-beam-concentration.md:80:3.1 Positive energy and real fields. For a compactly supported complex $q$ define $e_l[q]=\tfrac12\int_{\mathbb R}(|q_t|^2+|q_x|^2+W_l|q|^2)\,dx$. Use the sum of the real and imaginary scalar energies for a complex field. From [F2]--[F3], its energy density in $x,\omega$ is $\tfrac12(r^2|u_t|^2+r^2|u_x|^2+f|\nabla_{S^2}u|^2)$. For $qY_l/r$, integration over $S^2$ gives $\tfrac12(|q_t|^2+|q_x-bq|^2+fl(l+1)|q|^2/r^2)$, $b=f/r$. The identity $b'+b^2=fs/r^3$ and integration of $-b\partial_x|q|^2$, with zero endpoint terms, prove that its total energy equals $e_l[q]$. The zero bilinear angular integrals in step 1.1 give exactly $E_T[\operatorname{Re}(qY_l/r)]=e_l[q]/2$. The original local energy density is nonnegative on every radial subset. The factor $1/2$ for real fields also holds before the radial integration by parts, so no false boundary-term identity on a finite annulus is being used. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]
items/lem-gr-fx-schwarzschild-gaussian-beam-concentration.md:86:4.1 Frequency-independent energy stability. The same integral construction with an added d'Alembert forcing term treats smooth forcing on finite slabs. For a smooth spatially compact solution of $q_{tt}-q_{xx}+W_lq=F$, direct differentiation and integration by parts give $e_l'[q]=\operatorname{Re}\int F\overline{q_t}\,dx$. For nonzero $A=\|F\|_2$ and $B=\|q_t\|_2$, integration of $(|F|/A-|q_t|/B)^2\ge0$ gives $\int|Fq_t|\le AB$; zero-norm cases give the same inequality immediately. Since $W_l\ge0$, this bounds the energy derivative in absolute value by $\sqrt{2e_l[q]}\,\|F\|_2$. Apply this first to $\sqrt{e_l+\eta}$, integrate and let $\eta\downarrow0$ to obtain $\sqrt{e_l[q](t)}\le\sqrt{e_l[q](0)}+2^{-1/2}\int_0^t\|F(a)\|_2\,da$. In particular the energy of a homogeneous wave from step 3.2 is conserved. Compact support on every finite slab justifies all integrations, even though the full spatial line is noncompact. [step 2.1, step 3.1, step 3.2, algebra]
items/lem-tgr-timelike-scalar-acceleration.md:189:1.1 Fix a compact set $K\Subset I$ containing $\operatorname{supp}f$ and let $d=\min_{\tau\in K}(\dot\gamma^0-|\dot{\boldsymbol\gamma}|)>0$. For $\mathbf k=r\mathbf n$, $r>0$, $|\mathbf n|=1$, the phase is $r\phi_{\mathbf n}(\tau)$ with $\phi'_{\mathbf n}=\dot\gamma^0-\mathbf n\cdot\dot{\boldsymbol\gamma}\ge d$. All derivatives of $1/\phi'_{\mathbf n}$ are uniformly bounded on $K\times S^2$. Since $e^{ir\phi}=(ir\phi')^{-1}\partial_\tau e^{ir\phi}$, repeated integration by parts gives $|K_\gamma f(r\mathbf n)|\le C_N r^{-N}$ for every $N$ and $r\ge1$, with $C_N$ bounded by finitely many test-function seminorms on $K$. Boundary terms vanish because $f$ is compactly supported. For $0<r\le1$, $|K_\gamma f(r\mathbf n)|\le\|f\|_{L^1(K)}$. [A1, F1, F2, given, construct]
items/lem-tgr-timelike-scalar-acceleration.md:199:4.1 For compact real tests $f,g$, the linear change $v=(\tau+\tau')/2$, $s=\tau-\tau'$ has Jacobian of absolute value one and takes $f(\tau)g(\tau')$ to a compact smooth test $\varphi(v,s)$. On the compact average-time projection of its support, write $A(v)=2\ell e^{-\kappa v}>0$ and $B(v)=2\ell e^{\kappa v}>0$. The factors $Y-X+i\epsilon$ and $Y+X-i\epsilon$ are respectively $-A(v)(x-i\epsilon/A(v))$ and $B(v)(x-i\epsilon/B(v))$, where $x=\sinh(\kappa s/2)$. For positive $\alpha,\beta$ the identity $$\frac{1}{(x-i\alpha\epsilon)(x-i\beta\epsilon)} =\int_0^1\frac{dt}{(x-i((1-t)\alpha+t\beta)\epsilon)^2}$$ follows by integrating the derivative of $[x-i((1-t)\alpha+t\beta)\epsilon]^{-1}$ in $t$ and dividing by $i(\beta-\alpha)\epsilon$ when $\alpha\ne\beta$; for $\alpha=\beta$ both sides agree directly. It shows that both dampings have the same boundary. To justify that boundary, for a compact smooth test $\psi$ choose an even compact smooth $\chi$ equal to one near zero and write $\psi(x)=\psi(0)\chi(x)+x\rho(x)$, where $\rho$ is smooth and compactly supported. The constant term satisfies $$\int\frac{\chi(x)}{x-i\lambda\epsilon}\,dx =i\lambda\epsilon\int\frac{\chi(x)}{x^2+(\lambda\epsilon)^2}\,dx\longrightarrow i\pi$$ for each $\lambda>0$, since its odd real part integrates to zero. The other term is $x\rho(x)/(x-i\lambda\epsilon)=\rho(x)+ i\lambda\epsilon\rho(x)/(x-i\lambda\epsilon)$; the last integral is $O(\epsilon|\log\epsilon|)$ uniformly for $\lambda$ in a compact positive interval. Let $\delta_0$ denote evaluation at zero, $\langle\delta_0,\psi\rangle=\psi(0)$, and define the distributional derivative by $\langle T',\psi\rangle=-\langle T,\psi'\rangle$ for compact smooth tests $\psi$, so $\langle\delta_0',\psi\rangle=-\psi'(0)$. Thus $(x-i0)^{-1}=\operatorname{PV}(1/x)+i\pi\delta_0$, where $\operatorname{PV}(1/x)$ acts by $\lim_{\delta\downarrow0}\int_{|x|>\delta}\psi(x)/x\,dx$. Differentiating this distributional identity gives $(x-i0)^{-2}=\operatorname{Pf}(1/x^2)-i\pi\delta_0'$ and $$\left\langle\operatorname{Pf}(1/x^2),\psi\right\rangle =\lim_{\delta\downarrow0}\left(\int_{|x|>\delta}\frac{\psi(x)}{x^2}\,dx-\frac{2\psi(0)}{\delta}\right).$$ Integration by parts on $|x|>\delta$ identifies this finite part with $-(\operatorname{PV}(1/x))'$. The bounds are uniform when the positive damping coefficient ranges in a compact subinterval of $(0,\infty)$, as it does for $v$ in a compact set. To identify the pullback with the usual $s-i0$ boundary, note that $\sinh(bs-i\epsilon b)=\sinh(bs)-i\epsilon b\cosh(bs)+O(\epsilon^2)$. More generally, for a positive smooth regulator $\lambda(v,x)$, Taylor expand $\lambda=\lambda_0(v)+\lambda_1(v)x+x^2r(v,x)$. The linear part factors as $$x-i\epsilon(\lambda_0+\lambda_1x) =(1-i\epsilon\lambda_1)\left(x-\frac{i\epsilon\lambda_0}{1-i\epsilon\lambda_1}\right).$$ Its pole has positive imaginary part comparable to $\epsilon$ and real displacement $O(\epsilon^2)$, so the preceding test-subtraction calculation gives the same boundary. The quadratic remainder changes the inverse square by at most $C\epsilon x^2/(x^2+\epsilon^2)^{3/2}$ on a small interval; its integral is $O(\epsilon|\log\epsilon|)$. Thus the smooth positive regulator, including the one induced by $\sinh(b(s-i\epsilon))$, has the same boundary as the constant positive regulator. The change $x=\sinh(\kappa s/2)$ is orientation-preserving with positive derivative. Consequently the test-paired limit of step 3.2 is $$C_a(s)=-\frac{1}{16\pi^2\ell^2}\operatorname{csch}^2\!\left(\frac{\kappa(s-i0)}{2}\right) =-\frac{a^2}{16\pi^2c^4}\operatorname{csch}^2\!\left(\frac{a(s-i0)}{2c}\right).$$ Thus $\langle K_{\gamma_a}f,K_{\gamma_a}g\rangle_2$ equals $\left\langle C_a(s),\int f(t)g(t-s)\,dt\right\rangle$. This proves stationarity and the stated covariance boundary from ordinary regulated shell pairings. To verify temperedness explicitly, set $q=\lim_{\epsilon\downarrow0}\coth(\kappa(s-i\epsilon)/2)$ in the distribution sense and $r(s)=\coth(\kappa s/2)-2/(\kappa s)$ for $s\ne0$. The Taylor expansion of $\coth$ at zero extends $r$ smoothly there, and $r'(s)=-(\kappa/2)\operatorname{csch}^2(\kappa s/2)+2/(\kappa s^2)$ is bounded: its apparent singularity cancels at zero, and both terms are bounded at infinity. Since $1/(s-i0)=\operatorname{PV}(1/s)+i\pi\delta_0$, the decomposition is $q=(2/\kappa)\operatorname{PV}(1/s)+r+(2\pi i/\kappa)\delta_0$. By the derivative definition and $q_\epsilon\to q$ on compact smooth tests, $q_\epsilon'\to q'$. Direct differentiation of $q_\epsilon(s)=\coth(\kappa(s-i\epsilon)/2)$ then gives $C_a=(a^2/(8\pi^2c^4\kappa))q'$. For $m,n\ge0$ set $p_{m,n}(\varphi)=\sup_s(1+s^2)^{m/2}|\varphi^{(n)}(s)|$, a Schwartz seminorm. For $\varphi\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R)$, the symmetric principal-value formula gives $\left|\left\langle\operatorname{PV}(1/s),\varphi'\right\rangle\right|\le 2p_{0,2}(\varphi)+C p_{2,1}(\varphi),$ because subtracting $\varphi'(0)$ on $|s|<1$ bounds the local integral by $2\sup|\varphi''|$, while the $|s|\ge1$ integral is bounded by $p_{2,1}\int_{|s|\ge1}[|s|(1+s^2)]^{-1}ds$. Also $|\int r'(s)\varphi(s)ds|\le C p_{2,0}(\varphi)$ and $|\langle\delta_0',\varphi\rangle|\le p_{0,1}(\varphi)$. Hence $|\langle C_a,\varphi\rangle|\le C_{a,c}\bigl(p_{0,2}(\varphi)+p_{2,1}(\varphi)+p_{2,0}(\varphi)+p_{0,1}(\varphi)\bigr),$ a finite Schwartz-seminorm bound. By [F2], $C_a\in\mathcal S'(\mathbb R)$, as required for its subsequent Fourier transform. [F1, F2, step 3.2, step 1.2, algebra]
