items/pthm-emx-helicity-rotation-weight.md:94:4.1 For the Stokes clause, one frame is defined on the open set containing the image of $\Sigma$. By the line-integral definition and the affine reparametrizations in $c_\gamma$, $\int_{c_\gamma}A=\oint_\gamma A$. Since $\partial\Sigma=c_\gamma$, the cited Stokes theorem [F4] gives $\int_\Sigma dA=\int_{\partial\Sigma}A=\int_{c_\gamma}A$. The local curvature is $F_\sigma=dA$ by [F3], so exponentiation gives $\mathrm{Hol}_\gamma=\exp(i\int_\Sigma F_\sigma)$. For paths or surfaces requiring more than one patch, the transition factors in step 2.2 are part of the holonomy; a bare integral of a nonexistent global one-form is not asserted. [F3, F4, step 2.2, algebra] ∎
items/def-emg-charged-scalar-functional.md:76:Allowed first variations are compactly supported smooth complex-section variations $\delta\varphi$, compactly supported real global one-form variations $\alpha=\delta A$ added to every local $A_i$ (so the same bundle transition functions are retained), and compactly supported symmetric inverse-metric variations $q^{ab}=\delta g^{ab}$. The formula defines a classical mathematical functional; it asserts no quantum interpretation, value of $\hbar$, or material-response model.
