items/lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure.md:68:[F1] **Coordinate integration by parts.** The scalar first Green identity ([[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]]) with its solution argument equal to $x_j$ gives $\int_\Omega\partial_jv=\int_{\partial\Omega}v n_j$ for $C^1$ scalar $v$. Applying this to products of components gives the vector identities used below. In particular, for smooth fields on a bounded $C^1$ domain, $\int_\Omega(\operatorname{curl}u\cdot\operatorname{curl}v-u\cdot\operatorname{curl}\operatorname{curl}v)=\int_{\partial\Omega}(u\times\operatorname{curl}v)\cdot n\,dS$ and the analogous divergence identity ([[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]]); the curl and divergence are those of the Euclidean cooordinates and $\Delta$ is the Euclidean Laplacian ([[def-laplacian-of-a-c2-function]]).
items/lem-em-smooth-pec-cavity-spectral-closure.md:90:1.1 **Uniform normal collar and Gaffney identity.** Put $S=\partial\Omega$. If $\Omega$ is empty, all spaces and integrals in the Statement are zero and the empty spectral family and zero wave prove the assertions; otherwise boundedness makes $S$ nonempty, and its closedness and boundedness make it compact [F7]. The graph charts give its smooth outward unit normal $n$ [given]. Apply normal addition [F7] to $S$ and take $0<\eta<\min_S\rho$. Then $\Phi(p,t)=p+tn(p)$ is a diffeomorphism for $|t|<\eta$. Shrink $\eta$ uniformly if needed: in each boundary graph chart an outward defining function has positive derivative along $n$ at $t=0$, so by continuity $t<0$ maps inside and $t>0$ outside on a smaller patch and interval; a finite compact cover gives one such interval for all $p$. On the smaller closed strip $|t|\le\eta/2$, the smooth positive volume Jacobian relative to $dS\,dt$ has constants $0<c\le J(p,t)\le C$ by [F7]; in a surface chart it is the absolute volume determinant divided by the positive Gram surface density, and the finite chart formula and change of variables of [F7] give $dx=J\,dS\,dt$. Extend the normal by $N(\Phi(p,t))=n(p)$. It is smooth and constant along each normal line, hence $(N\cdot\nabla)N=0$. Also $\nabla t=N$: the derivative of $t$ is zero on the vectors $D\Phi(\tau,0)=\tau+tD_\tau n$, which are orthogonal to $n$, and is one on $\partial_t\Phi=n$. This is the signed-distance coordinate: for $x=\Phi(p,t)$ a closest point $q\in S$ exists by compactness, and minimizing squared distance in surface charts makes $x-q$ normal to $S$ with $|x-q|\le|t|<\min\rho$; injectivity of normal addition forces $q=p$, so $\operatorname{dist}(x,S)=|t|$ with the above inside/outside sign. For smooth $u$ with $n\times u=0$, expansion and coordinate integration by parts [F1] give $\int_\Omega(|\operatorname{curl}u|^2+|\operatorname{div}u|^2-|\nabla u|^2)=\sum_{i,j}\int_S(n_ju_j\partial_i u_i-n_ju_i\partial_i u_j)\,dS$. On $S$, $u=(u\cdot n)n$ and $\operatorname{div}u=\partial_N(u\cdot N)+(\operatorname{div}N)(u\cdot n)$: tangential derivatives of the zero tangential trace vanish there and $\partial_NN=0$. Substitution reduces the boundary term to $\int_S(\operatorname{div}N)|u\cdot n|^2dS$, the claimed Gaffney identity; its curvature is bounded on compact $S$. [given, F1, F7, algebra]
