items/thm-fd-rankine-hugoniot.md:100:1.1 Fix a test $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(I\times\Omega;\mathbb R^m)$. Choose an orthonormal basis of $n^\perp$ and write $x=y+(st+r)n$, with $y\in n^\perp$ and $r=n\cdot x-st$. In these coordinates $\Phi(t,y,r)=(t,y+(st+r)n)$ is a linear bijection with absolute determinant $1$, and $y\mapsto y+stn$ is an isometry from $n^\perp$ onto the full spatial plane $\{x\in\mathbb R^d:n\cdot x=st\}$, whose restriction gives the slice in $\Omega$. [F4] states scaling for measurable sets. For a nonnegative simple function, [F6] and the set-scaling identity for $\Phi^{-1}$ give equality of the integrals of the function and its pullback by summing over the measurable level sets; since $\Phi$ is bijective, pullback gives a bijection between the simple minorants of a nonnegative measurable function and those of its pullback, so [F7] gives the same integral identity for nonnegative measurable functions. Apply that identity to the positive and negative parts of each signed integrable real integrand; [F8] then gives equality for the integrable integrands used here. Every test-supported integrand is integrable after extension by zero, since the coefficients are locally integrable and the test derivatives are bounded on compact support. Thus this chart preserves the required volume integrals, and [F5] permits their iterated evaluation. The coordinate measure $dy$ is the pushforward of $\lambda_{d-1}$ by the chosen linear isometry $E$. Replacing $E$ by another such isometry changes $z$ by an orthogonal linear map, whose determinant has absolute value $1$, so [F4] and the same simple-function argument prove independence of the basis; when $d=1$ this is the one-point measure specified in the Statement. Let $H(r)=1$ for $r>0$ and $H(r)=0$ for $r<0$. By [F3], one-dimensional integration by parts in $r$ gives, for every $C^1$ coefficient field $a$, $\partial_t(aH)=H\partial_ta-s\,a|_\Sigma\delta_\Sigma$ and $\partial_{x_j}(aH)=H\partial_{x_j}a+n_j\,a|_\Sigma\delta_\Sigma$, where $\delta_\Sigma$ pairs with $dt\,dy$ on the spatial plane, with the coordinate Lebesgue measure just defined. The time derivative is at fixed $x$, so in this chart $\partial_t|_x=\partial_t|_{y,r}-s\partial_r$; this produces the factor $-s$ and the stated normalization. Tangential spatial derivatives have no boundary term, while the normal derivative contributes $n_j$. [given, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7, F8, A2, algebra]
items/prop-emg-xs-kn-dyonic-flux.md:96:2.1 For $R_0<r_1<r_2$, in the fixed-$x^0$ slice of step 1.1, take the product of each of the same eight projected triangular faces with $[r_1,r_2]$. Each triangular prism splits into three tetrahedra; their oriented sum is a finite smooth singular three-chain $\mathcal C$, internal faces cancel, and its boundary is $S_{r_2}-S_{r_1}$. By steps 1.1-1.2 and [F1], $d(\star_g\mathcal F)=0$ on this shell. Stokes [F5] therefore gives $$\int_{S_{r_2}}\star_g\mathcal F-\int_{S_{r_1}}\star_g\mathcal F=\int_{\partial\mathcal C}\star_g\mathcal F=\int_\mathcal C d(\star_g\mathcal F)=0.$$ Step 1.2 identifies the common value as $4\pi q$. [F1, F3, F5, A1, step 1.1, step 1.2, construct, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-continuum-spectral-density.md:140:6.1 [Poisson approximation from the PVM] For continuous $h$ compactly supported in $(0,\infty)$, extended by zero to $\mathbb R$, [F3] and Fubini give $\pi^{-1}\int h(E)\operatorname{Im}\langle(A_l-E-i\varepsilon)^{-1}g,g\rangle dE=\int(P_\varepsilon*h)(\lambda)d\mu_g(\lambda)$, where $P_\varepsilon(t)=\varepsilon/[\pi(t^2+\varepsilon^2)]$. Absolute integrability follows from $\int P_\varepsilon=1$ and finite mass $\|g\|^2$. Uniform continuity bounds $|h(x-t)-h(x)|$ by any prescribed $\eta>0$ for $|t|\le\delta$; the remainder is at most $2\|h\|_\infty\int_{|t|>\delta}P_\varepsilon\le4\|h\|_\infty\varepsilon/(\pi\delta)$. Hence $P_\varepsilon*h\to h$ uniformly. For compact-source $g$, step 5.1 computes the other limit, yielding $\int h\,d\mu_g=\int_0^\infty h(E)d_g(E)dE$, with continuous nonnegative $d_g(E)=\rho_l(E)|\int\varphi_l(\sqrt E,r)g(r)dr|^2$. [step 5.1, F3, F4, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-intervals-delta-rank-one-resolvent.md:72:1.1 [Zero-coupling identification and nonvanishing denominator] Let $A_0^{\mathrm{int}}$ be the interface operator with parameter $\alpha=0$. If $u\in D(A_0^{\mathrm{int}})$, its piecewise second derivative $q$ is in $L^2(\mathbb R)$ and the two one-sided derivative traces agree. For every $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R)$, integration by parts on the two half-lines gives $\int_{\mathbb R}u'\varphi'=(u'^*(0-)-u'^*(0+))\varphi(0)-\int_{\mathbb R}q\varphi=-\int_{\mathbb R}q\varphi$; hence $D^2u=q$ globally and $u\in H^2(\mathbb R)$. Conversely, if $u\in H^2(\mathbb R)$, then $u'\in H^1(\mathbb R)$ has a single continuous representative, so its one-sided traces at zero agree; the restrictions of $u$ lie in $H^2$ on each half-line and satisfy the zero-jump condition. Thus $D(A_0^{\mathrm{int}})=H^2(\mathbb R)$ and both $A_0^{\mathrm{int}}$ and $A_{\mathrm{free}}$ act as $-D^2$ there, proving the identification asserted in the Statement. Now, if $1+\alpha/(2k)=0$, then $2k=-\alpha\in\mathbb R$; but $k^2=-z$ is nonreal because $z$ is nonreal, so $k$ itself is nonreal. Hence $1+\alpha G_z(0)=1+\alpha/(2k)\ne0$. [F1, given, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-balanced-local-functional-representatives.md:52:After smearing, integration by parts therefore makes the resulting maps independent of the chosen local density presentation. Differentiation adds only a finite derivative order to any finite scaling upper bound.
items/lem-qftx-balanced-local-functional-representatives.md:76:2.1 Expand a density using step 1.1 as a finite sum $p(\partial)B_b$. In its integral against $f$, move $p(\partial)$ onto the compact coefficient by integration by parts, obtaining $p(-\partial)f$ multiplying $B_b$. This gives existence of the displayed representation and preserves compact support. Degree zero gives the scalar $c=F(0)$ and is separated from the positive field degrees. To prove uniqueness, suppose a balanced expression represents zero. Separate its field degrees by replacing $\phi$ by $t\phi$, polarize, and evaluate its $r$ slots on the smooth exponential fields $e^{2\pi i k_a\cdot x}$; complex exponentials are permitted by complex multilinear extension from real configurations. The resulting identity is a finite sum of $\widehat f_{r,b}(-\sum_a k_a)$ times the balanced polynomial $b(2\pi ik_1,\ldots,2\pi ik_r)$. For fixed total momentum, vary freely the relative momenta. Independence of the basis polynomials on $\sum_aq_a=0$ makes every coefficient Fourier transform zero at that total momentum. Since that momentum is arbitrary, [F2] gives every $f_{r,b}=0$; also $c=0$. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
items/prop-gr-fx-s1-loop-noncontractible.md:43:      locator: "PDF pp. 6–7, fixed-endpoint thin homotopies and the thin path groupoid; the circle obstruction below is proved locally by Stokes."
items/prop-gr-fx-s1-loop-noncontractible.md:110:2.1 Suppose a thin homotopy $H:[0,1]^2\to S^1$ joined $\gamma$ to the constant loop while fixing the basepoint at the path endpoints. Parametrize every curvilinear triangle in its finite subdivision by the specified smooth map from the standard two-simplex and compose that parametrization with $H$. By the smooth-up-to-boundary convention of [[def-gr-fx-thin-paths]], each composition has a smooth extension to a neighborhood of the standard simplex, so it is a smooth singular simplex by [F7]. Orient these finitely many simplices compatibly with the square and apply [F5] to each, then add the formulas. Each shared geometric edge occurs twice with opposite orientations. Choose one smooth reference parametrization $\sigma$ for such an edge; the two oriented face restrictions are $\sigma\circ\phi$ and the reverse of $\sigma\circ\psi$, for increasing smooth reparametrizations $\phi,\psi:[0,1]\to[0,1]$. By [F6] and one-variable substitution, their oriented integrals are $\int_\sigma\alpha$ and $-\int_\sigma\alpha$, so they cancel. The remaining terms are exactly the oriented square boundary under $H$. Step 1.1 gives $d\alpha=0$, hence Stokes gives zero for the total boundary integral. The two path edges have signed integral $2\pi$ or $-2\pi$ because one is $\gamma$ and the other is constant; the endpoint edges are constant and contribute zero. Thus the boundary integral is $\pm2\pi\ne0$, a contradiction. Therefore $[\gamma]$ is not the identity class. [given, F1, F5, F6, F7, step 1.1, assume-contra, discharge-contradiction] ∎
