items/lem-qft-photon-local-field-strength.md:76:where integration by parts has no boundary term because $h$ is compactly supported. The current $j^b=-\partial_a h^{ab}$ obeys $\partial_bj^b=-\partial_b\partial_a h^{ab}=0$, since exchanging $a,b$ changes the sign of $h$. Adding $\partial_b\lambda$ to $A_b$ changes the last integral by $\int\lambda\,\partial_b\partial_a h^{ab}=0$. The spatial components of $j$ are compact smooth, so [F1] supplies the vector $g_h=g_j$ and the stated finite-particle operator. [A1, F1, given, algebra]
items/lem-qft-photon-local-field-strength.md:78:2.1 For a class $[\epsilon]\in p^\perp/\mathbb Cp$, let $T_p[\epsilon]=\boldsymbol\epsilon-(\mathbf k/\omega)\epsilon^0$ as in [F2]. The representative $\widetilde\epsilon=(0,T_p[\epsilon])$ has the same class, since $\epsilon-\widetilde\epsilon=(\epsilon^0/\omega)p$. Thus we may use a transverse representative $\widetilde\epsilon=(0,\boldsymbol\epsilon)$. With $p_a=(-\omega,\mathbf k)$ and Fourier phase $e^{-ip_ax^a}$, compact support and integration by parts give $\widehat{j^b}(p)=-ip_a\widehat h^{ab}(p)$ for $j^b=-\partial_a h^{ab}$. Its contraction with $\epsilon_b$ is therefore $-i\widehat h^{ab}p_a\epsilon_b=-\frac i2\widehat h^{ab}(p_a\epsilon_b-p_b\epsilon_a)$ by antisymmetry of $h$. Replacing $\epsilon$ by $\epsilon+c p$ leaves this expression unchanged because $p_ap_b-p_bp_a=0$. Under a Lorentz transformation both $p$ and $\epsilon$ transform linearly, so their antisymmetric product transforms as a two-form. The unitary quotient action [F2] therefore intertwines the smeared tensor test with its Lorentz pullback, proving covariance without choosing a global polarization frame. [F1, F2, step 1.1, given, algebra]
items/thm-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-uniqueness-stability.md:105:1.2 ($L^2$ uniqueness for all stated classical solutions.) Set $E_0(t)=\int w^{\mathsf T}a w$. Symmetry and integration by parts give $E_0'=\int w^{\mathsf T}(\dot a+\partial_i a_i)w+2\int w^{\mathsf T}f$. Pointwise coefficient Lipschitz bounds give $|f|\le C|w|$, since $\partial_tv$ and $\partial_i v$ are bounded in $L^\infty$. Thus $|E_0'|\le CE_0$. If $w(0)=0$, Gronwall on either oriented segment from $0$ to $t$ gives $E_0(t)=0$. As $t\in J$ was arbitrary, this proves (a). [L1, L2, L3, L4, L5, algebra]
items/thm-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-uniqueness-stability.md:107:1.3 (Energy identity at the stated regularity.) Work on any compact time segment and define $E=\sum_{|\alpha|\le r}\int(D^\alpha w)^{\mathsf T}aD^\alpha w$. Put $w_N=P_Nw$, $h=\partial_tw$, and $y_\alpha=D^\alpha w$, $h_\alpha=D^\alpha h$. Classical $C^1$ regularity makes each Fourier coefficient $C^1$ with derivative the coefficient of $h$, so $w_N$ is a $C^1$ finite-dimensional curve. The bounds $w\in H^{r+1}$ and $h\in H^r$ are uniform at every time: the former is assumed and the latter follows pointwise from the PDE and [L4]. The coefficients $a$ and $\dot a$ are continuous, with $a$ uniformly $C^1$ in space and $\dot a$ uniformly bounded. Let $E_N=\sum_{|\alpha|\le r}\int(P_Ny_\alpha)^{\mathsf T}a(P_Ny_\alpha)$. The tail bound [L6] gives uniform $L^2$ convergence $P_Ny_\alpha\to y_\alpha$ at rate $O((1+N^2)^{-1/2})$ since $y_\alpha\in H^1$ uniformly. Hence $E_N\to E$ uniformly. Finite-dimensional differentiation gives $E_N'=\sum_\alpha[2\langle P_Nh_\alpha,aP_Ny_\alpha\rangle+\langle P_Ny_\alpha,\dot aP_Ny_\alpha\rangle]$. By self-adjointness the first pairing equals $\langle h_\alpha,P_N(aP_Ny_\alpha)\rangle$. Now $ay_\alpha$ has uniformly bounded $H^1$ norm by the Leibniz rule, and $\|P_N(aP_Ny_\alpha)-ay_\alpha\|_2\le\|a\|_\infty\|P_Ny_\alpha-y_\alpha\|_2+\|(P_N-I)(ay_\alpha)\|_2\to0$ uniformly by [L6]. The quadratic terms also converge uniformly. Thus $E_N'$ converges uniformly to $G=\sum_\alpha[2\langle h_\alpha,ay_\alpha\rangle+\langle y_\alpha,\dot ay_\alpha\rangle]$. Integrating the scalar identities $E_N(t)-E_N(t')=\int_{t'}^tE_N'$ and passing uniformly to the limit gives $E(t)-E(t')=\int_{t'}^tG$; $G$ is continuous as a uniform limit of continuous functions. Therefore $E'=G$. The differentiated difference equation is an $L^2$ identity because $w\in H^{r+1}$ and $h\in H^r$; spatial integration by parts is justified by Fourier density and the $H^1$ bounds for $y_\alpha$. It may therefore be substituted into $G$ without additional time regularity assumptions. [L1, L3, L4, L6, algebra]
items/thm-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-uniqueness-stability.md:111:3.1 (Order-$r$ difference energy.) Put $E=\sum_{|\alpha|\le r}\int(D^\alpha w)^{\mathsf T}aD^\alpha w$, so $a_0\|w\|_{H^r}^2\le E\le b_0\|w\|_{H^r}^2$. Differentiating [L1] gives $a\partial_tD^\alpha w+a_i\partial_iD^\alpha w=D^\alpha f-[D^\alpha,a]\partial_tw-[D^\alpha,a_i]\partial_iw$. After integration by parts the energy derivative consists of the $\dot a+\partial_i a_i$ quadratic terms and twice the pairings with these three right-side terms. Step 1.1 bounds the quadratic and forcing terms by $C\|w\|_{H^r}^2$. Homogeneous commutators from [L3], together with step 2.1, bound the time commutators by the same expression. Spatial commutators also have that bound since $\|\partial_iw\|_{H^{r-1}}\le\|w\|_{H^r}$. Therefore $|E'|\le CE$. The energy identity is justified at this regularity by step 1.3. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 1.3, L1, L2, L3, L4, algebra]
items/pthm-fd-two-dimensional-viscous-model-globality.md:84:**Proof technique:** Reduce the constant-viscosity physical model to the normalized periodic Navier–Stokes equation, apply the current conditional two-dimensional global mathematical supplier, and invert the scaling.
items/cex-cm-dalembert-and-vakonomic-evolutions-differ.md:62:[F1] On the interval $[a,b]$ the maps $q\mapsto\Phi(q,\eta)$ are computed by differentiation under the integral sign on a compact rectangle ([[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle]]), and the resulting coefficients are continuous; integration by parts ([[thm-integration-by-parts]], [[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]], [[def-darboux-integral]], [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]) reduces the derivative to a linear combination of the coefficients, and the bumps of [[lem-cm-c2-bump-functions]] detect a nonzero continuous coefficient against an admissible variation.
items/thm-fd-expected-weak-reynolds.md:80:For vectors $a,b\in\mathbb R^d$, $(a\otimes b)_{ij}:=a_i b_j$. Here $A:B:=\sum_{i,j=1}^d A_{ij}B_{ij}$ and $|\cdot|_F$ is the Frobenius norm. If $U$ is assigned SI velocity units $\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, then $\bar u$ has units $\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and $R$ has units $\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-2}}$; the test field's units are chosen so both pairings have the same units. Here “weak” means tested against an essentially bounded tensor field. $R$ is the covariance tensor; no density factor, stress sign convention, incompressibility, Navier–Stokes equation, time-stationarity, or turbulence closure is assumed.
