items/thm-nrqm-magnetic-and-detection-conditional-stern-gerlach-force.md:105:[F7] Compactly supported integration by parts: if $u\in W^{1,2}(\mathbb R^3)$ is compactly supported and $v\in W^{1,2}(\mathbb R^3)$, then $\int u\,D_jv=-\int v\,D_ju$ ([[lem-sobolev-integration-by-parts-for-dual-exponents]], case $p=p'=2$). More generally a smooth compactly supported multiplier times a bounded Lipschitz function lies in $W^{1,2}$ with the Leibniz derivative ([[lem-weak-leibniz-rule-with-a-smooth-factor]]).
items/pthm-emx-sheet-charge-continuity.md:138:[F9] A compactly supported temporal test permits ordinary integration by parts on $I$ with no endpoint term. [[thm-integration-by-parts]]
items/pthm-emx-sheet-charge-continuity.md:150:1.2 Let $\psi\in C_c^\infty(U)$ and choose a compact interval $J\subset I$ containing its time support in its interior. The function $\Phi(x,t):=\sigma(x,t)\psi(x,t)$ is $C^1$ on the compact set $S\times J$. Its time difference quotients converge uniformly in $x$ on compact subintervals to $\partial_t\Phi$, by uniform continuity of $\partial_t\Phi$; since $S$ has finite surface measure, $G(t):=\int_S\Phi(x,t)\,dS(x)$ is $C^1$ and $G'(t)=\int_S((\partial_t\sigma)\psi+\sigma\partial_t\psi)\,dS$. Also $G$ vanishes near the endpoints of $J$. Using the surface-sheet pairing and one-dimensional integration by parts [F9], $ \langle\partial_t(\sigma\delta_S),\psi\rangle=-\int_J\int_S\sigma\,\partial_t\psi\,dS\,dt=\int_J\int_S(\partial_t\sigma)\psi\,dS\,dt. $ Hence $\partial_t(\sigma\delta_S)=(\partial_t\sigma)\delta_S$, with no transport term because $S$ is fixed. [F1, F2, F6, F9, F12, given, algebra]
items/pthm-emx-sheet-charge-continuity.md:152:1.3 For any locally integrable scalar or vector field $f$ that is $C^1$ on the two fixed sides of $S$ and has the stated joint one-sided $C^1$ extensions, its distributional time derivative is the regular distribution of its sidewise classical derivative. Indeed, on a compact spacetime cylinder, $f=\mathbf 1_{\Omega_-}f_-+\mathbf 1_{\Omega_+}f_+$ almost everywhere because $S$ is three-dimensionally null by [F8]. Pairing with a compactly supported test $\psi$, Fubini gives $-\sum_{\pm}\int_{\Omega_\pm}\int_J f_\pm(x,t)\partial_t\psi(x,t)\,dt\,d\lambda_3(x)$. For almost every fixed $x\in\Omega_\pm$, one-dimensional integration by parts in $t$ has no endpoint term because $\psi$ is compactly supported in $I$; the joint one-sided extensions make the sidewise derivatives locally bounded near $S$, and ordinary $C^1$ regularity gives local integrability away from $S$. Fubini therefore gives $\int_U\{\partial_t f\}\psi\,d\lambda_3dt$. For complex-valued tests the same calculation is applied to real and imaginary parts. The indicators of the two sides are independent of time, so no spatial-interface delta occurs. This applies componentwise to $D,B$, and $\rho_v$. [F8, F9, F12, given, algebra]
items/prop-fd-viscous-entropy-passage.md:74:2.1 Multiply by $\psi\geq0$ and integrate. By [F4], the integrals can be taken iteratively in time and each spatial coordinate. Applying [F3] to the smooth compactly supported products gives integration by parts twice in each spatial coordinate and once in time, with $\psi$ vanishing near $T$, and yields
items/thm-fd-x05.md:62:[F1] [[def-fd-x00]]: divergence, curl and componentwise Laplacian are defined by Cartesian partial derivatives; the constant-density Navier–Stokes equation is pointwise, with $\rho_0,\mu>0$ and $\nu=\mu/\rho_0$.
items/thm-fd-x05.md:72:2.2 Differentiation gives $\nabla p_v=\rho_0\kappa^2(x,y,0)/r^4$. This cancels $\rho_0(u_v\cdot\nabla)u_v$; together with $\Delta u_v=0$ and $\partial_tu_v=0$, the displayed Navier–Stokes expression vanishes for every $\nu>0$. [F1, A1, step 1.1, algebra]
items/thm-fd-x05.md:74:3.1 For the solid-rotation field, $\operatorname{div}u_s=0$, $\Delta u_s=0$, $(u_s\cdot\nabla)u_s=-\Omega_0^2(x,y,0)$, and $\nabla p_s=\rho_0\Omega_0^2(x,y,0)$, so its steady Navier–Stokes expression vanishes. Writing a streamline as $\gamma(s)=(x(s),y(s),z(s))$, its defining equation gives $\gamma'(s)=(-\Omega_0y(s),\Omega_0x(s),0)$ and therefore $\frac{d}{ds}(x(s)^2+y(s)^2)=2x(-\Omega_0y)+2y(\Omega_0x)=0$. Thus $r$ is fixed along the streamline and $B=p_0/\rho_0+\Omega_0^2r^2$ is constant there; because $\Omega_0\ne0$, distinct radii give distinct values. [F1, A1, algebra] ∎
