items/lem-em-dirichlet-resolvent-h-two-and-smooth-test-density.md:87:[F5] **Difference quotients.** $\delta_h^iu(x)=(u(x+he_i)-u(x))/h$ on $\Omega_{i,h}$ ([[def-first-difference-quotient]]). Discrete integration by parts holds ([[lem-difference-quotient-integration-by-parts]]); for $u\in H^1$ and a cutoff $\eta$, the class $v=-\delta_{-h}^i(\eta^2\delta_h^iu)$ is an admissible compactly supported $H_0^1$ test, and on the half-space with a tangential index $j<n$ it lies in $H_0^1(H)$ and is admissible for an $L^2$ datum ([[lem-tangential-difference-quotient-test-function]]). For $u\in W^{1,2}$ the increment bound $\|\delta_h^iu\|_{L^2(\Omega')}\le\|D_iu\|_{L^2(\Omega)}$ holds on $\Omega'\Subset\Omega$, and the converse characterizes $W^{1,p}$ by uniform increment bounds ([[thm-difference-quotient-characterisation-of-w-one-p-for-p-greater-than-one]]).
items/lem-em-dirichlet-resolvent-h-two-and-smooth-test-density.md:109:1.3 A localized flattened $U$ lies in $H_0^1$ of the half-space: multiplying the test functions approximating $u$ in $H_0^1(\Omega)$ by the patch cutoff and pulling back through the flattening gives smooth compactly supported functions on the open half-space converging in $H^1$ by [F4], so that the closure characterization of [F1] applies; their supports need not have a uniform distance from the boundary. Consequently tangential difference quotients and smooth cutoffs preserve this closure by [F5]. [given, F3, F4, F5]
items/lem-em-dirichlet-resolvent-h-two-and-smooth-test-density.md:115:3.1 Substituting $V$ into the local equation of step 1.2 and using the discrete integration by parts and the quotient product rule of [F5], with $S=F-cU\in L^2$, the principal term satisfies $\int\eta^2A_{ij}(y+he_k)\partial_jw_h\partial_iw_h\ge\alpha\|\eta\nabla w_h\|_2^2$ and the remaining principal terms are $\int\eta^2(\delta_hA_{ij})\partial_jU\partial_iw_h$ and $\int[A_{ij}(y+he_k)\partial_jw_h+(\delta_hA_{ij})\partial_jU](\partial_i\eta^2)w_h$, whose absolute sum is at most $C(\|U\|_{H^1}+\|w_h\|_2)\|\eta\nabla w_h\|_2+C\|U\|_{H^1}\|w_h\|_2$. [step 1.2, step 2.1, F4, F5, F10, algebra]
items/lem-em-dirichlet-resolvent-h-two-and-smooth-test-density.md:117:3.2 Unrestricted smooth $H^2$ approximants of $W$ are obtained from the half-space extension of [F7] followed by multiplication by a fixed compact cutoff equal to one on the support and by whole-space mollification [F8]; they converge in $H^2$, so the traces of the functions and of their first derivatives converge in $L^2$ by the flat trace estimate [F7]; for $\zeta\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^2)$ and tangential $k$, the classical boundary integration by parts for the smooth approximants gives $\int T(\partial_kW)\zeta=-\int TW\,\partial_k\zeta=0$, hence $T(\partial_kW)=0$ as a distribution and almost everywhere. [step 2.2, F7, F8, F9, algebra]
items/lem-em-dirichlet-resolvent-h-two-and-smooth-test-density.md:127:6.1 On a smaller patch where $\eta=1$, for every compactly supported smooth test $\psi$ the discrete integration by parts [F5] gives $\int\delta_h^k(\partial_jU)\psi=-\int\partial_jU\,\delta_{-h}^k\psi$, whose limit as $h\to0$ is $-\int\partial_jU\,\partial_k\psi$; the uniform bound of step 5.1 makes this a bounded linear functional of $\psi$ in the $L^2$ norm, so it extends continuously to the closed $L^2$ subspace generated by those tests, which is Hilbert by [F6], and the Riesz representation theorem [F6] gives an $L^2$ function representing $\partial_k\partial_jU$ distributionally with the same bound. [step 2.1, step 5.1, F5, F6, algebra]
items/pthm-em-guide-dispersion-energy.md:49:(a) [Cross-section identities] $\int_{R_{ab}}|\nabla_th_{mn}|^2=k_c^2\int_{R_{ab}}h_{mn}^2$ and $\int_{R_{ab}}|\nabla_te_{mn}|^2=k_c^2\int_{R_{ab}}e_{mn}^2$, with the boundary terms of the integration by parts vanishing;
items/pthm-em-guide-dispersion-energy.md:63:[F3] Cross-section identities and norms: for the profiles of [[lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities]], $\int_{R_{ab}}|\nabla_th_{mn}|^2=k_{mn}^2\int_{R_{ab}}h_{mn}^2$ and $\int_{R_{ab}}|\nabla_te_{mn}|^2=k_{mn}^2\int_{R_{ab}}e_{mn}^2$, with all boundary terms of the integration by parts vanishing ([[lem-em-rectangle-mode-profile-identities]] (d)); and the transverse identities $|e_z\times\nabla_th|=|\nabla_th|$, $\nabla_th\cdot(e_z\times\nabla_th)=0$ and $e_z\times(e_z\times\nabla_th)=-\nabla_th$ hold, together with their analogues for $e_{mn}$ ([[lem-em-transverse-vector-identities]] (a)). The additional cross identities follow from [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]: for $g=(g_1,g_2,0)$, $w=e_z\times g=(-g_2,g_1,0)$ gives $w\times g=-e_z(g_1^2+g_2^2)$, $g\times w=e_z|g|^2$ and $w\times e_z=g$.
