items/lem-qftx-wavefront-chart-invariance.md:73:[F1] A manifold distribution is a continuous bilinear-functional dual to compactly supported smooth densities; its chart representatives transform by the density Jacobian ([[def-qftx-distributions-on-smooth-manifolds]]).
items/lem-qftx-wavefront-chart-invariance.md:79:[F4] A compactly supported distribution has a smooth polynomially bounded localized Fourier transform with the cutoff-pairing formula in the stated $2\pi$ convention ([[thm-fourier-transform-of-a-compactly-supported-distribution-is-a-smooth-polynomially-bounded-multiplier]]).
items/lem-qftx-wavefront-chart-invariance.md:97:2.1 Suppose $(p,\xi_0)$ is regular for $u$ in the $x$ chart, witnessed by a cutoff $\chi_x$ and an open cone $\Gamma_x$. Let $y=\psi(x)$ be a smooth coordinate change, restrict to a compact patch about $p$, and put $\eta_0=(D\psi(p))^{-T}\xi_0$. Shrink the patch and choose nested cones $\Gamma_0\Subset\Gamma_1\Subset\Gamma_x$ around $\xi_0$ and a target cone $V$ around $\eta_0$ so that $D\psi(x)^TV\subset\Gamma_0$ throughout the patch. Choose a target cutoff $\chi_y$ equal to one near $p$ and supported where $\chi_x=1$, using [F8]. The target localized Fourier value is the source distribution paired with $a_\eta(x)=\chi_y(\psi(x))|\det D\psi(x)|e^{-2\pi i\psi(x)\cdot\eta}$; because $\chi_x=1$ on its support this is also the pairing with $\chi_xu$. Fourier inversion [F5], passed through this compactly supported distribution by [F6], expresses the value as $\int\widehat{\chi_xu}(\xi)\widehat{a_\eta}(-\xi)\,d\xi$. The phase in $\widehat{a_\eta}(-\xi)$ is $2\pi(x\cdot\xi-\psi(x)\cdot\eta)$, with gradient $2\pi(\xi-D\psi(x)^T\eta)$. In the near-stationary region $|\xi-D\psi(x)^T\eta|\le c(|\xi|+|\eta|)$, for sufficiently small $c>0$, cone separation gives $\xi\in\Gamma_x$ and $|\xi|\asymp|\eta|$; there $\widehat{\chi_xu}(\xi)$ decreases faster than every power, while $|\widehat{a_\eta}(-\xi)|\le\|a_\eta\|_1$ is uniformly bounded, and the region has only polynomial volume in $|\eta|$. On the complementary region the phase gradient is bounded below by $c(|\xi|+|\eta|)$; repeated integration by parts gives arbitrary powers of joint decay for $\widehat{a_\eta}(-\xi)$. The polynomial bound [F3] for $\widehat{\chi_xu}$ then makes this part rapidly decreasing after integration. This proves regularity in the target chart. [A1, F1, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7, F8, step 1.1, construct]
items/lem-qftx-wavefront-chart-invariance.md:101:3.2 Suppose $\chi_1$ is a regularity witness for $u$ on an open cone $\Gamma$, and let $\rho\in C_c^\infty$ have support where $\chi_1=1$. If $\Gamma'$ is an open cone whose closure of directions on the unit sphere is contained in $\Gamma$, then $\widehat{\rho u}$ is rapidly decreasing on $\Gamma'$. Indeed, $\rho u=\rho(\chi_1u)$ and Fourier inversion gives $\widehat{\rho u}=\widehat\rho*\widehat{\chi_1u}$; passing the Schwartz integral through the compactly supported distribution uses [F6]. Repeated integration by parts makes $\widehat\rho$ rapidly decreasing, and [F3] bounds $\widehat{\chi_1u}$ polynomially. Choose an intermediate cone with closure of directions between those of $\Gamma'$ and $\Gamma$. In the convolution, for frequencies $\eta$ in this intermediate cone with $|\eta|\ge|\xi|/2$, the second factor is rapidly decreasing at frequency comparable to $|\xi|$. If $|\eta|<|\xi|/2$, then $|\xi-\eta|>|\xi|/2$; for the remaining frequencies, $\eta$ lies outside the intermediate cone and $|\eta|\ge|\xi|/2$, so angular separation gives $|\xi-\eta|\ge c(|\xi|+|\eta|)$ for some $c>0$. In these latter regions the first factor is rapidly decreasing, while [F3] bounds the other factor polynomially. These estimates give the arbitrary-power bound for $|\xi|\ge1$ on $\Gamma'$, and [F4] gives boundedness on bounded sets. The convolution is absolutely integrable by the same estimates, and [F9] justifies its Fourier-integral identity. Applying this cutoff transfer to a common $\rho$ for witnesses for $u$ and $v$, and intersecting their smaller cones, gives rapid decay for $\widehat{\rho(u+v)}=\widehat{\rho u}+\widehat{\rho v}$. This proves $\operatorname{WF}(u+v)\subseteq\operatorname{WF}(u)\cup\operatorname{WF}(v)$. For a diffeomorphism, step 2.1 gives the cotangent image inclusion for pullback; applying the same argument to $\psi^{-1}$ gives the reverse inclusion. [A1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F8, F9, step 2.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-wavefront-chart-invariance.md:103:4.1 If $u$ is smooth, integration by parts in a compact chart cutoff gives rapid decay in every cone. Conversely, suppose $\operatorname{WF}(u)=\varnothing$ and fix a point $p$ in a chart. In dimension zero every distribution is locally a scalar function, so assume the chart dimension is at least one. Form the set of all open cones $\Gamma'$ for which there exist a regularity witness $(\chi,\Gamma)$ at $p$ with the closure of the directions of $\Gamma'$ on the unit sphere contained in $\Gamma$. This set covers the unit covector sphere: every direction has a witness by emptiness of the local wavefront set, and its open cone contains a smaller cone about that direction. Compactness gives a finite subcover $\Gamma'_1,\ldots,\Gamma'_m$. For each member of this finite family, take a corresponding witness $\chi_j,\Gamma_j$; finite choice suffices. Their witness cutoffs all equal one on a neighborhood of $p$. By [F8], choose one cutoff $\rho$ equal to one near $p$ and supported in the intersection of those neighborhoods. Step 3.2 shows that the single localization $\rho u$ has rapidly decreasing Fourier transform on each $\Gamma'_j$. Since these finitely many cones cover the unit sphere, $\widehat{\rho u}$ is rapidly decreasing in every direction: for each $N$, take the maximum of the finitely many cone bounds outside a bounded ball. Therefore every differentiated inverse Fourier integral is absolutely convergent and defines a smooth function. Fourier inversion [F5] and the pairing interchange [F6], applied after a cutoff equal to one near $\operatorname{supp}(\rho u)$, identify this function with the compactly supported distribution $\rho u$. Hence $u$ is smooth near $p$; as $p$ was arbitrary, it is smooth in every chart. [A1, F2, F5, F6, F8, step 3.2, algebra] ∎
items/thm-emg-einstein-gauge-propagation.md:73:3.1 Fix a point of $\Sigma_0$ and choose local coordinates $(s,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ with spacelike $s$-levels, $s=0$ on $\Sigma_0$, and $\partial_s$ normal to the slice at that point. Such coordinates follow by a linear change of the spatial coordinates by multiples of $s$ in any slice chart. At that point $g^{00}<0$ and $(g^{ij})>0$; these strict inequalities persist on a smaller cylinder. Set $P_b:=\partial_sH_b$ and $Q_{bi}:=\partial_iH_b$. The wave equation in [F1] has scalar principal part $g^{cd}\partial_c\partial_dH_b$; connection and Ricci terms are lower order. In the ordering $W=(H_b,P_b,Q_{bi})$, its first-order reduction consists of $\partial_sH_b-P_b=0$, the wave equation solved for $\partial_sP_b$, and the compatibility equations $\partial_sQ_{bi}-\partial_iP_b=0$. Multiplying the wave equation by $-1$ and the compatibility equations by $g^{ji}$ gives time matrix $A^0=\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,(g^{ij})I)$ and, for each spatial index $k$, the only nonzero spatial blocks $A^k_{PP}=-2g^{0k}I$, $A^k_{P Q_j}=-g^{kj}I$, and $A^k_{Q_iP}=-g^{ik}I$. Thus $A^0$ is positive definite on a sufficiently small cylinder with spacelike $s$-levels, and each $A^k$ is symmetric; all remaining coefficients and their first derivatives are bounded there by $g\in C^3$ and $H\in C^2$. Choose a compact coordinate ball about the chosen point and shrink the time interval so that the shrinking balls $B_s$ of radius $r-\lambda s$ remain in this cylinder. If $A^0\succeq aI$ and $M:=\sup_{|\nu|=1}\|\nu_iA^i\|$, choose $\lambda>M/a$. For $s\ge0$ set $\mathcal E(s)=\int_{B_s}W^{\mathsf T}A^0W\,dx$. The symmetric system's product rule and spatial integration by parts give $\mathcal E'(s)\le C\mathcal E(s)-\int_{\partial B_s}W^{\mathsf T}(\lambda A^0+\nu_iA^i)W\,dS$. The boundary matrix is positive semidefinite because $\lambda a>M$, so $\mathcal E'\le C\mathcal E$. The zero initial values established in steps 1.1 and 2.1 and Gronwall force $\mathcal E=0$ in the shrinking cone. Reversing time proves the same on the past cone. Thus $H=0$ in a neighborhood of the chosen point, and the point was arbitrary. [step 1.1, step 2.1, F1, F2, algebra]
items/lem-em-thin-current-limit.md:77:Here its scalar component distributions are $j_k(\varphi):=I\int_0^L\gamma_k'(s)\varphi(\gamma(s))ds$ for scalar tests $\varphi$, $k=1,2,3$; for a scalar test the notation $j(\varphi)$ below denotes the vector $(j_1(\varphi),j_2(\varphi),j_3(\varphi))$. In the vector-test formula the pairing is the complex-bilinear Euclidean pairing (no conjugation) of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]; restricted to real-valued $\psi$ this is the stated real functional. Then $\operatorname{div}j=0$ in the distributional sense ([[def-distributional-derivative]], [[def-distribution]]). For any family $(\eta_\varepsilon)_{\varepsilon>0}$ of nonnegative smooth radial kernels with $\int_{\mathbb R^3}\eta_\varepsilon=1$ and $\operatorname{supp}\eta_\varepsilon\subseteq\overline B_\varepsilon(0)$ — including the rescalings $\eta_\varepsilon(x)=\varepsilon^{-3}\eta(x/\varepsilon)$ of a chosen nonnegative unit-mass radial kernel supported in $\overline B_1(0)$ — the mollified fields $j_\varepsilon$ defined by the distribution pairing $j_\varepsilon(x):=j(\eta_\varepsilon(x-\cdot))=I\int_0^L\gamma'(s)\eta_\varepsilon(x-\gamma(s))\,ds$ are smooth, compactly supported and divergence-free, and their Biot-Savart fields converge, with every derivative uniformly on every nonempty compact $K\subseteq\mathbb R^3$ disjoint from $\gamma$, to
items/lem-em-thin-current-limit.md:144:4.3 Let $K$ be nonempty compact with $d=\operatorname{dist}(K,\gamma([0,L]))>0$ by [F11], let $0<\varepsilon<d/2$, and let $A_\varepsilon(x)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int\frac{j_\varepsilon(y)}{|x-y|}dy$ be the Newtonian potential of the smooth compactly supported $j_\varepsilon$; differentiating under the integral sign [F3] on $K$, where $|x-y|\ge d/2$ for $y\in\operatorname{supp}j_\varepsilon$ by step 3.1, gives the Biot-Savart field $B_\varepsilon(x)=\operatorname{curl}A_\varepsilon(x)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int\frac{j_\varepsilon(y)\times(x-y)}{|x-y|^3}dy$; substituting the formula of step 2.2 for $j_\varepsilon$, applying Fubini [F5] and translating $y=\gamma(s)+u$ with the measure-preserving change of variables [F5] yields $B_\varepsilon(x)=\frac{\mu_0I}{4\pi}\int_0^L\int_{\overline B_\varepsilon(0)}\frac{\gamma'(s)\times(x-\gamma(s)-u)}{|x-\gamma(s)-u|^3}\eta_\varepsilon(u)\,du\,ds$, all integrals absolutely convergent because $|x-\gamma(s)-u|\ge d-\varepsilon>0$. [step 2.2, step 3.1, F3, F5, F11, algebra]
items/thm-nrqmx-free-laplacian-form-domain.md:141:1.3 Conversely, suppose $u\in Q_0$ and $f\in H$ satisfy $q_0(u,v)=\langle f,v\rangle$ for every $v\in Q_0$. Testing with Schwartz $v$ and using Plancherel gives $\int(4\pi^2|\xi|^2\widehat u(\xi)-\widehat f(\xi))\overline{\widehat v(\xi)}\,d\xi=0$. The Fourier transform maps Schwartz space onto itself by [F5], and complex conjugation preserves Schwartz space, so the locally integrable function $4\pi^2|\xi|^2\widehat u-\widehat f$ pairs to zero with every compactly supported smooth test function. It is locally integrable because $\widehat u,\widehat f\in L^2$ and the polynomial weight is bounded on compact sets. Injectivity of the regular-distribution map [F7] gives $4\pi^2|\xi|^2\widehat u=\widehat f$ almost everywhere; hence $|\xi|^2\widehat u\in L^2$. By [F3], $u\in W^{2,2}=D(H_0)$, and [F1] then gives $H_0u=f$. Thus the form-associated operator domain and action are exactly those of $H_0$. [F1, F2, F3, F5, F7, given]
