items/thm-fd-compatible-wall-shear-heat.md:68:1.1 Let $\widetilde F$ be the stated smooth odd $2h$-periodic extension. Repeated integration by parts on the circle, with no boundary term because the integrands and derivatives are periodic, shows its Fourier coefficients decay faster than every inverse power of $n$. By [F2] and oddness, the sine coefficients are exactly $b_n$ and the sine series equals $F$ in $L^2(0,h)$; since both are continuous, the equality holds pointwise. Countable Choice [A1] is the Fourier-basis theorem's stated inherited assumption. [given, F2, A1, algebra]
items/thm-fd-compatible-wall-shear-heat.md:74:4.1 Let $u$ be this solution and $v$ any other smooth solution with the same data and mean-zero pressure; put $w=v-u$. Subtraction gives $w_t+v\cdot\nabla w+w\cdot\nabla u+\nabla(\pi_v-\pi_u)=\nu\Delta w$. Dot with $w$ and integrate on $\Omega$. Periodic side integrals cancel; the transport term is $\int v\cdot\nabla(|w|^2/2)=0$ because $\operatorname{div}v=0$ and $v\cdot n=0$ on the walls; pressure contributes $-\int(\pi_v-\pi_u)\operatorname{div}w=0$; integration by parts gives $-\nu\|\nabla w\|_2^2$ since $w=0$ on the walls. The remaining term is bounded by $\|\nabla u\|_\infty\|w\|_2^2$, so $z'= (\|w\|_2^2)'\le2\|\nabla u\|_\infty z$. Since $z(0)=0$, multiplying by $e^{-2\int_0^t\|\nabla u(s)\|_\infty ds}$ proves $z=0$. [given, step 3.1, F1, algebra]
