items/lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface.md:92:1.2 In an oriented coordinate plane, a compactly supported $C^1$ form $a\,dx+b\,dy$ satisfies $\int_{\mathbb R^2}(\partial_xb-\partial_ya)=0$, $\int_{y\geq0}(\partial_xb-\partial_ya)=\int_{\mathbb R}a(x,0)\,dx$, and $\int_{x,y\geq0}(\partial_xb-\partial_ya)=\int_0^\infty a(x,0)\,dx-\int_0^\infty b(0,y)\,dy$. For complex coefficients, apply the calculation to real and imaginary parts. Put the support in a rectangle; [F9] makes the coefficients and their first derivatives Riemann integrable, and [F10] makes them uniformly continuous. Double Riemann sums and iterated one-dimensional Riemann sums have the same limit, since their difference is bounded by the rectangle area times the modulus of continuity at the mesh diameter. Then [F7] evaluates the one-dimensional integrals of $\partial_xb$ and $\partial_ya$ at their endpoints, and the compact support removes the artificial outer edges. The half-plane and quadrant boundary signs are exactly the outward-normal-first convention [F6]. On the reentrant model, subtract the missing open quadrant from the plane; the two axis integrals have the opposite orientation, so the same identity holds. [F5, F6, F7, F9, F10, algebra]
