items/lem-csm-frontier-mft-small-action-bridge.md:71:1.1 Since $\rho$ is smooth and periodic, repeated integration by parts gives $(1+4\pi^2|k|^2)^m\widehat\rho(k)=\widehat{(1-\Delta)^m\rho}(k)$, so $|\widehat\rho(k)|\le C_m(1+|k|^2)^{-m}$ for every integer $m\ge1$. The number of lattice points with $\|k\|_\infty=r$ is at most $(2r+1)^d-(2r-1)^d\le C_dr^{d-1}$. Thus for every multi-index $\alpha$, choosing $m>(d+|\alpha|)/2$ makes $\sum_k(2\pi|k|)^{|\alpha|}|\widehat\rho(k)|$ finite, so the Fourier series and its differentiated series converge uniformly on compact time intervals including $s=0$. For $s\ge1$, the same shell bound and the Fourier formula in [F2] give $\|\partial^\alpha(\rho_s-\bar\rho)\|_\infty\le e^{-4\pi^2s}\sum_{k\ne0}(2\pi|k|)^{|\alpha|}|\widehat\rho(k)|e^{-4\pi^2(|k|^2-1)}\le C_{\alpha,\rho}e^{-4\pi^2s}$. The zero Fourier coefficient is $\widehat\rho(0)=\bar\rho$, proving the asserted smoothness and $C^\infty$ convergence. [A1, F2, algebra]
items/lem-csm-frontier-mft-small-action-bridge.md:77:4.1 Put $g_t=\partial_t\sigma_t^S$, which has zero mean. Define the smooth periodic potential $\phi_t$ by its Fourier coefficients $\widehat\phi_t(0)=0$ and $\widehat\phi_t(k)=\widehat g_t(k)/(4\pi^2|k|^2)$ for $k\ne0$, and set $\kappa_t^S=\nabla\phi_t$. Repeated integration by parts applied to the smooth $g_t$ gives rapid coefficient decay, so the series for $\phi_t$ and all its space and time derivatives converge uniformly. Termwise differentiation gives $\Delta\phi_t=-g_t$, hence $\partial_t\sigma^S+\nabla\cdot\kappa^S=0$. At $t=1$, $g_1=-\Delta\rho_S$, so $\phi_1=\rho_S-\bar\rho$ and $\kappa_1^S=\nabla\rho_S$. Flatness of $\zeta-1$ at $1$ makes every derivative of $\sigma^S$ agree there with the reversed heat segment; the fixed linear Fourier inverse gives the same matching for $\kappa^S$. [F2, step 3.1, construct, algebra]
items/lem-csm-frontier-mft-small-action-bridge.md:79:5.1 For $k\ne0$, the Fourier coefficient of $\nabla\phi_t$ has magnitude $|\widehat g_t(k)|/(2\pi|k|)$. Repeated integration by parts gives $|\widehat g_t(k)|\le C_m\|g_t\|_{C^{2m}}(1+|k|^2)^{-m}$; choosing $m$ with $2m>d-1$ makes $\sum_{k\ne0}(1+|k|^2)^{-m}/|k|$ finite by the lattice-shell bound in step 1.1, hence $\|\kappa_t^S\|_\infty\le C_m'\|g_t\|_{C^{2m}}$. The formula for $g_t$ and the $C^\infty$ decay in step 1.1, together with $\partial_s\rho_s=\Delta\rho_s$ from [F2], imply $\sup_{0\le t\le1}\|g_t\|_{C^{2m}}\to0$ and $\sup_{0\le t\le1}\|\nabla\sigma_t^S\|_\infty\to0$ as $S\to\infty$. By step 2.1, $\chi(\sigma_t^S)\ge\varepsilon(1-\varepsilon)$; since the space-time domain has measure one, [F1] bounds the connector action by $\frac{1}{4\varepsilon(1-\varepsilon)}\sup_{t,x}|\kappa_t^S+\nabla\sigma_t^S|^2$, which tends to zero. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 4.1, algebra] ∎
