items/lem-em-moving-flux.md:86:[F7] Parametric Stokes prerequisites, with no rank or injectivity hypothesis: [[lem-the-stokes-integrand-identity-on-a-c2-patch]] gives $\partial_uQ^*-\partial_vP^*=\langle\operatorname{curl}G\circ\varphi,\varphi_u\times\varphi_v\rangle$ for $P^*=\langle G\circ\varphi,\varphi_u\rangle$, $Q^*=\langle G\circ\varphi,\varphi_v\rangle$ on an open parameter neighbourhood. [[thm-greens-theorem-for-finite-unions-of-elementary-regions]] integrates this over any supplied finite elementary Green region, and [[lem-a-vector-line-integral-pulls-back-to-the-parameter-region]] identifies each resulting boundary arc integral with its image line integral.
items/lem-em-moving-flux.md:92:**Proof technique:** direct; differentiate the parametrized flux under the integral sign, express the derivative of the area vector by the determinant identity, convert the resulting curl term into a boundary circulation by Stokes, and identify each term with the corresponding surface integral.
items/lem-em-moving-flux.md:104:4.1 Parametric Stokes and conclusion. Fix a time $t$ of the nondegenerate $I$. The spatial slices of $v,B$ are $C^1$ on the open slice of $W\cap V$ containing $r(D,t)$; hence $G=(v\times B)(\cdot,t)$ is $C^1$ there. Restrict the $C^2$ extension of $\varphi=r(\cdot,t)$ to an open neighbourhood $O\supseteq D$ mapping into that slice. The first supplier in [F7] makes $P^*,Q^*$ $C^1$ on $O$ with the displayed curl integrand. Green then gives its integral over $D$ as the positive boundary integral, and the pullback supplier identifies each arc integral with $\int_{\varphi\circ\sigma_\ell}G\cdot d\mathbf r$. Thus $\int_D\langle\operatorname{curl}(v\times B)(r,t),w\rangle\,dp=\oint_{\partial S(t)}(v\times B)\cdot d\mathbf r$ without requiring a published regular patch. Substituting into steps 2.1 and 3.1 gives the always-defined parameter identity $\Phi'(t)=\int_D\langle\partial_tB(r,t)+v(r,t)\operatorname{div}B(r,t),w\rangle\,dp-\oint_{\partial S(t)}(v\times B)\cdot d\mathbf r$. At a rank-two slice its first two integrals are exactly the surface shorthand defined in the Statement (and, for an ordinary regular patch, [F8]); if $\operatorname{div}B=0$ the middle term vanishes. This proves the stated identity with the minus sign, including relative endpoint derivatives whenever the normal shorthand applies. [step 2.1, step 3.1, F7, F8, algebra] ∎
items/lem-em-moving-flux.md:108:- **Sign bookkeeping.** The boundary term appears with a minus sign because Stokes converts the flux of the curl into the circulation in the induced orientation; the additional term $(v\cdot n)\operatorname{div}B$ vanishes whenever the field is divergence-free. The example on the companion page checks both signs by direct computation.
items/lem-gr-periodic-fourier-differentiation-and-norm-equivalence.md:26:    - title: "E. Wiedemann (ed. J. Skipper), Lecture Notes: Navier-Stokes Equations (Universitat Ulm, WS 2018/19), Section 2.1 (Fourier characterisation of the Sobolev spaces H^s(T^3), the continuous embedding H^s(T^3) into L-infinity and the Banach-algebra property for s>3/2) and Section 4.3 (Agmon's inequality)"
items/lem-gr-periodic-fourier-differentiation-and-norm-equivalence.md:71:1.1 (One-variable periodic integration by parts.) For smooth $2\pi$-periodic $G,H$ and each $j$, $\int_{\mathbb T_{2\pi}}G\,\partial_jH\,d\mu_{\mathbb T}=-\int_{\mathbb T_{2\pi}}(\partial_jG)\,H\,d\mu_{\mathbb T}$: indeed $\int_0^{2\pi}\partial_j(GH)\,dx_j=(GH)|_0^{2\pi}=0$ by the second fundamental theorem of calculus and periodicity [L3], and the product rule gives the claim; the remaining coordinates integrate out by the product-measure convention. Consequently, for every smooth $\varphi$, every $k$ and every $\alpha$, $\int \chi_kD^\alpha\varphi\,d\mu=(-1)^{|\alpha|}(ik)^\alpha\int\chi_k\varphi\,d\mu$: induct on $|\alpha|$, moving one variable derivative from $\varphi$ onto $\chi_k$ by the one-variable identity and using $\partial_j\chi_k=ik_j\chi_k$. In particular $D^\alpha\chi_k=(ik)^\alpha\chi_k$ in the weak sense of [L2], the case $\varphi=\chi_{-l}$ tested against $\chi_k$ having Fourier coefficients $\widehat{\chi_k}(l)=\delta_{kl}$. [L2, L3, algebra]
items/thm-fd-surrogate-residual-error.md:41:    - title: "Carlo Morosi and Livio Pizzocchero, On approximate solutions of the incompressible Euler and Navier-Stokes equations"
