items/thm-fd-surrogate-residual-error.md:84:2.1 Define the real $L^2$ pairing by $\langle a,b\rangle_2=\int_{Q_d}a(x)\cdot b(x)\,d\mu(x)$ for $a,b\in L^2(Q_d;\mathbb R^d)$. Pair the resulting equation with $e$ in this pairing. By [F2], $Pe=e$ and $P$ is self-adjoint, so each projected transport term paired with $e$ equals its unprojected pairing. Periodicity gives $\int_{Q_d}\operatorname{div}_x(v|e|^2)\,d\mu=0$; since $\operatorname{div}_xv=0$, this yields $\langle(v\cdot\nabla_x)e,e\rangle_2=\frac12\int v\cdot\nabla_x|e|^2\,d\mu=0$. The Jacobian convention in the Statement gives $(e\cdot\nabla_x)u=(\nabla_xu)e$, so $|(e\cdot\nabla_x)u|\le\sup_x\|\nabla_xu\|_{\mathrm{op}}|e|$ pointwise and $|\langle(e\cdot\nabla_x)u,e\rangle_2|\le\sup_x\|\nabla_xu\|_{\mathrm{op}}\|e\|_2^2$. Periodic integration by parts gives $\langle-\nu\Delta e,e\rangle_2=\nu\sum_{i,j}\int_{Q_d}|\partial_je_i|^2\,d\mu\ge0$. Pointwise Euclidean Cauchy–Schwarz gives $|r_v\cdot e|\le|r_v||e|$; the real scalar functions $|r_v|$ and $|e|$ belong to $L^2(Q_d)$, so [F3] applied to them gives $|\langle r_v,e\rangle_2|\le\int_{Q_d}|r_v||e|\,d\mu\le\|r_v\|_2\|e\|_2$. Using $\langle\partial_t e,e\rangle_2=\frac12\frac{d}{dt}\|e\|_2^2$ now gives $\frac12\frac{d}{dt}\|e\|_2^2\le \sup_x\|\nabla_xu\|_{\mathrm{op}}\|e\|_2^2+\|r_v\|_2\|e\|_2$. [given, A1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]
items/thm-gr-killing-and-on-shell-matter-identities.md:125:[L4] The integral of the exterior derivative of a compactly supported smooth $3$-form on the oriented four-manifold $M$ vanishes; in particular $\int_M\nabla_aJ^a\,\mathrm{vol}_g=0$ for every smooth compactly supported vector field $J$, since $\nabla_aJ^a\mathrm{vol}_g=d(\iota_J\mathrm{vol}_g)$ and $\iota_J\mathrm{vol}_g$ has compact support ([[lem-finite-chart-localization-defines-choice-free-integration-and-compact-stokes]], [[thm-gr-density-divergence-and-finite-chain-flux]] clause (a), [[def-gr-metric-volume-form-and-current-flux]]).
items/thm-gr-killing-and-on-shell-matter-identities.md:127:[L5] If a continuous covector field $W$ on a chart domain satisfies $\int_UW_aq^aw\,d^4x=0$ for every smooth compactly supported vector field $q$ supported in the chart, then $W=0$; and the chart integral of a compactly supported divergence is zero ([[lem-gr-compact-variation-coefficient-vanishing]], [[def-gr-metric-volume-form-and-current-flux]]).
items/thm-nrqmx-bloch-projector-chern.md:96:[F4] [[lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface]] gives Stokes' theorem for a $C^1$ one-form on a compact oriented surface.
items/lem-emg-classical-charged-scalar-compact-variation.md:83:[F2] [[lem-finite-chart-localization-defines-choice-free-integration-and-compact-stokes]] supplies finitely many smooth chart cutoffs summing to one near a given compact set. Its smooth Stokes conclusion is not being applied to a merely $C^1$ field; the necessary classical integration by parts is proved below.
items/lem-emg-classical-charged-scalar-compact-variation.md:99:1.2 Let $W$ be any compactly supported $C^1$ vector field. Write $w=\sqrt{|\det g|}$. The Christoffel formula and determinant derivative give $\Gamma^a{}_{ab}=\partial_b\log w$, hence $\nabla_aW^a\mathrm{vol}_g=\partial_a(wW^a)d^4x$ in a positive chart. For each finite cutoff $\chi_i$ in [F2], $w\chi_iW^a$ extends by zero to a $C^1$ compactly supported coefficient on a bounding rectangle. Its partial derivatives and all sections are continuous, so [F3]–[F5] integrate its coordinate divergence to zero: the opposite-face values vanish. Summing over $i$ gives the same result for $W$, because $\sum_i\chi_i=1$ and $\sum_i d\chi_i=0$ near its support. Therefore $\mathcal I_M((\nabla_aW^a)\mathrm{vol}_g)=0$ for this $C^1$ field. No smoothness approximation or unproved rough Stokes assertion is used. [given, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, algebra]
items/thm-cm-smooth-string-fourier-series-solution.md:99:[F9] For $C^2$ data, integration by parts in $x$ on $[0,L]$ gives $\int_0^Lv_xv_{xt}dx=[v_xv_t]_0^L-\int_0^Lv_{xx}v_tdx=-\int_0^Lv_{xx}v_tdx$ when $v_t(t,0)=v_t(t,L)=0$, using [F8] to identify $v_{xt}=\partial_xv_t$ ([[thm-integration-by-parts]], [[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[def-oriented-integral]], [[thm-additivity-over-subintervals]], [[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]], [[def-derivative]]).
items/thm-fd-global-two-dimensional-periodic-navier-stokes.md:4:title: "Global smooth two-dimensional periodic Navier–Stokes"
items/thm-fd-global-two-dimensional-periodic-navier-stokes.md:58:This is a theorem for positive viscosity on the two-dimensional periodic domain; it asserts no global regularity for three-dimensional Navier–Stokes or for inviscid Euler.
items/thm-fd-global-two-dimensional-periodic-navier-stokes.md:102:1.3 Fix an integer $m\ge4$ and put $Y(t)=\|u(t)\|_{H^m}$ and $S(t)=\sum_{k\ne0}|k||\widehat u(t,k)|$. Pair the full equation after applying $\Lambda^m$ with $\Lambda^m u$. The pressure term vanishes because $\operatorname{div}\Lambda^mu=0$ and periodic integration by parts applies. Writing $\Lambda^m(u\cdot\nabla u)=u\cdot\nabla\Lambda^m u+[\Lambda^m,u\cdot\nabla]u$, the first term has integral $\frac12\int u\cdot\nabla|\Lambda^mu|^2d\mu=0$ by incompressibility and periodicity. The viscous term is $-\nu\|\nabla u\|_{H^m}^2\le0$, while [F4] and Cauchy–Schwarz bound the remaining commutator pairing by $C_mSY^2$. Hence $\frac12(Y^2)'\le C_mSY^2$. For $Y_\epsilon=(Y^2+\epsilon^2)^{1/2}$ with $\epsilon>0$, differentiation gives $Y_\epsilon'\le C_mSY^2/Y_\epsilon\le C_mSY_\epsilon$. [given, F4, F6, algebra]
