items/ex-fd-stokes-gradient-forcing-gauge.md:68:[F2] [[thm-na-weak-stokes-solvability-lipschitz-domain]]: every bounded load in $V'$ has a unique weak Stokes pair with its pressure in $Q$.
items/ex-fd-stokes-gradient-forcing-gauge.md:82:2.1 For $v\in C_c^\infty(D;\mathbb R^d)$, weak integration by parts gives $\int_D\nabla\phi\cdot v\,dx=-\int_D\phi\,\operatorname{div}v\,dx$. By the $H^1$-closure in [F1], approximate any $v\in V$ by such fields; both integrals converge by Cauchy–Schwarz, so the identity holds for all $v\in V$. Pointwise $|\operatorname{div}v|=|\operatorname{tr}\nabla v|\le\sqrt d\,|\nabla v|_F$, whence $|F_\phi(v)|\le\sqrt d\,\|\phi\|_2\|\nabla v\|_2$ and $F_\phi\in V'$. [F1, F6, step 1.1, algebra]
items/ex-fd-stokes-gradient-forcing-gauge.md:84:3.1 For every $v\in V$, $a_\nu(0,v)+b(v,\phi)=-\int_D\phi\,\operatorname{div}v\,dx=F_\phi(v)$ by step 2.1, while $b(0,q)=0$ for every $q\in Q$. Thus $(0,\phi)$ satisfies both weak Stokes equations. Uniqueness in [F2] identifies it with the solution, so the pressure notation in [F3] gives $p_{F_\phi}=\phi$. [given, A1, F1, F2, F3, step 2.1]
