items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-stationary-dynamical-sector-identification.md:153:2.3 [The angular normalization, with its derivation] The explicit associated-polynomial harmonics are restrictions of homogeneous harmonic polynomials of degree $l$: substitute their Rodrigues formulas into $r^lY_{lm}(x/r)$ and the Cartesian Laplacian to check polynomiality and harmonicity. This space has dimension $2l+1$. Indeed the Laplacian from degree $l$ polynomials onto degree $l-2$ polynomials is surjective: twice integrating a target polynomial in $x_3$ gives an operator $J$ with $\partial_3^2Jq=q$, and the finite sum $\sum_n(-J(\partial_1^2+\partial_2^2))^nJq$ cancels the other two second derivatives; the sum terminates because their degree in $x_1,x_2$ drops. Subtract the two polynomial-space dimensions. The $2l+1$ orthonormal $Y_{lm}$ therefore span this space, which is invariant under orthogonal rotations. Its projection kernel $K_l(\omega,d)=\sum_mY_{lm}(\omega)\overline{Y_{lm}(d)}$ is rotation invariant. At the north pole only $m=0$ survives, with $Y_{l0}=\sqrt{(2l+1)/(4\pi)}P_l$ and $P_l(1)=1$; hence $K_l(\omega,d)=(2l+1)P_l(\omega\cdot d)/(4\pi)$ after rotation. Define $j_l(z)=z^l\int_{-1}^1e^{izt}(1-t^2)^ldt/(2^{l+1}l!)$. Integrating Rodrigues' formula by parts $l$ times gives $\int_{-1}^1e^{izt}P_l(t)dt=2i^lj_l(z)$. Endpoint integration by parts in the polynomial $P_l$ gives $j_l(z)=\sin(z-l\pi/2)/z+O(z^{-2})$; the power series at zero gives $j_l(z)\sim z^l/(2l+1)!!$. Differentiating the integral and integrating its $t$ derivative verifies $z^2j_l''+2zj_l'+(z^2-l(l+1))j_l=0$. Project the zonal exponential at the north pole onto degree $l$: integration in the azimuth kills $m\ne0$ and the displayed Legendre integral fixes its $m=0$ coefficient. Rotate the result and use $K_l$ to obtain $\int_{S^2}e^{iz\omega\cdot d}Y_{lm}(d)d\sigma(d)=4\pi i^lj_l(z)Y_{lm}(\omega)$. Thus its factor, phase and origin/free asymptotics have all been computed. Angular channel projections commute with the radial Hamiltonian: on smooth compact spatial tests this follows from the polar Laplacian and this finite harmonic projection; Taylor expansion at the origin ensures the projected tests remain smooth there. The projections preserve the $H^2$ domain by density and commutation with the free graph norm, so the regular radial reduction is legitimate. [F3, step 1.1, algebra]
items/pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell.md:84:[L7] One-coordinate integration by parts holds for $C^1$ line restrictions on compact intervals ([[thm-integration-by-parts]]); their continuous products have the same Riemann and Lebesgue integrals ([[thm-bounded-riemann-integrable-functions-are-lebesgue-measurable-and-have-the-same-integral]]). Fubini applies to the resulting compactly supported integrable products ([[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]); smooth partitions localize tests to rectangular boxes compactly contained in $U$ ([[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]]).
items/pthm-em-macroscopic-maxwell.md:98:3.1 Suppose $\partial_t\rho$ and $\partial_{x_i}J_i$ exist and are continuous on $U$. For a continuous scalar $f$ with a continuous derivative in just one coordinate $q$, localize any test to rectangular boxes by [L7]. On each box, integration by parts on the $q$-lines has zero endpoint terms because the test vanishes there; Fubini then gives $-\int f\partial_q\psi=\int(\partial_q f)\psi$. Summing the localized tests proves $\partial_q u_f=u_{\partial_q f}$ without assuming derivatives in other coordinates. Apply this to $(f,q)=(\rho,t)$ and $(J_i,x_i)$: the zero distribution of step 1.2 is the regular distribution of the continuous sum $\partial_t\rho+\sum_i\partial_{x_i}J_i$, which vanishes pointwise by [L5]. Since $\rho_b,J_b$ are $C^1$, the same argument applies to $\rho_f,J_f$ and gives their classical continuity equation. Steps 1.1, 1.3 and 2.1 establish the remaining claims and their stated model-conditional scope. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 2.1, L1, L5, L7] ∎
items/lem-tgr-photon-box-riemann-limits.md:62:1.1 For a continuous compactly supported $h:\mathbb R^3\to\mathbb R$, the cubes $Q_{n,d}=dn+[-d/2,d/2]^3$ tile $\mathbb R^3$ up to boundaries of volume zero. Uniform continuity of $h$ bounds $|d^3\sum_nh(dn)-\int h|$ by the volume of one fixed enlarged support box times the modulus of continuity at $\sqrt3d/2$, which tends to zero. Taking $h(x)=\psi(|x|)$ proves the compactly supported radial lattice limit. To rewrite its integral when $\psi$ is signed, apply [F2] separately to the nonnegative radial functions $h_+(x)=\max(h(x),0)$ and $h_-(x)=\max(-h(x),0)$; both are continuous and compactly supported, so their integrals are finite. Subtracting the resulting identities and using $\sigma(S^2)=4\pi$ from [F3] gives $\int_{\mathbb R^3}h(x)\,d^3x=4\pi\int_0^\infty r^2\psi(r)\,dr$. Omitting $n=0$ changes the sum by $d^3\psi(0)\to0$. [given, F2, F3, F4, algebra]
