items/pthm-nrqm-scattering-stationary-dynamical-identification.md:123:[F1] **Stationary and dynamical sector identification.** Assume Countable Choice. For real bounded compactly supported $V_{0}$, with $H_{0}=-\Delta$, $H=H_{0}+M_{V_{0}}$ and $S=\Omega_{+}^{*}\Omega_{-}$ its complete scattering operator, the free trace transform $F_{0}g(E,\omega)=E^{1/4}2^{-1/2}\widehat g(\sqrt E\omega)$ is unitary onto $L^{2}((0,\infty),dE;\mathfrak h)$, $F_{0}SF_{0}^{*}=M_{S(E)}$ with $S(E)=I+\frac{ik}{2\pi}\mathcal A_{k}$ unitary, $\mathcal A_{k}$ built from the stationary amplitude, $S[V_{R}](E)=U_{R}S[V](E)U_{R}^{-1}$, and for radial $V_{0}$ the fiber is diagonal with $S_{l}(E)=\overline{W_{l}}/W_{l}$, $|S_{l}|=1$ ([[thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-stationary-dynamical-sector-identification]] clauses (a), (b), (c), (d)).
items/thm-nrqmx-periodic-flux-spectrum.md:122:1.1 Set $A_0=-\kappa\partial_\phi^2$ on $H^2_{\mathrm{per}}(S^1)$. In the boundary coordinates $\Gamma_0f=(f(0),f(2\pi))$ and $\Gamma_1f=(f'(0),-f'(2\pi))$, the unitary swap matrix in [F1] makes the boundary equation equivalent to $f(0)=f(2\pi)$ and $f'(0)=f'(2\pi)$: adding and subtracting its two scalar equations gives these two equalities. Hence $-\partial_\phi^2$ is self-adjoint on this domain, and multiplication by the positive real $\kappa$ shows that $A_0$ is self-adjoint. Let $P=-i\partial_\phi$. Integration by parts [F5] and equality of the endpoint traces show that $P$ is symmetric on $H^2_{\mathrm{per}}$. For $f=\sum_nc_ne_n$ in this domain, integration by parts gives $\langle Pf,e_n\rangle=nc_n$ and $\langle A_0f,e_n\rangle=\kappa n^2c_n$. Parseval for [F2] therefore implies $\|Pf\|_2^2=\sum_n n^2|c_n|^2$ and $\|A_0f\|_2^2=\kappa^2\sum_n n^4|c_n|^2$. [A1, F1, F2, F5, construct]
items/def-qftx-off-shell-local-polynomial-functionals.md:51:The finite polynomial expansion makes this a symmetric multilinear form. Repeated integration by parts in each $h_j$ writes it as a compactly supported distribution kernel on the thin diagonal $\{(x,\ldots,x):x\in\mathbb M\}\subset\mathbb M^r$. Integrals use the fixed Minkowski density $d^4x$; all kernels are paired bilinearly with compactly supported smooth tests.
