items/pthm-fd-bounded-weak-continuation.md:52:Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb R^3$ be any nonempty bounded open set in Cartesian coordinates measured in metres. Define $D_\sigma(\Omega)=\{a\in C_c^\infty(\Omega;\mathbb R^3):\operatorname{div}a=0\}$, let $H$ be its $L^2(\Omega)$ closure, and let $V$ be its closure in $H_0^1(\Omega;\mathbb R^3)$, where $H_0^1$ is the $H^1$ closure of compactly supported smooth fields and $H^1$ has the norm formed from the $L^2$ norms of a field and its first weak derivatives. Let $\rho_0>0$ in $\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, $\mu>0$ in $\mathrm{Pa\,s}$, $\nu=\mu/\rho_0$, and $u_0\in H$ be an initial velocity in $\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Then the adopted unforced no-slip model has
items/pthm-fd-bounded-weak-continuation.md:76:[Berselli and Spirito, *On the Existence of Leray–Hopf Weak Solutions to the Navier–Stokes Equations*, Theorem 3, §5.2, printed pp. 14–16](https://www.mdpi.com/2311-5521/6/1/42/pdf)
items/pthm-fd-bounded-weak-continuation.md:98:1.1 For smooth fields, $\operatorname{div}(u\otimes u)=(u\cdot\nabla)u$ because $\operatorname{div}u=0$, and $\operatorname{div}(2\mu D(u))=\mu\Delta u$. In the weak form, $-pI_3:\nabla\phi=-p\operatorname{div}\phi=0$. Also $2D(u):\nabla\phi=\nabla u:\nabla\phi+\partial_j u_i\partial_i\phi_j$. For compactly supported smooth solenoidal $u,\phi$, integration by parts makes the integral of the cross term $-\int u_i\partial_i(\operatorname{div}\phi)=0$; density of those fields in $V$ and continuity of the bilinear forms extend this equality to $u,\phi\in V$. Thus the weak stress balance reduces to the identity in [F2] with $\nu=\mu/\rho_0>0$. [given, A1, F1, F2, algebra]
items/thm-tgr-constrained-entropy-tov-criticality.md:101:1.1 For a smooth compactly supported variation $(\dot\epsilon,\dot n)$ in $(0,R)$, differentiating the mass definition and $b=1-2GM/(c^2r)$ gives $$\dot E=4\pi\int_0^R\dot\epsilon r^2dr,\quad \dot b(r)=-\frac{2K}{r}\int_0^r t^2\dot\epsilon(t)dt,\quad \frac{d}{d\lambda}\bigg|_0 b_\lambda(r)^{-1/2} =\frac{K}{r}b(r)^{-3/2}\int_0^r t^2\dot\epsilon(t)dt.$$ The compact-range and positive-$b$ hypotheses give uniform differentiability under the integrals by [F6]. Small multiples of these variations remain admissible because the profile range is compactly contained in the open chart and $b\ge b_*>0$. [given, F1, F6, F7, F8, algebra]
items/thm-tgr-constrained-entropy-tov-criticality.md:109:3.2 Put $q=b^{-1/2}$ and $\mathcal L=S+\alpha N_b-\beta E$. Substituting the variations from steps 1.1 and 2.1 and integrating by parts in the single radial variable gives $$\dot{\mathcal L} =4\pi\int_0^Rr^2\left[ q\left(\alpha-\frac{\mu}{\Theta}\right)\dot n+ \left\{\frac q\Theta+ K\int_r^R\frac{t(s+\alpha n)}{b(t)^{3/2}}dt-\beta\right\}\dot\epsilon \right]dr.$$ For the nonlocal term, set $Q(r)=\int_0^r t^2\dot\epsilon(t)dt$ and $H(r)=K\int_r^R t(s+\alpha n)b^{-3/2}dt$. By [F8]–[F9], $Q,H$ are differentiable with $Q'(r)=r^2\dot\epsilon(r)$ and $H'(r)=-Kr(s+\alpha n)b^{-3/2}$. Also $H(R)=Q(0)=0$, so integration by parts gives $K\int_0^Rr(s+\alpha n)b^{-3/2}Q(r)dr=\int_0^Rr^2H(r)\dot\epsilon(r)dr$. Thus the displayed formula has no omitted boundary term. [F1, F2, F6, F8, F9, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]
items/lem-nrqmx-cubic-moyal-commutator-remainder.md:100:[F7] Complex integration by parts on a line is valid when both derivative products are integrable and the endpoint product vanishes. Applied coordinatewise with Fubini, it applies below because the Fourier factors are Schwartz and the differentiated compactly supported symbols have vanishing boundary products ([[lem-complex-integration-by-parts-on-intervals-and-decaying-lines]]).
items/lem-nrqmx-cubic-moyal-commutator-remainder.md:108:1.1 Since $a$ is compactly supported and smooth, Fourier inversion in its $\xi$ variable identifies $K_{\epsilon,a}((x+y)/2,x-y)$ with the kernel of $\operatorname{Op}^W_\epsilon(a)$. Its derivatives have rapid decay in $x-y$, uniformly on compact sets of $(x+y)/2$, by [F3]. To justify the potential term on Schwartz vectors, fix $x\in\mathbb R^d$ and put $g(s)=V(sx)$ on $[0,1]$. By [F6] applied twice and Fubini [F4], $$ V(x)=V(0)+\nabla V(0)\cdot x+\int_0^1(1-s)\,x^T D^2V(sx)x\,ds. $$ Thus boundedness of $D^2V$ gives $|V(x)|\le C(1+|x|^2)$. Applying [F6] once to each component of $\nabla V(sx)$ also gives $|\nabla V(x)|\le C(1+|x|)$, while all derivatives of $V$ of order at least $2$ are bounded by hypothesis. Hence every derivative of $V$ has at most polynomial growth. The product rule [F5] and Schwartz seminorm bounds show $Vu\in\mathcal S$ whenever $u\in\mathcal S$; differentiation also preserves $\mathcal S$. Therefore $H_\epsilon^0$ and the commutator in the Statement are well defined on $\mathcal S$. [given, A1, F3, F4, F5, F6, algebra]
items/lem-nrqmx-cubic-moyal-commutator-remainder.md:115:4.1 The kernel $K_{\epsilon,a}(q,r)$ is compactly supported in $q$ and Schwartz in $r$, since $a\in C_c^\infty$. The formula derived in step 3.1, applied also to each $\partial_q^\alpha V$, and the bounded derivatives of $V$ show that $\Delta_V(q,r)$ and all its $q,r$ derivatives grow at most polynomially in $r$, uniformly for $q$ in that compact set. Hence $G(q,r):=\Delta_V(q,r)K_{\epsilon,a}(q,r)$ is Schwartz in $(q,r)$; its partial Fourier transform is Schwartz by [F3], and Fourier inversion [F3] recovers $G$. Define the remainder symbol by the inverse partial Fourier transform $$ r_{\epsilon,a,V}(q,\eta):=\frac{i}{\epsilon^3}\int e^{-ir\cdot\eta/\epsilon}\Delta_V(q,r)K_{\epsilon,a}(q,r)\,dr. $$ By construction its Weyl kernel is $i\epsilon^{-3}\Delta_V(q,r)K_{\epsilon,a}(q,r)$, so the remaining potential commutator kernel, after multiplication by $i/\epsilon$, is $\epsilon^2$ times that kernel. Apply [F8] to the positive scaling $s\mapsto r=\epsilon s$ in this absolutely integrable complex Fourier integral. This gives $$ r_{\epsilon,a,V}(q,\eta)=i(2\pi)^{-d}\int e^{-is\cdot\eta}\frac{\Delta_V(q,\epsilon s)}{\epsilon^3}\left(\int e^{is\cdot\xi}a(q,\xi)\,d\xi\right)ds. $$ For $|\alpha|\le N_d$, the formula from step 3.1 applied to $\partial_q^\alpha V$ gives $|\partial_q^\alpha\Delta_V(q,\epsilon s)|\le C_{\alpha,V}\epsilon^3|s|^3$, since every third derivative of $\partial_q^\alpha V$ is bounded by hypothesis. Derivatives in $\eta$ insert powers of $s$; derivatives in $q$ of the partial Fourier transform insert derivatives of $a$. Repeated coordinatewise integration by parts in $\xi$, justified by [F7], gives decay factor $(1+|s|^2)^{-4d-5}$ uniformly in $q$, with zero boundary terms because $a$ is compactly supported in $\xi$. For $|\alpha|\le N_d$, the largest remaining radial power is $|s|^{N_d+3}=|s|^{4d+7}$; after the $d$-dimensional radial measure, the tail is bounded by a constant times $\int_1^\infty\rho^{-3d-4}d\rho<\infty$. More explicitly, bounding all $q,\eta$ derivatives of total order at most $N_d$ uses $(1-\Delta_\xi)^{4d+5}$ on $a$, hence at most $N_d+8d+10=12d+14$ derivatives, within $M_d=12d+16$. This proves the stated uniform derivative bound. [step 3.1, given, A1, F3, F6, F7, F8, algebra]
items/prop-fd-entropy-jump.md:68:[F3] [[thm-fd-rankine-hugoniot]] proves the interface distribution calculation from the distributional derivative definition and one-dimensional integration by parts in a normal coordinate.
items/lem-qft-example-oscillator-feynman-prescription.md:64:Use the complex Schwartz space of [[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]], with topology generated by $p_{jk}(\varphi)=\sup_x|x^j\varphi^{(k)}(x)|$ for integers $j,k\ge0$: a neighborhood of zero restricts finitely many such seminorms. A tempered distribution is a continuous complex-linear functional $u:\mathcal S(\mathbb R)\to\mathbb C$, with bilinear pairing $\langle u,\varphi\rangle=u(\varphi)$ (no conjugation). A bounded kernel $f$ acts by $\langle f,\varphi\rangle=\int f\varphi$. Define $\langle\partial_t^k u,\varphi\rangle=(-1)^k\langle u,\varphi^{(k)}\rangle$ and $\delta_a(\varphi)=\varphi(a)$. These derivatives also restrict to compactly supported smooth tests. Convergence of distributions here means convergence against every Schwartz test, the weak topology $\sigma(\mathcal S',\mathcal S)$. For $u\in\mathcal S'$ define its positive-sign Fourier transform by $\langle\mathcal T_+u,\varphi\rangle=\langle u,\mathcal T_+\varphi\rangle$, where $\mathcal T_+\varphi(t)=\int e^{i\nu t}\varphi(\nu)\,d\nu$. Both kernels have units of seconds, so the differential identities have units of inverse seconds. In particular, $C_F(t)\ne0$ for $t<0$ while $G_R(t)=0$ there.
items/lem-nrqm-coulomb-three-dimensional-hardy-bound.md:61:[F1] Divergence theorem and its vector forms: for a bounded domain with a finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation and a $C^1$ vector field $F$ on a neighbourhood of its closure, the integral of $\operatorname{div}F$ equals the outward flux; in particular, if $F$ is $C^1$ on $\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\}$ and compactly supported there, then $\int_{\mathbb R^3}\operatorname{div}F=0$ ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]], [[cor-vector-forms-of-the-divergence-theorem]]).
items/pthm-em-open-rectangular-guide-completeness.md:91:1.1 [Complete mixed transform] With the characters of [F2], first form for each component the transverse coefficients $F_{mn}(z)=\int_{T_{ab}^2}F(x_t,z)\overline{\phi_{mn}(x_t)}dx_t$ and then $\widehat F_{mn}(\xi)=\mathcal F_2F_{mn}(\xi)$ for the exact $2\pi$-convention transform of [F3]; write $\beta=2\pi\xi$. Then the map $F\mapsto(\widehat F_{mn})_{(m,n)\in\mathbb Z^2}$ is a unitary isomorphism of $L^2(T_{ab}^2\times\mathbb R)$ onto $\ell^2(\mathbb Z^2;L^2(\mathbb R))$ and, for the reflection fields of the Statement, $\|F\|^2_{L^2(Q)}=\frac14\sum_{m,n}\int_{\mathbb R}|\widehat F_{mn}(\xi)|^2d\xi$. Indeed the characters are orthonormal on the fundamental rectangle and, being products of the complete one-dimensional character systems, their finite span is dense in the transverse $L^2$ of each torus fibre by [F2]; Fubini–Tonelli therefore gives, for almost every $z$, the transverse expansion together with $\int_{T_{ab}^2}|F(x_t,z)|^2dx_t=\sum_{m,n}|F_{mn}(z)|^2$ and, after integration in $z$, $\|F\|^2_{L^2(T_{ab}^2\times\mathbb R)}=\sum_{m,n}\|F_{mn}\|^2_{L^2(\mathbb R)}$. Applying the unitary Plancherel transform [F3] to each of the countably many scalar functions $F_{mn}\in L^2(\mathbb R)$ gives the coefficient map and the isometry on the torus product, with inverse from the $L^2$ inversion theorem of [F3]; the reflection extension doubles each transverse period, so the torus fundamental rectangle has $4ab$-area and the physical norm is one quarter of the extended norm, which is the displayed factor $\frac14$. The parity relations follow from $\phi_{-m,-n}=\overline{\phi_{mn}}$ and the transverse parities by the coordinate changes $x_j\mapsto-x_j$ on the torus, and the reality relation $\widehat F_{-m,-n}(-\xi)=\overline{\widehat F_{mn}(\xi)}$ holds first for real integrable compactly supported longitudinal data by the defining integral and passes to $L^2$ by density and continuity of the transform [F3]. No pointwise reconstruction is asserted. [F2, F3, algebra]
items/pthm-em-open-rectangular-guide-completeness.md:93:2.1 [Divergence constraints in coefficient form] For $K:=(\pi m/a,\pi n/b,2\pi\xi)$ the distributionally zero divergence of the extended field is equivalent to $K\cdot\widehat E=K\cdot\widehat B=0$ for almost every $\xi$, and the finite-energy reflection class is exactly the class of coefficient families satisfying these almost-everywhere constraints together with the parities and $L^2$ summability. The derivative correspondence is proved on the dense test class of finite transverse-character sums times longitudinal Schwartz functions: for such a test $\psi$, finite transverse integration by parts and the exact identity $\mathcal F_2(\partial_z\psi)=2\pi i\xi\widehat\psi$ of [F3] turn the pairing $\int F\cdot\nabla\psi$ into the sum of the coefficient pairings against $iK\cdot\widehat F$, and the compactly supported smooth tests used in the definition are obtained by multiplying with cutoffs $\chi_R$ of [F3] and passing to the limit, since the tests and their first derivatives converge in $L^2$ and all pairings are bounded by Cauchy–Schwarz [F6]; conversely a test with $K\cdot\widehat F=0$ almost everywhere has zero weak divergence pairing, and the general compact smooth tests are reached through the transverse $L^2$ expansions of their derivatives and the Schwartz transform isomorphism [F3]. Hence the exact constrained class corresponds to the stated almost-everywhere coefficient constraints, not merely to a scalar basis without Gauss equations; for fields smooth in the interior the divergences vanish pointwise, and if their reflected extensions are continuous across the walls then the four-wall PEC conditions hold by [[def-em-guide-cavity-geometry]](c). [step 1.1, F2, F3, F6]
items/ex-emx-topological-flux-sector.md:130:4.1 Suppose a smooth divergence-free magnetic field on $\mathbb R^3$ agreed with $B$ on $S_a^2$. Its spatial 2-form $\widetilde\beta=\widetilde B_1dx^2\wedge dx^3+\widetilde B_2dx^3\wedge dx^1+\widetilde B_3dx^1\wedge dx^2$ would be smooth and closed by [F4], and would agree with $C\beta$ on $S_a^2$. Since $\mathbb R^3$ is star-shaped, [F10] gives $\widetilde\beta=dA$ for a global smooth 1-form $A$. Stokes and $\partial z=0$ from step 1.2 then give $\int_z\widetilde\beta=\int_{\partial z}A=0$, while step 2.1 gives $\int_z\widetilde\beta=4\pi C=\Phi\ne0$. This contradiction excludes a smooth divergence-free full-space extension; it does not turn the exterior field into a magnetic-charge source law. [A1, F4, F10, step 1.2, step 2.1] ∎
items/thm-qftx-wavefront-product.md:117:1.1 In a pair of coordinate charts, localize compactly supported $u$ and $v$. By [F5], the localized tensor transform factors as $\widehat{u\otimes v}(\xi,\zeta)=\widehat u(\xi)\widehat v(\zeta)$. If both covectors are regular, each factor decreases faster than every power on a conic neighborhood; if only one is singular, rapid decay in the regular block controls the other polynomial factor whenever that regular block has nonzero central covector. On a sufficiently small cone about such a full covector, the nonzero block frequency is comparable to the total frequency, so its decay can be taken above every polynomial order and the fixed dimension. The cases with one central covector zero use the other nonzero regular block in the same way. The finite-order bounds [F3] control the remaining factor polynomially. Thus $\operatorname{WF}(u\otimes v)\subseteq(\operatorname{WF}(u)\times\operatorname{WF}(v))\cup(\operatorname{WF}(u)\times0_M)\cup(0_M\times\operatorname{WF}(v))$. Finite chart covers and cutoffs [F7] give the same inclusion on manifolds. [A1, F1, F3, F5, F7, algebra]
items/lem-em-exterior-vector-helmholtz-mixed-boundary-existence.md:112:[L17] Difference quotients are $\delta_h^iu(x)=(u(x+he_i)-u(x))/h$ ([[def-first-difference-quotient]]). Discrete integration by parts and shifted product rules hold on common localized domains, with no boundary remainder for tangential quotients on a half-space ([[lem-difference-quotient-integration-by-parts]]). The increment estimate $\|\delta_h^iu\|_{L^2(U)}\le\|\partial_iu\|_{L^2}$ holds when the used segments stay in the enlarged domain, including tangential half-space directions ([[thm-difference-quotient-characterisation-of-w-one-p-for-p-greater-than-one]]). Smooth cutoff commutators obey [[lem-cutoff-difference-quotient-commutator-estimate]].
items/lem-em-exterior-vector-helmholtz-mixed-boundary-existence.md:148:3.2 **The kernel of the form is trivial.** Let $E\in X$ satisfy $a(E,v)=0$ for every $v\in X$. Taking $v=E$ and imaginary parts in step 2.1 gives $0=\operatorname{Im}a(E,E)=-\operatorname{Im}\langle T_RE,E\rangle$, where $f=E|_{S_R}$; by [L1] the imaginary pairing equals $\sum_m\sum_{l,m'}(\operatorname{Im}t_l)|(f_m)_{lm'}|^2$ with $\operatorname{Im}t_l>0$, so every coefficient vanishes, $f=0$ on $S_R$, and consequently $T_Rf=0$. For every $v\in X$ supported away from $\partial O$ the identity $a(E,v)=0$ has no inner-boundary contribution and reads $\int_A(\nabla E:\overline{\nabla v}-k^2E\cdot\overline v)\,dx=\langle T_RE,v\rangle_{S_R}=\langle T_Rf,v|_{S_R}\rangle=0$; in particular every $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(D)$, whose restriction to $A$ is an admissible test vanishing near $\partial O$, satisfies $\int_A\nabla E:\overline{\nabla\varphi}\,dx=k^2\int_AE\cdot\overline\varphi\,dx$, and taking $v$ compactly supported in the interior of $A$ gives $-\Delta E=k^2E$ weakly on $A$. Define $\widetilde E:=E$ on $A$ and $\widetilde E:=0$ on $\{|x|>R\}$; assign any value on the null sphere $S_R$, so $\widetilde E$ defines an $L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}(D)$ class, and its only interface is the sphere $S_R$, across which the full trace of the field is $f=0$. For $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(D)$ the Sobolev-Gauss-Green formula [L11] on the bounded $C^1$ domain $A$, applied componentwise with $u=E_m\in W^{1,2}(A)$ and $v=\varphi|_A$, gives $\int_AE_m\overline{\partial_i\varphi}\,dx=-\int_A(\partial_iE_m)\overline\varphi\,dx+\int_{\partial A}(TE_m)(T\varphi)\nu_i\,dS$; the boundary term over $\partial O$ vanishes because $\varphi$ is compactly supported in $D$, and the term over $S_R$ vanishes because $TE_m=f_m=0$ there; hence $\int_D\widetilde E_m\overline{\partial_i\varphi}\,dx=-\int_A(\partial_iE_m)\overline\varphi\,dx$, so the distributional gradient of $\widetilde E$ is the field $(\nabla E)\mathbf 1_A\in L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}(D)$, $\widetilde E\in H^1_{\mathrm{loc}}(D)$, and the weak identity above gives $\int_D\nabla\widetilde E:\overline{\nabla\varphi}\,dx=k^2\int_D\widetilde E\cdot\overline\varphi\,dx$. By [L6] each real and imaginary part of each component of $\widetilde E$ lies in $H^2_{\mathrm{loc}}(D)$ and satisfies $\Delta w=-k^2w$ distributionally; $D$ is nonempty, open and connected and $\widetilde E=0$ on the nonempty open set $D\cap\{|x|>R\}$, so [L5] gives $\widetilde E=0$ on $D$, in particular $E=0$ on $A$. [L1, L5, L6, L11, step 2.1]
items/lem-em-exterior-vector-helmholtz-mixed-boundary-existence.md:160:8.1 **Tangential second derivatives and recovery of the normal derivatives.** Let $k\in\{1,2\}$ be a tangential direction, put $W:=\delta_h^kU=(U(y+he_k,s)-U(y,s))/h$ and test the system of step 7.2 with $V:=-\delta_{-h}^k(\eta^2W)$ on a nested pair of half-boxes; this test has $V_1=V_2=0$ on $B$ and compact support, so it is admissible. The discrete integration by parts of [L17] and the shifted product rule give a principal term bounded below by $\alpha\|\eta\nabla W\|_{L^2}^2$ with $\alpha>0$ the ellipticity constant of $a^{ij}$, while all remaining terms involve $\delta_h a$, $\nabla\eta$, $W$, $\nabla U$, $f$ or the transferred quotients of $G$, and are bounded by $\epsilon\|\eta\nabla W\|_2^2+C_\epsilon(\|U\|_{H^1}^2+\|f\|_{L^2}^2+\|G\|_{H^1}^2)$ using [L17], the increment estimate $\|\delta_h^kU\|_{L^2}\le\|\partial_kU\|_{L^2}$, and [L19]. On $B$ the Robin term gives $\int_B\beta(y+he_k)\eta^2|W_3|^2+\int_B(\delta_h^k\beta)U_3\eta^2\overline{W_3}$; the trace estimate $\|Tz\|_{L^2(B)}^2\le\epsilon\|\nabla z\|_{L^2}^2+C_\epsilon\|z\|_{L^2}^2$ of step 1.2 (with $B$ the flattened $\partial O$) absorbs the first term for either sign of $\beta$, and bounded $\delta_h\beta$, the trace bound for $U_3$ and [L19] bound the second; the smooth datum $h$ contributes $\int_B(\delta_h^kh)\overline{W_3}$, bounded by $\|h\|_{H^1(B)}\|T(\eta^2W_3)\|_{L^2(B)}$ and absorbed in the same way. Choosing the finitely many $\epsilon$ small gives $\|\eta\nabla\delta_h^kU\|_{L^2}\le C(\|U\|_{H^1}+\|f\|_{L^2}+\|G\|_{H^1}+\|h\|_{H^1(B)})$ uniformly in $h$, hence every tangential second derivative of $U$ lies in $L^2$ after the limit argument in which the bounded functional $\psi\mapsto\lim_h\int(\partial_iU_m)\delta_{-h}^k(\eta^2\psi)$ is represented in $L^2$ by [L8]. From the interior equation $-\partial_i(a^{ij}\partial_jU_m)=f_m-\partial_iG_{i,m}$ one then recovers $a^{33}\partial_{ss}U_m=\partial_iG_{i,m}-f_m-\sum_{(i,j)\ne(3,3)}a^{ij}\partial_{ij}U_m-\sum_{i,j}(\partial_ia^{ij})\partial_jU_m$, whose right side is now $L^2$ and whose coefficient satisfies $a^{33}\ge\alpha>0$; so all second derivatives of $U$ lie in $L^2$ on the smaller half-box, and a finite cover of the compact boundary gives $u\in H^2$ up to $\partial O$ and hence $E=u+G\in H^2$, since $G$ is smooth. [L8, L19, step 1.2, step 7.2, algebra]
items/lem-em-exterior-vector-helmholtz-mixed-boundary-existence.md:162:9.1 **Higher-order bootstrap and the classical boundary conclusion.** Suppose $U\in H^{r+1}$ on the larger half-box for some $r\ge1$; the data of the local system then satisfy $f\in H^r$, each divergence datum $G_i\in H^{r+1}$ and the lift-adjusted boundary datum $h$ is smooth because all coefficients are smooth. For a tangential multi-index $\alpha$ of order $r$ put $Z:=D_y^\alpha U$; differentiating the system of step 7.2 by $D_y^\alpha$ keeps the principal matrix $a^{ij}$ and the Robin coefficient $\beta$, and replaces the data by $f_\alpha\in L^2$, $G_\alpha\in H^1$ and $h_\alpha\in H^1(B)$ built from $D_y^\alpha f$, $D_y^\alpha G$ and the finitely many commutator terms $D_y^\gamma(\text{coefficient})\,D_y^{\alpha-\gamma}U$ with $0<\gamma\le\alpha$, all known by the induction hypothesis; the tangential traces of $Z$ are again zero in the first two components and free in the third, and localized tests for $Z$ are admissible by the test approximation of step 7.2; the differentiated Robin commutators contain traces of tangential derivatives of $U$ of order at most $r-1$, their tangential derivatives of one further order have $L^2$ traces because the corresponding interior derivatives of order at most $r$ belong to $H^1$; trace continuity and smooth approximation therefore place those commutators in $H^1(B)$ by [L10]. Applying step 8.1 to $Z$ gains two further tangential or mixed derivatives, and differentiating the normal-recovery equation of step 8.1 and inducting on the number of normal derivatives recovers the remaining ones; nested half-boxes and a finite boundary cover give $U\in H^m$ on each smaller patch for every $m$, with constants depending on the data, $\kappa$, the chart and the fixed nested covers. By the Sobolev embedding [L18] on a rounded bounded smooth subdomain of the chart that agrees with $s=0$ near the smaller patch and lies inside the larger half-box (hence an extension domain by [L13]) each $H^m$ class with $m>j+\tfrac32$ has a $C^j$ representative on the closed patch, compatible for different $j$; hence $u$, and therefore $E=u+G$, has all derivatives continuous up to $\partial O$; combining with the smooth gluing at $S_R$, [L22] on the bounded annulus $A$ supplies one smooth ambient representative near its closure, and the classical integration by parts in the local frame (equivalently [L11] with classical traces) turns $a(E,v)=-\int_{\partial O}(\operatorname{div}_{\partial O}e_T)\overline{v_n}$ for admissible inner-collar tests into the boundary identities $\partial_nE_n+\kappa E_n=-\operatorname{div}_{\partial O}e_T$ and $E_{\text{tangential}}=e_T$ on $\partial O$. [L10, L11, L13, L18, L21, L22, step 7.2, step 8.1, algebra]
items/lem-em-exterior-vector-helmholtz-mixed-boundary-existence.md:166:11.1 **Uniqueness of the classical problem.** Let $E_1,E_2\in C^\infty(\overline D;\mathbb C^3)$ both satisfy the conditions of the Statement. Their difference $W:=E_1-E_2$ is $C^\infty$ on $\overline D$, solves $(\Delta+k^2)W=0$ componentwise on $D$, has $\operatorname{div}W=0$ and $W_{\text{tangential}}=0$ on $\partial O$, and is outgoing on $S_R$. Then $W\in X$ with $W|_{S_R}\in H^{1/2}$, and the integration by parts of step 3.2 and step 6.1 shows that $a(W,v)=0$ for every $v\in X$: the volume terms reproduce the Helmholtz equation, the inner boundary term cancels against $\operatorname{div}W=0$ on $\partial O$ through the exact identity of step 10.1, and the outer term cancels because $\partial_rW=T_R(W|_{S_R})$ by outgoingness. By step 3.2, $W=0$ on $A$; its trace on $S_R$ is zero, so [L1] gives $W=0$ outside that sphere as well, hence on $D$. [L1, step 3.2, step 6.1, step 10.1, algebra]
items/thm-fd-rectangular-no-slip-stokes-velocity-pressure.md:4:title: "Rectangular no-slip Stokes velocity and distribution pressure"
items/thm-fd-rectangular-no-slip-stokes-velocity-pressure.md:39:      locator: "Chapter 6 §6.1, PDF pp. 55–56: standard no-slip Stokes velocity-pressure formulation; its L² pressure relies on inf–sup, unlike the local H⁻¹ construction proved here."
items/lem-emg-einstein-action-variation.md:101:[A1] The compact variations are smooth families fixed off a common compact set $K$ for all sufficiently small parameters, within the fixed Lorentzian signature and fixed bundle sector; $\Lambda,c,G_N,\mu_0$ are held constant. For any compactly supported direction $q$, such a family is realized by $g(s)=g+sh$, $h=-gqg$, on a sufficiently small uniform parameter interval as proved in the Maxwell supplier.
items/lem-qft-hermite-oscillator-research-supplier.md:92:2.1 For $v\in C_c^\infty$, integration by parts and the eigenvalue identity in step 1.1 give
