items/lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality.md:101:1.1 For every integer $N\ge2$, repeated integration by parts in $\mathbf x$ gives $(1+|\mathbf k|^2)^N\widehat F(t,\mathbf k)=\widehat{(1-\Delta_{\mathbf x})^NF}(t,\mathbf k)$. Since $F$ is Schwartz, the right side is bounded by a finite Schwartz seminorm times $(1+|t|)^{-2}$. Thus $|\widehat F(t,\mathbf k)|\le C_N(F)(1+|t|)^{-2}(1+|\mathbf k|)^{-2N}$. The matrix multiplier $W_D(t,\mathbf k)$ is pointwise unitary by [F2]; integrating this bound in $t$ yields $|u_F(\mathbf k)|\le C'_N(F)(1+|\mathbf k|)^{-2N}$. This is square-integrable in three dimensions for $N\ge2$, with norm bounded by the displayed Schwartz seminorm, so $u_F\in\mathcal H_D$ continuously. [F2, given, algebra]
