items/thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence.md:65:[L3] Weak Neumann and transmission existence: with $\ell(v)=\int_\Omega\rho v+\langle g,Tv\rangle$ on $H^1(\Omega)$ for $\rho\in L^2$ and $g\in H^{-1/2}(\partial\Omega)$, a weak Neumann solution $\int_\Omega\nabla v\cdot\varepsilon\nabla u=\ell(v)$ for all $v\in H^1(\Omega)$ exists if and only if $\ell(1)=0$, and is then unique up to an additive constant, the normalisation $\int_\Omega u=0$ making it unique; on finitely many components compatibility and normalisation are imposed separately on each component; for finitely many disjoint smooth stationary interfaces with piecewise smooth uniformly elliptic $\varepsilon$, bulk $\rho\in L^2$ and prescribed sheet charge $\sigma$ defining a bounded trace functional on $H^1$, the same argument gives weak Dirichlet or compatible Neumann existence for the transmission problem, the globally $H^1$ potential has identical one-sided traces, and where the solution is classically smooth on each side integration by parts gives the jump $n\cdot[\varepsilon\nabla u]=-\sigma$ ([[lem-em-weak-neumann-and-transmission-existence]]).
items/thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence.md:87:1.3 **Transmission: weak existence, unique traces and the conditional jump.** In the configuration of part (c), let $\Lambda(v):=\int_\Omega\rho v+\sum_\Gamma\int_\Gamma\sigma\,Tv$, a bounded functional on $H^1(\Omega)$ because $\sigma$ is smooth on the compact interface union and $T$ is bounded into $L^2$ of that finite union. For outer Dirichlet data, obtain an exact $H^1$ boundary lift $G$ from [L1] applied with coefficient $I$ and zero bulk datum (this is only a lift, not the transmission solution). Restrict the bounded functional $\Lambda$ to $H_0^1$ and apply [L6] with the actual piecewise coefficient; it gives the unique transmission solution in $G+H_0^1$ with the prescribed outer trace. For outer Neumann data, the weak Neumann part of [L3] applies directly to the bounded functional including the sheet and outer flux terms; hence a globally $H^1$ weak solution exists, is unique for outer Dirichlet data, and for outer Neumann data exists exactly when $\Lambda(v)+\int_{\partial\Omega}g\,Tv$ vanishes on the constants of every component, that is when the stated compatibility holds, and is then unique modulo componentwise constants, the zero componentwise means fixing it. By the transmission part of [L3] the globally $H^1$ potential has identical one-sided traces, and wherever it is classically smooth on each side, integration by parts gives the jump $n\cdot[\varepsilon\nabla u]=-\sigma$ in the stated sign convention. [L1, L3, L6, L7]
items/thm-em-elliptic-boundary-existence.md:91:2.2 **Transmission: one-sided classical regularity and the unconditional jump.** The weak solution of step 1.3 is the weak solution of the transmission problem for the piecewise smooth positive $\varepsilon$, piecewise smooth $\rho$ and smooth sheet charge $\sigma$ of the Statement, with either the smooth outer Dirichlet datum or the smooth compatible outer Neumann datum. By the interface part of [L2] it is $C^\infty$ on each closed side; in particular it is classically smooth on each side of every interface, so the conditional jump identity of step 1.3 applies and the flux jump $n\cdot[\varepsilon\nabla u]=-\sigma$ holds unconditionally in the stated class. The equal one-sided traces are those of step 1.3, and on each closed side the classical equation $-\operatorname{div}(\varepsilon\nabla u)=\rho$ holds because the weak identity tested against compactly supported fields localized in the interior of a side is the distributional equation there. The interface integration by parts underlying the jump is the divergence theorem [L4] applied to the $C^1$ field $v\,\varepsilon\nabla u$ on the two pieces and the Green identity [L5]; the smooth chain and product rules of [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]] and [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]] are the calculus conventions used in transporting the equation to the closed pieces, and the Euclidean pairings are those of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. [L2, L4, L5, step 1.3]
items/lem-emg-sphere-flux-holonomy.md:137:[F15] [[prop-integration-of-top-forms-by-finite-parametrizations]]: a compactly supported top form on an oriented manifold is the sum of its pullback integrals over finitely many bounded open Jordan parameter domains when each parametrization extends smoothly to the closure, is an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism onto a disjoint open image, and the closures of those images cover the support. The parametrization includes no contribution from the image boundaries.
items/lem-emg-sphere-flux-holonomy.md:154:The radial projection therefore preserves the face orientation. Every point away from the projected edges has exactly one preimage, with positive local orientation, so its signed preimage count (the degree in this piecewise-smooth setting) is $+1$. For each odd $j$, precompose $\sigma_j$ with the affine transposition $\rho(u_1,u_2)=(u_2,u_1)$ of the standard triangle, and for each even $j$ leave it unchanged; call the resulting parametrizations $\widetilde\sigma_j$. By the orientation convention in [[def-integral-of-a-form-over-a-smooth-singular-simplex]], $\int_{\widetilde\sigma_j}F_b=(-1)^j\int_{\sigma_j}F_b$, and each $R\circ\widetilde\sigma_j$ is orientation-preserving onto its face image. Their domains are bounded open Jordan triangles; they extend smoothly to their closures, and the whole sphere is covered by the closures of the four images. Applying [F15] to $F_b|_{S^2}$ gives the exact reparameterization identity $\int_cF_b=\sum_j\int_{\widetilde\sigma_j}F_b=\int_{S^2}F_b=4\pi b$; the projected edges have zero two-dimensional content and contribute no integral. By Stokes [F2], $\int_cF_b=\int_c dA=\int_{\partial c}A=0$, contradicting $4\pi b\ne0$. Thus no global potential exists when $b\ne0$. [given, F2, F15, step 1.1, construct]
items/thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence.md:94:[F6] If $F:[a,b]\to\mathbb R$ is differentiable at every point of $[a,b]$ and $F'$ is integrable there, then $\int_a^bF'=F(b)-F(a)$ ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]); complex integration by parts on $\mathbb R$ is valid when both derivative products are in $L^1$ and the product vanishes at both ends ([[lem-complex-integration-by-parts-on-intervals-and-decaying-lines]]). For $\varphi,\psi\in P$ one has $\varphi(y)\overline{\psi(y)}\to0$ as $|y|\to\infty$, because its modulus is bounded by $C(1+y^{2k})e^{-y^2}$ for some $C>0$ and $k\in\mathbb N$; [F3] bounds this by $C(1+2^kk!)e^{-y^2/2}$, which tends to zero since $e^{y^2/2}\ge1+y^2/2$ by the same nonnegative series and [F5].
