items/prop-emg-fx-nonlinear-maxwell-variation.md:73:Use the fixed-metric, fixed-bundle-sector model of the nonlinear Lagrangian Definition: $F$ is its smooth closed real curvature two-form, $L\in C^2(\mathbb R^2)$, $X=-F_{ab}F^{ab}/4$, $Y=-F_{ab}(\star_gF)^{ab}/4$, and $H^{ab}=L_XF^{ab}+L_Y(\star_gF)^{ab}$. For dimensionless $\epsilon\in\mathbb R$, a permitted variation is a smooth compactly supported global real one-form $a$ added to each compatible local physical-potential representative, so $F_\epsilon=F+\epsilon da$; stationarity differentiates the relative $L(X,Y)\,\mathrm{vol}_g$ action difference. Its compactly supported density is integrated by the finite chart-partition rule for continuous top forms, agreeing with the usual integral on smooth forms; this convention retains $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, meaning every countable family of nonempty sets has a choice function. If the connection is stationary under every such variation and the source coupling is absent, then
items/prop-emg-fx-nonlinear-maxwell-variation.md:91:**Given:** The Definition's smooth Lorentzian metric, fixed bundle connection sector, smooth curvature $F$, $C^2$ function $L$, and all compactly supported smooth global one-form variations.
items/prop-emg-fx-nonlinear-maxwell-variation.md:101:[A1] $F$ and the metric are smooth and $L\in C^2(\mathbb R^2)$, so $H$ is $C^1$ and $\nabla_aH^{ab}$ is continuous; stationarity is imposed for every permitted compactly supported one-form.
items/prop-emg-fx-nonlinear-maxwell-variation.md:109:2.1 Put $K:=\operatorname{supp}a$ and $W^a:=H^{ab}a_b$. Then $W$ is a compactly supported $C^1$ vector field, not necessarily smooth. If $K$ is empty the variation is zero, so assume $K\ne\varnothing$. Cover $K$ by smaller relatively compact coordinate balls $U_i$ and choose finitely many larger positively oriented coordinate balls $V_i$ with $\overline{U_i}\subset V_i$; compactness gives a finite subcover. In each chart choose a nonnegative smooth bump $\rho_i$ supported compactly in $V_i$ and equal to $1$ on $U_i$, which is obtained from the standard smooth Euclidean bump by a radial rescaling in the chart. For $B:=\sum_i\rho_i$, one has $B\ge1$ on $K$. Choose a smooth function $q:\mathbb R\to[0,1]$ with $q(s)=0$ for $s\le1/4$ and $q(s)=1$ for $s\ge1/2$ (such a step is obtained from $u(s)=e^{-1/s}$ for $s>0$ and $u(s)=0$ for $s\le0$); set $\eta:=q\circ B$. Then $\eta=1$ on the open neighborhood $V:=\{B>1/2\}$ of $K$, its support is compact and contained in $\{B>0\}$, and $\chi_i:=\eta\rho_i/B$ on $\{B>0\}$, extended by zero elsewhere, are smooth, compactly supported in $V_i$, and satisfy $\sum_i\chi_i=1$ on $V$. This construction of local cutoffs uses compactness only; the inherited $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ condition for the global integral is stated in [F4]. In a positive chart put $w:=\sqrt{|\det(g_{ab})|}>0$. Contracting the Christoffel formula gives $\Gamma^a{}_{ac}=\frac12g^{ab}\partial_cg_{ab}=\partial_c\log w$, where the last equality is the determinant derivative formula. Hence, for $W_i:=\chi_iW$, $\nabla_aW_i^a=\partial_aW_i^a+\Gamma^a{}_{ac}W_i^c=w^{-1}\partial_a(wW_i^a)$. Each $wW_i^a$ is $C^1$ and compactly supported inside its chart, so extend it by zero to a containing coordinate rectangle. Its coordinate derivative is continuous and each one-variable section of $wW_i^a$ is continuously differentiable, so [F3] applies: Fubini reduces the integral of each derivative to an interval integral, and the fundamental theorem makes that integral zero because the section vanishes near the interval endpoints. Thus $\int_{V_i}\nabla_aW_i^a\,\mathrm{vol}_g=0$. Since $\sum_i\chi_i=1$ on the open neighborhood $V$ of $K$ and $W$ vanishes outside $K$, $\sum_i\nabla_aW_i^a=\nabla_aW^a$ everywhere. The finite cutoffs near $K$ compute the continuous compact-support integral by [F4], so $\int_M\nabla_aW^a\,\mathrm{vol}_g=\sum_i\int_{V_i}\nabla_aW_i^a\,\mathrm{vol}_g=0$. The relative-action density difference is $C^1$ in the parameter and supported in $K$. Multiplying it by the same $\chi_i$ gives finitely many compactly supported chart densities; on a fixed coordinate rectangle their parameter derivatives are continuous and hence uniformly continuous on a compact parameter interval. [F3] makes these derivatives integrable, and the mean-value theorem shows their difference quotients converge uniformly to the derivatives at $\epsilon=0$. The Darboux definition gives $|\int_Qf|\le\operatorname{vol}(Q)\sup_Q|f|$, so these uniform limits pass to the integrals and the finite sum differentiates termwise. Integrating the product identity $\nabla_aW^a=(\nabla_aH^{ab})a_b+H^{ab}\nabla_aa_b$ now yields
items/prop-emg-fx-nonlinear-maxwell-variation.md:113:3.1 Put $P^b=\nabla_aH^{ab}$, which is continuous by [A1]. If $P^{b_0}$ were nonzero at a point in a chart, continuity would give a closed nondegenerate coordinate rectangle $Q$ around that point, with $Q$ contained in the chart, on which $P^{b_0}$ has one strict sign. Choose a nonnegative smooth bump $\phi$ compactly supported in the interior of $Q$ and positive near the point, set $a_{b_0}=\phi$ and all other coordinate components to zero, and extend by zero. Such a bump is obtained as a product of one-dimensional smooth bumps, each built from $u(s)=e^{-1/s}$ for $s>0$ and $u(s)=0$ for $s\le0$. If units are attached, multiply this test one-form by a fixed positive potential-component unit so it has the units required by [F1]; this does not change its sign. After multiplying by the sign of $P^{b_0}$, the coefficient $h:=P^{b_0}\phi w$ is nonnegative on $Q$ and bounded below by some $\eta>0$ on a smaller nondegenerate rectangle $R\subseteq Q$ around the point. By [F3], a grid of $Q$ with $R$ as one cell has a strictly positive lower sum, so the local integral over $Q$ is positive. Its support lies in $Q$, and [F4] identifies this with the global integral in step 2.1. This is nonzero, contradicting stationarity. Thus $P^b=0$ in every chart. Conversely, $P=0$ makes the variation in step 2.1 zero for every allowed $a$. [given, A1, F3, F4, step 2.1, construct, algebra]
items/lem-emg-matter-noether-exchange.md:115:1.2 Let $\xi$ be a smooth vector field compactly supported inside an arbitrary positively oriented coordinate ball in a bundle trivialization, and use the flows and matter transport specified in the Statement. In a local circle angle $\theta$, $\omega=d\theta+\kappa_0^{-1}A_a dx^a$, so $\xi^H=\xi^a\partial_a-\kappa_0^{-1}(\xi^a A_a)\partial_\theta$. Differentiating $H_s^*\omega$ gives $\kappa_0^{-1}(\mathcal L_\xi A-d(\xi^aA_a))=\kappa_0^{-1}\iota_\xi F$ on the base: explicitly $(\mathcal L_\xi A)_a=\xi^b\partial_bA_a+A_b\partial_a\xi^b=\xi^bF_{ba}+\partial_a(\xi^bA_b)$. Consequently the physical connection variation is $\alpha_a=\xi^bF_{ba}$. Since $F$ is global by [F2], this formula agrees on overlaps. The supplied matter transport has compact vertical derivative as in [A1]. Such coordinate balls cover $M$, so restricting to their tests loses no pointwise conclusion. [given, A1, F1, F2, F4, F5, algebra]
items/thm-em-retarded-point-distributional-maxwell.md:101:[F12] Newton-Leibniz with an interior derivative, one-dimensional integration by parts, rectangle Riemann-Fubini, Riemann-to-Lebesgue comparison for compact test integrands, translations preserve Lebesgue measure, and smooth compact cutoffs exist in ZF ([[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]], [[thm-integration-by-parts]], [[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]], [[lem-riemann-lebesgue-comparison-for-distribution-test-integrands]], [[thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-and-measurability-are-translation-invariant]], [[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]]).
items/thm-em-retarded-point-distributional-maxwell.md:119:2.2 Zero-extension representatives of the first derivatives. Let $f$ be one of the components of $\varphi$ and $A$ on $V$, with zero extension $\widetilde f$, and let $D\in\{\partial_t,\partial_1,\partial_2,\partial_3\}$. Since $\widetilde f$ and $Df$ (zero extended) are locally integrable by step 1.3, both $u_{\widetilde f}$ and $u_{(Df)^{\sim}}$ are distributions. Then $Du_{\widetilde f}=u_{(Df)^{\sim}}$: choose the spatial cutoff $\chi_\varepsilon(x,t):=\chi((x-z(t))/\varepsilon)$ built from a smooth cutoff $\chi$ that is $0$ on $\overline B_1(0)$ and $1$ outside $B_2(0)$; it is $C^3$ and its first derivatives satisfy $|D\chi_\varepsilon|\le C'/\varepsilon$ with support in the shell $\varepsilon\le d\le2\varepsilon$, where $d:=|x-z(t)|$ and $(1-v_*/c)r\le d\le(1+v_*/c)r$; the product $\chi_\varepsilon\widetilde f$ is $C^1$ on $Q$ because it vanishes on a neighbourhood of the singular set. Classical integration by parts in each coordinate over a large box containing $\operatorname{supp}\psi$ (boundary terms vanish, Riemann-Fubini iterates the one-dimensional identities, and the Riemann-Lebesgue comparison converts to Lebesgue integrals) gives $\int_Q\chi_\varepsilon\widetilde f\,D\psi=-\int_Q\chi_\varepsilon(Df)^{\sim}\psi-\int_Q\widetilde f(D\chi_\varepsilon)\psi$, and dominated convergence with the locally integrable bounds of step 1.3 lets $\varepsilon\to0$ in the first two integrals; the third is $O(\varepsilon)$ because $|\widetilde f|\le C_0'/\varepsilon$ on the shell of volume $O(\varepsilon^3)$ in space and $O(1)$ in time. Hence $-\int_Q\widetilde fD\psi=\int_Q(Df)^{\sim}\psi$, that is, $\langle Du_{\widetilde f},\psi\rangle=\langle u_{(Df)^{\sim}},\psi\rangle$ for every test, proving the claim. Consequently $\mathcal E=-\nabla\Phi-\partial_t\Upsilon=u_{E_0^{\sim}}$ and $\mathcal B=\operatorname{curl}\Upsilon=u_{B_0^{\sim}}$ by linearity of the regular functional, with $E_0,B_0$ the classical fields of step 1.3. [step 1.3, given, F1, F4, F5, F10, F12]
items/lem-qsmx-filtered-spectral-generator.md:108:1.1 Let $u$ be the standard flat function of [F5] and put $q(\omega):=1-(\omega/\gamma)^2$ and $\chi(\omega):=e\,u(q(\omega))$. Since $\gamma>0$, $q$ is a smooth real polynomial. The chain and product rules in [F5], iterated at each derivative order, make $\chi$ smooth on all of $\mathbb R$: every positive-order derivative is a finite sum of products of a derivative $u^{(m)}\circ q$, with $m\ge1$, and derivatives of $q$. At $\omega=\pm\gamma$, one has $q(\omega)=0$ and every such $u^{(m)}(0)=0$, so all these derivatives vanish and match the exterior zero branch. The value itself also vanishes there because $u(0)=0$. Since $q>0$ exactly when $|\omega|<\gamma$, this formula is exactly $e\exp(-1/(1-(\omega/\gamma)^2))$ inside that interval and zero outside. Thus $\chi$ is real, even, smooth, compactly supported in $[-\gamma,\gamma]$, and $\chi(0)=e\exp(-1)=1$. Define $w(t)=(2\pi)^{-1}\int\chi(\omega)e^{-it\omega}\,d\omega$. By [A1], [F3], and the sign/scale conversion stated in [F3], $w$ is Schwartz, real and even, its Fourier transform $\widehat w(\omega)=\int w(t)e^{it\omega}dt$ equals $\chi(\omega)$, and $\int w=1$. For $t>0$ put $W_\gamma(t)=\int_t^\infty w(v)\,dv$; for $t<0$ put $W_\gamma(t)=-\int_{-\infty}^t w(v)\,dv$; set $W_\gamma(0)=0$. Evenness and unit integral make $W_\gamma$ odd with one-sided limits $W_\gamma(0^\pm)=\pm\frac12$. For every $N$, a Schwartz bound $|w(v)|\le c(1+|v|)^{-N-2}$ on either tail gives $|W_\gamma(t)|\le c'(1+|t|)^{-N-1}$; hence $W_\gamma\in L^1$ and has the stated tail estimate. Integration by parts on the two half-lines, accounting for the jump of size $1$ at zero and using $W_\gamma'=-w$ away from zero, gives $-\widehat w(\omega)=-1-i\omega\widehat W_\gamma(\omega)$. Thus $\widehat W_\gamma(\omega)=i(1-\chi(\omega))/\omega$ for $\omega\ne0$, so it equals $i/\omega$ for $|\omega|\ge\gamma$. [given, A1, F3, F5, construct, algebra]
items/ex-fd-x07-application.md:4:title: Bounded-domain Stokes residual estimate — worked interpretation
items/ex-fd-x07-application.md:50:The two velocity fields are smooth and divergence-free, have the same wall velocity, and satisfy respectively the stationary constant-density incompressible Navier–Stokes equation and the restricted steady Stokes comparison equation with zero body acceleration. Let $C_{B_R}>0$ be an explicitly assumed constant such that $\|v\|_{L^2(B_R)}\le C_{B_R}\|\nabla v\|_{L^2(B_R)}$ for every $C^1$ zero-trace vector field; this example does not establish a value for it. Their velocity difference is zero, while the convective residual is $f=\rho_0\Omega_r^2(x,y,0)=\nabla(p_{\rm NS}-p_{\rm S})$. The residual estimates therefore hold for this supplied Poincare input. This exact example has nonzero pressure residual but zero velocity error; it does not demonstrate a nonzero velocity discrepancy.
