items/lem-qft-photon-transverse-projector-kernel.md:106:2.1 For every test $\varphi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^3)$ [F8], the bound $|G_\varepsilon(\mathbf x)|\le(4\pi|\mathbf x|)^{-1}$ and [F3] make $G_\varepsilon\varphi$ dominated by an integrable function; [F5] gives the distributional limit $G_\varepsilon\to G$. To identify the same limit in Fourier variables, repeated integration by parts in a coordinate with $|k_j|\ge|\mathbf k|/\sqrt3$ gives $|\widehat\varphi(\mathbf k)|\le C_N(1+|\mathbf k|)^{-N}$ for every integer $N$, while $|\widehat\varphi|\le\|\varphi\|_1$ near zero. Hence $|\mathbf k|^{-2}\widehat\varphi(-\mathbf k)$ is integrable by [F3]. Fubini [F7] identifies $\int G_\varepsilon\varphi$ with $(2\pi)^{-3}\int m_\varepsilon(\mathbf k)\widehat\varphi(-\mathbf k)d^3\mathbf k$. Since $|m_\varepsilon|\le|\mathbf k|^{-2}$, [F5] shows this tends to the inverse Fourier pairing with $|\mathbf k|^{-2}$. Both limits are therefore the same distribution $G$, proving $\mathcal F^{-1}(|\mathbf k|^{-2})=G$. By [F1] this is the published normalized fundamental solution. [A1, F1, F3, F5, F7, F8, step 1.1, given]
