items/ex-fd-x10-formal-verification.md:43:[F1] [[thm-fd-x10]]: for an already-existing smooth incompressible Navier-Stokes solution on a periodic channel of height $h$ with Couette shear $S$, if $|S|h^2/\nu<2$ and the theorem's stated dependencies hold, its perturbation energy satisfies $E(t)\le e^{-(2\nu/h^2-|S|)t}E(0)$ on the assumed smooth interval.
items/lem-qftx-green-propagator-symplectic-quotient.md:77:1.2 Let $f,h\in\mathcal D_g(M)$, $u=E_-f$ and $v=E_+h$. Their supports meet in the closed set $C:=\operatorname{supp}u\cap\operatorname{supp}v$, contained in $J^-(\operatorname{supp}f)\cap J^+(\operatorname{supp}h)$ and compact by [F2]. If $C=\varnothing$, both $fv$ and $uh$ vanish; otherwise choose by [F3] a compactly supported smooth cutoff $\eta$ equal to $1$ on a neighborhood of $C$. Since $f=Pu$ and $h=Pv$, formal self-adjointness applied to the compactly supported functions $\eta u$ and $\eta v$ gives $\int_M f v\,\mu_g=\int_M u h\,\mu_g$: after expanding $P(\eta u)$ and $P(\eta v)$, each commutator term is supported where a derivative of $\eta$ meets $\operatorname{supp}u\cap\operatorname{supp}v\subseteq C$, so it vanishes because $\eta=1$ near $C$; the remaining integrands $fv$ and $uh$ are supported in $C$. Applying the same calculation with $u=E_+f$ and $v=E_-h$ gives $\int_M f(E_-h)\,\mu_g=\int_M (E_+f)h\,\mu_g$. Subtracting these two Green adjoint identities yields $E^*=-E$ under the compact test-density pairing. [A1, F1, F2, F3, given, algebra]
items/def-qftx-green-hyperbolic-interface.md:53:Write the positive metric density in a chart as $\mu_g=|\det(g_{ab})|^{1/2}|dx^0dx^1dx^2dx^3|$. This is a coordinate-independent smooth density: if $y=\psi(x)$, then $g_y=(D\psi^{-1})^Tg_xD\psi^{-1}$, so $|\det(g_y)|^{1/2}=|\det D\psi^{-1}|\,|\det(g_x)|^{1/2}$, the test-density transformation rule of [[def-qftx-distributions-on-smooth-manifolds]]. Let $\mathcal D_g(M):=C_c^\infty(M;\mathbb R)$ have the real test-function topology transported by $f\mapsto f\mu_g$ from that supplier's compactly supported smooth test densities. Give $C^\infty(M;\mathbb R)$ its usual topology of uniform convergence of every coordinate derivative on each compact subset.
