items/pthm-em-image-method-certificate.md:145:3.1 Clause (e), extension identity. Extend $E_I$ by zero to $\{x_3\le0\}$, obtaining $\widetilde E\in L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb R^3)$ by the point-field integrability in [F1] and smoothness away from $p$. The plane pairing is a distribution: on tests supported in a compact box its absolute value is bounded by $\sup|\sigma|$ times the finite area of the box's planar section times $\sup|\eta|$. Choose $0<r<R<a$ and $\chi$ from [F8], so $\chi=1$ near $p$ and its support lies in $H$. Split an arbitrary scalar test as $\eta_1=\chi\eta$, $\eta_2=(1-\chi)\eta$. For $\eta_1$ the half-space identities of step 1.2 give the divergence pairing $(q/\varepsilon_0)\eta(p)$ and zero curl pairing; its plane trace is zero. For $\eta_2$, all field/test products are smooth away from the pole and absolutely integrable on a compact box. Fubini and one-dimensional integration by parts [F2, F6] therefore give, for every $i,j$, $-\int_H E_{I,j}\partial_i\eta_2=\int_H(\partial_iE_{I,j})\eta_2+\mathbf1_{\{i=3\}}\int_{\mathbb R^2}E_{I,j}(x',0)\eta_2(x',0)dx'$. Horizontal boundary terms vanish by compact support; the vertical integral over $(0,\infty)$ has precisely the displayed lower-end term. Summing $i=j$ gives only $\int_\Pi E_{I,3}\eta_2=\varepsilon_0^{-1}\int_\Pi\sigma\eta$, since $\operatorname{div}E_I=0$ away from $p$. Taking the antisymmetric derivative combinations gives the sheet term $e_3\times E_+=0$, because the smooth zero plane trace of $\varphi_I$ has zero horizontal derivatives; the classical curl also vanishes away from $p$ by step 1.2. Adding the two test pieces gives $\operatorname{div}\widetilde E=(q\delta_p+\sigma\delta_\Pi)/\varepsilon_0$ and $\operatorname{curl}\widetilde E=0$ in $\mathcal D'(\mathbb R^3)$. No limit at infinity or unproved divergence theorem on a cornered truncation is used. [F1, F2, F6, F8, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 2.2]
