items/pthm-fd-no-slip-cfd-observations.md:77:The physical weak stress pairing uses $\varphi(x):=v(x/\ell_0)\in H^1_0(\Omega_{\mathrm{phys}};\mathbb R^3)$ and is $2\mu_0\int_{\Omega_{\mathrm{phys}}}D_{\mathrm{phys}}:\varepsilon_x(\varphi)\,dx$, where $\varepsilon_x(\varphi)=(\nabla_x\varphi+\nabla_x\varphi^{\mathsf T})/2$; the declared scaling gives the displayed $2\nu^*$ strain term. The MINI interface's real skew convective form is $c(a;v,w)=\tfrac12\int_{D^*}\bigl((a\cdot\nabla)v\cdot w-(a\cdot\nabla)w\cdot v\bigr)\,dy$; for the divergence-free, zero-trace $u^*$ above, integration by parts gives $c(u^*;u^*,v)=\int_{D^*}(u^*\cdot\nabla)u^*\cdot v$. Thus the displayed weak equation is exactly the continuous exact-field equation in the MINI interface. This weak formulation is explicitly adopted at the stated Sobolev regularity; those hypotheses do not imply the physical model definition's classical $C^2/C^1$ regularity. It asserts no extension to weak limits or lower-regularity solutions.
items/ex-em-causal-response-and-radiation-prerequisites-hypothesis-boundaries.md:70:1.1 The kernel $K(s)=-e^{-s}$ is of class $C^1$ on $[0,\infty)$ with $K'=e^{-s}=-K$ by [F1] and [F6]; $K(s)<0$ for every $s$, and $|K(s)|=e^{-s}$, $|K'(s)|=e^{-s}$, $|sK(s)|=se^{-s}$. For $N>0$, the functions $G(s)=-e^{-s}$ and $u(s)=s$ are smooth on $\mathbb R$, so their restrictions are continuous on $[0,N]$, differentiable there (relative to the interval at endpoints), and have continuous, hence Riemann-integrable derivatives. Thus [F2] applies to $G$ and, for integration by parts, to $u$ and $v=G$: it gives $\int_0^Ne^{-s}ds=1-e^{-N}$ and $\int_0^Nse^{-s}ds=1-(1+N)e^{-N}$, and converts these to Lebesgue integrals; the nonnegative exponential series gives $e^N\ge N^2/2$ by [F1], so $e^{-N}\to0$ and $Ne^{-N}\le2/N\to0$; since the integrands are nonnegative, [F3] lets integer $N\to\infty$, giving $\int_0^\infty e^{-s}ds=1$ and $\int_0^\infty se^{-s}ds=1$. Hence $K,K',sK\in L^1(0,\infty)$ and, by [F5], $K$ is locally absolutely continuous on $[0,\infty)$. [F1, F2, F3, F5, F6]
items/lem-fd-2d-fourier-log-velocity-bound.md:58:1.1 In each periodic coefficient integral, one-coordinate integration by parts has boundary term $f(x)e^{-ik_jx_j}|_{0}^{2\pi}=0$ because both factors are periodic, so $\widehat{\partial_j f}(k)=ik_j\widehat f(k)$. Thus $k\cdot\widehat u(k)=0$ for $k\ne0$ and $\widehat\omega(k)=i(k_1\widehat u_2(k)-k_2\widehat u_1(k))$. Since a vector in $\mathbb C^2$ orthogonal to the nonzero real vector $k$ is a complex multiple of $(-k_2,k_1)$, these identities imply $|\widehat\omega(k)|=|k|\,|\widehat u(k)|$. [given, A1, F1, algebra]
items/lem-nrqm-intervals-free-line-resolvent.md:93:2.1 [$G_z*f$ solves the resolvent equation] Let $f\in L^2(\mathbb R)$ and $u:=G_z*f$. By [F3], $u\in L^2$ with $\|u\|_{L^2}\le\|G_z\|_{L^1}\|f\|_{L^2}$ and $\|u\|_\infty\le\|G_z\|_{L^2}\|f\|_{L^2}$. We show that $u$ has weak first derivative $w:=DG_z*f$: for a test function $\varphi$, Fubini for the absolutely integrable integrand $|G_z(x-y)f(y)\varphi'(x)|$ (its integral is at most $\|G_z\|_{L^1}\|f\|_{L^2}\|\varphi'\|_{L^2}$) gives $\int_{\mathbb R}u(x)\varphi'(x)dx =\int_{\mathbb R}f(y)\Bigl(\int_{\mathbb R}G_z(x-y)\varphi'(x)dx\Bigr)dy .$ For fixed $y$, the inner integral equals $-\int_{\mathbb R}DG_z(x-y)\varphi(x)dx$ by [F2] and the integration by parts identity [[lem-nrqm-intervals-integration-by-parts]] clause (d) applied to the $H^1$ classes $G_z(\cdot-y)$ and $\varphi$. Substituting and applying Fubini again gives $\int u\varphi'=-\int w\varphi$ for every test $\varphi$, so $w=Du$ is the weak first derivative of $u$. [step 1.1, F2, F3, given, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-controlled-dynamics-airy-contour-and-fresnel-normalization.md:67:[F2] **Deformation and oscillatory representation.** On the joining arc $t=Re^{i(\pi/2-s)}$, $0\le s\le\pi/6$, the cubic real part is $-R^3\sin(3s)/3\le-cR^3s$, while $|\operatorname{Re}(zt)|\le KRs$ for real $|z|\le K$. Consequently the arc integral, with an extra factor $t^k$, is $O_K(R^{k-2})$ for $k=0,1$; the lower arc has the same bound. These arcs vanish for precisely the orders needed for the value and first derivative representation. Integration by parts against $t^2+z$ on sufficiently distant real tails proves uniform improper convergence on compact $z$-sets for amplitudes $1$ and $t$. For higher orders use the entire contour integral of [F1]. Distributional integration is also valid: pairing against a compactly supported smooth function of $z$ replaces the oscillatory amplitude by its rapidly decreasing Fourier transform, so all differentiated integrals converge after pairing ([[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]], [[thm-fourier-inversion-on-schwartz-space]], algebra).
items/thm-cm-endpoint-action-differential.md:99:1.1 At each $(t,q)\in T\times Q$, the compact integration segment lies in open $D$. A finite cover by product neighbourhoods yields a common parameter neighbourhood and an open $s$-interval containing that segment where $G$ is $C^1$; shrinking the parameter neighbourhood also covers nearby upper limits. Thus [F2] applies locally, with the integral oriented if $t<t_0$, and $W$ is $C^1$. Put $J_q:=\partial_qX$ and $P(s):=\mathcal L_v(s,X,\partial_sX)$, both evaluated at fixed $(t,q)$. Using column gradients, $$W_q=\int_{t_0}^{t}\bigl[J_q^{\mathsf T}\mathcal L_q+(\partial_sJ_q)^{\mathsf T}P\bigr]ds=[J_q^{\mathsf T}P]_{t_0}^{t}+\int_{t_0}^{t}J_q^{\mathsf T}(\mathcal L_q-\partial_sP)ds=[J_q^{\mathsf T}P]_{t_0}^{t}.$$ The middle identity is componentwise integration by parts [F3], and the last uses [F4]. [F1, F2, F3, F4, algebra]
