items/pthm-fd-px07-prediction.md:102:[F2] [[def-fd-x00]]: for constants $\rho_0,\mu>0$, the formal pointwise incompressible Navier–Stokes equation is $\partial_tu+(u\cdot\nabla)u+\rho_0^{-1}\nabla p-(\mu/\rho_0)\Delta u=0$, $\operatorname{div}u=0$; its steady Stokes system omits the time derivative and convection. This is mathematical notation and asserts no physical solution.
items/pthm-fd-px07-prediction.md:108:[F5] [[def-fd-creeping-stokes-model]]: the current Definition selects $\operatorname{div}u_{\rm S}=0$, $\sigma_{\rm S}=-p_{\rm S}I_3+2\mu D(u_{\rm S})$, and $\operatorname{div}\sigma_{\rm S}+\rho_0b=0$, equivalently $-\nabla p_{\rm S}+\mu\Delta u_{\rm S}+\rho_0b=0$. For this consumer $b=0$, so it gives the stated restricted Stokes equation. Its current carrier was inspected and the interface matches this zero-body-acceleration specialization.
items/pthm-fd-px07-prediction.md:116:1.1 Within the adopted continuum, wall, and Newtonian assumptions [A1]–[A3] and Cartesian SI conventions [F1], take the full Navier–Stokes equation as the explicit strong-solution hypothesis [A4], not as a consequence of the integral balances alone. For $u_{\rm S}$, the current model Definition [F5] and zero-body-force Postulate specialization [A5] give $\operatorname{div}u_{\rm S}=0$ and $\operatorname{div}\sigma_{\rm S}=0$. With constant $\mu$, componentwise differentiation gives $(\operatorname{div}\sigma_{\rm S})_i=-\partial_i p_{\rm S}+\mu\sum_j(\partial_{jj}u_{{\rm S},i}+\partial_j\partial_i u_{{\rm S},j})=-\partial_i p_{\rm S}+\mu\Delta u_{{\rm S},i}+\mu\partial_i(\operatorname{div}u_{\rm S})=-\partial_i p_{\rm S}+\mu\Delta u_{{\rm S},i}$, so $-\mu\Delta u_{\rm S}+\nabla p_{\rm S}=0$. Set $w=u_{\rm NS}-u_{\rm S}$ and $\pi=p_{\rm NS}-p_{\rm S}$. The shared boundary data give $w|_{\partial\Omega}=0$, and the divergence-free assumptions give $\nabla\cdot w=0$. Rearranging the Navier–Stokes equation gives $-\mu\Delta u_{\rm NS}+\nabla p_{\rm NS}=f$. Subtracting yields $-\mu\Delta w+\nabla\pi=f$, with the exact smoothness and Poincare hypotheses of [F3]. [given, A1, A2, A3, A4, A5, F1, F2, F3, F5, algebra]
items/pthm-fd-px07-prediction.md:126:The deduction is conditional on the strong-solution hypotheses and the adopted restricted Stokes definition recorded in Facts & Assumptions. The zero-inertia force balance is a physical assumption; its algebraically equivalent form does not prove that assumption. No pointwise equations are inferred from the integral balance postulate alone, and no empirical claim is made.
items/thm-qftx-tempered-massive-gaussian-field.md:137:2.1 For a finite initial segment $I_N$ of the fixed enumeration set $S_N=\sum_{\alpha\in I_N}(1+|\alpha|)^{-4}\xi_\alpha^2$. The density in [F5] is normalized, and integration by parts using $(-xe^{-x^2/2})'=(x^2-1)e^{-x^2/2}$ gives $\mathbb E\xi_\alpha^2=1$. Hence $\mathbb E S_N=\sum_{\alpha\in I_N}(1+|\alpha|)^{-4}\le\sum_{n\ge0}(n+1)(n+1)^{-4}=\sum_{n\ge0}(n+1)^{-3}=:C<\infty$: grouping $n+1$ in dyadic blocks $[2^j,2^{j+1})$ bounds each block by $2^j2^{-3j}=2^{-2j}$, a summable geometric series. Markov's inequality, which follows from $\mathbf1_{\{S_N>M\}}\le S_N/M$, gives $\mathbb P(S_N>M)\le C/M$. Since the events increase to $\{S:=\sum_\alpha(1+|\alpha|)^{-4}\xi_\alpha^2>M\}$, countable additivity gives $\mathbb P(S>M)\le C/M$; letting integer $M\to\infty$ shows $S<\infty$ on a common event $\Omega_0$ of probability one. [F5, F6, step 1.1, algebra]
items/ex-fd-bgk-initial-layer.md:78:[F2] [[thm-integration-by-parts]] and [[thm-nonnegative-improper-riemann-integral-agrees-with-the-lebesgue-integral-on-a-half-line]]: The centered one-dimensional Gaussian has mean zero by oddness and variance $\sigma_i^2$ by integration by parts on compact truncations and passage to the limit. Under the adopted Countable Choice, the nonnegative improper integrals equal their Lebesgue values; their factors are continuous on every compact truncation, with reflection used on the negative half-line.
items/ex-em-dipole-antenna-example.md:82:[L5] Constructions and integration by parts in Euclidean space: a nonnegative $C^\infty$ bump of unit mass supported in a prescribed ball exists; a smooth compact time cutoff equal to $1$ near a chosen period exists; for compactly supported $C^1$ fields the divergence theorem gives $\int_{\mathbb R^3}\partial_jF\,dy=0$, hence $\int_{\mathbb R^3}y_i\partial_j\sigma\,dy=-\delta_{ij}\int_{\mathbb R^3}\sigma\,dy$. ([[cor-c-one-change-of-variables-for-l-one-functions]], [[def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump]], [[lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization]], [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]], [[thm-integration-by-parts]])
items/thm-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-smooth-persistence.md:107:2.1 (Higher energy controlled by the lower norm.) Define $E_n=\sum_{|\alpha|\le n}\int(D^\alpha z)^{\mathsf T}A^0(z)D^\alpha z$. The energy identity of [L5] applies at order $n$: its derivation uses only smoothness, symmetry, projection commutation and integration by parts. The errors are commutators with $g$ and $\partial_i z$, the $H^n$ composition of $B$, and quadratic terms with $\dot A^0$ and $\partial_i A^i$. By [L2](e), step 1.1 and the lower $H^{n-2}$ bounds, each commutator and the $B$ term has $L^2$ norm at most $C_\rho(1+\|z\|_{H^n})$. Total coefficient derivatives have bounded $L^\infty$ norms by the lower bounds for $z,g$. Pairing with $D^\alpha z$, summing and using $2xy\le x^2+y^2$ gives $|E_n'|\le C_\rho(1+E_n)$ and $a\|z\|_{H^n}^2\le E_n\le b\|z\|_{H^n}^2$, uniformly in $N$. This establishes the high-order refinement before applying induction to the limit. [step 1.1, L2, L5, algebra]
items/rem-fd-kinetic-limit-status.md:41:The Euler-passage theorem in this collection assumes weighted strong convergence to local Maxwellians and proves only the resulting conservation-law passage. The separate homogeneous BGK theorem gives an explicit relaxation solution and its limit. Neither result proves convergence for general inhomogeneous Boltzmann solutions, a Boltzmann–Grad limit from deterministic many-particle dynamics, or a Navier–Stokes–Fourier limit. Those questions require additional hypotheses and separate theorem chains.
items/rem-fd-kinetic-limit-status.md:61:2.1 The second theorem [F2] has no spatial dependence and uses the BGK operator, so its exact solution does not supply an existence or convergence argument for inhomogeneous Boltzmann dynamics. Neither supplier states a many-particle Boltzmann–Grad or Navier–Stokes–Fourier result; this remark records only the limits of these two carriers. [F1, F2] ∎
items/lem-cm-sine-coefficient-decay-by-integration-by-parts.md:4:title: "Repeated integration by parts gives $|b_n(F)|\\le 2L^5M_5/(\\pi^5n^5)$ for odd $2L$-periodic $C^5$ functions and $|b_n(G)|\\le 2L^4M_4/(\\pi^4n^4)$ for $C^4$"
items/lem-cm-sine-coefficient-decay-by-integration-by-parts.md:66:1.1 Put $I_0:=\int_0^LF(x)\sin(n\pi x/L)dx$ and $I_j:=\int_0^LF^{(j)}(x)\cdot\bigl(\sin\text{ if }j\text{ even},\ \cos\text{ if }j\text{ odd}\bigr)(n\pi x/L)dx$ for $j=1,\dots,5$. Applying [F1] successively, each integration by parts produces the boundary term $$\bigl[F^{(j)}(x)\cdot(\pm\tfrac{L}{n\pi})(\text{the other trigonometric factor})\bigr]_0^L ,$$ which vanishes by [F2]: at $j$ even the boundary factor is $\cos$, nonzero at the endpoints, but the multiplying derivative $F^{(j)}$ is odd, hence vanishes at $0$ and $L$; at $j$ odd the boundary factor is $\sin$, which vanishes at $0$ and $L$. Therefore $I_0=\frac{L}{n\pi}I_1$, $I_1=-\frac{L}{n\pi}I_2$, $I_2=\frac{L}{n\pi}I_3$, $I_3=-\frac{L}{n\pi}I_4$, $I_4=\frac{L}{n\pi}I_5$, and chaining, $$I_0=\Bigl(\frac{L}{n\pi}\Bigr)^5I_5 .$$ [given, F1, F2, algebra]
items/def-emg-fx-nonlinear-lagrangian.md:70:A permitted variation is specified by a smooth compactly supported global real one-form $a\in\Omega^1(M)$, with the same component units as each $A_i$ if units are attached. For a dimensionless parameter $\epsilon\in\mathbb R$, define $A_{i,\epsilon}:=A_i+\epsilon a|_{U_i}$. Because $a$ is global, these local representatives retain the same transition functions and define a smooth connection $\mathcal A_\epsilon$ in the same bundle sector. Equivalently, the normalized local representatives shift as $a_{i,\epsilon}=a_i+\epsilon\kappa_0^{-1}a|_{U_i}$. Thus $F_\epsilon|_{U_i}=dA_{i,\epsilon}=F|_{U_i}+\epsilon da$, so globally $F_\epsilon=F+\epsilon da$. The shorthand $\mathcal A_\epsilon=\mathcal A+\epsilon a$ denotes this affine shift of local physical-potential representatives; it does not assert direct addition of a base one-form to a principal-bundle connection one-form on its total space.
items/def-emg-fx-nonlinear-lagrangian.md:76:The source-free local action density is $L(X,Y)\,\mathrm{vol}_g$. For a continuous compactly supported top form, use the finite chart-partition integral $\mathcal I_M$ of the continuous-integral definition linked below; it agrees with the usual manifold integral on smooth forms. This convention retains Countable Choice, meaning that every countable family of nonempty sets has a choice function, as inherited from the smooth integral. Its relative action difference under a compact variation is $\mathcal I_M([L(X(F_\epsilon),Y(F_\epsilon))-L(X(F),Y(F))]\,\mathrm{vol}_g)$; this difference has compact support even when the unvaried integral over $M$ is not finite. The connection is stationary when the derivative of this relative action at $\epsilon=0$ vanishes for every permitted $a$.
items/def-emg-fx-nonlinear-lagrangian.md:102:[F4] [[def-emg-fx-continuous-compact-top-form-integral]] defines a linear, local finite chart-partition integral for continuous compactly supported top forms, proves chart and cutoff independence directly for continuous coefficients, and gives the finite rectangle bound that permits uniform coefficient limits to pass through the integral. Under the stated Countable Choice convention ([[def-countable-choice]]), it agrees on smooth forms with [[def-integral-of-a-compactly-supported-top-form-on-an-oriented-manifold]] and [[thm-global-form-integration-is-independent-of-the-atlas-partition-and-refinement]]. Those imported smooth-only statements are used only for this compatibility, not to assert smoothness of a density made from a merely $C^2$ Lagrangian.
items/def-emg-fx-nonlinear-lagrangian.md:110:2.1 For a permitted compactly supported variation $a$, [F3] gives $F_\epsilon-F=\epsilon da$, which vanishes off $\operatorname{supp}a$. Hence the difference of the two $L$ densities is compactly supported. Its coefficient is at least continuous, so the integral $\mathcal I_M$ in [F4] is finite without requiring a smooth density. On finitely many fixed chart rectangles near that support, the chain rule gives continuous parameter derivatives; their uniform convergence of difference quotients on compact rectangles allows passage to the integrals by the rectangle bound in [F4]. Hence its first variation is well-defined for $L\in C^2$, with no stronger regularity assumption. Thus the Definition specifies a relative action and stationarity predicate without assuming the unvaried action is finite or that a stationary connection exists. [given, F3, F4, A1, algebra] ∎
