items/thm-tgr-supported-static-heat-evolution.md:156:5.1 Put $w=\vartheta-\overline\vartheta$, so $\int_\Omega w=0$. Expanding the square and using [F11] to integrate the continuous product over $\Omega\times\Omega$ gives $$\int_\Omega\int_\Omega(w(x)-w(y))^2\,dx\,dy=2|\Omega|\int_\Omega w^2\,dx-2\left(\int_\Omega w\,dx\right)^2=2|\Omega|\|w\|_2^2.$$ Define $b_1=w(x_1,x_2,x_3)-w(y_1,x_2,x_3)$, $b_2=w(y_1,x_2,x_3)-w(y_1,y_2,x_3)$, and $b_3=w(y_1,y_2,x_3)-w(y_1,y_2,y_3)$, so $w(x)-w(y)=b_1+b_2+b_3$. For each $i$, the fundamental theorem of calculus expresses $b_i$ as the integral of $\partial_iw$ over an interval of length at most $L_i$; [F11] therefore gives $|b_i|^2\le L_i\int_0^{L_i}|\partial_iw|^2\,dx_i$. Integrating this bound over $x,y$ gives $$\int_{\Omega\times\Omega}|b_i|^2\,dx\,dy\le |\Omega|L_i^2\int_\Omega|\partial_iw|^2\,dx,$$ because the two unused coordinates in direction $i$ contribute $L_i^2$, one unused coordinate in each other direction contributes $\prod_{j\ne i}L_j$, and the Cauchy–Schwarz factor contributes the remaining $L_i$. Since $(b_1+b_2+b_3)^2\le3\sum_i b_i^2$, division by $2|\Omega|$ yields $$\|w\|_2^2\le\frac32\sum_iL_i^2\|\partial_iw\|_2^2\le C_\Omega\|\nabla_xw\|_2^2,$$ where $C_\Omega=\frac32(\max_iL_i)^2$. For $t_K>0$, integration by parts and $\partial_\nu w=0$ give $$\frac12\frac{d}{dt_K}\|w\|_2^2=-\frac{A_0}{C_V}\|\nabla_xw\|_2^2\le-\frac{A_0}{C_VC_\Omega}\|w\|_2^2.$$ Multiplying by $\exp(2A_0t_K/(C_VC_\Omega))$ and differentiating proves the exponential estimate after taking square roots; continuity at $t_K=0$ supplies the initial norm. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 4.2, A2, F10, F11, algebra]
items/pthm-nrqm-potentials-barrier-flux-and-transmission.md:138:[F3] **The model's generalized-solution reading.** A declared generalized stationary solution has real energy, is bounded and classically $C^{1}$ with continuous derivative, is $C^{2}$ on each interval where $V$ is continuous, and satisfies the stationary equation there. It also satisfies the global weak stationary equation of that definition; for a piecewise constant potential, the matching $C^1$ piecewise $C^2$ solutions satisfy it by integration by parts on the pieces, with internal boundary terms cancelling. A generalized solution can belong to $L^2$; exclusion from the preparation space must therefore be checked for the particular scattering solution. Its declared amplitude has dimension $\ell^{-1/2}$, so its continuous current is read in $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, and the scattering reading uses the declared current ratios ([[def-nrqm-potentials-one-dimensional-line-model]] clause (e), [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]] clauses (d)1 and (d)5).
