items/pthm-em-coulomb-solution.md:156:3.1 Coulomb representation: since Lebesgue measure is translation invariant, $N\rho(x)=\int\Phi(x-y)\rho(y)\,d\lambda_3(y)=\int\Phi(z)\rho(x-z)\,d\lambda_3(z)$ ([F6]); fix $i\in\{1,2,3\}$, $x_0\in\mathbb R^3$, $\delta>0$ and the interval $J=(-\delta,\delta)$ of coordinate increments, so $x_0+se_i\in B(x_0,\delta)$ for $s\in J$. For such $x$ the integrand $\Phi(z)\rho(x-z)$ is supported in the compact ball $K:=\overline B_{|x_0|+\delta+R}(0)$, and its $s$-derivative $\Phi(z)\partial_i\rho(x-z)$ is dominated on $J$ by $\|\nabla\rho\|_\infty|\Phi(z)|\mathbf 1_K(z)$, which is integrable ([F5], [F8]); differentiation under the integral sign [F6] therefore gives $\partial_iN\rho(x)=\int\Phi(z)\partial_i\rho(x-z)\,d\lambda_3(z)$. For fixed $x$ put $h(z):=\rho(x-z)$, a $C^1$ compactly supported function; then $\partial_i\rho(x-z)=-\partial_{z_i}h(z)$, so $$\partial_iN\rho(x)=-\int\Phi(z)\partial_{z_i}h(z)\,d\lambda_3(z).$$ The kernel $\Phi$ is locally integrable, continuous and smooth off the single point $0$, its classical partials are locally integrable, so [[lem-em-distributional-gradient-smooth-off-a-point]] applies with $u=\Phi$ and gives $\partial_iT_\Phi=T_{\partial_i\Phi}$; This identity is initially paired only with smooth tests: for $h_\delta$ of [F11], $\int\Phi\partial_i h_\delta=-\int\partial_i\Phi\,h_\delta$ by [F7]. Since $h\in C_c^1$, [F11] supplies uniform convergence of both $h_\delta$ and $\partial_i h_\delta$ on a fixed compact support; the two locally integrable kernels therefore allow the limit $\int\Phi\partial_i h=-\int\partial_i\Phi\,h$, without pairing any distribution against a nonsmooth test. Substituting back and translating gives $$\partial_iN\rho(x)=\int\partial_i\Phi(x-y)\rho(y)\,d\lambda_3(y)=-\frac{1}{4\pi}\int\frac{x_i-y_i}{|x-y|^3}\rho(y)\,d\lambda_3(y)$$ for every $x\in\mathbb R^3$, the integral being absolutely convergent because $\operatorname{supp}\rho\subseteq\overline B_R$ lies in the ball $B(x,|x|+2R)$ and $\int_{B(x,|x|+2R)}|x-y|^{-2}d\lambda_3(y)=4\pi(|x|+2R)<\infty$, while $|\rho|\le\|\rho\|_\infty$ on the support ([F1], [F5], [F8]). Multiplying by $-\varepsilon_0^{-1}$ gives exactly the displayed Coulomb representation of $E=-\nabla\varphi$. [step 2.1, F1, F5, F6, F7, F8, F11]
items/lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain.md:79:3.1 For every $\varphi\in D_0$, integration by parts gives $\int u_n\overline{\partial_j\varphi}=-\int (\partial_j u_n)\overline\varphi$. Passing to the limit in both integrals by $L^2$ convergence and Cauchy–Schwarz yields $\int u\overline{\partial_j\varphi}=-\int g_j\overline\varphi$, so $g_j$ is the stated weak derivative. [step 2.2, F2, F3, algebra]
