[F2] [[def-qftx-principal-connection-form]] and [[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi]]: local curvature is $F_i=dA_i+\frac12[A_i\wedge A_i]$, and the exterior covariant derivative is $D_{A_i}\eta=d\eta+[A_i\wedge\eta]$ with the graded bracket convention in the definition.

[F3] [[def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket]]: the bracket is graded skew and satisfies the graded derivation identity for the exterior derivative.

[F4] [[def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative]]: the adjoint representation gives local adjoint-bundle coordinates and the induced local covariant derivative.

[F5] [[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]]: on an open Minkowski coordinate region, compactly supported $C^1$ coefficients admit differentiation under the integral sign and integration by parts; if a continuous scalar coefficient integrates to zero against every smooth compactly supported scalar test, it vanishes pointwise.

[F6] [[prop-adjoint-is-a-smooth-lie-group-representation]] and [[thm-the-differential-of-adjoint-is-ad]]: $\operatorname{Ad}$ is a smooth representation and its differential is $\operatorname{ad}$, so a varying local transition map differentiates by its Lie-algebra-valued Maurer–Cartan form.

## Proof

1.1 In one local section, expand the Cartan formula at $A_i+\varepsilon a$:
$F_{A_i+\varepsilon a}
=F_i+\varepsilon(da+[A_i\wedge a])
+\frac{\varepsilon^2}{2}[a\wedge a]
=F_i+\varepsilon D_{A_i}a+\frac{\varepsilon^2}{2}[a\wedge a]$.
Here $[A_i\wedge a]=[a\wedge A_i]$ because both inputs have degree one and the graded bracket is symmetric in that degree. [F2, F3, algebra]

1.2 The difference of the two action densities vanishes outside the compact support of $a$ and its derivatives. Apply the parameter-differentiation clause of [F5] to its smooth coordinate coefficients. Symmetry of $\kappa$ and the even degree of $F_i$ then give
$\left.\frac{d}{d\varepsilon}\right|_0\Delta(S/\hbar)_i(\varepsilon;a)
=-\frac{1}{g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2}\int
\kappa(D_{A_i}a\wedge *_\eta F_i)$. [A1, A3, A4, F1, F5, step 1.1, algebra]

2.1 In a Lie-algebra basis, differentiate the scalar-valued form
$\kappa(a\wedge *_\eta F_i)$. The ordinary graded product rule gives
$\kappa(da\wedge *_\eta F_i)-\kappa(a\wedge d *_\eta F_i)$. Invariance of
$\kappa$, together with moving the degree-one factors $A_i$ and $a$ past
one another, gives
$\kappa([A_i\wedge a]\wedge *_\eta F_i)
=\kappa(a\wedge[A_i\wedge *_\eta F_i])$. Therefore
$d\kappa(a\wedge *_\eta F_i)
=\kappa(D_{A_i}a\wedge *_\eta F_i)
-\kappa(a\wedge D_{A_i} *_\eta F_i)$. [F1, F2, F3, algebra]

3.1 Write the compactly supported scalar three-form as
$\kappa(a\wedge *_\eta F_i)=\sum_{\mu=0}^3h_\mu\widehat{dx^\mu}$ with
$h_\mu\in C_c^1(U_i)$. Its exterior derivative is
$\sum_\mu(\partial_\mu h_\mu)d^4x$. Apply the integration-by-parts clause
of [F5] with the constant function $1$ to each coefficient $h_\mu$; each
integral is zero, so $\int d\kappa(a\wedge *_\eta F_i)=0$. Substitution in
step 1.2 yields
$\left.\frac{d}{d\varepsilon}\right|_0\Delta(S/\hbar)_i(\varepsilon;a)
=-\frac{1}{g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2}\int
\kappa(a\wedge D_{A_i} *_\eta F_i)$. [A1, A4, F5, step 1.2, step 2.1]

4.1 If $D_{A_i} *_\eta F_i=0$, step 3.1 shows that the first variation vanishes for every compactly supported $a$ in every chart. Thus the connection is stationary. [A3, step 3.1, algebra]

4.2 Conversely, assume stationarity. In a local coordinate chart and the orthonormal basis from [F1], write
$J_i=D_{A_i} *_\eta F_i
=\sum_{\mu=0}^3\sum_{b=1}^3J_{\mu b}T_b\,\widehat{dx^\mu}$,
where $\widehat{dx^\mu}$ is the oriented coordinate three-form satisfying
$dx^\mu\wedge\widehat{dx^\mu}=d^4x$. For fixed $\mu,b$, choose
$a=\psi T_b dx^\mu$ with arbitrary smooth compactly supported real $\psi$.
Stationarity and step 3.1 give $\int\psi J_{\mu b}\,d^4x=0$ for every such
$\psi$. The fundamental-lemma clause of [F5] implies $J_{\mu b}=0$
throughout the chart. All coefficients vanish, so the local equation holds.
[A4, F1, F5, step 3.1, algebra]

5.1 It remains to check that the local equations agree on overlaps. If
$s_j=s_i g_{ij}$, then [F2] gives
$A_j=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}A_i+\theta_L(g_{ij})$ and
$(*_\eta F_j)=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}(*_\eta F_i)$. For
$X\in T_x(U_i\cap U_j)$, put $Z=\theta_L(g_{ij})(X)$ and use the path
$g_{ij}\exp(tZ)$ with initial tangent $X$. Differentiating the adjoint
representation by [F6] gives
$d(\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}})(X)
=-\operatorname{ad}_Z\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}$. Hence
$d(\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}} *_\eta F_i)
=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}d(*_\eta F_i)
-[\theta_L(g_{ij})\wedge\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}(*_\eta F_i)]$.
The added Maurer–Cartan term cancels the corresponding term in
$[A_j\wedge *_\eta F_j]$, leaving
$D_{A_j} *_\eta F_j
=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}(D_{A_i} *_\eta F_i)$.
Thus the local zero equations define one global adjoint-bundle equation.
[F2, F4, F6, step 4.2, algebra] ∎
## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The trivial-bundle specialization of the adopted pure $SU(2)$ model and the constant $a\ne0$.

[F1] [[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]: the spacetime metric is $\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ with the stated orientation.

[A1] [[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]: the action density is $-\kappa(F\wedge *_\eta F)/(2g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2)$ with fixed $g_{\mathrm{YM}}>0$.

[F2] [[lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-invariant-form]]: $[T_1,T_2]=T_3$ and $\kappa(T_3,T_3)=1$.

[F3] [[def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket]] and [[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi]]: local curvature is $F=dA+\frac12[A\wedge A]$ with the alternating-form bracket convention.

## Verification

1.1 The coefficients of $A$ are constant, so $dA=0$. The degree-one bracket convention gives
$\frac12[A\wedge A](\partial_1,\partial_2)
=[aT_1,aT_2]=a^2T_3$,
while its other independent coordinate components vanish. Thus
$F=a^2T_3\,dx^1\wedge dx^2$. If $a=0$, this same formula gives zero
curvature; the Statement assumes $a\ne0$. [F2, F3, algebra]

1.2 For the stated orientation and metric,
$*_\eta(dx^1\wedge dx^2)=dx^0\wedge dx^3$. Therefore
$\kappa(F\wedge *_\eta F)
=a^4\kappa(T_3,T_3)\,
dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^0\wedge dx^3
=a^4\,dx^0\wedge dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3$;
the last reordering is even. Multiplying by the adopted coefficient gives
the stated action density. This verifies only a configuration and its
nonzero density; no Euler–Lagrange equation is asserted. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The adopted pure $SU(2)$ model specialized to its trivial bundle, canonical section, and length parameter $\ell>0$.

[F1] [[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]: each model instance may select the trivial bundle $P=\mathbb R^{1,3}\times SU(2)$, and the local curvature convention is $F=dA+\frac12[A\wedge A]$.

[F2] [[lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-invariant-form]]: $T_3$ lies in $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ and $[T_3,T_3]=0$.

[F3] [[def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket]] and [[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi]]: if two local sections satisfy $s_j=s_i g_{ij}$, then $A_j=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}A_i+g_{ij}^{-1}dg_{ij}$ and $F_j=dA_j+\frac12[A_j\wedge A_j]$.

[A1] [[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]: the action density is $-\kappa(F\wedge *_\eta F)/(2g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2)$.

## Verification

1.1 The section-transition law in [F3], applied to $s^u=su$ and $A=0$, gives
$A^u=u^{-1}du$. Since $u=\exp(T_3\sin(x^0/\ell))$ and $T_3$ commutes with itself, differentiation yields
$u^{-1}du=T_3\ell^{-1}\cos(x^0/\ell)\,dx^0$. If the phase were constant,
the same formula would give the zero potential; the chosen sine has
nonzero derivative, so $A^u$ is nonzero. [F1, F2, F3, algebra]

1.2 The coefficient of $A^u$ depends only on $x^0$ and its one-form factor is $dx^0$, so $dA^u=0$. Every value of $A^u$ is proportional to $T_3$, hence $[A^u\wedge A^u]=0$ by [F2]. The Cartan formula in [F3] gives $F^u=0$, and therefore $\kappa(F^u\wedge *_\eta F^u)=0$. The asserted zero density is exact for this classical field. [A1, F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra] ∎

[Matthijs Vákár, Principal Bundles and Gauge Theories](https://arxiv.org/html/2110.06334)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The trivial-bundle specialization of the adopted pure $SU(2)$ model, $b\ne0$, and $\ell>0$.

[F1] [[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]: the metric is $\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, the orientation is $dx^0\wedge dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3$, and the action density uses the positive invariant form $\kappa$.

[F2] [[lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-invariant-form]]: $T_3\in\mathfrak{su}(2)$ and $\kappa(T_3,T_3)=1$.

[F3] [[def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket]] and [[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi]]: $F=dA+\frac12[A\wedge A]$ and $D_A\eta=d\eta+[A\wedge\eta]$.

[F4] [[pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-euler-lagrange]]: within the adopted model, the source-free equation $D_A(*_\eta F)=0$ is equivalent to stationarity under all smooth compactly supported connection variations.

[A1] [[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]: the local action density is $-\kappa(F\wedge *_\eta F)/(2g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2)$.

## Verification

1.1 Write $q=x^0-x^3$. Since the coefficient depends only on $q$,
$dA=T_3(b/\ell)\cos(q/\ell)(dx^0-dx^3)\wedge dx^1$. The bracket term
vanishes because $[T_3,T_3]=0$, so this is $F$; it is not identically zero
because $b\ne0$; if $b=0$, both the potential and curvature vanish.
[F1, F2, F3, algebra]

1.2 The Lorentzian Hodge operator for the stated orientation gives
$*_\eta((dx^0-dx^3)\wedge dx^1)=-(dx^0-dx^3)\wedge dx^2$. Hence
$*_\eta F=-T_3(b/\ell)\cos(q/\ell)(dx^0-dx^3)\wedge dx^2$. Its exterior
derivative vanishes because differentiating its coefficient contributes a
factor $(dx^0-dx^3)$ already present in the wedge product; the covariant
bracket term also vanishes because both Lie-algebra factors are $T_3$.
Thus $D_A(*_\eta F)=0$. The same repeated null one-form gives
$F\wedge *_\eta F=0$, so the action density vanishes. [A1, F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]

2.1 By [F4] and step 1.2, the connection is stationary under every
admissible compactly supported variation. The curvature is nonzero by step
1.1, so this is a nontrivial classical solution with zero Lorentzian
quadratic density. [F1, F4, step 1.1, step 1.2] ∎

