## Scope and plan comparison

The assigned pair is qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model and its examples companion, order 975, in advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry, library physics. The canonical plan entry at research/plan-spec.json and the complete design section at research/plan-advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry-track.md lines 261–273 agree on the page IDs, title, category, library, scope, required pages, and three planned physical item IDs. No design/plan conflict was found.

The dated scaffold inventory's page_pairs row lists only qftx-gauge-geometry in requires; the canonical plan and current batch-19 manifest also require the published qft-c01 page. The plan and dispatch control, so batch 19 retains both page prerequisites. The scaffold's external supplier list names def-qft-framework-units and post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement; only the former is used, for its exact four-dimensional natural-action normalization. The measurement postulate is not used: this carrier adopts a classical model with no matter, apparatus, measurement, or empirical premise.

## Inspected prerequisite interfaces

The current draft items def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data, def-qftx-principal-connection-form, def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket, lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi, and def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative were read. Current loadStep1Record / step1Decision rows bind the first three at level 0 and ready, the curvature/Bianchi lemma at level 1 and ready, and the associated covariant derivative at level 1 and ready. In particular, the curvature lemma's actual right-action convention is
A_j = Ad_(g_ij^-1) A_i + theta_L(g_ij),
F_i = dA_i + 1/2[A_i wedge A_i],
and F_j = Ad_(g_ij^-1)F_i; it proves horizontality, equivariance, the sign relation to the imported vertical-valued curvature, and D_A F_A = 0 under AC_omega. Its complete current proof was inspected and matches the planned use. These draft carriers are current and ready; their draft status and pending prechecks are not prerequisite gaps.

The published root mathematics interfaces def-gauge-group-of-a-smooth-principal-bundle and prop-gauge-transformations-act-on-principal-connections were read. A gauge transformation is a smooth equivariant bundle diffeomorphism over id_M; it acts by pushforward on horizontal distributions and transports vertical-valued curvature. The proof below uses local sections of a possibly nontrivial bundle and does not identify a global map M -> G with a gauge transformation.

The published lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma was read in full. Its compact-support integration-by-parts, parameter-differentiation, and scalar fundamental-lemma clauses apply in a Minkowski coordinate chart, choice-free. They support the Euler–Lagrange argument; the item still derives delta_A F_A = D_A delta A, the invariant-pairing integration identity, and the componentwise conclusion locally.

## Source reading and harvested-result ledger

Three independent full-text treatments were inspected for the A page. No retrieval failed, so no recovery retry or source_resolution disposition is required. All proofs in the authored carriers are local arguments; no source is used as a substitute for them.

1. **Matthijs Vákár, “Principal Bundles and Gauge Theories,” arXiv:2110.06334**, full HTML https://arxiv.org/html/2110.06334. Read the relevant arguments in §§1.3.1, 2.1, 2.6.4, and 2.6.5. These support the right-principal/connection convention, gauge automorphism distinction, compactly supported variational setup, and source-free Yang–Mills equation. The thesis does not construct a quantum theory or replace the local proofs here.

2. **Karl-Hermann Neeb, “Differential Topology of Fiber Bundles,” full lecture notes (2024)**, PDF https://www.math.fau.de/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/bundle-lect.pdf. Read Chapter 5 §5.4 and Chapter 6 §6.1, including the connection-form gauge action, local section transition, Cartan curvature, and curvature descent argument. These support geometry and convention checks; they do not supply a Yang–Mills action or physical conclusion.

3. **David Tong, “Gauge Theory,” full lecture notes, Chapter 2**, PDF https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/gauge-theory/gauge2.pdf. Read §§2.1.1–2.1.2, printed pp. 28–31 (PDF pp. 3–5). These state the natural-unit quadratic action, the classical equation, local gauge covariance, and the trace normalization convention. The displayed equation is a source check only; the author proof derives the variation and signs explicitly.

