items/pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-classical-gauge-invariance.md:86:[A2] [[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]: the global group is $SU(2)$, $P$ is the selected right principal bundle, there are no matter fields, and $\kappa$ is the normalized positive $\operatorname{Ad}$-invariant form.
items/pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-classical-gauge-invariance.md:88:[A3] [[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]: the adopted action density is $-\kappa(F\wedge *_\eta F)/(2g_{\mathrm{YM}}^2)$ with fixed $g_{\mathrm{YM}}>0$.
items/pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-classical-gauge-invariance.md:90:[F1] [[lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-invariant-form]]: $\kappa$ is positive definite and satisfies $\kappa(\operatorname{Ad}_gX,\operatorname{Ad}_gY)=\kappa(X,Y)$ for every $g\in SU(2)$.
items/pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-classical-gauge-invariance.md:92:[F2] [[def-gauge-group-of-a-smooth-principal-bundle]]: a gauge transformation is a smooth diffeomorphism $\Phi$ of $P$ with $\pi\circ\Phi=\pi$ and $\Phi(pg)=\Phi(p)g$.
items/pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-classical-gauge-invariance.md:94:[F3] [[prop-gauge-transformations-act-on-principal-connections]]: $H^\Phi=d\Phi(H)$ is a principal connection and its vertical-valued curvature obeys $K_{H^\Phi}(d\Phi X,d\Phi Y)=d\Phi(K_H(X,Y))$.
items/pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-classical-gauge-invariance.md:96:[F4] [[def-qftx-principal-connection-form]]: a connection form reproduces fundamental vertical vectors, has kernel $H$, and converts the vertical-valued curvature to the Lie-algebra-valued curvature.
items/pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-classical-gauge-invariance.md:98:[F5] [[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi]]: the Cartan curvature is horizontal and equivariant, equals $\omega\circ K_H$, and its local components obey the stated section-transition law.
items/pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-classical-gauge-invariance.md:100:[F6] [[def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative]]: the adjoint representation defines the associated bundle $\operatorname{ad}(P)=P\times_{\operatorname{Ad}}\mathfrak{su}(2)$; local adjoint-valued forms with the stated transition law represent global sections of its exterior-power bundles.
